    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this proposal specifically against NCEMS's community-scale synthesis criteria, I find the case for requiring a working group and center-level support reasonably well made in sections 7-9, where the team explicitly states they lack in-house bioinformatics expertise for large-scale multi-omics integration and see no alternative funding mechanism for this transitional analytical work. This aligns well with the RFP's requirement that the project exceed the capacity of a single lab. The team also appears to bring complementary cell biology, membrane trafficking, and biochemistry expertise, though the proposal does not explicitly name additional collaborators, their institutions, career stages, or geographic diversity, which is a notable gap relative to the RFP's explicit call for diverse, multi-institutional teams. Similarly, there is no explicit mention of trainee involvement or training plan for graduate students/postdocs, despite this being a required emphasis of the RFP ('Train the Next Generation'). Open science commitments are also thin: the proposal mentions writing manuscripts and a review article but does not describe data/code deposition, adherence to FAIR principles, or use of any community-endorsed reproducibility standards. These omissions are significant given how heavily the RFP weights collaborative team composition, training, and open science commitments alongside scientific merit."",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear articulation of why center-level bioinformatics support is needed beyond individual lab capacity; specific resource requests (staff scientist time, storage, compute) are itemized and justified relative to aims; genuine unmet need for computational partnership is well-argued."",
      ""weakness"": ""No description of team composition diversity (institutions, career stages, geography); no explicit trainee/training plan despite RFP's strong emphasis on this; no concrete open science/data-sharing plan (repositories, licensing, code availability); vague treatment of how findings would be made openly reproducible."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""While the scientific case for needing synthesis-center support is reasonably argued, the proposal falls short on several core RFP-specific requirements around team diversity, trainee involvement, and open science commitments, which are central to the funding mechanism's mission."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The robustness/bottleneck framing has emergent-systems relevance, but the proposal itself does not engage with the emergence framing explicitly enough to strongly satisfy this specific RFP theme."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is worthwhile, though the proposal's significance case is made mostly through cell biology framing rather than through the broader molecular/cellular emergence lens the RFP emphasizes."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Approach is reasonably staged but methodological detail is generic, and there is no mention of pre-registration, benchmarking, or validation plan for computational predictions."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope (five species, three omics layers) seems appropriately matched to the requested 2-year staffing, though a detailed timeline/milestone schedule is not provided."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is unambiguously a data synthesis effort using only public omics resources, well matching the core synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""General omics data types and species are identified, but no explicit list of public repositories/accessions is provided in the text, limiting assessment of data sufficiency."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal lacks any explicit commitment to depositing reanalyzed data, sharing analysis code/workflows, or adhering to specific open/reproducible science standards, which is a core RFP requirement.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"As a systems biologist focused on network robustness and modularity, I appreciate the proposal's engagement with robustness theory, particularly the citation that genetic networks contain trait-specific rather than master robustness alleles. This is a solid theoretical anchor. The concept of 'bottlenecks' as points of fragility in a buffered network is well-suited to network science approaches, and the plan to build cytokinetic gene networks and compare their topology and dynamics between heat-tolerant and heat-susceptible species is a legitimate systems-level question. That said, the proposal's use of the term 'genetic circuits' and 'networks' is largely metaphorical in Aim 1 and 2, since the actual analyses described (differential expression, co-expression clustering, benchmark transcript selection) are standard omics workflows rather than true network-theoretic analyses (e.g., centrality analysis, robustness/fragility metrics, in silico perturbation). Aim 3's multiplex network construction is the only place true network science is invoked, but details on how 'bottleneck' will be operationalized as a network metric (e.g., betweenness centrality, edge fragility) are absent. I would want to see clearer linkage between the stated conceptual framework of modular robustness and the specific graph-theoretic metrics that will be computed to identify bottlenecks.",Well-articulated conceptual framework rooted in established robustness theory; the tolerant vs. susceptible species comparison is a clever natural experiment for probing network robustness; three-species (or more) comparative design increases the generalizability of findings.,"The disconnect between stated theoretical framework (modular robustness, bottlenecks) and the fairly standard differential expression/co-expression methods described is significant; no explicit graph/network metrics are defined for identifying bottlenecks; the theory-building output (a 'review article') is a soft deliverable rather than a testable, quantitative model.",3,Theoretically motivated but methodologically under-formalized; the systems/network framing promises more than the described omics workflows can deliver without a more explicit graph-theoretic operationalization of bottlenecks.,4,"Robustness and its breakdown are quintessential emergent properties arising from interactions among many molecular components, and the comparative species approach to probe this is conceptually strong.",4,Testing robustness theory (trait-specific rather than master robustness alleles) empirically across species using heat stress on cytokinesis is a novel test case with broad significance for stress biology.,3,The theoretical framing is rigorous but the analytical methods proposed do not clearly operationalize network robustness/bottleneck concepts with specific graph metrics or perturbation simulations.,3,"Building single-layer networks, comparing across species, then constructing multiplex networks is a reasonable staged approach, though feasibility within stated resources is uncertain without more detail.",4,"Entirely dependent on integrating public omics datasets across species, which is squarely within the synthesis mission.",3,"General dataset types (single-cell transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics under heat stress) are named but specific public repositories or dataset IDs are not enumerated in the provided text.",3,The proposal mentions manuscript and theory-review publication but does not detail specific open data/code deposition plans or adherence to community standards.,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-10T08:07:49.964429,0,success,,"The proposal presents a strong theoretical framework based on network robustness and utilizes an effective comparative species design to study cytokinetic gene networks. However, the methodology in the first two aims relies on standard omics workflows rather than rigorous network-theoretic analyses. Furthermore, the final aim lacks specific details regarding the graph-theoretic metrics that will be used to operationalize and identify network bottlenecks.","The conceptual framework is strongly rooted in established network robustness theory. Additionally, the comparative design utilizing three or more heat-tolerant and heat-susceptible species serves as an effective natural experiment that enhances the generalizability of the findings.","There is a significant disconnect between the proposed theoretical framework and the standard differential expression methods described, with no specific graph-theoretic metrics defined to identify bottlenecks. Furthermore, the primary theoretical deliverable is a review article rather than a quantitative, testable model."
baseline::y1::1::claude-sonnet-5::004,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,4,baseline::y1::1::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:41:09.524240,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a plant cell biologist specializing in cytokinesis, I find the mechanistic grounding of this proposal to be its strongest feature. The PI team clearly has deep expertise in phragmoplast dynamics, cell plate maturation, and the molecular players involved (RABA2a, HINKEL, AtFH8, AIR9), and the preliminary data comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize responses to 38-45°C treatments is compelling and biologically grounded. The framing of 'cytokinetic bottlenecks' as genes whose products become dysfunctional under heat stress is a useful operational definition. However, I am concerned that the connection to 'emergence phenomena' at the molecular/cellular scale is somewhat implicit rather than explicit - the proposal would benefit from articulating how bottleneck identification reveals emergent network-level robustness rather than simply cataloging individual gene responses. Additionally, the plan to rely on existing single-cell and bulk omics datasets across five species collected under heterogeneous conditions (different ages, tissues, heat regimes, durations) raises concerns about comparability that are only superficially addressed in section 5.4. The biology is sound, but the computational strategy to handle batch effects and experimental heterogeneity needs more detail from a wet-lab perspective."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong mechanistic foundation in phragmoplast/cytokinesis biology; compelling preliminary data across three species with graded heat sensitivity; clear operational definitions (bottleneck concept); logical three-aim structure building from network construction to comparative analysis to integration."",
      ""weakness"": ""Insufficient explicit connection to emergence as a conceptual framework; the comparability of disparate public datasets (different developmental stages, heat stress durations, tissue types) is underexplored; over-reliance on transcript-level correlation as a proxy for functional bottlenecks without protein-level validation strategy."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically rich and well-motivated proposal grounded in genuine cell biology expertise, but it under-articulates the emergence framing and glosses over significant heterogeneity issues in the source datasets across five plant species."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project addresses robustness of a cellular process (cytokinesis) as an emergent property of an interconnected genetic network, but this framing is more implicit in the aims than explicitly developed; the proposal focuses more on cataloging differential gene expression than on emergent systems-level behavior."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Identifying cross-species cytokinetic robustness mechanisms under heat stress is a genuinely underexplored question with significant implications for climate resilience in agriculture and ecology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The biological rationale is rigorous, but the computational pipeline for cross-species, cross-dataset harmonization is described at a high level without sufficient methodological detail on batch correction or validation of predicted bottlenecks."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Five species, three omics layers, and multilayer network construction within what appears to be a 2-year timeline (given staff scientist requests) is ambitious and may need to be scoped down."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal appropriately relies entirely on reanalysis of existing public omics datasets rather than generating new data, consistent with the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""While datasets are referenced generally (single-cell transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics for five species), specific accessions or public repositories are not enumerated in the text provided, making it hard to assess data availability and quality."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal mentions manuscript and review article outputs but does not explicitly commit to depositing reanalyzed data, code, or workflows in public repositories.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From a computational biology and multi-omics integration standpoint, this proposal has an ambitious but underspecified analytical plan. The description of Aim 1's pipeline (compiling mitotic gene lists, orthology inference, co-expression benchmarking) is reasonable but generic and lacks specifics on which orthology tools, co-expression metrics, or statistical thresholds will be used. The plan to construct 'multiplex networks' integrating transcriptomic, translatomic, and proteomic layers in Aim 3 is conceptually appealing but technically very challenging given that translatome and proteome data are typically sparser and noisier than transcriptome data, and cross-species multi-omic integration methods are still maturing. The proposal does not specify which multilayer network algorithms will be used (e.g., MEGENA, WGCNA-based consensus networks, or true multiplex frameworks like muxViz), which is a critical gap for judging feasibility. The requested resources (staff scientist 4 months/year, assistant staff scientist 6 months/year, 50TB storage, 25,000 CPU hours) seem generally reasonable for a 2-year synthesis project, though the CPU hour estimate is not justified against specific dataset sizes."",
      ""strengths"": ""Multi-omics integration on heat stress cytokinesis is a novel angle; the staged aims (single-omics networks to multilayer integration) show logical planning; resource requests are itemized and plausible."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lack of specificity on computational tools and statistical methods for orthology mapping, co-expression thresholds, and multilayer network construction; no clear power analysis or dataset inventory demonstrating sufficient sample sizes exist publicly for five species under heat stress; validation/benchmarking strategy for predicted bottlenecks is missing."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually interesting multi-omics synthesis plan that would benefit substantially from more rigorous methodological specificity and an explicit data inventory before being fully fundable."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Network-level bottleneck identification is a reasonable proxy for emergent robustness, but the proposal does not engage deeply with emergence theory or systems-level formalisms beyond citing 'robustness alleles' literature."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Cross-omics, cross-species comparison of cytokinetic stress responses is novel; few if any studies have attempted this systematic comparison."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The methodological description is too generic; no specific bioinformatics tools, statistical cutoffs, or validation approaches are named, which undermines confidence in feasibility and reproducibility."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Three aims spanning gene network construction, cross-species comparison, and multilayer integration across five species and three omics types is a heavy scope for the apparent 2-year timeline implied by the staffing request."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is explicitly built on reanalysis of existing public multi-omics datasets, a good fit for the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal references a 'data sources table' that is not included in the provided text, and no specific database accessions, repositories, or sample sizes are given, making it impossible to verify data sufficiency."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Plans to publish a manuscript and review article are mentioned, but no explicit commitment to open code, standardized pipelines being shared, or FAIR data practices is stated.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a systems biologist focused on network robustness and modularity, I appreciate the proposal's engagement with robustness theory, particularly the citation that genetic networks contain trait-specific rather than master robustness alleles. This is a solid theoretical anchor. The concept of 'bottlenecks' as points of fragility in a buffered network is well-suited to network science approaches, and the plan to build cytokinetic gene networks and compare their topology and dynamics between heat-tolerant and heat-susceptible species is a legitimate systems-level question. That said, the proposal's use of the term 'genetic circuits' and 'networks' is largely metaphorical in Aim 1 and 2, since the actual analyses described (differential expression, co-expression clustering, benchmark transcript selection) are standard omics workflows rather than true network-theoretic analyses (e.g., centrality analysis, robustness/fragility metrics, in silico perturbation). Aim 3's multiplex network construction is the only place true network science is invoked, but details on how 'bottleneck' will be operationalized as a network metric (e.g., betweenness centrality, edge fragility) are absent. I would want to see clearer linkage between the stated conceptual framework of modular robustness and the specific graph-theoretic metrics that will be computed to identify bottlenecks."",
      ""strengths"": ""Well-articulated conceptual framework rooted in established robustness theory; the tolerant vs. susceptible species comparison is a clever natural experiment for probing network robustness; three-species (or more) comparative design increases the generalizability of findings."",
      ""weakness"": ""The disconnect between stated theoretical framework (modular robustness, bottlenecks) and the fairly standard differential expression/co-expression methods described is significant; no explicit graph/network metrics are defined for identifying bottlenecks; the theory-building output (a 'review article') is a soft deliverable rather than a testable, quantitative model."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Theoretically motivated but methodologically under-formalized; the systems/network framing promises more than the described omics workflows can deliver without a more explicit graph-theoretic operationalization of bottlenecks."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Robustness and its breakdown are quintessential emergent properties arising from interactions among many molecular components, and the comparative species approach to probe this is conceptually strong."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Testing robustness theory (trait-specific rather than master robustness alleles) empirically across species using heat stress on cytokinesis is a novel test case with broad significance for stress biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The theoretical framing is rigorous but the analytical methods proposed do not clearly operationalize network robustness/bottleneck concepts with specific graph metrics or perturbation simulations."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Building single-layer networks, comparing across species, then constructing multiplex networks is a reasonable staged approach, though feasibility within stated resources is uncertain without more detail."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Entirely dependent on integrating public omics datasets across species, which is squarely within the synthesis mission."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""General dataset types (single-cell transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics under heat stress) are named but specific public repositories or dataset IDs are not enumerated in the provided text."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal mentions manuscript and theory-review publication but does not detail specific open data/code deposition plans or adherence to community standards.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From the perspective of a plant stress physiologist working on climate adaptation, this proposal tackles an important and timely applied question: why do some crop and wild species maintain reproductive cell division under heat stress while others fail? The choice of maize as a heat-tolerant comparator is well justified by real-world yield data from the 2021 heat wave, and the inclusion of sorghum and rice alongside Arabidopsis and tobacco gives a reasonable phylogenetic and agronomic spread. However, I am concerned that the proposal conflates organismal heat tolerance (survival at high temperature) with cytokinetic robustness at the cellular level, when these may not be equivalent - a species could be organismally heat tolerant due to non-cytokinetic mechanisms (e.g., transpirational cooling, protein chaperone systems) unrelated to phragmoplast dynamics. The proposal does not sufficiently justify that available public datasets for these five species were generated under comparable developmental stages, tissue types, and heat stress regimes (temperature, duration) to allow meaningful cross-species comparison; this is a common pitfall in cross-species stress transcriptomics that is only briefly acknowledged in section 5.4 without a concrete mitigation plan beyond 'reanalyzing' and 'averaging noise via large datasets,' which is not a rigorous statistical justification."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong applied motivation tied to real agricultural outcomes (2021 heat wave, maize resilience); sensible choice of contrasting model and crop species; the biological question of cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is genuinely understudied and has translational potential for breeding heat-resilient crops."",
      ""weakness"": ""Conflation of organismal thermotolerance with cellular/cytokinetic robustness is not adequately addressed; insufficient discussion of confounds from differing developmental stages/tissues/heat regimes across the five species' public datasets; the mitigation strategy for dataset heterogeneity (aggregating many datasets to 'average out noise') is statistically weak and could mask true biological signal."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An agriculturally relevant and well-motivated question, but the proposal underestimates the confounds inherent in comparing heterogeneous public stress datasets across species with different physiologies and experimental designs."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal touches on emergent cellular robustness but frames it primarily through an applied lens of crop resilience rather than deeply exploring the emergent systems biology angle."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Linking cytokinetic robustness mechanisms to real-world crop heat resilience data is a novel and practically significant angle that could inform breeding programs."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The cross-species comparative design does not adequately control for confounding variables (developmental stage, tissue, stress regime) inherent to disparate public datasets, and the proposed noise-averaging strategy is not a substitute for rigorous statistical harmonization."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Comparing five species across three omics types is feasible in principle but risks running into data availability gaps for some species/conditions not discussed in the proposal."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort reanalyzing existing heat-stress omics datasets across species rather than generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""No specific dataset accessions or repositories are cited for the five species under heat stress conditions, and comparability across studies is not empirically demonstrated."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Publication plans exist but no explicit statement of open data repository deposition or standardized reproducible workflow sharing is provided.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this proposal specifically against NCEMS's community-scale synthesis criteria, I find the case for requiring a working group and center-level support reasonably well made in sections 7-9, where the team explicitly states they lack in-house bioinformatics expertise for large-scale multi-omics integration and see no alternative funding mechanism for this transitional analytical work. This aligns well with the RFP's requirement that the project exceed the capacity of a single lab. The team also appears to bring complementary cell biology, membrane trafficking, and biochemistry expertise, though the proposal does not explicitly name additional collaborators, their institutions, career stages, or geographic diversity, which is a notable gap relative to the RFP's explicit call for diverse, multi-institutional teams. Similarly, there is no explicit mention of trainee involvement or training plan for graduate students/postdocs, despite this being a required emphasis of the RFP ('Train the Next Generation'). Open science commitments are also thin: the proposal mentions writing manuscripts and a review article but does not describe data/code deposition, adherence to FAIR principles, or use of any community-endorsed reproducibility standards. These omissions are significant given how heavily the RFP weights collaborative team composition, training, and open science commitments alongside scientific merit."",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear articulation of why center-level bioinformatics support is needed beyond individual lab capacity; specific resource requests (staff scientist time, storage, compute) are itemized and justified relative to aims; genuine unmet need for computational partnership is well-argued."",
      ""weakness"": ""No description of team composition diversity (institutions, career stages, geography); no explicit trainee/training plan despite RFP's strong emphasis on this; no concrete open science/data-sharing plan (repositories, licensing, code availability); vague treatment of how findings would be made openly reproducible."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""While the scientific case for needing synthesis-center support is reasonably argued, the proposal falls short on several core RFP-specific requirements around team diversity, trainee involvement, and open science commitments, which are central to the funding mechanism's mission."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The robustness/bottleneck framing has emergent-systems relevance, but the proposal itself does not engage with the emergence framing explicitly enough to strongly satisfy this specific RFP theme."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is worthwhile, though the proposal's significance case is made mostly through cell biology framing rather than through the broader molecular/cellular emergence lens the RFP emphasizes."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Approach is reasonably staged but methodological detail is generic, and there is no mention of pre-registration, benchmarking, or validation plan for computational predictions."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope (five species, three omics layers) seems appropriately matched to the requested 2-year staffing, though a detailed timeline/milestone schedule is not provided."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is unambiguously a data synthesis effort using only public omics resources, well matching the core synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""General omics data types and species are identified, but no explicit list of public repositories/accessions is provided in the text, limiting assessment of data sufficiency."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal lacks any explicit commitment to depositing reanalyzed data, sharing analysis code/workflows, or adhering to specific open/reproducible science standards, which is a core RFP requirement.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"From the perspective of a plant stress physiologist working on climate adaptation, this proposal tackles an important and timely applied question: why do some crop and wild species maintain reproductive cell division under heat stress while others fail? The choice of maize as a heat-tolerant comparator is well justified by real-world yield data from the 2021 heat wave, and the inclusion of sorghum and rice alongside Arabidopsis and tobacco gives a reasonable phylogenetic and agronomic spread. However, I am concerned that the proposal conflates organismal heat tolerance (survival at high temperature) with cytokinetic robustness at the cellular level, when these may not be equivalent - a species could be organismally heat tolerant due to non-cytokinetic mechanisms (e.g., transpirational cooling, protein chaperone systems) unrelated to phragmoplast dynamics. The proposal does not sufficiently justify that available public datasets for these five species were generated under comparable developmental stages, tissue types, and heat stress regimes (temperature, duration) to allow meaningful cross-species comparison; this is a common pitfall in cross-species stress transcriptomics that is only briefly acknowledged in section 5.4 without a concrete mitigation plan beyond 'reanalyzing' and 'averaging noise via large datasets,' which is not a rigorous statistical justification.","Strong applied motivation tied to real agricultural outcomes (2021 heat wave, maize resilience); sensible choice of contrasting model and crop species; the biological question of cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is genuinely understudied and has translational potential for breeding heat-resilient crops.",Conflation of organismal thermotolerance with cellular/cytokinetic robustness is not adequately addressed; insufficient discussion of confounds from differing developmental stages/tissues/heat regimes across the five species' public datasets; the mitigation strategy for dataset heterogeneity (aggregating many datasets to 'average out noise') is statistically weak and could mask true biological signal.,3,"An agriculturally relevant and well-motivated question, but the proposal underestimates the confounds inherent in comparing heterogeneous public stress datasets across species with different physiologies and experimental designs.",3,The proposal touches on emergent cellular robustness but frames it primarily through an applied lens of crop resilience rather than deeply exploring the emergent systems biology angle.,4,Linking cytokinetic robustness mechanisms to real-world crop heat resilience data is a novel and practically significant angle that could inform breeding programs.,2,"The cross-species comparative design does not adequately control for confounding variables (developmental stage, tissue, stress regime) inherent to disparate public datasets, and the proposed noise-averaging strategy is not a substitute for rigorous statistical harmonization.",3,Comparing five species across three omics types is feasible in principle but risks running into data availability gaps for some species/conditions not discussed in the proposal.,4,The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort reanalyzing existing heat-stress omics datasets across species rather than generating new experimental data.,2,"No specific dataset accessions or repositories are cited for the five species under heat stress conditions, and comparability across studies is not empirically demonstrated.",3,Publication plans exist but no explicit statement of open data repository deposition or standardized reproducible workflow sharing is provided.,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:14:35.585224,0,success,,"This proposal addresses the timely and understudied question of how heat stress affects reproductive cell division across diverse plant species. While the study's applied motivation and selection of contrasting model and crop species are well justified, the methodology raises significant concerns. Specifically, the proposal conflates organismal thermotolerance with cellular cytokinetic robustness and lacks a rigorous statistical plan to address confounding variables in the heterogeneous public datasets.","The proposal tackles an important, understudied biological question with high translational potential for breeding heat-resilient crops. The study is grounded in real-world agricultural outcomes and features a well-justified selection of both model and crop species.","A major conceptual limitation is the conflation of organismal thermotolerance with cellular cytokinetic robustness. Furthermore, the reliance on heterogeneous public datasets introduces unaddressed confounding factors, such as differing developmental stages and heat regimes, which are poorly mitigated by the proposed strategy of averaging out data noise."
baseline::y1::1::claude-sonnet-5::005,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,5,baseline::y1::1::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:41:09.524240,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a plant cell biologist specializing in cytokinesis, I find the mechanistic grounding of this proposal to be its strongest feature. The PI team clearly has deep expertise in phragmoplast dynamics, cell plate maturation, and the molecular players involved (RABA2a, HINKEL, AtFH8, AIR9), and the preliminary data comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize responses to 38-45°C treatments is compelling and biologically grounded. The framing of 'cytokinetic bottlenecks' as genes whose products become dysfunctional under heat stress is a useful operational definition. However, I am concerned that the connection to 'emergence phenomena' at the molecular/cellular scale is somewhat implicit rather than explicit - the proposal would benefit from articulating how bottleneck identification reveals emergent network-level robustness rather than simply cataloging individual gene responses. Additionally, the plan to rely on existing single-cell and bulk omics datasets across five species collected under heterogeneous conditions (different ages, tissues, heat regimes, durations) raises concerns about comparability that are only superficially addressed in section 5.4. The biology is sound, but the computational strategy to handle batch effects and experimental heterogeneity needs more detail from a wet-lab perspective."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong mechanistic foundation in phragmoplast/cytokinesis biology; compelling preliminary data across three species with graded heat sensitivity; clear operational definitions (bottleneck concept); logical three-aim structure building from network construction to comparative analysis to integration."",
      ""weakness"": ""Insufficient explicit connection to emergence as a conceptual framework; the comparability of disparate public datasets (different developmental stages, heat stress durations, tissue types) is underexplored; over-reliance on transcript-level correlation as a proxy for functional bottlenecks without protein-level validation strategy."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically rich and well-motivated proposal grounded in genuine cell biology expertise, but it under-articulates the emergence framing and glosses over significant heterogeneity issues in the source datasets across five plant species."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project addresses robustness of a cellular process (cytokinesis) as an emergent property of an interconnected genetic network, but this framing is more implicit in the aims than explicitly developed; the proposal focuses more on cataloging differential gene expression than on emergent systems-level behavior."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Identifying cross-species cytokinetic robustness mechanisms under heat stress is a genuinely underexplored question with significant implications for climate resilience in agriculture and ecology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The biological rationale is rigorous, but the computational pipeline for cross-species, cross-dataset harmonization is described at a high level without sufficient methodological detail on batch correction or validation of predicted bottlenecks."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Five species, three omics layers, and multilayer network construction within what appears to be a 2-year timeline (given staff scientist requests) is ambitious and may need to be scoped down."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal appropriately relies entirely on reanalysis of existing public omics datasets rather than generating new data, consistent with the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""While datasets are referenced generally (single-cell transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics for five species), specific accessions or public repositories are not enumerated in the text provided, making it hard to assess data availability and quality."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal mentions manuscript and review article outputs but does not explicitly commit to depositing reanalyzed data, code, or workflows in public repositories.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From a computational biology and multi-omics integration standpoint, this proposal has an ambitious but underspecified analytical plan. The description of Aim 1's pipeline (compiling mitotic gene lists, orthology inference, co-expression benchmarking) is reasonable but generic and lacks specifics on which orthology tools, co-expression metrics, or statistical thresholds will be used. The plan to construct 'multiplex networks' integrating transcriptomic, translatomic, and proteomic layers in Aim 3 is conceptually appealing but technically very challenging given that translatome and proteome data are typically sparser and noisier than transcriptome data, and cross-species multi-omic integration methods are still maturing. The proposal does not specify which multilayer network algorithms will be used (e.g., MEGENA, WGCNA-based consensus networks, or true multiplex frameworks like muxViz), which is a critical gap for judging feasibility. The requested resources (staff scientist 4 months/year, assistant staff scientist 6 months/year, 50TB storage, 25,000 CPU hours) seem generally reasonable for a 2-year synthesis project, though the CPU hour estimate is not justified against specific dataset sizes."",
      ""strengths"": ""Multi-omics integration on heat stress cytokinesis is a novel angle; the staged aims (single-omics networks to multilayer integration) show logical planning; resource requests are itemized and plausible."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lack of specificity on computational tools and statistical methods for orthology mapping, co-expression thresholds, and multilayer network construction; no clear power analysis or dataset inventory demonstrating sufficient sample sizes exist publicly for five species under heat stress; validation/benchmarking strategy for predicted bottlenecks is missing."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually interesting multi-omics synthesis plan that would benefit substantially from more rigorous methodological specificity and an explicit data inventory before being fully fundable."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Network-level bottleneck identification is a reasonable proxy for emergent robustness, but the proposal does not engage deeply with emergence theory or systems-level formalisms beyond citing 'robustness alleles' literature."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Cross-omics, cross-species comparison of cytokinetic stress responses is novel; few if any studies have attempted this systematic comparison."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The methodological description is too generic; no specific bioinformatics tools, statistical cutoffs, or validation approaches are named, which undermines confidence in feasibility and reproducibility."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Three aims spanning gene network construction, cross-species comparison, and multilayer integration across five species and three omics types is a heavy scope for the apparent 2-year timeline implied by the staffing request."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is explicitly built on reanalysis of existing public multi-omics datasets, a good fit for the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal references a 'data sources table' that is not included in the provided text, and no specific database accessions, repositories, or sample sizes are given, making it impossible to verify data sufficiency."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Plans to publish a manuscript and review article are mentioned, but no explicit commitment to open code, standardized pipelines being shared, or FAIR data practices is stated.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a systems biologist focused on network robustness and modularity, I appreciate the proposal's engagement with robustness theory, particularly the citation that genetic networks contain trait-specific rather than master robustness alleles. This is a solid theoretical anchor. The concept of 'bottlenecks' as points of fragility in a buffered network is well-suited to network science approaches, and the plan to build cytokinetic gene networks and compare their topology and dynamics between heat-tolerant and heat-susceptible species is a legitimate systems-level question. That said, the proposal's use of the term 'genetic circuits' and 'networks' is largely metaphorical in Aim 1 and 2, since the actual analyses described (differential expression, co-expression clustering, benchmark transcript selection) are standard omics workflows rather than true network-theoretic analyses (e.g., centrality analysis, robustness/fragility metrics, in silico perturbation). Aim 3's multiplex network construction is the only place true network science is invoked, but details on how 'bottleneck' will be operationalized as a network metric (e.g., betweenness centrality, edge fragility) are absent. I would want to see clearer linkage between the stated conceptual framework of modular robustness and the specific graph-theoretic metrics that will be computed to identify bottlenecks."",
      ""strengths"": ""Well-articulated conceptual framework rooted in established robustness theory; the tolerant vs. susceptible species comparison is a clever natural experiment for probing network robustness; three-species (or more) comparative design increases the generalizability of findings."",
      ""weakness"": ""The disconnect between stated theoretical framework (modular robustness, bottlenecks) and the fairly standard differential expression/co-expression methods described is significant; no explicit graph/network metrics are defined for identifying bottlenecks; the theory-building output (a 'review article') is a soft deliverable rather than a testable, quantitative model."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Theoretically motivated but methodologically under-formalized; the systems/network framing promises more than the described omics workflows can deliver without a more explicit graph-theoretic operationalization of bottlenecks."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Robustness and its breakdown are quintessential emergent properties arising from interactions among many molecular components, and the comparative species approach to probe this is conceptually strong."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Testing robustness theory (trait-specific rather than master robustness alleles) empirically across species using heat stress on cytokinesis is a novel test case with broad significance for stress biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The theoretical framing is rigorous but the analytical methods proposed do not clearly operationalize network robustness/bottleneck concepts with specific graph metrics or perturbation simulations."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Building single-layer networks, comparing across species, then constructing multiplex networks is a reasonable staged approach, though feasibility within stated resources is uncertain without more detail."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Entirely dependent on integrating public omics datasets across species, which is squarely within the synthesis mission."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""General dataset types (single-cell transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics under heat stress) are named but specific public repositories or dataset IDs are not enumerated in the provided text."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal mentions manuscript and theory-review publication but does not detail specific open data/code deposition plans or adherence to community standards.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From the perspective of a plant stress physiologist working on climate adaptation, this proposal tackles an important and timely applied question: why do some crop and wild species maintain reproductive cell division under heat stress while others fail? The choice of maize as a heat-tolerant comparator is well justified by real-world yield data from the 2021 heat wave, and the inclusion of sorghum and rice alongside Arabidopsis and tobacco gives a reasonable phylogenetic and agronomic spread. However, I am concerned that the proposal conflates organismal heat tolerance (survival at high temperature) with cytokinetic robustness at the cellular level, when these may not be equivalent - a species could be organismally heat tolerant due to non-cytokinetic mechanisms (e.g., transpirational cooling, protein chaperone systems) unrelated to phragmoplast dynamics. The proposal does not sufficiently justify that available public datasets for these five species were generated under comparable developmental stages, tissue types, and heat stress regimes (temperature, duration) to allow meaningful cross-species comparison; this is a common pitfall in cross-species stress transcriptomics that is only briefly acknowledged in section 5.4 without a concrete mitigation plan beyond 'reanalyzing' and 'averaging noise via large datasets,' which is not a rigorous statistical justification."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong applied motivation tied to real agricultural outcomes (2021 heat wave, maize resilience); sensible choice of contrasting model and crop species; the biological question of cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is genuinely understudied and has translational potential for breeding heat-resilient crops."",
      ""weakness"": ""Conflation of organismal thermotolerance with cellular/cytokinetic robustness is not adequately addressed; insufficient discussion of confounds from differing developmental stages/tissues/heat regimes across the five species' public datasets; the mitigation strategy for dataset heterogeneity (aggregating many datasets to 'average out noise') is statistically weak and could mask true biological signal."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An agriculturally relevant and well-motivated question, but the proposal underestimates the confounds inherent in comparing heterogeneous public stress datasets across species with different physiologies and experimental designs."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal touches on emergent cellular robustness but frames it primarily through an applied lens of crop resilience rather than deeply exploring the emergent systems biology angle."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Linking cytokinetic robustness mechanisms to real-world crop heat resilience data is a novel and practically significant angle that could inform breeding programs."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The cross-species comparative design does not adequately control for confounding variables (developmental stage, tissue, stress regime) inherent to disparate public datasets, and the proposed noise-averaging strategy is not a substitute for rigorous statistical harmonization."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Comparing five species across three omics types is feasible in principle but risks running into data availability gaps for some species/conditions not discussed in the proposal."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort reanalyzing existing heat-stress omics datasets across species rather than generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""No specific dataset accessions or repositories are cited for the five species under heat stress conditions, and comparability across studies is not empirically demonstrated."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Publication plans exist but no explicit statement of open data repository deposition or standardized reproducible workflow sharing is provided.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this proposal specifically against NCEMS's community-scale synthesis criteria, I find the case for requiring a working group and center-level support reasonably well made in sections 7-9, where the team explicitly states they lack in-house bioinformatics expertise for large-scale multi-omics integration and see no alternative funding mechanism for this transitional analytical work. This aligns well with the RFP's requirement that the project exceed the capacity of a single lab. The team also appears to bring complementary cell biology, membrane trafficking, and biochemistry expertise, though the proposal does not explicitly name additional collaborators, their institutions, career stages, or geographic diversity, which is a notable gap relative to the RFP's explicit call for diverse, multi-institutional teams. Similarly, there is no explicit mention of trainee involvement or training plan for graduate students/postdocs, despite this being a required emphasis of the RFP ('Train the Next Generation'). Open science commitments are also thin: the proposal mentions writing manuscripts and a review article but does not describe data/code deposition, adherence to FAIR principles, or use of any community-endorsed reproducibility standards. These omissions are significant given how heavily the RFP weights collaborative team composition, training, and open science commitments alongside scientific merit."",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear articulation of why center-level bioinformatics support is needed beyond individual lab capacity; specific resource requests (staff scientist time, storage, compute) are itemized and justified relative to aims; genuine unmet need for computational partnership is well-argued."",
      ""weakness"": ""No description of team composition diversity (institutions, career stages, geography); no explicit trainee/training plan despite RFP's strong emphasis on this; no concrete open science/data-sharing plan (repositories, licensing, code availability); vague treatment of how findings would be made openly reproducible."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""While the scientific case for needing synthesis-center support is reasonably argued, the proposal falls short on several core RFP-specific requirements around team diversity, trainee involvement, and open science commitments, which are central to the funding mechanism's mission."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The robustness/bottleneck framing has emergent-systems relevance, but the proposal itself does not engage with the emergence framing explicitly enough to strongly satisfy this specific RFP theme."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is worthwhile, though the proposal's significance case is made mostly through cell biology framing rather than through the broader molecular/cellular emergence lens the RFP emphasizes."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Approach is reasonably staged but methodological detail is generic, and there is no mention of pre-registration, benchmarking, or validation plan for computational predictions."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope (five species, three omics layers) seems appropriately matched to the requested 2-year staffing, though a detailed timeline/milestone schedule is not provided."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is unambiguously a data synthesis effort using only public omics resources, well matching the core synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""General omics data types and species are identified, but no explicit list of public repositories/accessions is provided in the text, limiting assessment of data sufficiency."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal lacks any explicit commitment to depositing reanalyzed data, sharing analysis code/workflows, or adhering to specific open/reproducible science standards, which is a core RFP requirement.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewing this proposal specifically against NCEMS's community-scale synthesis criteria, I find the case for requiring a working group and center-level support reasonably well made in sections 7-9, where the team explicitly states they lack in-house bioinformatics expertise for large-scale multi-omics integration and see no alternative funding mechanism for this transitional analytical work. This aligns well with the RFP's requirement that the project exceed the capacity of a single lab. The team also appears to bring complementary cell biology, membrane trafficking, and biochemistry expertise, though the proposal does not explicitly name additional collaborators, their institutions, career stages, or geographic diversity, which is a notable gap relative to the RFP's explicit call for diverse, multi-institutional teams. Similarly, there is no explicit mention of trainee involvement or training plan for graduate students/postdocs, despite this being a required emphasis of the RFP ('Train the Next Generation'). Open science commitments are also thin: the proposal mentions writing manuscripts and a review article but does not describe data/code deposition, adherence to FAIR principles, or use of any community-endorsed reproducibility standards. These omissions are significant given how heavily the RFP weights collaborative team composition, training, and open science commitments alongside scientific merit.","Clear articulation of why center-level bioinformatics support is needed beyond individual lab capacity; specific resource requests (staff scientist time, storage, compute) are itemized and justified relative to aims; genuine unmet need for computational partnership is well-argued.","No description of team composition diversity (institutions, career stages, geography); no explicit trainee/training plan despite RFP's strong emphasis on this; no concrete open science/data-sharing plan (repositories, licensing, code availability); vague treatment of how findings would be made openly reproducible.",2,"While the scientific case for needing synthesis-center support is reasonably argued, the proposal falls short on several core RFP-specific requirements around team diversity, trainee involvement, and open science commitments, which are central to the funding mechanism's mission.",3,"The robustness/bottleneck framing has emergent-systems relevance, but the proposal itself does not engage with the emergence framing explicitly enough to strongly satisfy this specific RFP theme.",3,"The biological question is worthwhile, though the proposal's significance case is made mostly through cell biology framing rather than through the broader molecular/cellular emergence lens the RFP emphasizes.",3,"Approach is reasonably staged but methodological detail is generic, and there is no mention of pre-registration, benchmarking, or validation plan for computational predictions.",3,"The scope (five species, three omics layers) seems appropriately matched to the requested 2-year staffing, though a detailed timeline/milestone schedule is not provided.",4,"The project is unambiguously a data synthesis effort using only public omics resources, well matching the core synthesis mandate.",3,"General omics data types and species are identified, but no explicit list of public repositories/accessions is provided in the text, limiting assessment of data sufficiency.",1,"The proposal lacks any explicit commitment to depositing reanalyzed data, sharing analysis code/workflows, or adhering to specific open/reproducible science standards, which is a core RFP requirement.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:14:43.901852,0,success,,"The proposal effectively justifies the need for center-level support and a working group, as the required multi-omics integration exceeds the capacity of a single laboratory. The research team possesses complementary expertise in cell biology, membrane trafficking, and biochemistry. However, the proposal lacks critical details required by the RFP, including team diversity metrics, explicit training plans for early-career researchers, and comprehensive open science commitments.","The proposal clearly justifies the need for center-level bioinformatics support and computational partnerships. Additionally, specific resource requests, such as staff time and computational storage, are well-itemized and aligned with the project aims.","The proposal fails to outline team composition diversity and lacks an explicit training plan for trainees. Furthermore, it does not provide concrete commitments to open science, such as data-sharing plans, adherence to FAIR principles, or reproducibility standards."
baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,1,baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:38:10.298599,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior plant cytokinesis and cytoskeletal cell biologist. This proposal addresses an important and underexplored question: why cytokinesis fails under heat stress in some plant species but remains robust in others. The biological framing is strong, and the focus on the phragmoplast, vesicle trafficking, callose-cellulose transition, microtubule turnover, and cytokinetic gene networks is well aligned with current gaps in plant cell biology. The preliminary observations comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize provide a credible motivation for a synthesis effort. I find the core idea compelling: publicly available transcriptomic, translatomic, proteomic, and single-cell datasets could be used to infer heat-sensitive modules and candidate cytokinetic bottlenecks. However, the proposal would benefit from clearer specification of datasets, explicit cytokinesis markers, and a more detailed plan for distinguishing true cytokinesis-specific effects from general heat-shock, cell-cycle, developmental, or tissue-composition effects. The proposal also does not sufficiently describe open science practices or trainee development, both of which are central to this call. Overall, I view this as a strong biological concept with moderate methodological underdevelopment."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal identifies a compelling molecular-cellular problem with broad relevance to plant resilience under heat waves. It has a strong mechanistic foundation in plant cytokinesis and phragmoplast biology. The comparative framing across heat-sensitive and heat-resilient species is attractive and could reveal conserved versus lineage-specific robustness mechanisms. The work is clearly based on synthesis of existing omics datasets and is beyond a typical single-lab effort if done rigorously across species and data modalities. The preliminary observations in Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize make the hypothesis plausible."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not provide a sufficiently concrete inventory of public datasets, accessions, sample types, stress regimes, developmental stages, or expected coverage of cytokinetic cells. It assumes that bulk heat-stress omics data will be informative for cytokinesis, but cytokinetic cells are rare and tissue-composition effects may dominate. The definition of a bottleneck as a gene product that becomes dysfunctional or less functional is biologically meaningful but difficult to infer from transcriptomics alone. The analysis plan is high level and lacks validation strategies, benchmark performance metrics, or explicit controls for cell cycle phase, orthology uncertainty, batch effects, and stress duration differences. Open science and training commitments are minimal."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically compelling and call-relevant synthesis project with strong plant cell biology motivation, but the proposal needs a more rigorous and detailed data-analysis plan and stronger commitments to open science and training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is plausibly an emergent property of interacting cytoskeletal, trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory networks. The proposal explicitly seeks network-level bottlenecks rather than isolated genes. However, the emergent-phenomena framing could be sharpened by defining measurable system-level properties of cytokinetic robustness."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The comparative analysis of cytokinesis under heat stress across temperate and tropical or heat-resilient plant species is novel and significant. The outcome could guide future mechanistic experiments and potentially inform crop resilience. The significance is strongest at the hypothesis-generation level rather than immediate translational application."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed aims are logical, but the computational strategy is described only broadly. Key issues such as cell-cycle annotation, cross-species orthology, confounding by tissue type and growth conditions, and integration of bulk and single-cell data are acknowledged only superficially. The proposal would be stronger with specific pipelines, benchmarks, and sensitivity analyses."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is plausible for a focused synthesis, but the scope across five species, multiple omics modalities, single-cell reprocessing, transcription factor inference, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex network construction is ambitious. The requested staff support is appropriate but may not be sufficient if dataset harmonization proves difficult."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is explicitly built around reanalysis and integration of existing publicly available omics datasets and does not propose new experimental data generation. It fits the synthesis-center model well."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies broad data types and target species, but it does not provide enough detail about actual datasets, accessions, metadata completeness, sample sizes, or heat-stress comparability. This is a significant limitation for assessing feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that manuscripts and a theory/review will be produced, but it provides little detail about public repositories, reproducible workflows, code release, metadata standards, licensing, or community governance. The open-science plan is underdeveloped.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist specializing in single-cell multi-omics integration. The proposal is well suited in spirit to a synthesis center because it proposes to reprocess public data across species and modalities to answer a biological question that cannot be answered from one dataset. However, from a computational standpoint, the application is under-specified. It lists standard analysis components such as normalization, dimensionality reduction, differential expression, correlation networks, trajectory analysis, and multiplex networks, but it does not explain how these methods will be selected, benchmarked, integrated, or made robust to severe heterogeneity in the source data. The central inference task is difficult: identifying cytokinesis-specific heat bottlenecks from public datasets that were likely generated for diverse biological purposes. The proposal needs a stronger metadata model, a dataset inclusion/exclusion plan, and explicit statistical approaches to cross-study integration. I am enthusiastic about the question but only moderately confident in the current analytic plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a real data-synthesis challenge involving cross-species, cross-platform, and multi-omic integration. It recognizes the need to reprocess raw data through standardized pipelines, which is essential. The plan to use known mitotic and cytokinetic genes as anchors for identifying coexpressed modules is reasonable. The project would benefit substantially from NCEMS bioinformatics support and computing infrastructure. The intended output, a harmonized cytokinesis network resource across plants, could be valuable to the community."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational plan is too generic. It does not specify which public datasets will be included, what metadata fields are required, how batch correction will be performed, how single-cell and bulk datasets will be jointly modeled, how proteomic/translatomic evidence will be weighted relative to transcriptomic evidence, or how uncertainty in orthology will be propagated. Correlation networks from heterogeneous public data can produce spurious hubs, and the proposal does not describe negative controls, permutation tests, replication thresholds, or validation against known cytokinesis components. The assumption that including many datasets will average out growth-condition noise is not statistically sufficient. There is little detail on workflow reproducibility."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising synthesis concept with clear need for computational collaboration, but the current proposal lacks the methodological specificity needed for high confidence in the proposed multi-omic network inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The goal of identifying robustness and failure points emerging from multilayer gene, protein, and regulatory networks is appropriate for emergent molecular and cellular phenomena. The proposal could more explicitly formulate network-level emergent properties and quantitative endpoints."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Cross-species identification of cytokinetic heat bottlenecks is novel and potentially significant. The integration of single-cell and whole-organ omics around cytokinesis is a useful direction. Significance would increase if the proposal committed to a reusable data product rather than only candidate bottlenecks and manuscripts."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists many standard tools but lacks a rigorous statistical framework. There is insufficient detail on dataset harmonization, batch correction, orthology mapping, cell-type and cell-cycle annotation, multi-omics integration, uncertainty quantification, and evaluation metrics."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope is broad but potentially manageable with strong staff support if narrowed to a well-curated dataset set. As written, five species plus transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics, single-cell analysis, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex modeling may exceed the proposed two-year effort."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal is squarely focused on public data reanalysis and integration. It does not depend on new experimental data, although the long-term motivation is future experimental testing."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The application mentions a data sources table, but the provided proposal text does not include dataset names, accessions, sample counts, platforms, or metadata quality. This makes feasibility difficult to evaluate."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not provide an adequate plan for version-controlled code, containerized workflows, public release of processed data, provenance tracking, or community documentation. These elements are especially important for a computational synthesis project.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior plant stress physiologist and evolutionary ecologist. The proposal addresses a timely problem: understanding plant cellular resilience to extreme heat. I appreciate that the investigators avoid treating heat tolerance as a single organismal trait and instead focus on cytokinesis as a possible cellular bottleneck for reproductive development and seed production. The comparative angle among Arabidopsis, tobacco, maize, rice, sorghum, and possibly other species could reveal how cell division programs have adapted across climates and life histories. That said, the ecological and stress-physiology framing is somewhat oversimplified. Heat treatments differ in temperature, duration, developmental context, acclimation history, and tissue water status, and these variables strongly shape heat-stress responses. The proposal does not yet explain how it will distinguish cytokinesis-specific robustness from general thermotolerance, developmental stage effects, or differences between monocots and dicots. The work is still worthwhile as a discovery synthesis, but the conclusions should be framed cautiously."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project is motivated by an important environmental challenge: heat waves and plant reproductive resilience. The comparison of heat-susceptible and heat-resilient species is compelling. The proposal appropriately recognizes that robustness may arise from modular buffering of cellular networks rather than a universal heat-tolerance allele. The synthesis of public omics data could identify hypotheses that would be difficult to generate from single-species experiments. The project has potential relevance to crop resilience and biodiversity under climate change."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal conflates heat tolerance at the whole-plant or crop-yield level with cytokinetic heat resilience without sufficient evidence that these scales map directly. Arabidopsis lethality at about 35°C versus maize tolerance at higher temperatures is a broad comparison that may reflect many traits unrelated to cytokinesis. Public heat-stress datasets are likely to vary dramatically in stress regime, acclimation, tissue, genotype, developmental stage, and sampling time. The proposal needs a stronger environmental metadata framework and a plan to classify heat exposures biologically. It also lacks a clear strategy for selecting tropical or heat-adapted comparators beyond maize and does not address phylogenetic non-independence."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A significant and timely synthesis question with good biological motivation, but the proposal needs a more sophisticated treatment of heat-stress physiology, environmental metadata, and comparative evolutionary inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal relates to emergent cellular robustness, but the link between molecular network responses and organismal heat resilience is not fully developed. Emergence is implicit rather than operationally defined."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The focus on cytokinesis as a heat-sensitive cellular bottleneck is novel and significant. It offers a fresh perspective on plant heat resilience. The broad claims about predicting ecosystem or crop performance are premature but the core scientific question is important."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall comparative approach is reasonable, but important confounders in stress physiology are not adequately handled. The plan to reanalyze datasets is necessary but not sufficient without a detailed metadata harmonization and stratified analysis strategy."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The aims are feasible if the project limits itself to carefully selected datasets and hypothesis generation. The current scope is broad, particularly if meaningful cross-species and cross-environmental comparisons are expected within two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis proposal in that it relies on existing public data and aims to integrate disparate omics resources. It does not propose new experiments during the working group period."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names species and data types but does not present sufficient detail on available heat-stress datasets or their environmental metadata. For this topic, metadata quality is central to feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is largely limited to publication intentions. There is no detailed commitment to public workflows, harmonized metadata release, community standards, or trainee-facing documentation.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist focused on biological robustness and network modeling. The proposal is intellectually attractive because it seeks to move from lists of heat-responsive genes to a theory of cytokinetic robustness. The conceptual framework invoking modular robustness, network hubs, less-connected nodes, and bottlenecks is appropriate for the funding call. The idea that cytokinesis depends on coordinated cytoskeletal, membrane-trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory layers makes this a good candidate for emergent network analysis. However, the network-modeling plan remains mostly aspirational. The proposal does not define what network topology or dynamic property would constitute a bottleneck, how causality will be distinguished from correlation, or how networks inferred from heterogeneous public omics data will be validated. A useful synthesis product could result, but the current proposal overstates the ability of transcriptomic and proteomic associations to identify dysfunctional gene products."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal has a strong systems-level premise and explicitly seeks to integrate multiple data layers into multilayer cytokinesis-specific networks. It asks a question that requires collaborative expertise in cell biology, plant stress biology, bioinformatics, and network modeling. The focus on conserved versus species-specific responses is well suited to synthesis research. If successful, the work could generate a valuable framework for prioritizing candidate robustness factors and bottlenecks for later experimental testing."",
      ""weakness"": ""The definition of bottleneck is not operationalized in a way that can be inferred from the proposed data. A gene product becoming dysfunctional cannot usually be inferred from transcript abundance, translation, or even protein abundance without activity, localization, interaction, or phenotype data. The proposal does not define network null models, edge-confidence metrics, criteria for multilayer integration, or methods for comparing networks across species. The theory-building outcome is interesting but may be premature without a clearer analytic framework. Training and open science elements are not described in sufficient detail."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong systems-biology proposal that fits the call, but its network and bottleneck inference methods require much more precision to support the stated conclusions."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal directly targets robustness as an emergent property of interacting cytokinetic modules. The multilayer network concept is highly aligned with emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Developing a cytokinetic robustness framework under heat stress is novel and potentially impactful. The significance lies in identifying organizing principles of cellular resilience, though practical applications are longer term."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposal lacks formal definitions and statistical machinery for network inference, multilayer integration, cross-species comparison, and bottleneck prioritization. Without these, the analyses may generate plausible but weakly supported candidate lists."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed activities are ambitious for two years but could be feasible as a hypothesis-generating synthesis if the network modeling is simplified and milestones are clearly staged. As written, the theory-generation goal may exceed the evidence base."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is an excellent example of synthesis in its reliance on existing public data and integration across modalities and species. It clearly requires expertise beyond one lab."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The relevant data categories are identified, but the absence of explicit datasets and metadata prevents assessment of whether enough high-quality data exist for robust network inference."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not describe how networks, code, processed data, or analysis notebooks will be shared. For a network synthesis project, public release of reproducible network-building workflows should be central.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior scholar in open, team, and reproducible science with experience managing synthesis-center working groups. This proposal has a strong scientific motivation and a clear need for synthesis-center support, particularly bioinformatics staff and computational infrastructure. The proposed topic is cross-disciplinary and could benefit from a working group that brings together plant cell biologists, stress physiologists, computational biologists, network scientists, and trainees. However, the proposal as written does not yet fully meet several community-scale working group expectations. The current team description is narrow and does not specify the broader collaborative partnership, geographic or institutional diversity, career-stage representation, trainee roles, governance, meeting structure, or mechanisms for community input. The open science plan is also much too limited for this call. The application would be much stronger if it presented concrete deliverables such as curated public dataset catalogs, standardized metadata, reproducible workflows, containers, tutorials, trainee-led hackathons, and a public knowledge base of cytokinetic genes and orthologs."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project clearly relies on public data and seeks to synthesize information beyond the capacity of one laboratory. The requested NCEMS resources are well justified at a general level, especially staff bioinformatics support and computing. The biological question is suitable for collaborative synthesis and could serve as an effective training platform for graduate students and postdocs in omics reanalysis, cross-species integration, and reproducible workflows. The proposal has potential to create community resources if deliverables are expanded."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides little evidence of an assembled multidisciplinary working group beyond the investigators' existing expertise. It does not describe team governance, collaboration practices, trainee training plans, authorship/data policies, code review, reproducibility standards, public engagement, or maintenance of outputs. The open-science commitment is mostly implicit and does not meet the expectations of a synthesis-center call. The request for staff time is reasonable, but the proposal risks treating NCEMS primarily as a bioinformatics service rather than as a platform for community-scale collaboration."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The scientific idea is strong and synthesis-oriented, but the proposal is underdeveloped as a community-scale working group with open, team-based, reproducible science and trainee training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The scientific focus on cytokinetic robustness emerging from interacting cellular modules is relevant to the call. The proposal could better articulate how working group synthesis will reveal emergent principles rather than produce only candidate genes."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is novel and significant, and the integration of plant cytokinesis with heat-stress omics is promising. The significance for the broader community would be higher if the proposal committed to reusable resources and training materials."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The aims are coherent, but the approach lacks operational detail about data curation, reproducibility, quality control, and collaborative workflow management. These are important components of rigor for synthesis research."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year plan is reasonable for producing harmonized datasets and a candidate bottleneck analysis, but too ambitious if it also aims to develop a broad theory of cytokinetic robustness without a more structured project-management plan."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly centered on existing public data and multi-dataset integration. However, the working group rationale is framed largely as access to professional bioinformatics help rather than as a fully developed community-scale synthesis collaboration."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies general sources by organism and modality, but it does not provide a dataset catalog, accessions, metadata plan, or prioritization criteria. A working group should begin with a clearer data landscape."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is inadequate for this call. There is no concrete plan for repositories, code release, workflow reproducibility, metadata standards, licensing, public documentation, training modules, or community-created policies.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 1: Senior plant cytokinesis and cytoskeletal cell biologist. This proposal addresses an important and underexplored question: why cytokinesis fails under heat stress in some plant species but remains robust in others. The biological framing is strong, and the focus on the phragmoplast, vesicle trafficking, callose-cellulose transition, microtubule turnover, and cytokinetic gene networks is well aligned with current gaps in plant cell biology. The preliminary observations comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize provide a credible motivation for a synthesis effort. I find the core idea compelling: publicly available transcriptomic, translatomic, proteomic, and single-cell datasets could be used to infer heat-sensitive modules and candidate cytokinetic bottlenecks. However, the proposal would benefit from clearer specification of datasets, explicit cytokinesis markers, and a more detailed plan for distinguishing true cytokinesis-specific effects from general heat-shock, cell-cycle, developmental, or tissue-composition effects. The proposal also does not sufficiently describe open science practices or trainee development, both of which are central to this call. Overall, I view this as a strong biological concept with moderate methodological underdevelopment.","The proposal identifies a compelling molecular-cellular problem with broad relevance to plant resilience under heat waves. It has a strong mechanistic foundation in plant cytokinesis and phragmoplast biology. The comparative framing across heat-sensitive and heat-resilient species is attractive and could reveal conserved versus lineage-specific robustness mechanisms. The work is clearly based on synthesis of existing omics datasets and is beyond a typical single-lab effort if done rigorously across species and data modalities. The preliminary observations in Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize make the hypothesis plausible.","The proposal does not provide a sufficiently concrete inventory of public datasets, accessions, sample types, stress regimes, developmental stages, or expected coverage of cytokinetic cells. It assumes that bulk heat-stress omics data will be informative for cytokinesis, but cytokinetic cells are rare and tissue-composition effects may dominate. The definition of a bottleneck as a gene product that becomes dysfunctional or less functional is biologically meaningful but difficult to infer from transcriptomics alone. The analysis plan is high level and lacks validation strategies, benchmark performance metrics, or explicit controls for cell cycle phase, orthology uncertainty, batch effects, and stress duration differences. Open science and training commitments are minimal.",4,"A biologically compelling and call-relevant synthesis project with strong plant cell biology motivation, but the proposal needs a more rigorous and detailed data-analysis plan and stronger commitments to open science and training.",4,"Cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is plausibly an emergent property of interacting cytoskeletal, trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory networks. The proposal explicitly seeks network-level bottlenecks rather than isolated genes. However, the emergent-phenomena framing could be sharpened by defining measurable system-level properties of cytokinetic robustness.",4,The comparative analysis of cytokinesis under heat stress across temperate and tropical or heat-resilient plant species is novel and significant. The outcome could guide future mechanistic experiments and potentially inform crop resilience. The significance is strongest at the hypothesis-generation level rather than immediate translational application.,3,"The proposed aims are logical, but the computational strategy is described only broadly. Key issues such as cell-cycle annotation, cross-species orthology, confounding by tissue type and growth conditions, and integration of bulk and single-cell data are acknowledged only superficially. The proposal would be stronger with specific pipelines, benchmarks, and sensitivity analyses.",3,"The two-year timeline is plausible for a focused synthesis, but the scope across five species, multiple omics modalities, single-cell reprocessing, transcription factor inference, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex network construction is ambitious. The requested staff support is appropriate but may not be sufficient if dataset harmonization proves difficult.",5,The project is explicitly built around reanalysis and integration of existing publicly available omics datasets and does not propose new experimental data generation. It fits the synthesis-center model well.,3,"The proposal identifies broad data types and target species, but it does not provide enough detail about actual datasets, accessions, metadata completeness, sample sizes, or heat-stress comparability. This is a significant limitation for assessing feasibility.",2,"The proposal states that manuscripts and a theory/review will be produced, but it provides little detail about public repositories, reproducible workflows, code release, metadata standards, licensing, or community governance. The open-science plan is underdeveloped.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:14:53.199466,0,success,,"This proposal investigates the mechanisms behind plant cytokinesis failure under heat stress by synthesizing multi-omics datasets across multiple species. While the biological concept and comparative framework are well-founded, the methodological approach requires significant refinement. Specifically, the proposal lacks concrete details regarding dataset selection, validation strategies, and methods to isolate cytokinesis-specific effects from general stress responses. Additionally, the application does not sufficiently address required open science practices and trainee development plans.","The proposal identifies a significant, highly relevant biological problem with a strong mechanistic foundation in plant cytokinesis. Its comparative framework across heat-sensitive and heat-resilient species offers a compelling approach to uncovering conserved and lineage-specific robustness mechanisms. Furthermore, preliminary observations effectively support the feasibility of the central hypothesis.","The proposal lacks a concrete inventory of datasets and fails to address how rare cytokinetic cells will be accurately analyzed using bulk omics data. The analytical plan is underdeveloped, missing essential validation strategies, benchmark metrics, and explicit controls for confounding variables like cell cycle phase and batch effects. Additionally, commitments to open science and trainee development are insufficiently detailed."
baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::002,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,2,baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:38:10.298599,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior plant cytokinesis and cytoskeletal cell biologist. This proposal addresses an important and underexplored question: why cytokinesis fails under heat stress in some plant species but remains robust in others. The biological framing is strong, and the focus on the phragmoplast, vesicle trafficking, callose-cellulose transition, microtubule turnover, and cytokinetic gene networks is well aligned with current gaps in plant cell biology. The preliminary observations comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize provide a credible motivation for a synthesis effort. I find the core idea compelling: publicly available transcriptomic, translatomic, proteomic, and single-cell datasets could be used to infer heat-sensitive modules and candidate cytokinetic bottlenecks. However, the proposal would benefit from clearer specification of datasets, explicit cytokinesis markers, and a more detailed plan for distinguishing true cytokinesis-specific effects from general heat-shock, cell-cycle, developmental, or tissue-composition effects. The proposal also does not sufficiently describe open science practices or trainee development, both of which are central to this call. Overall, I view this as a strong biological concept with moderate methodological underdevelopment."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal identifies a compelling molecular-cellular problem with broad relevance to plant resilience under heat waves. It has a strong mechanistic foundation in plant cytokinesis and phragmoplast biology. The comparative framing across heat-sensitive and heat-resilient species is attractive and could reveal conserved versus lineage-specific robustness mechanisms. The work is clearly based on synthesis of existing omics datasets and is beyond a typical single-lab effort if done rigorously across species and data modalities. The preliminary observations in Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize make the hypothesis plausible."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not provide a sufficiently concrete inventory of public datasets, accessions, sample types, stress regimes, developmental stages, or expected coverage of cytokinetic cells. It assumes that bulk heat-stress omics data will be informative for cytokinesis, but cytokinetic cells are rare and tissue-composition effects may dominate. The definition of a bottleneck as a gene product that becomes dysfunctional or less functional is biologically meaningful but difficult to infer from transcriptomics alone. The analysis plan is high level and lacks validation strategies, benchmark performance metrics, or explicit controls for cell cycle phase, orthology uncertainty, batch effects, and stress duration differences. Open science and training commitments are minimal."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically compelling and call-relevant synthesis project with strong plant cell biology motivation, but the proposal needs a more rigorous and detailed data-analysis plan and stronger commitments to open science and training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is plausibly an emergent property of interacting cytoskeletal, trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory networks. The proposal explicitly seeks network-level bottlenecks rather than isolated genes. However, the emergent-phenomena framing could be sharpened by defining measurable system-level properties of cytokinetic robustness."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The comparative analysis of cytokinesis under heat stress across temperate and tropical or heat-resilient plant species is novel and significant. The outcome could guide future mechanistic experiments and potentially inform crop resilience. The significance is strongest at the hypothesis-generation level rather than immediate translational application."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed aims are logical, but the computational strategy is described only broadly. Key issues such as cell-cycle annotation, cross-species orthology, confounding by tissue type and growth conditions, and integration of bulk and single-cell data are acknowledged only superficially. The proposal would be stronger with specific pipelines, benchmarks, and sensitivity analyses."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is plausible for a focused synthesis, but the scope across five species, multiple omics modalities, single-cell reprocessing, transcription factor inference, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex network construction is ambitious. The requested staff support is appropriate but may not be sufficient if dataset harmonization proves difficult."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is explicitly built around reanalysis and integration of existing publicly available omics datasets and does not propose new experimental data generation. It fits the synthesis-center model well."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies broad data types and target species, but it does not provide enough detail about actual datasets, accessions, metadata completeness, sample sizes, or heat-stress comparability. This is a significant limitation for assessing feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that manuscripts and a theory/review will be produced, but it provides little detail about public repositories, reproducible workflows, code release, metadata standards, licensing, or community governance. The open-science plan is underdeveloped.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist specializing in single-cell multi-omics integration. The proposal is well suited in spirit to a synthesis center because it proposes to reprocess public data across species and modalities to answer a biological question that cannot be answered from one dataset. However, from a computational standpoint, the application is under-specified. It lists standard analysis components such as normalization, dimensionality reduction, differential expression, correlation networks, trajectory analysis, and multiplex networks, but it does not explain how these methods will be selected, benchmarked, integrated, or made robust to severe heterogeneity in the source data. The central inference task is difficult: identifying cytokinesis-specific heat bottlenecks from public datasets that were likely generated for diverse biological purposes. The proposal needs a stronger metadata model, a dataset inclusion/exclusion plan, and explicit statistical approaches to cross-study integration. I am enthusiastic about the question but only moderately confident in the current analytic plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a real data-synthesis challenge involving cross-species, cross-platform, and multi-omic integration. It recognizes the need to reprocess raw data through standardized pipelines, which is essential. The plan to use known mitotic and cytokinetic genes as anchors for identifying coexpressed modules is reasonable. The project would benefit substantially from NCEMS bioinformatics support and computing infrastructure. The intended output, a harmonized cytokinesis network resource across plants, could be valuable to the community."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational plan is too generic. It does not specify which public datasets will be included, what metadata fields are required, how batch correction will be performed, how single-cell and bulk datasets will be jointly modeled, how proteomic/translatomic evidence will be weighted relative to transcriptomic evidence, or how uncertainty in orthology will be propagated. Correlation networks from heterogeneous public data can produce spurious hubs, and the proposal does not describe negative controls, permutation tests, replication thresholds, or validation against known cytokinesis components. The assumption that including many datasets will average out growth-condition noise is not statistically sufficient. There is little detail on workflow reproducibility."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising synthesis concept with clear need for computational collaboration, but the current proposal lacks the methodological specificity needed for high confidence in the proposed multi-omic network inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The goal of identifying robustness and failure points emerging from multilayer gene, protein, and regulatory networks is appropriate for emergent molecular and cellular phenomena. The proposal could more explicitly formulate network-level emergent properties and quantitative endpoints."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Cross-species identification of cytokinetic heat bottlenecks is novel and potentially significant. The integration of single-cell and whole-organ omics around cytokinesis is a useful direction. Significance would increase if the proposal committed to a reusable data product rather than only candidate bottlenecks and manuscripts."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists many standard tools but lacks a rigorous statistical framework. There is insufficient detail on dataset harmonization, batch correction, orthology mapping, cell-type and cell-cycle annotation, multi-omics integration, uncertainty quantification, and evaluation metrics."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope is broad but potentially manageable with strong staff support if narrowed to a well-curated dataset set. As written, five species plus transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics, single-cell analysis, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex modeling may exceed the proposed two-year effort."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal is squarely focused on public data reanalysis and integration. It does not depend on new experimental data, although the long-term motivation is future experimental testing."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The application mentions a data sources table, but the provided proposal text does not include dataset names, accessions, sample counts, platforms, or metadata quality. This makes feasibility difficult to evaluate."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not provide an adequate plan for version-controlled code, containerized workflows, public release of processed data, provenance tracking, or community documentation. These elements are especially important for a computational synthesis project.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior plant stress physiologist and evolutionary ecologist. The proposal addresses a timely problem: understanding plant cellular resilience to extreme heat. I appreciate that the investigators avoid treating heat tolerance as a single organismal trait and instead focus on cytokinesis as a possible cellular bottleneck for reproductive development and seed production. The comparative angle among Arabidopsis, tobacco, maize, rice, sorghum, and possibly other species could reveal how cell division programs have adapted across climates and life histories. That said, the ecological and stress-physiology framing is somewhat oversimplified. Heat treatments differ in temperature, duration, developmental context, acclimation history, and tissue water status, and these variables strongly shape heat-stress responses. The proposal does not yet explain how it will distinguish cytokinesis-specific robustness from general thermotolerance, developmental stage effects, or differences between monocots and dicots. The work is still worthwhile as a discovery synthesis, but the conclusions should be framed cautiously."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project is motivated by an important environmental challenge: heat waves and plant reproductive resilience. The comparison of heat-susceptible and heat-resilient species is compelling. The proposal appropriately recognizes that robustness may arise from modular buffering of cellular networks rather than a universal heat-tolerance allele. The synthesis of public omics data could identify hypotheses that would be difficult to generate from single-species experiments. The project has potential relevance to crop resilience and biodiversity under climate change."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal conflates heat tolerance at the whole-plant or crop-yield level with cytokinetic heat resilience without sufficient evidence that these scales map directly. Arabidopsis lethality at about 35°C versus maize tolerance at higher temperatures is a broad comparison that may reflect many traits unrelated to cytokinesis. Public heat-stress datasets are likely to vary dramatically in stress regime, acclimation, tissue, genotype, developmental stage, and sampling time. The proposal needs a stronger environmental metadata framework and a plan to classify heat exposures biologically. It also lacks a clear strategy for selecting tropical or heat-adapted comparators beyond maize and does not address phylogenetic non-independence."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A significant and timely synthesis question with good biological motivation, but the proposal needs a more sophisticated treatment of heat-stress physiology, environmental metadata, and comparative evolutionary inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal relates to emergent cellular robustness, but the link between molecular network responses and organismal heat resilience is not fully developed. Emergence is implicit rather than operationally defined."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The focus on cytokinesis as a heat-sensitive cellular bottleneck is novel and significant. It offers a fresh perspective on plant heat resilience. The broad claims about predicting ecosystem or crop performance are premature but the core scientific question is important."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall comparative approach is reasonable, but important confounders in stress physiology are not adequately handled. The plan to reanalyze datasets is necessary but not sufficient without a detailed metadata harmonization and stratified analysis strategy."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The aims are feasible if the project limits itself to carefully selected datasets and hypothesis generation. The current scope is broad, particularly if meaningful cross-species and cross-environmental comparisons are expected within two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis proposal in that it relies on existing public data and aims to integrate disparate omics resources. It does not propose new experiments during the working group period."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names species and data types but does not present sufficient detail on available heat-stress datasets or their environmental metadata. For this topic, metadata quality is central to feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is largely limited to publication intentions. There is no detailed commitment to public workflows, harmonized metadata release, community standards, or trainee-facing documentation.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist focused on biological robustness and network modeling. The proposal is intellectually attractive because it seeks to move from lists of heat-responsive genes to a theory of cytokinetic robustness. The conceptual framework invoking modular robustness, network hubs, less-connected nodes, and bottlenecks is appropriate for the funding call. The idea that cytokinesis depends on coordinated cytoskeletal, membrane-trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory layers makes this a good candidate for emergent network analysis. However, the network-modeling plan remains mostly aspirational. The proposal does not define what network topology or dynamic property would constitute a bottleneck, how causality will be distinguished from correlation, or how networks inferred from heterogeneous public omics data will be validated. A useful synthesis product could result, but the current proposal overstates the ability of transcriptomic and proteomic associations to identify dysfunctional gene products."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal has a strong systems-level premise and explicitly seeks to integrate multiple data layers into multilayer cytokinesis-specific networks. It asks a question that requires collaborative expertise in cell biology, plant stress biology, bioinformatics, and network modeling. The focus on conserved versus species-specific responses is well suited to synthesis research. If successful, the work could generate a valuable framework for prioritizing candidate robustness factors and bottlenecks for later experimental testing."",
      ""weakness"": ""The definition of bottleneck is not operationalized in a way that can be inferred from the proposed data. A gene product becoming dysfunctional cannot usually be inferred from transcript abundance, translation, or even protein abundance without activity, localization, interaction, or phenotype data. The proposal does not define network null models, edge-confidence metrics, criteria for multilayer integration, or methods for comparing networks across species. The theory-building outcome is interesting but may be premature without a clearer analytic framework. Training and open science elements are not described in sufficient detail."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong systems-biology proposal that fits the call, but its network and bottleneck inference methods require much more precision to support the stated conclusions."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal directly targets robustness as an emergent property of interacting cytokinetic modules. The multilayer network concept is highly aligned with emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Developing a cytokinetic robustness framework under heat stress is novel and potentially impactful. The significance lies in identifying organizing principles of cellular resilience, though practical applications are longer term."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposal lacks formal definitions and statistical machinery for network inference, multilayer integration, cross-species comparison, and bottleneck prioritization. Without these, the analyses may generate plausible but weakly supported candidate lists."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed activities are ambitious for two years but could be feasible as a hypothesis-generating synthesis if the network modeling is simplified and milestones are clearly staged. As written, the theory-generation goal may exceed the evidence base."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is an excellent example of synthesis in its reliance on existing public data and integration across modalities and species. It clearly requires expertise beyond one lab."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The relevant data categories are identified, but the absence of explicit datasets and metadata prevents assessment of whether enough high-quality data exist for robust network inference."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not describe how networks, code, processed data, or analysis notebooks will be shared. For a network synthesis project, public release of reproducible network-building workflows should be central.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior scholar in open, team, and reproducible science with experience managing synthesis-center working groups. This proposal has a strong scientific motivation and a clear need for synthesis-center support, particularly bioinformatics staff and computational infrastructure. The proposed topic is cross-disciplinary and could benefit from a working group that brings together plant cell biologists, stress physiologists, computational biologists, network scientists, and trainees. However, the proposal as written does not yet fully meet several community-scale working group expectations. The current team description is narrow and does not specify the broader collaborative partnership, geographic or institutional diversity, career-stage representation, trainee roles, governance, meeting structure, or mechanisms for community input. The open science plan is also much too limited for this call. The application would be much stronger if it presented concrete deliverables such as curated public dataset catalogs, standardized metadata, reproducible workflows, containers, tutorials, trainee-led hackathons, and a public knowledge base of cytokinetic genes and orthologs."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project clearly relies on public data and seeks to synthesize information beyond the capacity of one laboratory. The requested NCEMS resources are well justified at a general level, especially staff bioinformatics support and computing. The biological question is suitable for collaborative synthesis and could serve as an effective training platform for graduate students and postdocs in omics reanalysis, cross-species integration, and reproducible workflows. The proposal has potential to create community resources if deliverables are expanded."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides little evidence of an assembled multidisciplinary working group beyond the investigators' existing expertise. It does not describe team governance, collaboration practices, trainee training plans, authorship/data policies, code review, reproducibility standards, public engagement, or maintenance of outputs. The open-science commitment is mostly implicit and does not meet the expectations of a synthesis-center call. The request for staff time is reasonable, but the proposal risks treating NCEMS primarily as a bioinformatics service rather than as a platform for community-scale collaboration."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The scientific idea is strong and synthesis-oriented, but the proposal is underdeveloped as a community-scale working group with open, team-based, reproducible science and trainee training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The scientific focus on cytokinetic robustness emerging from interacting cellular modules is relevant to the call. The proposal could better articulate how working group synthesis will reveal emergent principles rather than produce only candidate genes."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is novel and significant, and the integration of plant cytokinesis with heat-stress omics is promising. The significance for the broader community would be higher if the proposal committed to reusable resources and training materials."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The aims are coherent, but the approach lacks operational detail about data curation, reproducibility, quality control, and collaborative workflow management. These are important components of rigor for synthesis research."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year plan is reasonable for producing harmonized datasets and a candidate bottleneck analysis, but too ambitious if it also aims to develop a broad theory of cytokinetic robustness without a more structured project-management plan."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly centered on existing public data and multi-dataset integration. However, the working group rationale is framed largely as access to professional bioinformatics help rather than as a fully developed community-scale synthesis collaboration."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies general sources by organism and modality, but it does not provide a dataset catalog, accessions, metadata plan, or prioritization criteria. A working group should begin with a clearer data landscape."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is inadequate for this call. There is no concrete plan for repositories, code release, workflow reproducibility, metadata standards, licensing, public documentation, training modules, or community-created policies.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist specializing in single-cell multi-omics integration. The proposal is well suited in spirit to a synthesis center because it proposes to reprocess public data across species and modalities to answer a biological question that cannot be answered from one dataset. However, from a computational standpoint, the application is under-specified. It lists standard analysis components such as normalization, dimensionality reduction, differential expression, correlation networks, trajectory analysis, and multiplex networks, but it does not explain how these methods will be selected, benchmarked, integrated, or made robust to severe heterogeneity in the source data. The central inference task is difficult: identifying cytokinesis-specific heat bottlenecks from public datasets that were likely generated for diverse biological purposes. The proposal needs a stronger metadata model, a dataset inclusion/exclusion plan, and explicit statistical approaches to cross-study integration. I am enthusiastic about the question but only moderately confident in the current analytic plan.","The proposal addresses a real data-synthesis challenge involving cross-species, cross-platform, and multi-omic integration. It recognizes the need to reprocess raw data through standardized pipelines, which is essential. The plan to use known mitotic and cytokinetic genes as anchors for identifying coexpressed modules is reasonable. The project would benefit substantially from NCEMS bioinformatics support and computing infrastructure. The intended output, a harmonized cytokinesis network resource across plants, could be valuable to the community.","The computational plan is too generic. It does not specify which public datasets will be included, what metadata fields are required, how batch correction will be performed, how single-cell and bulk datasets will be jointly modeled, how proteomic/translatomic evidence will be weighted relative to transcriptomic evidence, or how uncertainty in orthology will be propagated. Correlation networks from heterogeneous public data can produce spurious hubs, and the proposal does not describe negative controls, permutation tests, replication thresholds, or validation against known cytokinesis components. The assumption that including many datasets will average out growth-condition noise is not statistically sufficient. There is little detail on workflow reproducibility.",3,"A promising synthesis concept with clear need for computational collaboration, but the current proposal lacks the methodological specificity needed for high confidence in the proposed multi-omic network inference.",4,"The goal of identifying robustness and failure points emerging from multilayer gene, protein, and regulatory networks is appropriate for emergent molecular and cellular phenomena. The proposal could more explicitly formulate network-level emergent properties and quantitative endpoints.",4,Cross-species identification of cytokinetic heat bottlenecks is novel and potentially significant. The integration of single-cell and whole-organ omics around cytokinesis is a useful direction. Significance would increase if the proposal committed to a reusable data product rather than only candidate bottlenecks and manuscripts.,2,"The approach lists many standard tools but lacks a rigorous statistical framework. There is insufficient detail on dataset harmonization, batch correction, orthology mapping, cell-type and cell-cycle annotation, multi-omics integration, uncertainty quantification, and evaluation metrics.",3,"The scope is broad but potentially manageable with strong staff support if narrowed to a well-curated dataset set. As written, five species plus transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics, single-cell analysis, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex modeling may exceed the proposed two-year effort.",5,"The proposal is squarely focused on public data reanalysis and integration. It does not depend on new experimental data, although the long-term motivation is future experimental testing.",2,"The application mentions a data sources table, but the provided proposal text does not include dataset names, accessions, sample counts, platforms, or metadata quality. This makes feasibility difficult to evaluate.",2,"The proposal does not provide an adequate plan for version-controlled code, containerized workflows, public release of processed data, provenance tracking, or community documentation. These elements are especially important for a computational synthesis project.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:15:06.073796,0,success,,"The proposal aligns well with synthesis center objectives by aiming to reprocess multi-omic public data across species to address complex biological questions. However, the computational framework lacks necessary specificity regarding method selection, benchmarking, and cross-study statistical integration. While the overarching biological inquiry is compelling, the current analytic plan requires a more rigorous approach to data heterogeneity and dataset inclusion.","The project tackles significant cross-species and multi-omic integration challenges and correctly identifies the need for standardized raw data reprocessing. Utilizing known mitotic genes as analytical anchors is a sound strategy, and the resulting harmonized cytokinesis network resource would offer substantial value to the scientific community.","The computational methodology is overly generic, lacking specific protocols for dataset selection, batch correction, multi-omics weighting, and statistical validation. The proposed correlation networks risk generating spurious hubs without appropriate negative controls or permutation testing, and the assumption that dataset volume will automatically mitigate noise is inadequately supported."
baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::003,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,3,baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:38:10.298599,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior plant cytokinesis and cytoskeletal cell biologist. This proposal addresses an important and underexplored question: why cytokinesis fails under heat stress in some plant species but remains robust in others. The biological framing is strong, and the focus on the phragmoplast, vesicle trafficking, callose-cellulose transition, microtubule turnover, and cytokinetic gene networks is well aligned with current gaps in plant cell biology. The preliminary observations comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize provide a credible motivation for a synthesis effort. I find the core idea compelling: publicly available transcriptomic, translatomic, proteomic, and single-cell datasets could be used to infer heat-sensitive modules and candidate cytokinetic bottlenecks. However, the proposal would benefit from clearer specification of datasets, explicit cytokinesis markers, and a more detailed plan for distinguishing true cytokinesis-specific effects from general heat-shock, cell-cycle, developmental, or tissue-composition effects. The proposal also does not sufficiently describe open science practices or trainee development, both of which are central to this call. Overall, I view this as a strong biological concept with moderate methodological underdevelopment."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal identifies a compelling molecular-cellular problem with broad relevance to plant resilience under heat waves. It has a strong mechanistic foundation in plant cytokinesis and phragmoplast biology. The comparative framing across heat-sensitive and heat-resilient species is attractive and could reveal conserved versus lineage-specific robustness mechanisms. The work is clearly based on synthesis of existing omics datasets and is beyond a typical single-lab effort if done rigorously across species and data modalities. The preliminary observations in Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize make the hypothesis plausible."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not provide a sufficiently concrete inventory of public datasets, accessions, sample types, stress regimes, developmental stages, or expected coverage of cytokinetic cells. It assumes that bulk heat-stress omics data will be informative for cytokinesis, but cytokinetic cells are rare and tissue-composition effects may dominate. The definition of a bottleneck as a gene product that becomes dysfunctional or less functional is biologically meaningful but difficult to infer from transcriptomics alone. The analysis plan is high level and lacks validation strategies, benchmark performance metrics, or explicit controls for cell cycle phase, orthology uncertainty, batch effects, and stress duration differences. Open science and training commitments are minimal."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically compelling and call-relevant synthesis project with strong plant cell biology motivation, but the proposal needs a more rigorous and detailed data-analysis plan and stronger commitments to open science and training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is plausibly an emergent property of interacting cytoskeletal, trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory networks. The proposal explicitly seeks network-level bottlenecks rather than isolated genes. However, the emergent-phenomena framing could be sharpened by defining measurable system-level properties of cytokinetic robustness."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The comparative analysis of cytokinesis under heat stress across temperate and tropical or heat-resilient plant species is novel and significant. The outcome could guide future mechanistic experiments and potentially inform crop resilience. The significance is strongest at the hypothesis-generation level rather than immediate translational application."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed aims are logical, but the computational strategy is described only broadly. Key issues such as cell-cycle annotation, cross-species orthology, confounding by tissue type and growth conditions, and integration of bulk and single-cell data are acknowledged only superficially. The proposal would be stronger with specific pipelines, benchmarks, and sensitivity analyses."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is plausible for a focused synthesis, but the scope across five species, multiple omics modalities, single-cell reprocessing, transcription factor inference, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex network construction is ambitious. The requested staff support is appropriate but may not be sufficient if dataset harmonization proves difficult."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is explicitly built around reanalysis and integration of existing publicly available omics datasets and does not propose new experimental data generation. It fits the synthesis-center model well."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies broad data types and target species, but it does not provide enough detail about actual datasets, accessions, metadata completeness, sample sizes, or heat-stress comparability. This is a significant limitation for assessing feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that manuscripts and a theory/review will be produced, but it provides little detail about public repositories, reproducible workflows, code release, metadata standards, licensing, or community governance. The open-science plan is underdeveloped.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist specializing in single-cell multi-omics integration. The proposal is well suited in spirit to a synthesis center because it proposes to reprocess public data across species and modalities to answer a biological question that cannot be answered from one dataset. However, from a computational standpoint, the application is under-specified. It lists standard analysis components such as normalization, dimensionality reduction, differential expression, correlation networks, trajectory analysis, and multiplex networks, but it does not explain how these methods will be selected, benchmarked, integrated, or made robust to severe heterogeneity in the source data. The central inference task is difficult: identifying cytokinesis-specific heat bottlenecks from public datasets that were likely generated for diverse biological purposes. The proposal needs a stronger metadata model, a dataset inclusion/exclusion plan, and explicit statistical approaches to cross-study integration. I am enthusiastic about the question but only moderately confident in the current analytic plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a real data-synthesis challenge involving cross-species, cross-platform, and multi-omic integration. It recognizes the need to reprocess raw data through standardized pipelines, which is essential. The plan to use known mitotic and cytokinetic genes as anchors for identifying coexpressed modules is reasonable. The project would benefit substantially from NCEMS bioinformatics support and computing infrastructure. The intended output, a harmonized cytokinesis network resource across plants, could be valuable to the community."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational plan is too generic. It does not specify which public datasets will be included, what metadata fields are required, how batch correction will be performed, how single-cell and bulk datasets will be jointly modeled, how proteomic/translatomic evidence will be weighted relative to transcriptomic evidence, or how uncertainty in orthology will be propagated. Correlation networks from heterogeneous public data can produce spurious hubs, and the proposal does not describe negative controls, permutation tests, replication thresholds, or validation against known cytokinesis components. The assumption that including many datasets will average out growth-condition noise is not statistically sufficient. There is little detail on workflow reproducibility."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising synthesis concept with clear need for computational collaboration, but the current proposal lacks the methodological specificity needed for high confidence in the proposed multi-omic network inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The goal of identifying robustness and failure points emerging from multilayer gene, protein, and regulatory networks is appropriate for emergent molecular and cellular phenomena. The proposal could more explicitly formulate network-level emergent properties and quantitative endpoints."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Cross-species identification of cytokinetic heat bottlenecks is novel and potentially significant. The integration of single-cell and whole-organ omics around cytokinesis is a useful direction. Significance would increase if the proposal committed to a reusable data product rather than only candidate bottlenecks and manuscripts."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists many standard tools but lacks a rigorous statistical framework. There is insufficient detail on dataset harmonization, batch correction, orthology mapping, cell-type and cell-cycle annotation, multi-omics integration, uncertainty quantification, and evaluation metrics."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope is broad but potentially manageable with strong staff support if narrowed to a well-curated dataset set. As written, five species plus transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics, single-cell analysis, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex modeling may exceed the proposed two-year effort."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal is squarely focused on public data reanalysis and integration. It does not depend on new experimental data, although the long-term motivation is future experimental testing."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The application mentions a data sources table, but the provided proposal text does not include dataset names, accessions, sample counts, platforms, or metadata quality. This makes feasibility difficult to evaluate."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not provide an adequate plan for version-controlled code, containerized workflows, public release of processed data, provenance tracking, or community documentation. These elements are especially important for a computational synthesis project.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior plant stress physiologist and evolutionary ecologist. The proposal addresses a timely problem: understanding plant cellular resilience to extreme heat. I appreciate that the investigators avoid treating heat tolerance as a single organismal trait and instead focus on cytokinesis as a possible cellular bottleneck for reproductive development and seed production. The comparative angle among Arabidopsis, tobacco, maize, rice, sorghum, and possibly other species could reveal how cell division programs have adapted across climates and life histories. That said, the ecological and stress-physiology framing is somewhat oversimplified. Heat treatments differ in temperature, duration, developmental context, acclimation history, and tissue water status, and these variables strongly shape heat-stress responses. The proposal does not yet explain how it will distinguish cytokinesis-specific robustness from general thermotolerance, developmental stage effects, or differences between monocots and dicots. The work is still worthwhile as a discovery synthesis, but the conclusions should be framed cautiously."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project is motivated by an important environmental challenge: heat waves and plant reproductive resilience. The comparison of heat-susceptible and heat-resilient species is compelling. The proposal appropriately recognizes that robustness may arise from modular buffering of cellular networks rather than a universal heat-tolerance allele. The synthesis of public omics data could identify hypotheses that would be difficult to generate from single-species experiments. The project has potential relevance to crop resilience and biodiversity under climate change."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal conflates heat tolerance at the whole-plant or crop-yield level with cytokinetic heat resilience without sufficient evidence that these scales map directly. Arabidopsis lethality at about 35°C versus maize tolerance at higher temperatures is a broad comparison that may reflect many traits unrelated to cytokinesis. Public heat-stress datasets are likely to vary dramatically in stress regime, acclimation, tissue, genotype, developmental stage, and sampling time. The proposal needs a stronger environmental metadata framework and a plan to classify heat exposures biologically. It also lacks a clear strategy for selecting tropical or heat-adapted comparators beyond maize and does not address phylogenetic non-independence."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A significant and timely synthesis question with good biological motivation, but the proposal needs a more sophisticated treatment of heat-stress physiology, environmental metadata, and comparative evolutionary inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal relates to emergent cellular robustness, but the link between molecular network responses and organismal heat resilience is not fully developed. Emergence is implicit rather than operationally defined."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The focus on cytokinesis as a heat-sensitive cellular bottleneck is novel and significant. It offers a fresh perspective on plant heat resilience. The broad claims about predicting ecosystem or crop performance are premature but the core scientific question is important."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall comparative approach is reasonable, but important confounders in stress physiology are not adequately handled. The plan to reanalyze datasets is necessary but not sufficient without a detailed metadata harmonization and stratified analysis strategy."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The aims are feasible if the project limits itself to carefully selected datasets and hypothesis generation. The current scope is broad, particularly if meaningful cross-species and cross-environmental comparisons are expected within two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis proposal in that it relies on existing public data and aims to integrate disparate omics resources. It does not propose new experiments during the working group period."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names species and data types but does not present sufficient detail on available heat-stress datasets or their environmental metadata. For this topic, metadata quality is central to feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is largely limited to publication intentions. There is no detailed commitment to public workflows, harmonized metadata release, community standards, or trainee-facing documentation.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist focused on biological robustness and network modeling. The proposal is intellectually attractive because it seeks to move from lists of heat-responsive genes to a theory of cytokinetic robustness. The conceptual framework invoking modular robustness, network hubs, less-connected nodes, and bottlenecks is appropriate for the funding call. The idea that cytokinesis depends on coordinated cytoskeletal, membrane-trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory layers makes this a good candidate for emergent network analysis. However, the network-modeling plan remains mostly aspirational. The proposal does not define what network topology or dynamic property would constitute a bottleneck, how causality will be distinguished from correlation, or how networks inferred from heterogeneous public omics data will be validated. A useful synthesis product could result, but the current proposal overstates the ability of transcriptomic and proteomic associations to identify dysfunctional gene products."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal has a strong systems-level premise and explicitly seeks to integrate multiple data layers into multilayer cytokinesis-specific networks. It asks a question that requires collaborative expertise in cell biology, plant stress biology, bioinformatics, and network modeling. The focus on conserved versus species-specific responses is well suited to synthesis research. If successful, the work could generate a valuable framework for prioritizing candidate robustness factors and bottlenecks for later experimental testing."",
      ""weakness"": ""The definition of bottleneck is not operationalized in a way that can be inferred from the proposed data. A gene product becoming dysfunctional cannot usually be inferred from transcript abundance, translation, or even protein abundance without activity, localization, interaction, or phenotype data. The proposal does not define network null models, edge-confidence metrics, criteria for multilayer integration, or methods for comparing networks across species. The theory-building outcome is interesting but may be premature without a clearer analytic framework. Training and open science elements are not described in sufficient detail."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong systems-biology proposal that fits the call, but its network and bottleneck inference methods require much more precision to support the stated conclusions."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal directly targets robustness as an emergent property of interacting cytokinetic modules. The multilayer network concept is highly aligned with emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Developing a cytokinetic robustness framework under heat stress is novel and potentially impactful. The significance lies in identifying organizing principles of cellular resilience, though practical applications are longer term."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposal lacks formal definitions and statistical machinery for network inference, multilayer integration, cross-species comparison, and bottleneck prioritization. Without these, the analyses may generate plausible but weakly supported candidate lists."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed activities are ambitious for two years but could be feasible as a hypothesis-generating synthesis if the network modeling is simplified and milestones are clearly staged. As written, the theory-generation goal may exceed the evidence base."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is an excellent example of synthesis in its reliance on existing public data and integration across modalities and species. It clearly requires expertise beyond one lab."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The relevant data categories are identified, but the absence of explicit datasets and metadata prevents assessment of whether enough high-quality data exist for robust network inference."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not describe how networks, code, processed data, or analysis notebooks will be shared. For a network synthesis project, public release of reproducible network-building workflows should be central.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior scholar in open, team, and reproducible science with experience managing synthesis-center working groups. This proposal has a strong scientific motivation and a clear need for synthesis-center support, particularly bioinformatics staff and computational infrastructure. The proposed topic is cross-disciplinary and could benefit from a working group that brings together plant cell biologists, stress physiologists, computational biologists, network scientists, and trainees. However, the proposal as written does not yet fully meet several community-scale working group expectations. The current team description is narrow and does not specify the broader collaborative partnership, geographic or institutional diversity, career-stage representation, trainee roles, governance, meeting structure, or mechanisms for community input. The open science plan is also much too limited for this call. The application would be much stronger if it presented concrete deliverables such as curated public dataset catalogs, standardized metadata, reproducible workflows, containers, tutorials, trainee-led hackathons, and a public knowledge base of cytokinetic genes and orthologs."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project clearly relies on public data and seeks to synthesize information beyond the capacity of one laboratory. The requested NCEMS resources are well justified at a general level, especially staff bioinformatics support and computing. The biological question is suitable for collaborative synthesis and could serve as an effective training platform for graduate students and postdocs in omics reanalysis, cross-species integration, and reproducible workflows. The proposal has potential to create community resources if deliverables are expanded."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides little evidence of an assembled multidisciplinary working group beyond the investigators' existing expertise. It does not describe team governance, collaboration practices, trainee training plans, authorship/data policies, code review, reproducibility standards, public engagement, or maintenance of outputs. The open-science commitment is mostly implicit and does not meet the expectations of a synthesis-center call. The request for staff time is reasonable, but the proposal risks treating NCEMS primarily as a bioinformatics service rather than as a platform for community-scale collaboration."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The scientific idea is strong and synthesis-oriented, but the proposal is underdeveloped as a community-scale working group with open, team-based, reproducible science and trainee training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The scientific focus on cytokinetic robustness emerging from interacting cellular modules is relevant to the call. The proposal could better articulate how working group synthesis will reveal emergent principles rather than produce only candidate genes."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is novel and significant, and the integration of plant cytokinesis with heat-stress omics is promising. The significance for the broader community would be higher if the proposal committed to reusable resources and training materials."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The aims are coherent, but the approach lacks operational detail about data curation, reproducibility, quality control, and collaborative workflow management. These are important components of rigor for synthesis research."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year plan is reasonable for producing harmonized datasets and a candidate bottleneck analysis, but too ambitious if it also aims to develop a broad theory of cytokinetic robustness without a more structured project-management plan."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly centered on existing public data and multi-dataset integration. However, the working group rationale is framed largely as access to professional bioinformatics help rather than as a fully developed community-scale synthesis collaboration."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies general sources by organism and modality, but it does not provide a dataset catalog, accessions, metadata plan, or prioritization criteria. A working group should begin with a clearer data landscape."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is inadequate for this call. There is no concrete plan for repositories, code release, workflow reproducibility, metadata standards, licensing, public documentation, training modules, or community-created policies.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 3: Senior plant stress physiologist and evolutionary ecologist. The proposal addresses a timely problem: understanding plant cellular resilience to extreme heat. I appreciate that the investigators avoid treating heat tolerance as a single organismal trait and instead focus on cytokinesis as a possible cellular bottleneck for reproductive development and seed production. The comparative angle among Arabidopsis, tobacco, maize, rice, sorghum, and possibly other species could reveal how cell division programs have adapted across climates and life histories. That said, the ecological and stress-physiology framing is somewhat oversimplified. Heat treatments differ in temperature, duration, developmental context, acclimation history, and tissue water status, and these variables strongly shape heat-stress responses. The proposal does not yet explain how it will distinguish cytokinesis-specific robustness from general thermotolerance, developmental stage effects, or differences between monocots and dicots. The work is still worthwhile as a discovery synthesis, but the conclusions should be framed cautiously.",The project is motivated by an important environmental challenge: heat waves and plant reproductive resilience. The comparison of heat-susceptible and heat-resilient species is compelling. The proposal appropriately recognizes that robustness may arise from modular buffering of cellular networks rather than a universal heat-tolerance allele. The synthesis of public omics data could identify hypotheses that would be difficult to generate from single-species experiments. The project has potential relevance to crop resilience and biodiversity under climate change.,"The proposal conflates heat tolerance at the whole-plant or crop-yield level with cytokinetic heat resilience without sufficient evidence that these scales map directly. Arabidopsis lethality at about 35°C versus maize tolerance at higher temperatures is a broad comparison that may reflect many traits unrelated to cytokinesis. Public heat-stress datasets are likely to vary dramatically in stress regime, acclimation, tissue, genotype, developmental stage, and sampling time. The proposal needs a stronger environmental metadata framework and a plan to classify heat exposures biologically. It also lacks a clear strategy for selecting tropical or heat-adapted comparators beyond maize and does not address phylogenetic non-independence.",3,"A significant and timely synthesis question with good biological motivation, but the proposal needs a more sophisticated treatment of heat-stress physiology, environmental metadata, and comparative evolutionary inference.",3,"The proposal relates to emergent cellular robustness, but the link between molecular network responses and organismal heat resilience is not fully developed. Emergence is implicit rather than operationally defined.",4,The focus on cytokinesis as a heat-sensitive cellular bottleneck is novel and significant. It offers a fresh perspective on plant heat resilience. The broad claims about predicting ecosystem or crop performance are premature but the core scientific question is important.,3,"The overall comparative approach is reasonable, but important confounders in stress physiology are not adequately handled. The plan to reanalyze datasets is necessary but not sufficient without a detailed metadata harmonization and stratified analysis strategy.",3,"The aims are feasible if the project limits itself to carefully selected datasets and hypothesis generation. The current scope is broad, particularly if meaningful cross-species and cross-environmental comparisons are expected within two years.",5,This is a strong synthesis proposal in that it relies on existing public data and aims to integrate disparate omics resources. It does not propose new experiments during the working group period.,3,"The proposal names species and data types but does not present sufficient detail on available heat-stress datasets or their environmental metadata. For this topic, metadata quality is central to feasibility.",2,"The open science plan is largely limited to publication intentions. There is no detailed commitment to public workflows, harmonized metadata release, community standards, or trainee-facing documentation.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:15:15.884262,0,success,,"This proposal addresses a timely problem by investigating plant cellular resilience to extreme heat, focusing specifically on cytokinesis as a bottleneck for reproductive development. While the comparative approach across multiple species offers significant potential for understanding how cell division adapts to climate, the ecological and physiological framing is somewhat oversimplified. The investigators must carefully distinguish cytokinesis-specific robustness from general thermotolerance and adequately account for high variability in experimental conditions.",The project tackles a critical environmental challenge by comparing heat-susceptible and heat-resilient species to understand modular buffering in cellular networks. Synthesizing public omics data offers a strong foundation for generating novel hypotheses relevant to crop resilience and biodiversity under climate change.,"The methodology conflates whole-plant heat tolerance with cytokinetic resilience and relies on broad species comparisons without addressing phylogenetic non-independence. Additionally, the approach lacks a robust metadata framework to control for significant variations in stress regimes, developmental stages, and tissue types across public datasets."
baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::004,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,4,baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:38:10.298599,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior plant cytokinesis and cytoskeletal cell biologist. This proposal addresses an important and underexplored question: why cytokinesis fails under heat stress in some plant species but remains robust in others. The biological framing is strong, and the focus on the phragmoplast, vesicle trafficking, callose-cellulose transition, microtubule turnover, and cytokinetic gene networks is well aligned with current gaps in plant cell biology. The preliminary observations comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize provide a credible motivation for a synthesis effort. I find the core idea compelling: publicly available transcriptomic, translatomic, proteomic, and single-cell datasets could be used to infer heat-sensitive modules and candidate cytokinetic bottlenecks. However, the proposal would benefit from clearer specification of datasets, explicit cytokinesis markers, and a more detailed plan for distinguishing true cytokinesis-specific effects from general heat-shock, cell-cycle, developmental, or tissue-composition effects. The proposal also does not sufficiently describe open science practices or trainee development, both of which are central to this call. Overall, I view this as a strong biological concept with moderate methodological underdevelopment."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal identifies a compelling molecular-cellular problem with broad relevance to plant resilience under heat waves. It has a strong mechanistic foundation in plant cytokinesis and phragmoplast biology. The comparative framing across heat-sensitive and heat-resilient species is attractive and could reveal conserved versus lineage-specific robustness mechanisms. The work is clearly based on synthesis of existing omics datasets and is beyond a typical single-lab effort if done rigorously across species and data modalities. The preliminary observations in Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize make the hypothesis plausible."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not provide a sufficiently concrete inventory of public datasets, accessions, sample types, stress regimes, developmental stages, or expected coverage of cytokinetic cells. It assumes that bulk heat-stress omics data will be informative for cytokinesis, but cytokinetic cells are rare and tissue-composition effects may dominate. The definition of a bottleneck as a gene product that becomes dysfunctional or less functional is biologically meaningful but difficult to infer from transcriptomics alone. The analysis plan is high level and lacks validation strategies, benchmark performance metrics, or explicit controls for cell cycle phase, orthology uncertainty, batch effects, and stress duration differences. Open science and training commitments are minimal."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically compelling and call-relevant synthesis project with strong plant cell biology motivation, but the proposal needs a more rigorous and detailed data-analysis plan and stronger commitments to open science and training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is plausibly an emergent property of interacting cytoskeletal, trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory networks. The proposal explicitly seeks network-level bottlenecks rather than isolated genes. However, the emergent-phenomena framing could be sharpened by defining measurable system-level properties of cytokinetic robustness."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The comparative analysis of cytokinesis under heat stress across temperate and tropical or heat-resilient plant species is novel and significant. The outcome could guide future mechanistic experiments and potentially inform crop resilience. The significance is strongest at the hypothesis-generation level rather than immediate translational application."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed aims are logical, but the computational strategy is described only broadly. Key issues such as cell-cycle annotation, cross-species orthology, confounding by tissue type and growth conditions, and integration of bulk and single-cell data are acknowledged only superficially. The proposal would be stronger with specific pipelines, benchmarks, and sensitivity analyses."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is plausible for a focused synthesis, but the scope across five species, multiple omics modalities, single-cell reprocessing, transcription factor inference, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex network construction is ambitious. The requested staff support is appropriate but may not be sufficient if dataset harmonization proves difficult."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is explicitly built around reanalysis and integration of existing publicly available omics datasets and does not propose new experimental data generation. It fits the synthesis-center model well."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies broad data types and target species, but it does not provide enough detail about actual datasets, accessions, metadata completeness, sample sizes, or heat-stress comparability. This is a significant limitation for assessing feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that manuscripts and a theory/review will be produced, but it provides little detail about public repositories, reproducible workflows, code release, metadata standards, licensing, or community governance. The open-science plan is underdeveloped.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist specializing in single-cell multi-omics integration. The proposal is well suited in spirit to a synthesis center because it proposes to reprocess public data across species and modalities to answer a biological question that cannot be answered from one dataset. However, from a computational standpoint, the application is under-specified. It lists standard analysis components such as normalization, dimensionality reduction, differential expression, correlation networks, trajectory analysis, and multiplex networks, but it does not explain how these methods will be selected, benchmarked, integrated, or made robust to severe heterogeneity in the source data. The central inference task is difficult: identifying cytokinesis-specific heat bottlenecks from public datasets that were likely generated for diverse biological purposes. The proposal needs a stronger metadata model, a dataset inclusion/exclusion plan, and explicit statistical approaches to cross-study integration. I am enthusiastic about the question but only moderately confident in the current analytic plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a real data-synthesis challenge involving cross-species, cross-platform, and multi-omic integration. It recognizes the need to reprocess raw data through standardized pipelines, which is essential. The plan to use known mitotic and cytokinetic genes as anchors for identifying coexpressed modules is reasonable. The project would benefit substantially from NCEMS bioinformatics support and computing infrastructure. The intended output, a harmonized cytokinesis network resource across plants, could be valuable to the community."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational plan is too generic. It does not specify which public datasets will be included, what metadata fields are required, how batch correction will be performed, how single-cell and bulk datasets will be jointly modeled, how proteomic/translatomic evidence will be weighted relative to transcriptomic evidence, or how uncertainty in orthology will be propagated. Correlation networks from heterogeneous public data can produce spurious hubs, and the proposal does not describe negative controls, permutation tests, replication thresholds, or validation against known cytokinesis components. The assumption that including many datasets will average out growth-condition noise is not statistically sufficient. There is little detail on workflow reproducibility."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising synthesis concept with clear need for computational collaboration, but the current proposal lacks the methodological specificity needed for high confidence in the proposed multi-omic network inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The goal of identifying robustness and failure points emerging from multilayer gene, protein, and regulatory networks is appropriate for emergent molecular and cellular phenomena. The proposal could more explicitly formulate network-level emergent properties and quantitative endpoints."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Cross-species identification of cytokinetic heat bottlenecks is novel and potentially significant. The integration of single-cell and whole-organ omics around cytokinesis is a useful direction. Significance would increase if the proposal committed to a reusable data product rather than only candidate bottlenecks and manuscripts."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists many standard tools but lacks a rigorous statistical framework. There is insufficient detail on dataset harmonization, batch correction, orthology mapping, cell-type and cell-cycle annotation, multi-omics integration, uncertainty quantification, and evaluation metrics."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope is broad but potentially manageable with strong staff support if narrowed to a well-curated dataset set. As written, five species plus transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics, single-cell analysis, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex modeling may exceed the proposed two-year effort."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal is squarely focused on public data reanalysis and integration. It does not depend on new experimental data, although the long-term motivation is future experimental testing."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The application mentions a data sources table, but the provided proposal text does not include dataset names, accessions, sample counts, platforms, or metadata quality. This makes feasibility difficult to evaluate."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not provide an adequate plan for version-controlled code, containerized workflows, public release of processed data, provenance tracking, or community documentation. These elements are especially important for a computational synthesis project.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior plant stress physiologist and evolutionary ecologist. The proposal addresses a timely problem: understanding plant cellular resilience to extreme heat. I appreciate that the investigators avoid treating heat tolerance as a single organismal trait and instead focus on cytokinesis as a possible cellular bottleneck for reproductive development and seed production. The comparative angle among Arabidopsis, tobacco, maize, rice, sorghum, and possibly other species could reveal how cell division programs have adapted across climates and life histories. That said, the ecological and stress-physiology framing is somewhat oversimplified. Heat treatments differ in temperature, duration, developmental context, acclimation history, and tissue water status, and these variables strongly shape heat-stress responses. The proposal does not yet explain how it will distinguish cytokinesis-specific robustness from general thermotolerance, developmental stage effects, or differences between monocots and dicots. The work is still worthwhile as a discovery synthesis, but the conclusions should be framed cautiously."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project is motivated by an important environmental challenge: heat waves and plant reproductive resilience. The comparison of heat-susceptible and heat-resilient species is compelling. The proposal appropriately recognizes that robustness may arise from modular buffering of cellular networks rather than a universal heat-tolerance allele. The synthesis of public omics data could identify hypotheses that would be difficult to generate from single-species experiments. The project has potential relevance to crop resilience and biodiversity under climate change."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal conflates heat tolerance at the whole-plant or crop-yield level with cytokinetic heat resilience without sufficient evidence that these scales map directly. Arabidopsis lethality at about 35°C versus maize tolerance at higher temperatures is a broad comparison that may reflect many traits unrelated to cytokinesis. Public heat-stress datasets are likely to vary dramatically in stress regime, acclimation, tissue, genotype, developmental stage, and sampling time. The proposal needs a stronger environmental metadata framework and a plan to classify heat exposures biologically. It also lacks a clear strategy for selecting tropical or heat-adapted comparators beyond maize and does not address phylogenetic non-independence."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A significant and timely synthesis question with good biological motivation, but the proposal needs a more sophisticated treatment of heat-stress physiology, environmental metadata, and comparative evolutionary inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal relates to emergent cellular robustness, but the link between molecular network responses and organismal heat resilience is not fully developed. Emergence is implicit rather than operationally defined."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The focus on cytokinesis as a heat-sensitive cellular bottleneck is novel and significant. It offers a fresh perspective on plant heat resilience. The broad claims about predicting ecosystem or crop performance are premature but the core scientific question is important."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall comparative approach is reasonable, but important confounders in stress physiology are not adequately handled. The plan to reanalyze datasets is necessary but not sufficient without a detailed metadata harmonization and stratified analysis strategy."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The aims are feasible if the project limits itself to carefully selected datasets and hypothesis generation. The current scope is broad, particularly if meaningful cross-species and cross-environmental comparisons are expected within two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis proposal in that it relies on existing public data and aims to integrate disparate omics resources. It does not propose new experiments during the working group period."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names species and data types but does not present sufficient detail on available heat-stress datasets or their environmental metadata. For this topic, metadata quality is central to feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is largely limited to publication intentions. There is no detailed commitment to public workflows, harmonized metadata release, community standards, or trainee-facing documentation.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist focused on biological robustness and network modeling. The proposal is intellectually attractive because it seeks to move from lists of heat-responsive genes to a theory of cytokinetic robustness. The conceptual framework invoking modular robustness, network hubs, less-connected nodes, and bottlenecks is appropriate for the funding call. The idea that cytokinesis depends on coordinated cytoskeletal, membrane-trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory layers makes this a good candidate for emergent network analysis. However, the network-modeling plan remains mostly aspirational. The proposal does not define what network topology or dynamic property would constitute a bottleneck, how causality will be distinguished from correlation, or how networks inferred from heterogeneous public omics data will be validated. A useful synthesis product could result, but the current proposal overstates the ability of transcriptomic and proteomic associations to identify dysfunctional gene products."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal has a strong systems-level premise and explicitly seeks to integrate multiple data layers into multilayer cytokinesis-specific networks. It asks a question that requires collaborative expertise in cell biology, plant stress biology, bioinformatics, and network modeling. The focus on conserved versus species-specific responses is well suited to synthesis research. If successful, the work could generate a valuable framework for prioritizing candidate robustness factors and bottlenecks for later experimental testing."",
      ""weakness"": ""The definition of bottleneck is not operationalized in a way that can be inferred from the proposed data. A gene product becoming dysfunctional cannot usually be inferred from transcript abundance, translation, or even protein abundance without activity, localization, interaction, or phenotype data. The proposal does not define network null models, edge-confidence metrics, criteria for multilayer integration, or methods for comparing networks across species. The theory-building outcome is interesting but may be premature without a clearer analytic framework. Training and open science elements are not described in sufficient detail."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong systems-biology proposal that fits the call, but its network and bottleneck inference methods require much more precision to support the stated conclusions."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal directly targets robustness as an emergent property of interacting cytokinetic modules. The multilayer network concept is highly aligned with emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Developing a cytokinetic robustness framework under heat stress is novel and potentially impactful. The significance lies in identifying organizing principles of cellular resilience, though practical applications are longer term."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposal lacks formal definitions and statistical machinery for network inference, multilayer integration, cross-species comparison, and bottleneck prioritization. Without these, the analyses may generate plausible but weakly supported candidate lists."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed activities are ambitious for two years but could be feasible as a hypothesis-generating synthesis if the network modeling is simplified and milestones are clearly staged. As written, the theory-generation goal may exceed the evidence base."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is an excellent example of synthesis in its reliance on existing public data and integration across modalities and species. It clearly requires expertise beyond one lab."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The relevant data categories are identified, but the absence of explicit datasets and metadata prevents assessment of whether enough high-quality data exist for robust network inference."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not describe how networks, code, processed data, or analysis notebooks will be shared. For a network synthesis project, public release of reproducible network-building workflows should be central.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior scholar in open, team, and reproducible science with experience managing synthesis-center working groups. This proposal has a strong scientific motivation and a clear need for synthesis-center support, particularly bioinformatics staff and computational infrastructure. The proposed topic is cross-disciplinary and could benefit from a working group that brings together plant cell biologists, stress physiologists, computational biologists, network scientists, and trainees. However, the proposal as written does not yet fully meet several community-scale working group expectations. The current team description is narrow and does not specify the broader collaborative partnership, geographic or institutional diversity, career-stage representation, trainee roles, governance, meeting structure, or mechanisms for community input. The open science plan is also much too limited for this call. The application would be much stronger if it presented concrete deliverables such as curated public dataset catalogs, standardized metadata, reproducible workflows, containers, tutorials, trainee-led hackathons, and a public knowledge base of cytokinetic genes and orthologs."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project clearly relies on public data and seeks to synthesize information beyond the capacity of one laboratory. The requested NCEMS resources are well justified at a general level, especially staff bioinformatics support and computing. The biological question is suitable for collaborative synthesis and could serve as an effective training platform for graduate students and postdocs in omics reanalysis, cross-species integration, and reproducible workflows. The proposal has potential to create community resources if deliverables are expanded."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides little evidence of an assembled multidisciplinary working group beyond the investigators' existing expertise. It does not describe team governance, collaboration practices, trainee training plans, authorship/data policies, code review, reproducibility standards, public engagement, or maintenance of outputs. The open-science commitment is mostly implicit and does not meet the expectations of a synthesis-center call. The request for staff time is reasonable, but the proposal risks treating NCEMS primarily as a bioinformatics service rather than as a platform for community-scale collaboration."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The scientific idea is strong and synthesis-oriented, but the proposal is underdeveloped as a community-scale working group with open, team-based, reproducible science and trainee training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The scientific focus on cytokinetic robustness emerging from interacting cellular modules is relevant to the call. The proposal could better articulate how working group synthesis will reveal emergent principles rather than produce only candidate genes."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is novel and significant, and the integration of plant cytokinesis with heat-stress omics is promising. The significance for the broader community would be higher if the proposal committed to reusable resources and training materials."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The aims are coherent, but the approach lacks operational detail about data curation, reproducibility, quality control, and collaborative workflow management. These are important components of rigor for synthesis research."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year plan is reasonable for producing harmonized datasets and a candidate bottleneck analysis, but too ambitious if it also aims to develop a broad theory of cytokinetic robustness without a more structured project-management plan."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly centered on existing public data and multi-dataset integration. However, the working group rationale is framed largely as access to professional bioinformatics help rather than as a fully developed community-scale synthesis collaboration."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies general sources by organism and modality, but it does not provide a dataset catalog, accessions, metadata plan, or prioritization criteria. A working group should begin with a clearer data landscape."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is inadequate for this call. There is no concrete plan for repositories, code release, workflow reproducibility, metadata standards, licensing, public documentation, training modules, or community-created policies.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist focused on biological robustness and network modeling. The proposal is intellectually attractive because it seeks to move from lists of heat-responsive genes to a theory of cytokinetic robustness. The conceptual framework invoking modular robustness, network hubs, less-connected nodes, and bottlenecks is appropriate for the funding call. The idea that cytokinesis depends on coordinated cytoskeletal, membrane-trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory layers makes this a good candidate for emergent network analysis. However, the network-modeling plan remains mostly aspirational. The proposal does not define what network topology or dynamic property would constitute a bottleneck, how causality will be distinguished from correlation, or how networks inferred from heterogeneous public omics data will be validated. A useful synthesis product could result, but the current proposal overstates the ability of transcriptomic and proteomic associations to identify dysfunctional gene products.","The proposal has a strong systems-level premise and explicitly seeks to integrate multiple data layers into multilayer cytokinesis-specific networks. It asks a question that requires collaborative expertise in cell biology, plant stress biology, bioinformatics, and network modeling. The focus on conserved versus species-specific responses is well suited to synthesis research. If successful, the work could generate a valuable framework for prioritizing candidate robustness factors and bottlenecks for later experimental testing.","The definition of bottleneck is not operationalized in a way that can be inferred from the proposed data. A gene product becoming dysfunctional cannot usually be inferred from transcript abundance, translation, or even protein abundance without activity, localization, interaction, or phenotype data. The proposal does not define network null models, edge-confidence metrics, criteria for multilayer integration, or methods for comparing networks across species. The theory-building outcome is interesting but may be premature without a clearer analytic framework. Training and open science elements are not described in sufficient detail.",3,"A conceptually strong systems-biology proposal that fits the call, but its network and bottleneck inference methods require much more precision to support the stated conclusions.",5,The proposal directly targets robustness as an emergent property of interacting cytokinetic modules. The multilayer network concept is highly aligned with emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences.,4,"Developing a cytokinetic robustness framework under heat stress is novel and potentially impactful. The significance lies in identifying organizing principles of cellular resilience, though practical applications are longer term.",2,"The proposal lacks formal definitions and statistical machinery for network inference, multilayer integration, cross-species comparison, and bottleneck prioritization. Without these, the analyses may generate plausible but weakly supported candidate lists.",3,"The proposed activities are ambitious for two years but could be feasible as a hypothesis-generating synthesis if the network modeling is simplified and milestones are clearly staged. As written, the theory-generation goal may exceed the evidence base.",5,The project is an excellent example of synthesis in its reliance on existing public data and integration across modalities and species. It clearly requires expertise beyond one lab.,3,"The relevant data categories are identified, but the absence of explicit datasets and metadata prevents assessment of whether enough high-quality data exist for robust network inference.",2,"The proposal does not describe how networks, code, processed data, or analysis notebooks will be shared. For a network synthesis project, public release of reproducible network-building workflows should be central.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:15:24.923552,0,success,,"This proposal aims to develop a theory of cytokinetic robustness by applying a network modeling framework to heat-responsive gene data. While the conceptual approach of analyzing coordinated cytoskeletal, membrane, and regulatory layers is highly relevant to the funding call, the current network-modeling plan lacks methodological specificity. Overall, the project has the potential to produce a valuable synthesis product, but it currently overestimates the capacity of public transcriptomic and proteomic data to identify dysfunctional gene products without clear validation strategies.","The project features a strong systems-level premise that integrates multiple data layers into cytokinesis-specific networks, demanding highly collaborative and multidisciplinary expertise. Additionally, the focus on conserved versus species-specific responses is well-suited for synthesis research and could yield a valuable framework for prioritizing candidate robustness factors for future experimental testing.","The proposal lacks operational definitions for key concepts like network bottlenecks and over-relies on abundance data to infer gene product dysfunction without essential activity or phenotype metrics. Furthermore, it omits critical methodological details regarding network null models, edge-confidence metrics, multilayer integration criteria, and cross-species comparison methods. Finally, the training and open science components are insufficiently detailed, rendering the proposed theory-building outcomes potentially premature."
baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,5,baseline::y1::1::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:38:10.298599,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior plant cytokinesis and cytoskeletal cell biologist. This proposal addresses an important and underexplored question: why cytokinesis fails under heat stress in some plant species but remains robust in others. The biological framing is strong, and the focus on the phragmoplast, vesicle trafficking, callose-cellulose transition, microtubule turnover, and cytokinetic gene networks is well aligned with current gaps in plant cell biology. The preliminary observations comparing Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize provide a credible motivation for a synthesis effort. I find the core idea compelling: publicly available transcriptomic, translatomic, proteomic, and single-cell datasets could be used to infer heat-sensitive modules and candidate cytokinetic bottlenecks. However, the proposal would benefit from clearer specification of datasets, explicit cytokinesis markers, and a more detailed plan for distinguishing true cytokinesis-specific effects from general heat-shock, cell-cycle, developmental, or tissue-composition effects. The proposal also does not sufficiently describe open science practices or trainee development, both of which are central to this call. Overall, I view this as a strong biological concept with moderate methodological underdevelopment."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal identifies a compelling molecular-cellular problem with broad relevance to plant resilience under heat waves. It has a strong mechanistic foundation in plant cytokinesis and phragmoplast biology. The comparative framing across heat-sensitive and heat-resilient species is attractive and could reveal conserved versus lineage-specific robustness mechanisms. The work is clearly based on synthesis of existing omics datasets and is beyond a typical single-lab effort if done rigorously across species and data modalities. The preliminary observations in Arabidopsis, tobacco, and maize make the hypothesis plausible."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not provide a sufficiently concrete inventory of public datasets, accessions, sample types, stress regimes, developmental stages, or expected coverage of cytokinetic cells. It assumes that bulk heat-stress omics data will be informative for cytokinesis, but cytokinetic cells are rare and tissue-composition effects may dominate. The definition of a bottleneck as a gene product that becomes dysfunctional or less functional is biologically meaningful but difficult to infer from transcriptomics alone. The analysis plan is high level and lacks validation strategies, benchmark performance metrics, or explicit controls for cell cycle phase, orthology uncertainty, batch effects, and stress duration differences. Open science and training commitments are minimal."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically compelling and call-relevant synthesis project with strong plant cell biology motivation, but the proposal needs a more rigorous and detailed data-analysis plan and stronger commitments to open science and training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Cytokinetic robustness under heat stress is plausibly an emergent property of interacting cytoskeletal, trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory networks. The proposal explicitly seeks network-level bottlenecks rather than isolated genes. However, the emergent-phenomena framing could be sharpened by defining measurable system-level properties of cytokinetic robustness."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The comparative analysis of cytokinesis under heat stress across temperate and tropical or heat-resilient plant species is novel and significant. The outcome could guide future mechanistic experiments and potentially inform crop resilience. The significance is strongest at the hypothesis-generation level rather than immediate translational application."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed aims are logical, but the computational strategy is described only broadly. Key issues such as cell-cycle annotation, cross-species orthology, confounding by tissue type and growth conditions, and integration of bulk and single-cell data are acknowledged only superficially. The proposal would be stronger with specific pipelines, benchmarks, and sensitivity analyses."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is plausible for a focused synthesis, but the scope across five species, multiple omics modalities, single-cell reprocessing, transcription factor inference, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex network construction is ambitious. The requested staff support is appropriate but may not be sufficient if dataset harmonization proves difficult."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is explicitly built around reanalysis and integration of existing publicly available omics datasets and does not propose new experimental data generation. It fits the synthesis-center model well."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies broad data types and target species, but it does not provide enough detail about actual datasets, accessions, metadata completeness, sample sizes, or heat-stress comparability. This is a significant limitation for assessing feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that manuscripts and a theory/review will be produced, but it provides little detail about public repositories, reproducible workflows, code release, metadata standards, licensing, or community governance. The open-science plan is underdeveloped.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist specializing in single-cell multi-omics integration. The proposal is well suited in spirit to a synthesis center because it proposes to reprocess public data across species and modalities to answer a biological question that cannot be answered from one dataset. However, from a computational standpoint, the application is under-specified. It lists standard analysis components such as normalization, dimensionality reduction, differential expression, correlation networks, trajectory analysis, and multiplex networks, but it does not explain how these methods will be selected, benchmarked, integrated, or made robust to severe heterogeneity in the source data. The central inference task is difficult: identifying cytokinesis-specific heat bottlenecks from public datasets that were likely generated for diverse biological purposes. The proposal needs a stronger metadata model, a dataset inclusion/exclusion plan, and explicit statistical approaches to cross-study integration. I am enthusiastic about the question but only moderately confident in the current analytic plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a real data-synthesis challenge involving cross-species, cross-platform, and multi-omic integration. It recognizes the need to reprocess raw data through standardized pipelines, which is essential. The plan to use known mitotic and cytokinetic genes as anchors for identifying coexpressed modules is reasonable. The project would benefit substantially from NCEMS bioinformatics support and computing infrastructure. The intended output, a harmonized cytokinesis network resource across plants, could be valuable to the community."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational plan is too generic. It does not specify which public datasets will be included, what metadata fields are required, how batch correction will be performed, how single-cell and bulk datasets will be jointly modeled, how proteomic/translatomic evidence will be weighted relative to transcriptomic evidence, or how uncertainty in orthology will be propagated. Correlation networks from heterogeneous public data can produce spurious hubs, and the proposal does not describe negative controls, permutation tests, replication thresholds, or validation against known cytokinesis components. The assumption that including many datasets will average out growth-condition noise is not statistically sufficient. There is little detail on workflow reproducibility."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising synthesis concept with clear need for computational collaboration, but the current proposal lacks the methodological specificity needed for high confidence in the proposed multi-omic network inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The goal of identifying robustness and failure points emerging from multilayer gene, protein, and regulatory networks is appropriate for emergent molecular and cellular phenomena. The proposal could more explicitly formulate network-level emergent properties and quantitative endpoints."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Cross-species identification of cytokinetic heat bottlenecks is novel and potentially significant. The integration of single-cell and whole-organ omics around cytokinesis is a useful direction. Significance would increase if the proposal committed to a reusable data product rather than only candidate bottlenecks and manuscripts."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists many standard tools but lacks a rigorous statistical framework. There is insufficient detail on dataset harmonization, batch correction, orthology mapping, cell-type and cell-cycle annotation, multi-omics integration, uncertainty quantification, and evaluation metrics."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope is broad but potentially manageable with strong staff support if narrowed to a well-curated dataset set. As written, five species plus transcriptomics, translatomics, proteomics, single-cell analysis, cis-regulatory analysis, and multiplex modeling may exceed the proposed two-year effort."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal is squarely focused on public data reanalysis and integration. It does not depend on new experimental data, although the long-term motivation is future experimental testing."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The application mentions a data sources table, but the provided proposal text does not include dataset names, accessions, sample counts, platforms, or metadata quality. This makes feasibility difficult to evaluate."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not provide an adequate plan for version-controlled code, containerized workflows, public release of processed data, provenance tracking, or community documentation. These elements are especially important for a computational synthesis project.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior plant stress physiologist and evolutionary ecologist. The proposal addresses a timely problem: understanding plant cellular resilience to extreme heat. I appreciate that the investigators avoid treating heat tolerance as a single organismal trait and instead focus on cytokinesis as a possible cellular bottleneck for reproductive development and seed production. The comparative angle among Arabidopsis, tobacco, maize, rice, sorghum, and possibly other species could reveal how cell division programs have adapted across climates and life histories. That said, the ecological and stress-physiology framing is somewhat oversimplified. Heat treatments differ in temperature, duration, developmental context, acclimation history, and tissue water status, and these variables strongly shape heat-stress responses. The proposal does not yet explain how it will distinguish cytokinesis-specific robustness from general thermotolerance, developmental stage effects, or differences between monocots and dicots. The work is still worthwhile as a discovery synthesis, but the conclusions should be framed cautiously."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project is motivated by an important environmental challenge: heat waves and plant reproductive resilience. The comparison of heat-susceptible and heat-resilient species is compelling. The proposal appropriately recognizes that robustness may arise from modular buffering of cellular networks rather than a universal heat-tolerance allele. The synthesis of public omics data could identify hypotheses that would be difficult to generate from single-species experiments. The project has potential relevance to crop resilience and biodiversity under climate change."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal conflates heat tolerance at the whole-plant or crop-yield level with cytokinetic heat resilience without sufficient evidence that these scales map directly. Arabidopsis lethality at about 35°C versus maize tolerance at higher temperatures is a broad comparison that may reflect many traits unrelated to cytokinesis. Public heat-stress datasets are likely to vary dramatically in stress regime, acclimation, tissue, genotype, developmental stage, and sampling time. The proposal needs a stronger environmental metadata framework and a plan to classify heat exposures biologically. It also lacks a clear strategy for selecting tropical or heat-adapted comparators beyond maize and does not address phylogenetic non-independence."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A significant and timely synthesis question with good biological motivation, but the proposal needs a more sophisticated treatment of heat-stress physiology, environmental metadata, and comparative evolutionary inference."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal relates to emergent cellular robustness, but the link between molecular network responses and organismal heat resilience is not fully developed. Emergence is implicit rather than operationally defined."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The focus on cytokinesis as a heat-sensitive cellular bottleneck is novel and significant. It offers a fresh perspective on plant heat resilience. The broad claims about predicting ecosystem or crop performance are premature but the core scientific question is important."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall comparative approach is reasonable, but important confounders in stress physiology are not adequately handled. The plan to reanalyze datasets is necessary but not sufficient without a detailed metadata harmonization and stratified analysis strategy."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The aims are feasible if the project limits itself to carefully selected datasets and hypothesis generation. The current scope is broad, particularly if meaningful cross-species and cross-environmental comparisons are expected within two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis proposal in that it relies on existing public data and aims to integrate disparate omics resources. It does not propose new experiments during the working group period."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names species and data types but does not present sufficient detail on available heat-stress datasets or their environmental metadata. For this topic, metadata quality is central to feasibility."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is largely limited to publication intentions. There is no detailed commitment to public workflows, harmonized metadata release, community standards, or trainee-facing documentation.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist focused on biological robustness and network modeling. The proposal is intellectually attractive because it seeks to move from lists of heat-responsive genes to a theory of cytokinetic robustness. The conceptual framework invoking modular robustness, network hubs, less-connected nodes, and bottlenecks is appropriate for the funding call. The idea that cytokinesis depends on coordinated cytoskeletal, membrane-trafficking, cell-wall, and regulatory layers makes this a good candidate for emergent network analysis. However, the network-modeling plan remains mostly aspirational. The proposal does not define what network topology or dynamic property would constitute a bottleneck, how causality will be distinguished from correlation, or how networks inferred from heterogeneous public omics data will be validated. A useful synthesis product could result, but the current proposal overstates the ability of transcriptomic and proteomic associations to identify dysfunctional gene products."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal has a strong systems-level premise and explicitly seeks to integrate multiple data layers into multilayer cytokinesis-specific networks. It asks a question that requires collaborative expertise in cell biology, plant stress biology, bioinformatics, and network modeling. The focus on conserved versus species-specific responses is well suited to synthesis research. If successful, the work could generate a valuable framework for prioritizing candidate robustness factors and bottlenecks for later experimental testing."",
      ""weakness"": ""The definition of bottleneck is not operationalized in a way that can be inferred from the proposed data. A gene product becoming dysfunctional cannot usually be inferred from transcript abundance, translation, or even protein abundance without activity, localization, interaction, or phenotype data. The proposal does not define network null models, edge-confidence metrics, criteria for multilayer integration, or methods for comparing networks across species. The theory-building outcome is interesting but may be premature without a clearer analytic framework. Training and open science elements are not described in sufficient detail."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong systems-biology proposal that fits the call, but its network and bottleneck inference methods require much more precision to support the stated conclusions."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal directly targets robustness as an emergent property of interacting cytokinetic modules. The multilayer network concept is highly aligned with emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Developing a cytokinetic robustness framework under heat stress is novel and potentially impactful. The significance lies in identifying organizing principles of cellular resilience, though practical applications are longer term."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposal lacks formal definitions and statistical machinery for network inference, multilayer integration, cross-species comparison, and bottleneck prioritization. Without these, the analyses may generate plausible but weakly supported candidate lists."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed activities are ambitious for two years but could be feasible as a hypothesis-generating synthesis if the network modeling is simplified and milestones are clearly staged. As written, the theory-generation goal may exceed the evidence base."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is an excellent example of synthesis in its reliance on existing public data and integration across modalities and species. It clearly requires expertise beyond one lab."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The relevant data categories are identified, but the absence of explicit datasets and metadata prevents assessment of whether enough high-quality data exist for robust network inference."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not describe how networks, code, processed data, or analysis notebooks will be shared. For a network synthesis project, public release of reproducible network-building workflows should be central.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior scholar in open, team, and reproducible science with experience managing synthesis-center working groups. This proposal has a strong scientific motivation and a clear need for synthesis-center support, particularly bioinformatics staff and computational infrastructure. The proposed topic is cross-disciplinary and could benefit from a working group that brings together plant cell biologists, stress physiologists, computational biologists, network scientists, and trainees. However, the proposal as written does not yet fully meet several community-scale working group expectations. The current team description is narrow and does not specify the broader collaborative partnership, geographic or institutional diversity, career-stage representation, trainee roles, governance, meeting structure, or mechanisms for community input. The open science plan is also much too limited for this call. The application would be much stronger if it presented concrete deliverables such as curated public dataset catalogs, standardized metadata, reproducible workflows, containers, tutorials, trainee-led hackathons, and a public knowledge base of cytokinetic genes and orthologs."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project clearly relies on public data and seeks to synthesize information beyond the capacity of one laboratory. The requested NCEMS resources are well justified at a general level, especially staff bioinformatics support and computing. The biological question is suitable for collaborative synthesis and could serve as an effective training platform for graduate students and postdocs in omics reanalysis, cross-species integration, and reproducible workflows. The proposal has potential to create community resources if deliverables are expanded."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides little evidence of an assembled multidisciplinary working group beyond the investigators' existing expertise. It does not describe team governance, collaboration practices, trainee training plans, authorship/data policies, code review, reproducibility standards, public engagement, or maintenance of outputs. The open-science commitment is mostly implicit and does not meet the expectations of a synthesis-center call. The request for staff time is reasonable, but the proposal risks treating NCEMS primarily as a bioinformatics service rather than as a platform for community-scale collaboration."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The scientific idea is strong and synthesis-oriented, but the proposal is underdeveloped as a community-scale working group with open, team-based, reproducible science and trainee training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The scientific focus on cytokinetic robustness emerging from interacting cellular modules is relevant to the call. The proposal could better articulate how working group synthesis will reveal emergent principles rather than produce only candidate genes."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The biological question is novel and significant, and the integration of plant cytokinesis with heat-stress omics is promising. The significance for the broader community would be higher if the proposal committed to reusable resources and training materials."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The aims are coherent, but the approach lacks operational detail about data curation, reproducibility, quality control, and collaborative workflow management. These are important components of rigor for synthesis research."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year plan is reasonable for producing harmonized datasets and a candidate bottleneck analysis, but too ambitious if it also aims to develop a broad theory of cytokinetic robustness without a more structured project-management plan."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly centered on existing public data and multi-dataset integration. However, the working group rationale is framed largely as access to professional bioinformatics help rather than as a fully developed community-scale synthesis collaboration."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies general sources by organism and modality, but it does not provide a dataset catalog, accessions, metadata plan, or prioritization criteria. A working group should begin with a clearer data landscape."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 1,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The open science plan is inadequate for this call. There is no concrete plan for repositories, code release, workflow reproducibility, metadata standards, licensing, public documentation, training modules, or community-created policies.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::1,y1,1,CYTOKINETIC BOTTLENECKS OF HEAT WAVES,Accepted,Andrei Smertenko; Carolyn Rasmussen; Dawn Nagel; Georgia Drakakaki; Stephen Ficklin,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 5: Senior scholar in open, team, and reproducible science with experience managing synthesis-center working groups. This proposal has a strong scientific motivation and a clear need for synthesis-center support, particularly bioinformatics staff and computational infrastructure. The proposed topic is cross-disciplinary and could benefit from a working group that brings together plant cell biologists, stress physiologists, computational biologists, network scientists, and trainees. However, the proposal as written does not yet fully meet several community-scale working group expectations. The current team description is narrow and does not specify the broader collaborative partnership, geographic or institutional diversity, career-stage representation, trainee roles, governance, meeting structure, or mechanisms for community input. The open science plan is also much too limited for this call. The application would be much stronger if it presented concrete deliverables such as curated public dataset catalogs, standardized metadata, reproducible workflows, containers, tutorials, trainee-led hackathons, and a public knowledge base of cytokinetic genes and orthologs.","The project clearly relies on public data and seeks to synthesize information beyond the capacity of one laboratory. The requested NCEMS resources are well justified at a general level, especially staff bioinformatics support and computing. The biological question is suitable for collaborative synthesis and could serve as an effective training platform for graduate students and postdocs in omics reanalysis, cross-species integration, and reproducible workflows. The proposal has potential to create community resources if deliverables are expanded.","The proposal provides little evidence of an assembled multidisciplinary working group beyond the investigators' existing expertise. It does not describe team governance, collaboration practices, trainee training plans, authorship/data policies, code review, reproducibility standards, public engagement, or maintenance of outputs. The open-science commitment is mostly implicit and does not meet the expectations of a synthesis-center call. The request for staff time is reasonable, but the proposal risks treating NCEMS primarily as a bioinformatics service rather than as a platform for community-scale collaboration.",3,"The scientific idea is strong and synthesis-oriented, but the proposal is underdeveloped as a community-scale working group with open, team-based, reproducible science and trainee training.",4,The scientific focus on cytokinetic robustness emerging from interacting cellular modules is relevant to the call. The proposal could better articulate how working group synthesis will reveal emergent principles rather than produce only candidate genes.,4,"The biological question is novel and significant, and the integration of plant cytokinesis with heat-stress omics is promising. The significance for the broader community would be higher if the proposal committed to reusable resources and training materials.",3,"The aims are coherent, but the approach lacks operational detail about data curation, reproducibility, quality control, and collaborative workflow management. These are important components of rigor for synthesis research.",3,"The two-year plan is reasonable for producing harmonized datasets and a candidate bottleneck analysis, but too ambitious if it also aims to develop a broad theory of cytokinetic robustness without a more structured project-management plan.",4,"The project is clearly centered on existing public data and multi-dataset integration. However, the working group rationale is framed largely as access to professional bioinformatics help rather than as a fully developed community-scale synthesis collaboration.",3,"The proposal identifies general sources by organism and modality, but it does not provide a dataset catalog, accessions, metadata plan, or prioritization criteria. A working group should begin with a clearer data landscape.",1,"The open science plan is inadequate for this call. There is no concrete plan for repositories, code release, workflow reproducibility, metadata standards, licensing, public documentation, training modules, or community-created policies.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:15:35.575563,0,success,,"The proposal presents a strong scientific motivation and effectively justifies the need for synthesis-center support, particularly regarding bioinformatics and computational infrastructure. However, it currently falls short of community-scale working group expectations due to a narrow team description and an underdeveloped open science plan. The application would be significantly improved by detailing team governance, collaboration strategies, and concrete, reproducible deliverables.","The project proposes a well-justified biological question that effectively utilizes public data and requires synthesis beyond a single laboratory's capacity. Furthermore, it presents a valuable training opportunity for early-career researchers and clearly justifies the requested computational and bioinformatics resources.","The proposal lacks a comprehensive description of the working group's diversity, governance, training plans, and collaborative practices. Additionally, the open science plan is insufficient, missing concrete deliverables like reproducible workflows and public datasets, which risks treating the synthesis center as a mere bioinformatics service rather than a collaborative community platform."
baseline::y1::10::claude-sonnet-5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,1,baseline::y1::10::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:44:54.494511,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a researcher who has spent my career studying transposable element biology, I find this proposal represents an important and technically overdue synthesis effort. The core premise—that repeat-aware reprocessing of ENCODE, Roadmap, FAANG, and GTEx datasets will reveal previously hidden TE-derived regulatory elements—is well grounded in current literature showing that multi-mapped read discarding has systematically underestimated TE contributions to genome function. The use of Allo and Rcount for probabilistic read allocation is appropriate and reflects recent methodological advances. However, the proposal could more explicitly define which TE families/subfamilies will be prioritized, as TEs vary enormously in age, activity, and regulatory potential (e.g., young SVA/L1 elements vs. ancient MIR/DNA transposons), and a blanket approach risks diluting biologically meaningful signals. I would also want more detail on how false-positive TE-TF associations will be controlled given the naturally repetitive sequence motifs many TFs recognize."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong biological rationale grounded in current TE literature; appropriate use of state-of-the-art repeat-aware computational tools; addresses a well-known analytical gap (multi-mapped reads) that has limited the field for over a decade; the T2T/pangenome integration is timely and maximizes genomic completeness."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lacks specificity on which TE families/clades will be the analytical focus; insufficient discussion of statistical controls for spurious TE-TF motif overlaps; limited discussion of how findings will be experimentally validated or cross-checked against orthogonal databases (e.g., FANTOM CAGE data for enhancers)."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically well-motivated and technically sound proposal that fills a genuine gap in TE functional annotation, though it would benefit from tighter focus on specific TE subfamilies and stronger statistical safeguards."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""TE-driven regulatory network formation is a clear example of mesoscale emergence, linking discrete insertion events to system-level regulatory innovation, though the proposal could better articulate the emergence framework beyond a single paragraph."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""While TE functional studies are not new, the scale and repeat-aware reprocessing approach is novel and significant, potentially resolving long-standing controversies about TE contributions to regulatory evolution."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Methodology is sound but underspecified regarding TE family prioritization and statistical control for confounders inherent in repetitive sequence analysis."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Reprocessing tens of thousands of datasets within two years is ambitious; Phase 1 alone could consume most of the allotted time given known bottlenecks in repeat-aware alignment pipelines."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is a quintessential synthesis effort, aggregating and reprocessing massive public datasets across consortia and species without generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Clear identification of major public data sources (ENCODE, Roadmap, FAANG, GTEx) though additional detail on data selection criteria (e.g., tissue/cell type coverage) would strengthen feasibility assessment."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Mentions public data resource generation but lacks specific commitments to standardized repositories, licensing, or version control practices for reproducibility.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From a computational genomics and bioinformatics infrastructure perspective, this proposal is ambitious in scale and technically grounded, but it underestimates the engineering challenges of reprocessing 75,000+ datasets with repeat-aware pipelines. Multi-mapping read allocation methods like Allo, while promising, are computationally expensive and not yet fully validated across diverse assay types (ATAC-seq vs. Ribo-seq have very different signal properties). The proposal does not specify benchmarking strategies to validate pipeline outputs against known ground truth (e.g., experimentally validated TE-derived enhancers), which is critical before drawing biological conclusions at this scale. The 10 million CPU-hour estimate seems plausible for reference-guided realignment of this many datasets, but I am skeptical this is sufficient once Ribo-seq and RNA-seq isoform reconstruction with TE-derived transcripts are included, as translation-aware repeat mapping is far less mature than ChIP/ATAC-seq pipelines."",
      ""strengths"": ""Well-conceived computational framework leveraging cutting-edge tools (T2T, pangenome, Allo); clear two-phase structure separating reprocessing from biological synthesis; ambitious data scale appropriate for a working-group-level (not single-lab) effort."",
      ""weakness"": ""No explicit benchmarking or validation plan for repeat-aware pipeline outputs; CPU-hour estimate may be insufficient for Ribo-seq and transcript-level analyses; reliance on external computational staff introduces coordination risk; no contingency plan if reprocessing timeline overruns into Phase 2."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Technically exciting but risks underestimating the computational and validation burden of repeat-aware reprocessing at this scale, especially for less mature data modalities like Ribo-seq."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The emergence framing is present but somewhat superficial; the connection between low-level insertion events and higher-order regulatory network emergence is asserted more than mechanistically developed in the analytic plan."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Applying repeat-aware pipelines at this unprecedented scale is a genuinely novel contribution to the field with high potential significance if executed well."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Approach uses appropriate tools but lacks validation benchmarks and quality-control metrics to ensure repeat-aware calls are biologically meaningful rather than pipeline artifacts."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Two years is likely insufficient for reprocessing this volume of heterogeneous data types plus downstream tensor decomposition analyses; the timeline appears overly optimistic."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is an exemplary synthesis project entirely reliant on reprocessing and integrating existing public datasets rather than generating new data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Data sources are well identified and appropriate, though the proposal does not address data heterogeneity issues (batch effects, differing experimental protocols) that complicate cross-dataset integration."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Mentions public data and pipeline release but does not specify a repository, versioning strategy, or containerization plan for reproducibility across computational environments.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As an evolutionary genomicist, I appreciate this proposal's ambition to connect TE-driven molecular changes to broader patterns of species diversification. The comparative angle—examining conservation of TE-derived elements across vertebrate multi-species alignments—is a valuable evolutionary lens, and the emphasis on species-specific enhancers and transcripts aligns well with known cases of TE co-option (e.g., primate-specific TF binding sites). However, the proposal's taxonomic scope is somewhat vague: it mentions 'vertebrates' broadly, but the described datasets (ENCODE, Roadmap, FAANG, GTEx) are heavily human- and mouse-centric, with FAANG covering select livestock species. This creates a mismatch between the stated cross-vertebrate diversification question and the actual comparative depth achievable with existing public data. A more explicit set of target species and phylogenetic hypotheses to be tested would strengthen the evolutionary rigor considerably."",
      ""strengths"": ""Compelling evolutionary framing connecting molecular TE activity to species diversification; appropriate use of comparative genomic resources like multi-species alignments; builds on established literature regarding TE co-option of regulatory elements."",
      ""weakness"": ""Vague taxonomic scope given limited species representation in the named datasets; no explicit phylogenetic comparative framework or statistical methods for testing evolutionary hypotheses (e.g., phylogenetic generalized least squares); insufficient discussion of how lineage-specific TE expansions will be disentangled from shared ancestral TE activity."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Evolutionarily compelling but insufficiently specific about comparative species scope and phylogenetic methodology, limiting confidence that the project will fully deliver on its species diversification framing."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Connecting molecular TE insertion events to emergent, lineage-specific regulatory networks and phenotypic diversification is a strong conceptual fit for the emergence theme."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The evolutionary question of how TEs drive lineage-specific regulatory innovation is significant and under-addressed at this data scale, particularly with repeat-aware methods."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Methodologically sound for regulatory genomics but lacks explicit phylogenetic/statistical comparative methods needed to rigorously test evolutionary hypotheses about diversification."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The comparative vertebrate scope is ambitious relative to the actual species coverage of the named datasets, raising feasibility concerns within the two-year timeline."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Strong synthesis of multiple public consortia datasets, though comparative species data integration could be described in greater depth."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Named datasets are appropriate but insufficiently diverse taxonomically for the stated cross-vertebrate diversification goals."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""General commitment to open data resources is stated, but no specifics on cross-species data harmonization standards or FAIR data principles are provided.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a systems biologist focused on emergence and complex regulatory networks, I find this proposal's articulation of the emergence phenomenon somewhat underdeveloped despite its stated centrality. The abstract and introduction assert that TE-driven regulatory network formation is a 'quintessential mesoscale emergence phenomenon,' but the analytical plan (Phase 2) primarily relies on statistical association and tensor decomposition to identify co-occurrence patterns between TE families, TFs, and expression—this is useful but does not by itself establish emergent network-level properties such as robustness, canalization, or nonlinear regulatory dynamics that are hallmarks of emergence. I would encourage the team to more explicitly model network-level properties (e.g., using regulatory network inference methods, entropy measures of network complexity, or comparisons of network topology across species) rather than relying solely on pairwise or tensor-based associations."",
      ""strengths"": ""Ambitious integrative framework spanning multiple omics layers; conceptually interesting use of tensor decomposition to capture higher-order dependencies; clear multi-lab collaborative structure necessary for problems of this scale."",
      ""weakness"": ""The emergence framing is asserted rather than operationalized through explicit network-level modeling approaches; lacks discussion of quantitative metrics for 'emergent' regulatory network properties; the connection between statistical associations and true causal or emergent regulatory dynamics is not clearly established."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Interesting integrative data science plan, but the emergence framing—central to this funding call—needs deeper operationalization through explicit network or dynamical systems modeling rather than reliance on statistical association alone."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""While the language of emergence is used prominently, the actual analytical methods do not clearly operationalize emergent network properties beyond statistical co-occurrence and tensor decomposition."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The scale of repeat-aware multi-omic integration is novel, and if emergence properties were more rigorously modeled, the significance would be very high."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Tensor decomposition and HMM-based chromatin state annotation are reasonable but insufficiently connected to formal definitions or measures of emergent network behavior."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed integrative analyses (tensor decomposition, chromatin state modeling) are computationally intensive and may be challenging to complete rigorously within the second year alone."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Strong reliance on synthesizing existing multi-omic datasets across consortia, well aligned with the RFP's synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Appropriate identification of large-scale public multi-omic datasets suitable for integrative network analysis."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""States intent to release data resources and pipelines but does not detail specific open science practices such as preregistration, workflow containerization, or community governance of the resource.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From the perspective of someone focused on open science infrastructure and training the next generation of data scientists, this proposal has real strengths in producing a lasting public resource but is comparatively weak in its explicit training and open science plans. The two-phase structure (reprocessing then synthesis) naturally lends itself to training opportunities—graduate students and postdocs could be heavily involved in pipeline development, quality control, and downstream biological interpretation—but the proposal does not describe specific training activities, mentorship structures, or how trainees across the collaborating labs will be integrated into the workflow. Similarly, while 'public data resource' and 'reproducible analysis pipelines' are mentioned as outcomes, there is no discussion of specific repositories (e.g., Zenodo, GEO, or a dedicated portal), licensing, version control practices (e.g., GitHub with DOI-linked releases), or community engagement plans to ensure long-term usability of this resource beyond the funding period."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong potential to generate a durable, high-value public resource for the TE and regulatory genomics community; naturally structured two-phase design offers built-in opportunities for hands-on trainee involvement in both computational reprocessing and biological analysis; multi-lab collaborative structure could offer rich cross-disciplinary training experiences."",
      ""weakness"": ""No explicit trainee mentorship plan, career-stage diversity discussion, or description of how trainees will be credited/co-authored; insufficient detail on data/pipeline release infrastructure, licensing, and long-term maintenance; no discussion of community engagement or governance for the resulting public resource; limited discussion of team diversity across institutions, geography, or career stage as encouraged by the RFP."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A resource-rich proposal with high potential community value, but it falls short of the RFP's explicit expectations around training plans, open science infrastructure specifics, and team diversity considerations."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The emergence framing is conceptually appropriate but is secondary to the proposal's primary focus on resource generation and functional annotation, which is valuable but not deeply tied to emergence theory."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The resulting repeat-aware data resource would be a significant and novel contribution usable by the broader community well beyond this specific working group."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Methods are reasonable for the stated goals, though rigor around reproducibility practices (e.g., containerized workflows, unit testing of pipelines) is not detailed."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Two years is tight not only for the analysis but also for establishing robust open science infrastructure and meaningful trainee integration alongside the core scientific work."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Excellent alignment with the synthesis mandate, reprocessing and integrating massive volumes of existing public data without new data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Public data sources are clearly identified and appropriate in scale and relevance to the scientific questions posed."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Open science commitments are mentioned only in general terms without specifying repositories, licensing, versioning, or long-term stewardship plans, falling short of the RFP's explicit open science expectations.""
    }
