}",0,y1::10,y1,10,Transposable elements and the emergence of genomic innovation,Accepted,Shaun Mahony,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"As a researcher who has spent my career studying transposable element biology, I find this proposal represents an important and technically overdue synthesis effort. The core premise—that repeat-aware reprocessing of ENCODE, Roadmap, FAANG, and GTEx datasets will reveal previously hidden TE-derived regulatory elements—is well grounded in current literature showing that multi-mapped read discarding has systematically underestimated TE contributions to genome function. The use of Allo and Rcount for probabilistic read allocation is appropriate and reflects recent methodological advances. However, the proposal could more explicitly define which TE families/subfamilies will be prioritized, as TEs vary enormously in age, activity, and regulatory potential (e.g., young SVA/L1 elements vs. ancient MIR/DNA transposons), and a blanket approach risks diluting biologically meaningful signals. I would also want more detail on how false-positive TE-TF associations will be controlled given the naturally repetitive sequence motifs many TFs recognize.",Strong biological rationale grounded in current TE literature; appropriate use of state-of-the-art repeat-aware computational tools; addresses a well-known analytical gap (multi-mapped reads) that has limited the field for over a decade; the T2T/pangenome integration is timely and maximizes genomic completeness.,"Lacks specificity on which TE families/clades will be the analytical focus; insufficient discussion of statistical controls for spurious TE-TF motif overlaps; limited discussion of how findings will be experimentally validated or cross-checked against orthogonal databases (e.g., FANTOM CAGE data for enhancers).",4,"A biologically well-motivated and technically sound proposal that fills a genuine gap in TE functional annotation, though it would benefit from tighter focus on specific TE subfamilies and stronger statistical safeguards.",4,"TE-driven regulatory network formation is a clear example of mesoscale emergence, linking discrete insertion events to system-level regulatory innovation, though the proposal could better articulate the emergence framework beyond a single paragraph.",4,"While TE functional studies are not new, the scale and repeat-aware reprocessing approach is novel and significant, potentially resolving long-standing controversies about TE contributions to regulatory evolution.",3,Methodology is sound but underspecified regarding TE family prioritization and statistical control for confounders inherent in repetitive sequence analysis.,3,Reprocessing tens of thousands of datasets within two years is ambitious; Phase 1 alone could consume most of the allotted time given known bottlenecks in repeat-aware alignment pipelines.,5,"The project is a quintessential synthesis effort, aggregating and reprocessing massive public datasets across consortia and species without generating new experimental data.",4,"Clear identification of major public data sources (ENCODE, Roadmap, FAANG, GTEx) though additional detail on data selection criteria (e.g., tissue/cell type coverage) would strengthen feasibility assessment.",3,"Mentions public data resource generation but lacks specific commitments to standardized repositories, licensing, or version control practices for reproducibility.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:15:44.990398,0,success,,"This proposal presents a well-grounded synthesis effort to identify transposable element (TE)-derived regulatory elements by applying repeat-aware reprocessing to major genomic datasets. The proposed use of probabilistic read allocation tools like Allo and Rcount appropriately addresses the historical analytical gap of discarded multi-mapped reads. However, the methodology would benefit from prioritizing specific TE families based on their age and regulatory potential to avoid diluting biological signals. Additionally, further methodological details are needed regarding statistical controls for false-positive TE-transcription factor associations.","The project offers a strong biological rationale and employs state-of-the-art repeat-aware computational tools to address the persistent analytical challenge of multi-mapped reads. Furthermore, the integration of T2T and pangenome data is highly timely and maximizes genomic completeness.",The proposal lacks specificity regarding which TE families or clades will be prioritized for analysis. It also provides insufficient details on statistical controls for spurious TE-transcription factor motif overlaps and lacks discussion on experimental validation or orthogonal cross-checking.
