    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""This project seeks to address a major blind spot in epigenomics: the systemic discarding of multi-mapping reads which heavily bias our current understanding of the genome away from repetitive elements. By revisiting ENCODE, Roadmap, and other functional datasets with repeat-aware pipelines, this working group could fundamentally rewrite the functional annotation of the vertebrate genome. The proposal is incredibly strong in its molecular rationale. However, from a practical standpoint, the scope is dangerously vast. Reprocessing 75,000 datasets—encompassing DNA-based (ATAC/ChIP), RNA-based (RNA-seq), and translation-based (Ribo-seq) reads—requires vastly different quality control metrics, mapping parameters, and bias corrections. Requesting 10 million CPU hours is indicative of the scale, but completing this in 12 months for Phase 1 is highly improbable. A more focused approach on a subset of high-quality data (e.g., just human and mouse ATAC/RNA-seq) would be much more feasible and still highly impactful."",
      ""strengths"": ""Tackles a massive, acknowledged bias in molecular biology (the discarding of repetitive reads). The potential to rescue this data and find novel TE-enhancers is huge."",
      ""weakness"": ""The timeline and scope are overly ambitious. Reprocessing 75,000 datasets across distinct molecular modalities (DNA, RNA, Ribosome) in one year is practically unfeasible."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A highly impactful and necessary project for the epigenetics field, offset by a dangerously optimistic timeline and an overwhelmingly massive scope of data reprocessing."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Successfully frames TE activation and co-option as the foundation of emergent genomic functions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Solving the 'junk DNA' multi-mapping problem on this scale will provide an invaluable and novel resource to the entire molecular biology community."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 5,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The methodology for rescuing multi-mapped reads is state-of-the-art and exactly what is needed to address this problem."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""One year to QA/QC, align, and probabilistically resolve 75,000 diverse datasets is nearly impossible without a massive dedicated software engineering team, which is not detailed."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Perfectly aligns with the goal of synthesizing community-scale data to answer long-standing puzzles."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Excellent choice of foundational datasets that represent the gold standard of molecular data."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Creating a public data resource of repeat-aware annotations will be a massive contribution to open science.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Evaluating this from a data science and infrastructure perspective, the proposal is a compelling use case for NCEMS support. The investigators clearly understand that moving beyond single-lab capabilities requires a working group equipped with significant computing power (10 million CPU hours). The integration of T2T assemblies with legacy short-read data is an excellent strategy to maximize the value of public data. However, as an infrastructure expert, I must point out a critical flaw: storage and sustainability. The intermediate BAM files and processed tensor matrices for 75,000 datasets will easily reach into the petabyte range. The proposal mentions requesting 'storage' but provides no estimation of the footprint, nor does it outline a data management plan for hosting this public resource after the 2-year grant period ends. Open science requires sustainable hosting. Additionally, the lack of an explicit training plan for data-savvy trainees is a missed opportunity to leverage this massive computational effort for educational purposes."",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear justification for NCEMS infrastructure support. Excellent strategy to synthesize existing data into a new, highly valuable format."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lack of a long-term data sustainability plan for a petabyte-scale resource. Absence of explicit trainee involvement."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A computationally brilliant proposal that perfectly justifies the need for NCEMS synthesis support, but requires a much more robust data management and sustainability plan."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The conceptual link between local TE insertions and global emergent regulatory properties is a strong fit for the call."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Methodologically innovative, providing a new lens through which to view legacy datasets."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Computational pipelines are well-reasoned, but the data engineering rigor regarding storage scaling and I/O bottlenecks at this magnitude is lacking."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Scope is vast; the timeline does not adequately account for the data transfer and I/O time required to simply move 75,000 raw datasets into the compute environment."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes thousands of isolated datasets into a unified, repeat-aware biological framework."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Data sources are well identified, but the sheer volume introduces unrecognized data engineering challenges."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Commitment to open data is stated, but true open science requires sustainable access. A 2-year project generating petabytes of data needs a concrete post-award hosting plan, which is missing. The training aspect is also neglected.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::10,y1,10,Transposable elements and the emergence of genomic innovation,Accepted,Shaun Mahony,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Evaluating this from a data science and infrastructure perspective, the proposal is a compelling use case for NCEMS support. The investigators clearly understand that moving beyond single-lab capabilities requires a working group equipped with significant computing power (10 million CPU hours). The integration of T2T assemblies with legacy short-read data is an excellent strategy to maximize the value of public data. However, as an infrastructure expert, I must point out a critical flaw: storage and sustainability. The intermediate BAM files and processed tensor matrices for 75,000 datasets will easily reach into the petabyte range. The proposal mentions requesting 'storage' but provides no estimation of the footprint, nor does it outline a data management plan for hosting this public resource after the 2-year grant period ends. Open science requires sustainable hosting. Additionally, the lack of an explicit training plan for data-savvy trainees is a missed opportunity to leverage this massive computational effort for educational purposes.","Clear justification for NCEMS infrastructure support. Excellent strategy to synthesize existing data into a new, highly valuable format.",Lack of a long-term data sustainability plan for a petabyte-scale resource. Absence of explicit trainee involvement.,4,"A computationally brilliant proposal that perfectly justifies the need for NCEMS synthesis support, but requires a much more robust data management and sustainability plan.",5,The conceptual link between local TE insertions and global emergent regulatory properties is a strong fit for the call.,4,"Methodologically innovative, providing a new lens through which to view legacy datasets.",4,"Computational pipelines are well-reasoned, but the data engineering rigor regarding storage scaling and I/O bottlenecks at this magnitude is lacking.",3,"Scope is vast; the timeline does not adequately account for the data transfer and I/O time required to simply move 75,000 raw datasets into the compute environment.",5,"Synthesizes thousands of isolated datasets into a unified, repeat-aware biological framework.",4,"Data sources are well identified, but the sheer volume introduces unrecognized data engineering challenges.",3,"Commitment to open data is stated, but true open science requires sustainable access. A 2-year project generating petabytes of data needs a concrete post-award hosting plan, which is missing. The training aspect is also neglected.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:17:06.049705,0,success,,"The proposal presents a strong use case for NCEMS computational support by integrating T2T assemblies with legacy short-read data. While the project effectively justifies its need for significant computing resources, it lacks necessary details regarding data storage and long-term sustainability. Furthermore, the proposal misses an opportunity to include an explicit educational training plan for leveraging the computational infrastructure.","The proposal provides clear justification for utilizing NCEMS infrastructure to process large-scale genomic datasets. Additionally, integrating existing legacy data into new, high-value formats is an effective and valuable scientific strategy.","The proposal lacks required storage estimations and a long-term data management plan for hosting petabyte-scale resources after the grant period. Additionally, it does not include an explicit training plan to engage trainees in this computational effort."
baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,1,baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:42:30.117632,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior transposable element evolutionary biologist. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: how transposable elements contribute to vertebrate genomic innovation by generating regulatory and coding novelty. The proposal is well aligned with the call because it is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public data and because the repetitive fraction of genomes remains underanalyzed in many major resources. The biological motivation is compelling, and the investigators correctly identify that standard short-read pipelines have systematically discarded or underused multi-mapping reads, biasing interpretation away from TE-rich regions. The planned integration of ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, RNA-seq, Ribo-seq, pangenome assemblies, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, and comparative annotations could generate a valuable community resource. However, the proposal is very broad. It aims to address transcription factor binding, enhancers, transcripts, translated sequences, cell specificity, species specificity, conservation, and phenotypic diversification across vertebrates in only two years. From a TE biology perspective, the proposal would be stronger if it more clearly defined the taxonomic scope, which TE classes and families are priorities, how orthology and lineage specificity will be established for repetitive elements, and how it will distinguish TE exaptation from passive overlap with regulatory marks. The planned intersections with RepeatMasker annotations are necessary but not sufficient for robust evolutionary inference, especially in non-model vertebrates and in nested or fragmented repeats. Overall, this is a high-impact synthesis concept with clear relevance to emergence, but it needs sharper biological hypotheses and more explicit validation strategies for TE-derived function."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal tackles a central problem in genome evolution and regulatory innovation; it uses only existing public datasets; it leverages new complete and pangenome assemblies; it addresses a known bias in conventional genomic analysis; it has strong potential to produce a broadly useful repeat-aware resource; and it requires cross-disciplinary expertise across TE biology, regulatory genomics, comparative genomics, and computational analysis."",
      ""weakness"": ""The biological scope is overly expansive; the proposal does not sufficiently specify which vertebrate species, TE families, or evolutionary time scales will be analyzed; methods for distinguishing functional exaptation from coincidental overlap are underdeveloped; comparative inference in repetitive regions is technically difficult and not described in adequate detail; and the two-year timeline appears optimistic for reprocessing and synthesizing more than 75,000 datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling and highly relevant proposal that could transform analysis of TE-associated function, but it would benefit from narrower biological aims and more rigorous evolutionary criteria for functional innovation."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal clearly links molecular events, TE insertions, to higher-order outcomes such as regulatory networks, tissue-specific expression, and species diversification. This is an excellent example of emergence across biological scales."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Repeat-aware reanalysis of large public functional genomics resources using complete and pangenome assemblies is significant and timely. The individual concepts are not entirely new, but the proposed scale and synthesis across modalities could be highly novel."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad computational strategy is appropriate, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on evolutionary inference, orthology of TE-derived loci, false discovery control, benchmarking of multi-mapping allocation, and criteria for defining TE-derived function."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Reprocessing tens of thousands of datasets and conducting integrative cross-species analyses within two years is likely unrealistic without a more staged plan and prioritization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely focused on reanalysis and synthesis of existing public data and explicitly does not propose new data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Major data sources are identified, including ENCODE, Roadmap Epigenomics, FAANG, GTEx, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, pangenomes, and multi-species alignments. However, the exact dataset inclusion criteria and species list are not sufficiently defined."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal commits to public datasets, public data resources, and reproducible pipelines. More detail is needed on repositories, metadata standards, versioning, long-term maintenance, and community governance.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior computational genomics and statistical bioinformatics researcher. The proposal identifies an important computational bottleneck in functional genomics: the loss of signal in repetitive regions caused by conventional handling of multi-mapped reads. Reprocessing public datasets with repeat-aware pipelines could be highly valuable, particularly if performed reproducibly and released in a standardized format. The proposal appropriately mentions probabilistic allocation methods such as Allo and Rcount, use of telomere-to-telomere and pangenome assemblies, peak calling, chromatin-state modeling, statistical association, and tensor decomposition. These are reasonable components of a modern synthesis pipeline. That said, the computational plan is currently more aspirational than operational. There is little discussion of benchmarking, scalability, error propagation, batch effects across consortia, assay-specific quality control, handling of variable read lengths and sequencing depths, genome-build harmonization, pangenome coordinate systems, or uncertainty estimates from multi-read allocation. These omissions matter because the central claim depends on accurately assigning reads in highly repetitive sequence contexts. The project also proposes 10 million CPU hours for more than 75,000 datasets, but no evidence is given that this is sufficient or that storage, I/O, metadata harmonization, and workflow orchestration have been fully costed. The proposal is a strong fit to NCEMS because it requires collaborative infrastructure and public-data synthesis, but it should be refined into a more rigorous computational production plan with milestones, pilot benchmarks, and clear quality metrics."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal targets a well-known computational blind spot; it would create a reusable community resource; it uses public data only; it integrates multiple assay types; it would benefit substantially from shared computational infrastructure; and the proposed collaboration is beyond the scale of a single laboratory."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational workflow is insufficiently specified; benchmarking and validation are not described in detail; pangenome coordinate handling is not adequately addressed; uncertainty from probabilistic read assignment is not propagated into downstream analyses; resource estimates are not well justified; and the plan lacks clear go/no-go milestones or prioritization if full-scale processing proves infeasible."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A potentially high-impact computational synthesis project, but the rigor and feasibility of the large-scale reprocessing plan need substantially more detail before confidence in execution is high."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The emergence framing is credible, connecting repeat-level sequence variation to regulatory network-level phenomena. However, the computational methods for inferring those higher-order relationships are not yet sufficiently developed."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The proposal is significant because it addresses a systematic bias in public genomics resources. The novelty lies primarily in scale and integration rather than in creating fundamentally new algorithms."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists appropriate tools and analyses but lacks operational details on benchmarking, quality control, reproducibility, statistical uncertainty, cross-assay harmonization, and validation of multi-mapping read allocation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed processing of over 75,000 datasets in two years is extremely ambitious. The timeline does not adequately account for metadata curation, failed runs, workflow debugging, storage constraints, and downstream integration."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis project that relies entirely on existing public data and aims to integrate datasets across modalities and species."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies relevant public resources and assay types, but lacks a concrete dataset manifest, inclusion/exclusion criteria, and prioritization strategy."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal promises public resources and reproducible workflows, but does not specify workflow languages, containerization strategy, cloud/HPC portability, metadata schemas, licenses, or maintenance plans.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior regulatory genomics and epigenomics scientist. This proposal addresses a major gap in regulatory genomics: many functional genomic annotations have been constructed using approaches that undercount repetitive and TE-derived regions. The proposal has particular strength in its plan to synthesize ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, histone modification data, RNA-seq, and Ribo-seq to identify TE-derived promoters, enhancers, transcription factor binding sites, transcripts, and translated elements. The biological questions are compelling, especially the proposed analysis of which transcription factors preferentially bind specific TE families and how TE-derived enhancers contribute to tissue- or cell-type-specific gene regulation. This is a strong match to the call's emphasis on community-scale synthesis. However, the regulatory interpretation needs more rigor. Peak calling in repeats is challenging, and probabilistic read allocation can increase apparent signal in repeats in ways that are difficult to distinguish from mapping artifacts. The proposal does not discuss how it will handle assay-specific confounders, input controls, mappability, copy-number variation, pangenome structural variation, or cell-type heterogeneity. The use of hidden Markov models for chromatin states is reasonable, but chromatin state models are sensitive to assay availability and normalization. The proposal would be improved by defining a smaller number of benchmark cell types, transcription factors, and TE families for initial analysis and by specifying orthogonal validation using already-public long-read, CAGE, eQTL, MPRA, CRISPR screen, or proteomics datasets where available. As written, the proposal is exciting but risks producing a very large catalog with uneven interpretability."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal focuses on an important missing component of regulatory annotation; it integrates multiple public epigenomic and transcriptomic modalities; it could reveal mechanisms of tissue-specific and species-specific regulatory innovation; it will generate a useful repeat-aware regulatory resource; and it fits the need for a multi-lab working group."",
      ""weakness"": ""Regulatory function criteria are not defined clearly enough; peak calling and chromatin-state annotation in repetitive regions require careful controls not described in detail; the proposal does not specify how it will account for mappability, input controls, copy number, or cell-type heterogeneity; and there is a risk of overinterpreting correlative overlap between TEs and regulatory marks."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and relevant proposal with excellent potential to reshape regulatory annotation, provided the group strengthens controls for repeat-associated artifacts and prioritizes interpretable biological analyses."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal convincingly frames TE-derived regulatory elements as molecular changes that can reshape gene regulatory networks. The emergence concept is strong, though direct links to phenotypic diversification are less developed."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A systematic repeat-aware reanalysis of regulatory genomics datasets could be a major contribution. The focus on TE-derived enhancers, TF binding, and cell-specific regulatory programs is highly significant."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall plan is plausible, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on controls, normalization, chromatin-state modeling choices, mappability correction, and criteria for regulatory function."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project is ambitious but potentially feasible if staged. As written, the breadth of assays, species, and biological questions is too large for the two-year timeline without prioritization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort based on existing public functional genomics data and integrative analysis."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies appropriate resources such as ENCODE, Roadmap, GTEx, FAANG, and comparative vertebrate datasets. It should provide more precise dataset selection criteria and metadata harmonization plans."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to public data resources and reproducible pipelines is appropriate. The proposal would benefit from more detailed plans for community access, visualization, versioning, and long-term sustainability.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior comparative vertebrate genomics and evolutionary developmental biology researcher. The proposal is ambitious and potentially important because it seeks to connect TE insertions to vertebrate diversification through regulatory and coding innovation. The emphasis on complete assemblies and pangenomes is timely, and the focus on public resources such as FAANG and comparative vertebrate datasets is appropriate for the call. A comparative framework is essential for determining whether TE-derived elements are lineage-specific, conserved, convergent, or associated with shifts in cell-type regulatory programs. However, the comparative component is underdeveloped. The proposal says it will analyze vertebrates but provides concrete alignment plans mainly for human and mouse. It does not explain how it will handle differences in annotation quality among species, incomplete or uneven assay coverage, lack of matched tissues across species, TE family turnover, or ambiguous orthology of repetitive elements. The goal of explaining phenotypic diversification is much broader than the proposed analyses can support unless specific phenotypes, lineages, and regulatory systems are chosen. The project could be excellent if it focused on a defined set of comparative contrasts, such as mammalian placenta, immune cells, brain development, limb development, or other cell types with rich public data and known TE contributions. Without this narrowing, the proposal risks conflating human-centric annotation expansion with vertebrate evolutionary synthesis. I view this as a strong concept with a weaker comparative implementation plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a major question in genome evolution; it uses existing public comparative datasets; it leverages new genome assemblies; it integrates regulatory, transcriptomic, and translational data; and it has the potential to reveal lineage-specific mechanisms of regulatory innovation."",
      ""weakness"": ""The vertebrate comparative scope is not well defined; the proposal is overly human and mouse centered relative to its evolutionary claims; cross-species tissue matching and orthology methods are not described; TE family evolution and annotation variability are not adequately addressed; and links to phenotypic diversification are asserted more than operationalized."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising proposal with strong evolutionary motivation, but the comparative vertebrate strategy needs clearer species selection, orthology framework, and biologically focused contrasts."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project addresses emergence from genome sequence changes to regulatory system evolution. The emergence framing is appropriate, but phenotypic emergence across vertebrates is not yet supported by a concrete analytical plan."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The question of TE-driven vertebrate innovation is significant, and large-scale repeat-aware reanalysis could provide new insight. The novelty would be greater with a more explicit comparative evolutionary design."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The general methods are reasonable, but the proposal lacks detail on cross-species alignment, TE orthology, tissue equivalence, annotation biases, and phylogenetic statistical models."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed vertebrate-wide scope, combined with tens of thousands of datasets and multiple data modalities, is too broad for two years without a prioritized comparative design."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project fits the synthesis mandate well, relying on public data and integrating diverse resources rather than generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Several relevant sources are named, but the proposal does not provide a species list, tissue list, comparative dataset inventory, or criteria for selecting vertebrate datasets with adequate quality and comparability."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that outputs, data, and workflows will be public. More specifics on comparative annotation standards, reusable genome-coordinate mappings, and user-facing access would strengthen this aspect.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior data-infrastructure, open-science, and team-science leader in molecular biosciences. This proposal is highly aligned with the NCEMS model of community-scale synthesis. It uses existing public data, requires expertise across multiple laboratories, and proposes to create a reusable public resource that could serve many communities beyond the immediate team. The scale of reprocessing, harmonizing, storing, and releasing repeat-aware functional genomics datasets clearly justifies support beyond a typical single-lab project. The proposal also identifies the need for staff scientist support, compute, storage, meetings, and personnel exchanges, which are appropriate NCEMS resources. However, the team-science and open-science implementation is not yet fully articulated. The proposal says the resource and pipelines will be public but does not discuss governance, authorship policies, community input, documentation standards, training modules, trainee roles, hackathons, release schedules, user support, or long-term maintenance after the two-year project. The call explicitly emphasizes tapping diverse talent and training the next data-savvy workforce; the proposal mentions personnel exchanges but gives little detail on graduate student or postdoctoral training. From a resource-building perspective, the plan also needs a clearer data management architecture: identifiers, metadata schema, workflow provenance, versioning, reproducibility audits, and deposition locations. I am enthusiastic about the scientific opportunity, but the proposal should more explicitly operate as a community working group rather than only a large computational production effort."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent fit to the public-data synthesis mission; strong justification for shared infrastructure and staff support; high potential to produce a valuable public resource; multidisciplinary expertise is clearly needed; and the project could train researchers in repeat-aware genomics and reproducible data integration."",
      ""weakness"": ""Open-science implementation is underspecified; trainee training plans are minimal; team governance and community engagement are not described; data management, metadata, provenance, and versioning plans are incomplete; and sustainability beyond the funding period is unclear."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong synthesis-resource proposal with major potential, but it needs a more concrete plan for open science, training, community engagement, and sustainable data infrastructure."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal makes a plausible case that TE insertions give rise to emergent regulatory networks and species-specific biological functions. The framing fits the call, although implementation focuses more on resource generation than explicit emergence modeling."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The resource would be significant and likely widely used. The novelty is strongest in the scale and harmonized repeat-aware reprocessing of existing datasets."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The scientific approach is broadly sound, but the proposal lacks detailed quality assurance, workflow provenance, reproducibility checks, and data release procedures needed for a large community resource."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project is large but may be feasible if NCEMS provides substantial staff and compute support. The timeline would be more convincing with staged releases, pilot datasets, and contingency plans."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal strongly satisfies the synthesis requirement by reusing and integrating public data across consortia, modalities, assemblies, and species."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names major public sources and assay types, but it does not yet provide a formal data inventory, metadata harmonization plan, or release matrix."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""There is a general commitment to public data and reproducible workflows, but the proposal lacks specifics on repositories, licenses, documentation, governance, training materials, community standards, and long-term maintenance.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::10,y1,10,Transposable elements and the emergence of genomic innovation,Accepted,Shaun Mahony,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer perspective: senior transposable element evolutionary biologist. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: how transposable elements contribute to vertebrate genomic innovation by generating regulatory and coding novelty. The proposal is well aligned with the call because it is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public data and because the repetitive fraction of genomes remains underanalyzed in many major resources. The biological motivation is compelling, and the investigators correctly identify that standard short-read pipelines have systematically discarded or underused multi-mapping reads, biasing interpretation away from TE-rich regions. The planned integration of ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, RNA-seq, Ribo-seq, pangenome assemblies, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, and comparative annotations could generate a valuable community resource. However, the proposal is very broad. It aims to address transcription factor binding, enhancers, transcripts, translated sequences, cell specificity, species specificity, conservation, and phenotypic diversification across vertebrates in only two years. From a TE biology perspective, the proposal would be stronger if it more clearly defined the taxonomic scope, which TE classes and families are priorities, how orthology and lineage specificity will be established for repetitive elements, and how it will distinguish TE exaptation from passive overlap with regulatory marks. The planned intersections with RepeatMasker annotations are necessary but not sufficient for robust evolutionary inference, especially in non-model vertebrates and in nested or fragmented repeats. Overall, this is a high-impact synthesis concept with clear relevance to emergence, but it needs sharper biological hypotheses and more explicit validation strategies for TE-derived function.","The proposal tackles a central problem in genome evolution and regulatory innovation; it uses only existing public datasets; it leverages new complete and pangenome assemblies; it addresses a known bias in conventional genomic analysis; it has strong potential to produce a broadly useful repeat-aware resource; and it requires cross-disciplinary expertise across TE biology, regulatory genomics, comparative genomics, and computational analysis.","The biological scope is overly expansive; the proposal does not sufficiently specify which vertebrate species, TE families, or evolutionary time scales will be analyzed; methods for distinguishing functional exaptation from coincidental overlap are underdeveloped; comparative inference in repetitive regions is technically difficult and not described in adequate detail; and the two-year timeline appears optimistic for reprocessing and synthesizing more than 75,000 datasets.",4,"A compelling and highly relevant proposal that could transform analysis of TE-associated function, but it would benefit from narrower biological aims and more rigorous evolutionary criteria for functional innovation.",5,"The proposal clearly links molecular events, TE insertions, to higher-order outcomes such as regulatory networks, tissue-specific expression, and species diversification. This is an excellent example of emergence across biological scales.",4,"Repeat-aware reanalysis of large public functional genomics resources using complete and pangenome assemblies is significant and timely. The individual concepts are not entirely new, but the proposed scale and synthesis across modalities could be highly novel.",3,"The broad computational strategy is appropriate, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on evolutionary inference, orthology of TE-derived loci, false discovery control, benchmarking of multi-mapping allocation, and criteria for defining TE-derived function.",2,Reprocessing tens of thousands of datasets and conducting integrative cross-species analyses within two years is likely unrealistic without a more staged plan and prioritization.,5,The project is entirely focused on reanalysis and synthesis of existing public data and explicitly does not propose new data generation.,4,"Major data sources are identified, including ENCODE, Roadmap Epigenomics, FAANG, GTEx, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, pangenomes, and multi-species alignments. However, the exact dataset inclusion criteria and species list are not sufficiently defined.",4,"The proposal commits to public datasets, public data resources, and reproducible pipelines. More detail is needed on repositories, metadata standards, versioning, long-term maintenance, and community governance.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:17:15.441060,0,success,,"This proposal addresses the significant role of transposable elements (TEs) in vertebrate genomic innovation by synthesizing existing public omics datasets. While the project presents a compelling, high-impact concept that corrects conventional short-read analysis biases, its broad scope limits its overall feasibility. To improve, the proposal requires sharper biological hypotheses, clearer taxonomic and TE-specific parameters, and more rigorous methods for establishing orthology and functional exaptation.","The project targets a critical problem in genome evolution by leveraging new pangenome assemblies to correct known analytical biases in repetitive regions. It efficiently utilizes existing public data and demonstrates strong potential to produce a valuable, cross-disciplinary community resource.","The project's biological scope is excessively broad, rendering the two-year timeline overly optimistic for processing over 75,000 datasets. Furthermore, it lacks sufficient detail regarding the targeted species, TE families, and the specific methodologies required to differentiate functional exaptation from coincidental overlap."
baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::002,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,2,baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:42:30.117632,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior transposable element evolutionary biologist. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: how transposable elements contribute to vertebrate genomic innovation by generating regulatory and coding novelty. The proposal is well aligned with the call because it is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public data and because the repetitive fraction of genomes remains underanalyzed in many major resources. The biological motivation is compelling, and the investigators correctly identify that standard short-read pipelines have systematically discarded or underused multi-mapping reads, biasing interpretation away from TE-rich regions. The planned integration of ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, RNA-seq, Ribo-seq, pangenome assemblies, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, and comparative annotations could generate a valuable community resource. However, the proposal is very broad. It aims to address transcription factor binding, enhancers, transcripts, translated sequences, cell specificity, species specificity, conservation, and phenotypic diversification across vertebrates in only two years. From a TE biology perspective, the proposal would be stronger if it more clearly defined the taxonomic scope, which TE classes and families are priorities, how orthology and lineage specificity will be established for repetitive elements, and how it will distinguish TE exaptation from passive overlap with regulatory marks. The planned intersections with RepeatMasker annotations are necessary but not sufficient for robust evolutionary inference, especially in non-model vertebrates and in nested or fragmented repeats. Overall, this is a high-impact synthesis concept with clear relevance to emergence, but it needs sharper biological hypotheses and more explicit validation strategies for TE-derived function."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal tackles a central problem in genome evolution and regulatory innovation; it uses only existing public datasets; it leverages new complete and pangenome assemblies; it addresses a known bias in conventional genomic analysis; it has strong potential to produce a broadly useful repeat-aware resource; and it requires cross-disciplinary expertise across TE biology, regulatory genomics, comparative genomics, and computational analysis."",
      ""weakness"": ""The biological scope is overly expansive; the proposal does not sufficiently specify which vertebrate species, TE families, or evolutionary time scales will be analyzed; methods for distinguishing functional exaptation from coincidental overlap are underdeveloped; comparative inference in repetitive regions is technically difficult and not described in adequate detail; and the two-year timeline appears optimistic for reprocessing and synthesizing more than 75,000 datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling and highly relevant proposal that could transform analysis of TE-associated function, but it would benefit from narrower biological aims and more rigorous evolutionary criteria for functional innovation."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal clearly links molecular events, TE insertions, to higher-order outcomes such as regulatory networks, tissue-specific expression, and species diversification. This is an excellent example of emergence across biological scales."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Repeat-aware reanalysis of large public functional genomics resources using complete and pangenome assemblies is significant and timely. The individual concepts are not entirely new, but the proposed scale and synthesis across modalities could be highly novel."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad computational strategy is appropriate, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on evolutionary inference, orthology of TE-derived loci, false discovery control, benchmarking of multi-mapping allocation, and criteria for defining TE-derived function."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Reprocessing tens of thousands of datasets and conducting integrative cross-species analyses within two years is likely unrealistic without a more staged plan and prioritization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely focused on reanalysis and synthesis of existing public data and explicitly does not propose new data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Major data sources are identified, including ENCODE, Roadmap Epigenomics, FAANG, GTEx, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, pangenomes, and multi-species alignments. However, the exact dataset inclusion criteria and species list are not sufficiently defined."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal commits to public datasets, public data resources, and reproducible pipelines. More detail is needed on repositories, metadata standards, versioning, long-term maintenance, and community governance.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior computational genomics and statistical bioinformatics researcher. The proposal identifies an important computational bottleneck in functional genomics: the loss of signal in repetitive regions caused by conventional handling of multi-mapped reads. Reprocessing public datasets with repeat-aware pipelines could be highly valuable, particularly if performed reproducibly and released in a standardized format. The proposal appropriately mentions probabilistic allocation methods such as Allo and Rcount, use of telomere-to-telomere and pangenome assemblies, peak calling, chromatin-state modeling, statistical association, and tensor decomposition. These are reasonable components of a modern synthesis pipeline. That said, the computational plan is currently more aspirational than operational. There is little discussion of benchmarking, scalability, error propagation, batch effects across consortia, assay-specific quality control, handling of variable read lengths and sequencing depths, genome-build harmonization, pangenome coordinate systems, or uncertainty estimates from multi-read allocation. These omissions matter because the central claim depends on accurately assigning reads in highly repetitive sequence contexts. The project also proposes 10 million CPU hours for more than 75,000 datasets, but no evidence is given that this is sufficient or that storage, I/O, metadata harmonization, and workflow orchestration have been fully costed. The proposal is a strong fit to NCEMS because it requires collaborative infrastructure and public-data synthesis, but it should be refined into a more rigorous computational production plan with milestones, pilot benchmarks, and clear quality metrics."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal targets a well-known computational blind spot; it would create a reusable community resource; it uses public data only; it integrates multiple assay types; it would benefit substantially from shared computational infrastructure; and the proposed collaboration is beyond the scale of a single laboratory."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational workflow is insufficiently specified; benchmarking and validation are not described in detail; pangenome coordinate handling is not adequately addressed; uncertainty from probabilistic read assignment is not propagated into downstream analyses; resource estimates are not well justified; and the plan lacks clear go/no-go milestones or prioritization if full-scale processing proves infeasible."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A potentially high-impact computational synthesis project, but the rigor and feasibility of the large-scale reprocessing plan need substantially more detail before confidence in execution is high."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The emergence framing is credible, connecting repeat-level sequence variation to regulatory network-level phenomena. However, the computational methods for inferring those higher-order relationships are not yet sufficiently developed."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The proposal is significant because it addresses a systematic bias in public genomics resources. The novelty lies primarily in scale and integration rather than in creating fundamentally new algorithms."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists appropriate tools and analyses but lacks operational details on benchmarking, quality control, reproducibility, statistical uncertainty, cross-assay harmonization, and validation of multi-mapping read allocation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed processing of over 75,000 datasets in two years is extremely ambitious. The timeline does not adequately account for metadata curation, failed runs, workflow debugging, storage constraints, and downstream integration."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis project that relies entirely on existing public data and aims to integrate datasets across modalities and species."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies relevant public resources and assay types, but lacks a concrete dataset manifest, inclusion/exclusion criteria, and prioritization strategy."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal promises public resources and reproducible workflows, but does not specify workflow languages, containerization strategy, cloud/HPC portability, metadata schemas, licenses, or maintenance plans.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior regulatory genomics and epigenomics scientist. This proposal addresses a major gap in regulatory genomics: many functional genomic annotations have been constructed using approaches that undercount repetitive and TE-derived regions. The proposal has particular strength in its plan to synthesize ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, histone modification data, RNA-seq, and Ribo-seq to identify TE-derived promoters, enhancers, transcription factor binding sites, transcripts, and translated elements. The biological questions are compelling, especially the proposed analysis of which transcription factors preferentially bind specific TE families and how TE-derived enhancers contribute to tissue- or cell-type-specific gene regulation. This is a strong match to the call's emphasis on community-scale synthesis. However, the regulatory interpretation needs more rigor. Peak calling in repeats is challenging, and probabilistic read allocation can increase apparent signal in repeats in ways that are difficult to distinguish from mapping artifacts. The proposal does not discuss how it will handle assay-specific confounders, input controls, mappability, copy-number variation, pangenome structural variation, or cell-type heterogeneity. The use of hidden Markov models for chromatin states is reasonable, but chromatin state models are sensitive to assay availability and normalization. The proposal would be improved by defining a smaller number of benchmark cell types, transcription factors, and TE families for initial analysis and by specifying orthogonal validation using already-public long-read, CAGE, eQTL, MPRA, CRISPR screen, or proteomics datasets where available. As written, the proposal is exciting but risks producing a very large catalog with uneven interpretability."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal focuses on an important missing component of regulatory annotation; it integrates multiple public epigenomic and transcriptomic modalities; it could reveal mechanisms of tissue-specific and species-specific regulatory innovation; it will generate a useful repeat-aware regulatory resource; and it fits the need for a multi-lab working group."",
      ""weakness"": ""Regulatory function criteria are not defined clearly enough; peak calling and chromatin-state annotation in repetitive regions require careful controls not described in detail; the proposal does not specify how it will account for mappability, input controls, copy number, or cell-type heterogeneity; and there is a risk of overinterpreting correlative overlap between TEs and regulatory marks."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and relevant proposal with excellent potential to reshape regulatory annotation, provided the group strengthens controls for repeat-associated artifacts and prioritizes interpretable biological analyses."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal convincingly frames TE-derived regulatory elements as molecular changes that can reshape gene regulatory networks. The emergence concept is strong, though direct links to phenotypic diversification are less developed."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A systematic repeat-aware reanalysis of regulatory genomics datasets could be a major contribution. The focus on TE-derived enhancers, TF binding, and cell-specific regulatory programs is highly significant."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall plan is plausible, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on controls, normalization, chromatin-state modeling choices, mappability correction, and criteria for regulatory function."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project is ambitious but potentially feasible if staged. As written, the breadth of assays, species, and biological questions is too large for the two-year timeline without prioritization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort based on existing public functional genomics data and integrative analysis."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies appropriate resources such as ENCODE, Roadmap, GTEx, FAANG, and comparative vertebrate datasets. It should provide more precise dataset selection criteria and metadata harmonization plans."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to public data resources and reproducible pipelines is appropriate. The proposal would benefit from more detailed plans for community access, visualization, versioning, and long-term sustainability.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior comparative vertebrate genomics and evolutionary developmental biology researcher. The proposal is ambitious and potentially important because it seeks to connect TE insertions to vertebrate diversification through regulatory and coding innovation. The emphasis on complete assemblies and pangenomes is timely, and the focus on public resources such as FAANG and comparative vertebrate datasets is appropriate for the call. A comparative framework is essential for determining whether TE-derived elements are lineage-specific, conserved, convergent, or associated with shifts in cell-type regulatory programs. However, the comparative component is underdeveloped. The proposal says it will analyze vertebrates but provides concrete alignment plans mainly for human and mouse. It does not explain how it will handle differences in annotation quality among species, incomplete or uneven assay coverage, lack of matched tissues across species, TE family turnover, or ambiguous orthology of repetitive elements. The goal of explaining phenotypic diversification is much broader than the proposed analyses can support unless specific phenotypes, lineages, and regulatory systems are chosen. The project could be excellent if it focused on a defined set of comparative contrasts, such as mammalian placenta, immune cells, brain development, limb development, or other cell types with rich public data and known TE contributions. Without this narrowing, the proposal risks conflating human-centric annotation expansion with vertebrate evolutionary synthesis. I view this as a strong concept with a weaker comparative implementation plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a major question in genome evolution; it uses existing public comparative datasets; it leverages new genome assemblies; it integrates regulatory, transcriptomic, and translational data; and it has the potential to reveal lineage-specific mechanisms of regulatory innovation."",
      ""weakness"": ""The vertebrate comparative scope is not well defined; the proposal is overly human and mouse centered relative to its evolutionary claims; cross-species tissue matching and orthology methods are not described; TE family evolution and annotation variability are not adequately addressed; and links to phenotypic diversification are asserted more than operationalized."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising proposal with strong evolutionary motivation, but the comparative vertebrate strategy needs clearer species selection, orthology framework, and biologically focused contrasts."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project addresses emergence from genome sequence changes to regulatory system evolution. The emergence framing is appropriate, but phenotypic emergence across vertebrates is not yet supported by a concrete analytical plan."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The question of TE-driven vertebrate innovation is significant, and large-scale repeat-aware reanalysis could provide new insight. The novelty would be greater with a more explicit comparative evolutionary design."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The general methods are reasonable, but the proposal lacks detail on cross-species alignment, TE orthology, tissue equivalence, annotation biases, and phylogenetic statistical models."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed vertebrate-wide scope, combined with tens of thousands of datasets and multiple data modalities, is too broad for two years without a prioritized comparative design."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project fits the synthesis mandate well, relying on public data and integrating diverse resources rather than generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Several relevant sources are named, but the proposal does not provide a species list, tissue list, comparative dataset inventory, or criteria for selecting vertebrate datasets with adequate quality and comparability."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that outputs, data, and workflows will be public. More specifics on comparative annotation standards, reusable genome-coordinate mappings, and user-facing access would strengthen this aspect.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior data-infrastructure, open-science, and team-science leader in molecular biosciences. This proposal is highly aligned with the NCEMS model of community-scale synthesis. It uses existing public data, requires expertise across multiple laboratories, and proposes to create a reusable public resource that could serve many communities beyond the immediate team. The scale of reprocessing, harmonizing, storing, and releasing repeat-aware functional genomics datasets clearly justifies support beyond a typical single-lab project. The proposal also identifies the need for staff scientist support, compute, storage, meetings, and personnel exchanges, which are appropriate NCEMS resources. However, the team-science and open-science implementation is not yet fully articulated. The proposal says the resource and pipelines will be public but does not discuss governance, authorship policies, community input, documentation standards, training modules, trainee roles, hackathons, release schedules, user support, or long-term maintenance after the two-year project. The call explicitly emphasizes tapping diverse talent and training the next data-savvy workforce; the proposal mentions personnel exchanges but gives little detail on graduate student or postdoctoral training. From a resource-building perspective, the plan also needs a clearer data management architecture: identifiers, metadata schema, workflow provenance, versioning, reproducibility audits, and deposition locations. I am enthusiastic about the scientific opportunity, but the proposal should more explicitly operate as a community working group rather than only a large computational production effort."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent fit to the public-data synthesis mission; strong justification for shared infrastructure and staff support; high potential to produce a valuable public resource; multidisciplinary expertise is clearly needed; and the project could train researchers in repeat-aware genomics and reproducible data integration."",
      ""weakness"": ""Open-science implementation is underspecified; trainee training plans are minimal; team governance and community engagement are not described; data management, metadata, provenance, and versioning plans are incomplete; and sustainability beyond the funding period is unclear."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong synthesis-resource proposal with major potential, but it needs a more concrete plan for open science, training, community engagement, and sustainable data infrastructure."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal makes a plausible case that TE insertions give rise to emergent regulatory networks and species-specific biological functions. The framing fits the call, although implementation focuses more on resource generation than explicit emergence modeling."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The resource would be significant and likely widely used. The novelty is strongest in the scale and harmonized repeat-aware reprocessing of existing datasets."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The scientific approach is broadly sound, but the proposal lacks detailed quality assurance, workflow provenance, reproducibility checks, and data release procedures needed for a large community resource."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project is large but may be feasible if NCEMS provides substantial staff and compute support. The timeline would be more convincing with staged releases, pilot datasets, and contingency plans."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal strongly satisfies the synthesis requirement by reusing and integrating public data across consortia, modalities, assemblies, and species."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names major public sources and assay types, but it does not yet provide a formal data inventory, metadata harmonization plan, or release matrix."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""There is a general commitment to public data and reproducible workflows, but the proposal lacks specifics on repositories, licenses, documentation, governance, training materials, community standards, and long-term maintenance.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::10,y1,10,Transposable elements and the emergence of genomic innovation,Accepted,Shaun Mahony,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer perspective: senior computational genomics and statistical bioinformatics researcher. The proposal identifies an important computational bottleneck in functional genomics: the loss of signal in repetitive regions caused by conventional handling of multi-mapped reads. Reprocessing public datasets with repeat-aware pipelines could be highly valuable, particularly if performed reproducibly and released in a standardized format. The proposal appropriately mentions probabilistic allocation methods such as Allo and Rcount, use of telomere-to-telomere and pangenome assemblies, peak calling, chromatin-state modeling, statistical association, and tensor decomposition. These are reasonable components of a modern synthesis pipeline. That said, the computational plan is currently more aspirational than operational. There is little discussion of benchmarking, scalability, error propagation, batch effects across consortia, assay-specific quality control, handling of variable read lengths and sequencing depths, genome-build harmonization, pangenome coordinate systems, or uncertainty estimates from multi-read allocation. These omissions matter because the central claim depends on accurately assigning reads in highly repetitive sequence contexts. The project also proposes 10 million CPU hours for more than 75,000 datasets, but no evidence is given that this is sufficient or that storage, I/O, metadata harmonization, and workflow orchestration have been fully costed. The proposal is a strong fit to NCEMS because it requires collaborative infrastructure and public-data synthesis, but it should be refined into a more rigorous computational production plan with milestones, pilot benchmarks, and clear quality metrics.",The proposal targets a well-known computational blind spot; it would create a reusable community resource; it uses public data only; it integrates multiple assay types; it would benefit substantially from shared computational infrastructure; and the proposed collaboration is beyond the scale of a single laboratory.,The computational workflow is insufficiently specified; benchmarking and validation are not described in detail; pangenome coordinate handling is not adequately addressed; uncertainty from probabilistic read assignment is not propagated into downstream analyses; resource estimates are not well justified; and the plan lacks clear go/no-go milestones or prioritization if full-scale processing proves infeasible.,3,"A potentially high-impact computational synthesis project, but the rigor and feasibility of the large-scale reprocessing plan need substantially more detail before confidence in execution is high.",4,"The emergence framing is credible, connecting repeat-level sequence variation to regulatory network-level phenomena. However, the computational methods for inferring those higher-order relationships are not yet sufficiently developed.",4,The proposal is significant because it addresses a systematic bias in public genomics resources. The novelty lies primarily in scale and integration rather than in creating fundamentally new algorithms.,2,"The approach lists appropriate tools and analyses but lacks operational details on benchmarking, quality control, reproducibility, statistical uncertainty, cross-assay harmonization, and validation of multi-mapping read allocation.",2,"The proposed processing of over 75,000 datasets in two years is extremely ambitious. The timeline does not adequately account for metadata curation, failed runs, workflow debugging, storage constraints, and downstream integration.",5,This is a strong synthesis project that relies entirely on existing public data and aims to integrate datasets across modalities and species.,4,"The proposal identifies relevant public resources and assay types, but lacks a concrete dataset manifest, inclusion/exclusion criteria, and prioritization strategy.",3,"The proposal promises public resources and reproducible workflows, but does not specify workflow languages, containerization strategy, cloud/HPC portability, metadata schemas, licenses, or maintenance plans.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:17:25.680829,0,success,,"The proposal addresses a critical bottleneck in functional genomics by suggesting the reprocessing of public datasets to recover signals in repetitive regions using repeat-aware pipelines. While the integration of probabilistic allocation methods and pangenome assemblies represents a strong conceptual approach, the computational plan currently lacks necessary operational detail. Crucial aspects such as benchmarking, error propagation, batch effect mitigation, and precise resource justifications are notably absent. Despite these omissions, the project aligns well with the program's focus on collaborative infrastructure, provided a more rigorous production plan is established.","The project targets a significant computational limitation in the field and leverages public data across multiple assay types to create a valuable, reusable community resource. Furthermore, its large scale and requirement for shared computational infrastructure make it an excellent fit for collaborative initiatives.","The proposed computational workflow lacks essential operational details, including rigorous benchmarking, validation, and the propagation of uncertainty from probabilistic read assignments. Additionally, the computational resource estimates are inadequately justified, and the plan is missing clear milestones or fallback strategies."
baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::003,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,3,baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:42:30.117632,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior transposable element evolutionary biologist. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: how transposable elements contribute to vertebrate genomic innovation by generating regulatory and coding novelty. The proposal is well aligned with the call because it is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public data and because the repetitive fraction of genomes remains underanalyzed in many major resources. The biological motivation is compelling, and the investigators correctly identify that standard short-read pipelines have systematically discarded or underused multi-mapping reads, biasing interpretation away from TE-rich regions. The planned integration of ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, RNA-seq, Ribo-seq, pangenome assemblies, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, and comparative annotations could generate a valuable community resource. However, the proposal is very broad. It aims to address transcription factor binding, enhancers, transcripts, translated sequences, cell specificity, species specificity, conservation, and phenotypic diversification across vertebrates in only two years. From a TE biology perspective, the proposal would be stronger if it more clearly defined the taxonomic scope, which TE classes and families are priorities, how orthology and lineage specificity will be established for repetitive elements, and how it will distinguish TE exaptation from passive overlap with regulatory marks. The planned intersections with RepeatMasker annotations are necessary but not sufficient for robust evolutionary inference, especially in non-model vertebrates and in nested or fragmented repeats. Overall, this is a high-impact synthesis concept with clear relevance to emergence, but it needs sharper biological hypotheses and more explicit validation strategies for TE-derived function."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal tackles a central problem in genome evolution and regulatory innovation; it uses only existing public datasets; it leverages new complete and pangenome assemblies; it addresses a known bias in conventional genomic analysis; it has strong potential to produce a broadly useful repeat-aware resource; and it requires cross-disciplinary expertise across TE biology, regulatory genomics, comparative genomics, and computational analysis."",
      ""weakness"": ""The biological scope is overly expansive; the proposal does not sufficiently specify which vertebrate species, TE families, or evolutionary time scales will be analyzed; methods for distinguishing functional exaptation from coincidental overlap are underdeveloped; comparative inference in repetitive regions is technically difficult and not described in adequate detail; and the two-year timeline appears optimistic for reprocessing and synthesizing more than 75,000 datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling and highly relevant proposal that could transform analysis of TE-associated function, but it would benefit from narrower biological aims and more rigorous evolutionary criteria for functional innovation."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal clearly links molecular events, TE insertions, to higher-order outcomes such as regulatory networks, tissue-specific expression, and species diversification. This is an excellent example of emergence across biological scales."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Repeat-aware reanalysis of large public functional genomics resources using complete and pangenome assemblies is significant and timely. The individual concepts are not entirely new, but the proposed scale and synthesis across modalities could be highly novel."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad computational strategy is appropriate, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on evolutionary inference, orthology of TE-derived loci, false discovery control, benchmarking of multi-mapping allocation, and criteria for defining TE-derived function."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Reprocessing tens of thousands of datasets and conducting integrative cross-species analyses within two years is likely unrealistic without a more staged plan and prioritization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely focused on reanalysis and synthesis of existing public data and explicitly does not propose new data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Major data sources are identified, including ENCODE, Roadmap Epigenomics, FAANG, GTEx, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, pangenomes, and multi-species alignments. However, the exact dataset inclusion criteria and species list are not sufficiently defined."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal commits to public datasets, public data resources, and reproducible pipelines. More detail is needed on repositories, metadata standards, versioning, long-term maintenance, and community governance.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior computational genomics and statistical bioinformatics researcher. The proposal identifies an important computational bottleneck in functional genomics: the loss of signal in repetitive regions caused by conventional handling of multi-mapped reads. Reprocessing public datasets with repeat-aware pipelines could be highly valuable, particularly if performed reproducibly and released in a standardized format. The proposal appropriately mentions probabilistic allocation methods such as Allo and Rcount, use of telomere-to-telomere and pangenome assemblies, peak calling, chromatin-state modeling, statistical association, and tensor decomposition. These are reasonable components of a modern synthesis pipeline. That said, the computational plan is currently more aspirational than operational. There is little discussion of benchmarking, scalability, error propagation, batch effects across consortia, assay-specific quality control, handling of variable read lengths and sequencing depths, genome-build harmonization, pangenome coordinate systems, or uncertainty estimates from multi-read allocation. These omissions matter because the central claim depends on accurately assigning reads in highly repetitive sequence contexts. The project also proposes 10 million CPU hours for more than 75,000 datasets, but no evidence is given that this is sufficient or that storage, I/O, metadata harmonization, and workflow orchestration have been fully costed. The proposal is a strong fit to NCEMS because it requires collaborative infrastructure and public-data synthesis, but it should be refined into a more rigorous computational production plan with milestones, pilot benchmarks, and clear quality metrics."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal targets a well-known computational blind spot; it would create a reusable community resource; it uses public data only; it integrates multiple assay types; it would benefit substantially from shared computational infrastructure; and the proposed collaboration is beyond the scale of a single laboratory."",
      ""weakness"": ""The computational workflow is insufficiently specified; benchmarking and validation are not described in detail; pangenome coordinate handling is not adequately addressed; uncertainty from probabilistic read assignment is not propagated into downstream analyses; resource estimates are not well justified; and the plan lacks clear go/no-go milestones or prioritization if full-scale processing proves infeasible."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A potentially high-impact computational synthesis project, but the rigor and feasibility of the large-scale reprocessing plan need substantially more detail before confidence in execution is high."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The emergence framing is credible, connecting repeat-level sequence variation to regulatory network-level phenomena. However, the computational methods for inferring those higher-order relationships are not yet sufficiently developed."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The proposal is significant because it addresses a systematic bias in public genomics resources. The novelty lies primarily in scale and integration rather than in creating fundamentally new algorithms."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The approach lists appropriate tools and analyses but lacks operational details on benchmarking, quality control, reproducibility, statistical uncertainty, cross-assay harmonization, and validation of multi-mapping read allocation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed processing of over 75,000 datasets in two years is extremely ambitious. The timeline does not adequately account for metadata curation, failed runs, workflow debugging, storage constraints, and downstream integration."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is a strong synthesis project that relies entirely on existing public data and aims to integrate datasets across modalities and species."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies relevant public resources and assay types, but lacks a concrete dataset manifest, inclusion/exclusion criteria, and prioritization strategy."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal promises public resources and reproducible workflows, but does not specify workflow languages, containerization strategy, cloud/HPC portability, metadata schemas, licenses, or maintenance plans.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior regulatory genomics and epigenomics scientist. This proposal addresses a major gap in regulatory genomics: many functional genomic annotations have been constructed using approaches that undercount repetitive and TE-derived regions. The proposal has particular strength in its plan to synthesize ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, histone modification data, RNA-seq, and Ribo-seq to identify TE-derived promoters, enhancers, transcription factor binding sites, transcripts, and translated elements. The biological questions are compelling, especially the proposed analysis of which transcription factors preferentially bind specific TE families and how TE-derived enhancers contribute to tissue- or cell-type-specific gene regulation. This is a strong match to the call's emphasis on community-scale synthesis. However, the regulatory interpretation needs more rigor. Peak calling in repeats is challenging, and probabilistic read allocation can increase apparent signal in repeats in ways that are difficult to distinguish from mapping artifacts. The proposal does not discuss how it will handle assay-specific confounders, input controls, mappability, copy-number variation, pangenome structural variation, or cell-type heterogeneity. The use of hidden Markov models for chromatin states is reasonable, but chromatin state models are sensitive to assay availability and normalization. The proposal would be improved by defining a smaller number of benchmark cell types, transcription factors, and TE families for initial analysis and by specifying orthogonal validation using already-public long-read, CAGE, eQTL, MPRA, CRISPR screen, or proteomics datasets where available. As written, the proposal is exciting but risks producing a very large catalog with uneven interpretability."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal focuses on an important missing component of regulatory annotation; it integrates multiple public epigenomic and transcriptomic modalities; it could reveal mechanisms of tissue-specific and species-specific regulatory innovation; it will generate a useful repeat-aware regulatory resource; and it fits the need for a multi-lab working group."",
      ""weakness"": ""Regulatory function criteria are not defined clearly enough; peak calling and chromatin-state annotation in repetitive regions require careful controls not described in detail; the proposal does not specify how it will account for mappability, input controls, copy number, or cell-type heterogeneity; and there is a risk of overinterpreting correlative overlap between TEs and regulatory marks."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and relevant proposal with excellent potential to reshape regulatory annotation, provided the group strengthens controls for repeat-associated artifacts and prioritizes interpretable biological analyses."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal convincingly frames TE-derived regulatory elements as molecular changes that can reshape gene regulatory networks. The emergence concept is strong, though direct links to phenotypic diversification are less developed."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A systematic repeat-aware reanalysis of regulatory genomics datasets could be a major contribution. The focus on TE-derived enhancers, TF binding, and cell-specific regulatory programs is highly significant."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall plan is plausible, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on controls, normalization, chromatin-state modeling choices, mappability correction, and criteria for regulatory function."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project is ambitious but potentially feasible if staged. As written, the breadth of assays, species, and biological questions is too large for the two-year timeline without prioritization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort based on existing public functional genomics data and integrative analysis."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies appropriate resources such as ENCODE, Roadmap, GTEx, FAANG, and comparative vertebrate datasets. It should provide more precise dataset selection criteria and metadata harmonization plans."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to public data resources and reproducible pipelines is appropriate. The proposal would benefit from more detailed plans for community access, visualization, versioning, and long-term sustainability.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior comparative vertebrate genomics and evolutionary developmental biology researcher. The proposal is ambitious and potentially important because it seeks to connect TE insertions to vertebrate diversification through regulatory and coding innovation. The emphasis on complete assemblies and pangenomes is timely, and the focus on public resources such as FAANG and comparative vertebrate datasets is appropriate for the call. A comparative framework is essential for determining whether TE-derived elements are lineage-specific, conserved, convergent, or associated with shifts in cell-type regulatory programs. However, the comparative component is underdeveloped. The proposal says it will analyze vertebrates but provides concrete alignment plans mainly for human and mouse. It does not explain how it will handle differences in annotation quality among species, incomplete or uneven assay coverage, lack of matched tissues across species, TE family turnover, or ambiguous orthology of repetitive elements. The goal of explaining phenotypic diversification is much broader than the proposed analyses can support unless specific phenotypes, lineages, and regulatory systems are chosen. The project could be excellent if it focused on a defined set of comparative contrasts, such as mammalian placenta, immune cells, brain development, limb development, or other cell types with rich public data and known TE contributions. Without this narrowing, the proposal risks conflating human-centric annotation expansion with vertebrate evolutionary synthesis. I view this as a strong concept with a weaker comparative implementation plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a major question in genome evolution; it uses existing public comparative datasets; it leverages new genome assemblies; it integrates regulatory, transcriptomic, and translational data; and it has the potential to reveal lineage-specific mechanisms of regulatory innovation."",
      ""weakness"": ""The vertebrate comparative scope is not well defined; the proposal is overly human and mouse centered relative to its evolutionary claims; cross-species tissue matching and orthology methods are not described; TE family evolution and annotation variability are not adequately addressed; and links to phenotypic diversification are asserted more than operationalized."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising proposal with strong evolutionary motivation, but the comparative vertebrate strategy needs clearer species selection, orthology framework, and biologically focused contrasts."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project addresses emergence from genome sequence changes to regulatory system evolution. The emergence framing is appropriate, but phenotypic emergence across vertebrates is not yet supported by a concrete analytical plan."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The question of TE-driven vertebrate innovation is significant, and large-scale repeat-aware reanalysis could provide new insight. The novelty would be greater with a more explicit comparative evolutionary design."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The general methods are reasonable, but the proposal lacks detail on cross-species alignment, TE orthology, tissue equivalence, annotation biases, and phylogenetic statistical models."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed vertebrate-wide scope, combined with tens of thousands of datasets and multiple data modalities, is too broad for two years without a prioritized comparative design."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project fits the synthesis mandate well, relying on public data and integrating diverse resources rather than generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Several relevant sources are named, but the proposal does not provide a species list, tissue list, comparative dataset inventory, or criteria for selecting vertebrate datasets with adequate quality and comparability."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that outputs, data, and workflows will be public. More specifics on comparative annotation standards, reusable genome-coordinate mappings, and user-facing access would strengthen this aspect.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior data-infrastructure, open-science, and team-science leader in molecular biosciences. This proposal is highly aligned with the NCEMS model of community-scale synthesis. It uses existing public data, requires expertise across multiple laboratories, and proposes to create a reusable public resource that could serve many communities beyond the immediate team. The scale of reprocessing, harmonizing, storing, and releasing repeat-aware functional genomics datasets clearly justifies support beyond a typical single-lab project. The proposal also identifies the need for staff scientist support, compute, storage, meetings, and personnel exchanges, which are appropriate NCEMS resources. However, the team-science and open-science implementation is not yet fully articulated. The proposal says the resource and pipelines will be public but does not discuss governance, authorship policies, community input, documentation standards, training modules, trainee roles, hackathons, release schedules, user support, or long-term maintenance after the two-year project. The call explicitly emphasizes tapping diverse talent and training the next data-savvy workforce; the proposal mentions personnel exchanges but gives little detail on graduate student or postdoctoral training. From a resource-building perspective, the plan also needs a clearer data management architecture: identifiers, metadata schema, workflow provenance, versioning, reproducibility audits, and deposition locations. I am enthusiastic about the scientific opportunity, but the proposal should more explicitly operate as a community working group rather than only a large computational production effort."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent fit to the public-data synthesis mission; strong justification for shared infrastructure and staff support; high potential to produce a valuable public resource; multidisciplinary expertise is clearly needed; and the project could train researchers in repeat-aware genomics and reproducible data integration."",
      ""weakness"": ""Open-science implementation is underspecified; trainee training plans are minimal; team governance and community engagement are not described; data management, metadata, provenance, and versioning plans are incomplete; and sustainability beyond the funding period is unclear."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong synthesis-resource proposal with major potential, but it needs a more concrete plan for open science, training, community engagement, and sustainable data infrastructure."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal makes a plausible case that TE insertions give rise to emergent regulatory networks and species-specific biological functions. The framing fits the call, although implementation focuses more on resource generation than explicit emergence modeling."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The resource would be significant and likely widely used. The novelty is strongest in the scale and harmonized repeat-aware reprocessing of existing datasets."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The scientific approach is broadly sound, but the proposal lacks detailed quality assurance, workflow provenance, reproducibility checks, and data release procedures needed for a large community resource."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project is large but may be feasible if NCEMS provides substantial staff and compute support. The timeline would be more convincing with staged releases, pilot datasets, and contingency plans."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal strongly satisfies the synthesis requirement by reusing and integrating public data across consortia, modalities, assemblies, and species."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal names major public sources and assay types, but it does not yet provide a formal data inventory, metadata harmonization plan, or release matrix."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""There is a general commitment to public data and reproducible workflows, but the proposal lacks specifics on repositories, licenses, documentation, governance, training materials, community standards, and long-term maintenance.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::10,y1,10,Transposable elements and the emergence of genomic innovation,Accepted,Shaun Mahony,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer perspective: senior regulatory genomics and epigenomics scientist. This proposal addresses a major gap in regulatory genomics: many functional genomic annotations have been constructed using approaches that undercount repetitive and TE-derived regions. The proposal has particular strength in its plan to synthesize ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, histone modification data, RNA-seq, and Ribo-seq to identify TE-derived promoters, enhancers, transcription factor binding sites, transcripts, and translated elements. The biological questions are compelling, especially the proposed analysis of which transcription factors preferentially bind specific TE families and how TE-derived enhancers contribute to tissue- or cell-type-specific gene regulation. This is a strong match to the call's emphasis on community-scale synthesis. However, the regulatory interpretation needs more rigor. Peak calling in repeats is challenging, and probabilistic read allocation can increase apparent signal in repeats in ways that are difficult to distinguish from mapping artifacts. The proposal does not discuss how it will handle assay-specific confounders, input controls, mappability, copy-number variation, pangenome structural variation, or cell-type heterogeneity. The use of hidden Markov models for chromatin states is reasonable, but chromatin state models are sensitive to assay availability and normalization. The proposal would be improved by defining a smaller number of benchmark cell types, transcription factors, and TE families for initial analysis and by specifying orthogonal validation using already-public long-read, CAGE, eQTL, MPRA, CRISPR screen, or proteomics datasets where available. As written, the proposal is exciting but risks producing a very large catalog with uneven interpretability.",The proposal focuses on an important missing component of regulatory annotation; it integrates multiple public epigenomic and transcriptomic modalities; it could reveal mechanisms of tissue-specific and species-specific regulatory innovation; it will generate a useful repeat-aware regulatory resource; and it fits the need for a multi-lab working group.,"Regulatory function criteria are not defined clearly enough; peak calling and chromatin-state annotation in repetitive regions require careful controls not described in detail; the proposal does not specify how it will account for mappability, input controls, copy number, or cell-type heterogeneity; and there is a risk of overinterpreting correlative overlap between TEs and regulatory marks.",4,"A strong and relevant proposal with excellent potential to reshape regulatory annotation, provided the group strengthens controls for repeat-associated artifacts and prioritizes interpretable biological analyses.",4,"The proposal convincingly frames TE-derived regulatory elements as molecular changes that can reshape gene regulatory networks. The emergence concept is strong, though direct links to phenotypic diversification are less developed.",5,"A systematic repeat-aware reanalysis of regulatory genomics datasets could be a major contribution. The focus on TE-derived enhancers, TF binding, and cell-specific regulatory programs is highly significant.",3,"The overall plan is plausible, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on controls, normalization, chromatin-state modeling choices, mappability correction, and criteria for regulatory function.",3,"The project is ambitious but potentially feasible if staged. As written, the breadth of assays, species, and biological questions is too large for the two-year timeline without prioritization.",5,The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort based on existing public functional genomics data and integrative analysis.,4,"The proposal identifies appropriate resources such as ENCODE, Roadmap, GTEx, FAANG, and comparative vertebrate datasets. It should provide more precise dataset selection criteria and metadata harmonization plans.",4,"The commitment to public data resources and reproducible pipelines is appropriate. The proposal would benefit from more detailed plans for community access, visualization, versioning, and long-term sustainability.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:17:35.643513,0,success,,"This proposal aims to address a significant gap in regulatory genomics by integrating multiple functional genomic and transcriptomic datasets to annotate transposable element (TE)-derived regions. While the biological questions regarding TE-derived regulatory elements are compelling and align well with the funding call, the proposed methodology lacks sufficient rigor in handling assay-specific confounders and the inherent challenges of mapping repetitive regions. Implementing a more focused benchmark analysis and incorporating orthogonal validation would substantially improve the reliability and interpretability of the resulting regulatory catalog.","The proposal addresses a critical gap in current regulatory annotations by comprehensively integrating diverse epigenomic and transcriptomic data modalities. It has strong potential to elucidate mechanisms of tissue-specific regulatory innovation and will generate a highly valuable, repeat-aware resource for the scientific community.","The methodology lacks sufficient detail on defining regulatory function criteria and controlling for confounders such as mappability, copy number variation, and cell-type heterogeneity. Additionally, peak calling and chromatin-state annotation in repetitive regions present significant mapping challenges that require careful controls and orthogonal validation to prevent the overinterpretation of correlative overlaps."
baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::004,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,4,baseline::y1::10::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:42:30.117632,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior transposable element evolutionary biologist. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: how transposable elements contribute to vertebrate genomic innovation by generating regulatory and coding novelty. The proposal is well aligned with the call because it is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public data and because the repetitive fraction of genomes remains underanalyzed in many major resources. The biological motivation is compelling, and the investigators correctly identify that standard short-read pipelines have systematically discarded or underused multi-mapping reads, biasing interpretation away from TE-rich regions. The planned integration of ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq, RNA-seq, Ribo-seq, pangenome assemblies, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, and comparative annotations could generate a valuable community resource. However, the proposal is very broad. It aims to address transcription factor binding, enhancers, transcripts, translated sequences, cell specificity, species specificity, conservation, and phenotypic diversification across vertebrates in only two years. From a TE biology perspective, the proposal would be stronger if it more clearly defined the taxonomic scope, which TE classes and families are priorities, how orthology and lineage specificity will be established for repetitive elements, and how it will distinguish TE exaptation from passive overlap with regulatory marks. The planned intersections with RepeatMasker annotations are necessary but not sufficient for robust evolutionary inference, especially in non-model vertebrates and in nested or fragmented repeats. Overall, this is a high-impact synthesis concept with clear relevance to emergence, but it needs sharper biological hypotheses and more explicit validation strategies for TE-derived function."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal tackles a central problem in genome evolution and regulatory innovation; it uses only existing public datasets; it leverages new complete and pangenome assemblies; it addresses a known bias in conventional genomic analysis; it has strong potential to produce a broadly useful repeat-aware resource; and it requires cross-disciplinary expertise across TE biology, regulatory genomics, comparative genomics, and computational analysis."",
      ""weakness"": ""The biological scope is overly expansive; the proposal does not sufficiently specify which vertebrate species, TE families, or evolutionary time scales will be analyzed; methods for distinguishing functional exaptation from coincidental overlap are underdeveloped; comparative inference in repetitive regions is technically difficult and not described in adequate detail; and the two-year timeline appears optimistic for reprocessing and synthesizing more than 75,000 datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling and highly relevant proposal that could transform analysis of TE-associated function, but it would benefit from narrower biological aims and more rigorous evolutionary criteria for functional innovation."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal clearly links molecular events, TE insertions, to higher-order outcomes such as regulatory networks, tissue-specific expression, and species diversification. This is an excellent example of emergence across biological scales."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Repeat-aware reanalysis of large public functional genomics resources using complete and pangenome assemblies is significant and timely. The individual concepts are not entirely new, but the proposed scale and synthesis across modalities could be highly novel."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad computational strategy is appropriate, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on evolutionary inference, orthology of TE-derived loci, false discovery control, benchmarking of multi-mapping allocation, and criteria for defining TE-derived function."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Reprocessing tens of thousands of datasets and conducting integrative cross-species analyses within two years is likely unrealistic without a more staged plan and prioritization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely focused on reanalysis and synthesis of existing public data and explicitly does not propose new data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Major data sources are identified, including ENCODE, Roadmap Epigenomics, FAANG, GTEx, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, pangenomes, and multi-species alignments. However, the exact dataset inclusion criteria and species list are not sufficiently defined."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal commits to public datasets, public data resources, and reproducible pipelines. More detail is needed on repositories, metadata standards, versioning, long-term maintenance, and community governance.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer perspective: senior computational genomics and statistical bioinformatics researcher. The proposal identifies an important computational bottleneck in functional genomics: the loss of signal in repetitive regions caused by conventional handling of multi-mapped reads. Reprocessing public datasets with repeat-aware pipelines could be highly valuable, particularly if performed reproducibly and released in a standardized format. The proposal appropriately mentions probabilistic allocation methods such as Allo and Rcount, use of telomere-to-telomere and pangenome assemblies, peak calling, chromatin-state modeling, statistical association, and tensor decomposition. These are reasonable components of a modern synthesis pipeline. That said, the computational plan is currently more aspirational than operational. There is little discussion of benchmarking, scalability, error propagation, batch effects across consortia, assay-specific quality control, handling of variable read lengths and sequencing depths, genome-build harmonization, pangenome coordinate systems, or uncertainty estimates from multi-read allocation. These omissions matter because the central claim depends on accurately assigning reads in highly repetitive sequence contexts. The project also proposes 10 million CPU hours for more than 75,000 datasets, but no evidence is given that this is sufficient or that storage, I/O, metadata harmonization, and workflow orchestration have been fully costed. The proposal is a strong fit to NCEMS because it requires collaborative infrastructure and public-data synthesis, but it should be refined into a more rigorous computational production plan with milestones, pilot benchmarks, and clear quality metrics."",
