      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and highly relevant synthesis proposal that could substantially improve the usability of public XL-MS data, though it needs a more rigorous and realistic implementation plan for heterogeneous legacy datasets."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein complexes and interaction networks are central examples of emergent molecular organization, and XL-MS offers residue-level evidence for how proteins assemble into higher-order systems. The proposal could illuminate emergent organization of interactomes, although the framing is more resource- and interaction-discovery oriented than explicitly focused on emergence principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The individual components are not entirely new, but the large-scale synthesis of public XL-MS data, reprocessing with modern methods, and integration into complex databases is novel and significant for structural proteomics."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad workflow is sound, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on quality control, search parameter harmonization, FDR estimation across datasets, treatment of ambiguous crosslinks, and validation thresholds for database deposition."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The fifteen-month timeline is plausible for a prioritized pilot but optimistic for reprocessing and manually curating approximately one hundred heterogeneous large-scale datasets plus structural modeling and database integration."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort using existing public XL-MS data and integrating it with public complex and structural prediction resources. It does not propose new experimental data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""PRIDE is clearly identified as the primary source, and the proposal recognizes that many nominal XL-MS datasets will require filtering. However, the exact dataset list, inclusion criteria, and metadata requirements need further specification."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to redeposit standardized datasets to PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is a strong open-science commitment. More explicit statements about public workflows, code, containers, and versioning would strengthen this aspect.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational structural biologist specializing in integrative modeling and protein complex prediction"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal is attractive because XL-MS data provide precisely the type of spatial restraint that can bridge interactomics and structural biology. The ambition to use public XL-MS data to validate predicted complexes and improve evidence levels in the Complex Portal is well aligned with the needs of the field. However, I am less convinced by the proposed structural validation strategy as written. The proposal appears to assume that crosslinks can be compared relatively directly to AlphaFold-generated structures, including AlphaFold3 predictions, to determine whether a PPI is supported. This can work in favorable cases, but in practice crosslink interpretation requires careful consideration of linker length distributions, side-chain flexibility, peptide-level ambiguity, protein isoforms, post-translational states, oligomeric stoichiometry, conformational ensembles, membrane environments, and indirect co-complex proximity. AlphaFold models are also not experimental truth; docking confidence, interface confidence, and multimer prediction reliability vary widely. A crosslink that violates a model may indicate a wrong model, a different conformational state, an alternative assembly, or a false positive. Conversely, a model satisfying a crosslink does not by itself establish a physiological complex. The proposal would be stronger if it framed AlphaFold as one component in an integrative scoring system rather than as a main validation criterion. Despite this concern, the synthesis objective is valuable, and the team includes relevant expertise and infrastructure. I would support the project if the milestones emphasize creation of a rigorously annotated XL-MS restraint resource and only secondarily the inference of new complexes."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal exploits the unique value of XL-MS as residue-level spatial evidence; integration with predicted structures and complex databases is potentially powerful; the project could generate restraints useful for integrative modeling across many systems; it uses existing data and requires a transdisciplinary team; the focus on improving evidence for human and yeast complexes is scientifically meaningful."",
      ""weakness"": ""The structural validation strategy is underdeveloped; AlphaFold-based confirmation may be overinterpreted; there is little discussion of conformational heterogeneity, stoichiometry, isoforms, ambiguous peptide mapping, or crosslink distance distributions; criteria for accepting or rejecting interactions are not described quantitatively; computational cost estimates and prioritization of AlphaFold runs are not sufficiently justified."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A worthwhile synthesis project with high potential resource value, but the proposed use of structural prediction to validate PPIs needs a more nuanced and rigorous integrative modeling framework."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The assembly of proteins into complexes and networks is directly relevant to emergent molecular function. The proposal could reveal higher-order organization from residue-level interaction data, although it does not deeply articulate mechanistic emergence questions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining public XL-MS datasets with structure prediction and curated complex resources is significant and relatively novel as a community-scale synthesis activity. The potential impact is high if validation is done carefully."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational structural biology component lacks necessary detail. The proposal does not adequately define model confidence thresholds, distance restraint interpretation, treatment of flexible/disordered regions, or how conflicting evidence will be represented."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed timeline may be feasible for resource assembly but is tight for large-scale reprocessing, structural modeling, validation, curation, and biological interpretation. The optional uncharacterized-protein aim should be deprioritized."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely centered on reusing and integrating existing public data, including PRIDE datasets, huMAP-like predictions, AlphaFold models or predictions, and Complex Portal records."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE as the data source and mentions selection criteria such as proteome-wide design and cleavable crosslinkers, but it lacks a concrete inventory of datasets, expected organisms, metadata fields, and exclusion rules."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Deposition into PRIDE and the Complex Portal is strong. The proposal would benefit from clearer plans for releasing modeling scripts, scoring criteria, failed or ambiguous cases, and provenance metadata.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior bioinformatics infrastructure and biological database scientist"",
      ""review_text"": ""From a data-infrastructure perspective, this proposal is highly responsive to the call. The central problem is not a lack of XL-MS data but lack of harmonization, metadata completeness, standardized processed outputs, and integration with authoritative resources. The proposed collaboration with EBI, PRIDE, and the Complex Portal is a major strength because it provides a realistic path for community adoption. I particularly appreciate the plan to link reprocessed submissions to original accessions, redeposit standardized outputs, and make the results available to non-specialists. These are exactly the kinds of synthesis activities that NCEMS is designed to support. However, the proposal is thin on details that determine long-term data value: which standards will be used, how metadata will be represented, how provenance will be tracked, whether the pipeline will be containerized, how versioning will be handled, and how updates to protein sequence databases, AlphaFold models, huMAP predictions, and ontology terms will be managed. There is also a risk that the project becomes a one-time reanalysis rather than a sustainable resource. The proposal should include governance for curation decisions, community feedback mechanisms, and explicit alignment with existing proteomics standards such as mzIdentML/mzTab variants or crosslink-specific reporting schemas. Overall, I find this to be a strong proposal with a clear need for collaborative infrastructure, but the data stewardship plan should be made more explicit."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent fit to public-data synthesis; strong connection to PRIDE and EBI Complex Portal; clear community need for standardized XL-MS metadata and processed results; likely to benefit both specialists and non-specialists; the work is beyond a single laboratory because it requires database engineering, proteomics expertise, and biological curation; the outcomes are concrete and disseminable."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not specify metadata schemas, controlled vocabularies, provenance models, software release plans, persistent identifiers, versioning, or long-term maintenance; no clear community governance or user-feedback plan is described; the training component is not well developed; sustainability beyond the fifteen-month project is uncertain."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling data-synthesis and infrastructure proposal with strong institutional positioning, but it requires a more mature data-management and sustainability plan."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal supports future studies of emergent protein complex organization, but the immediate deliverable is primarily a harmonized data resource rather than a sharply defined emergent-phenomena analysis."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The harmonization of XL-MS datasets and their integration into major community databases would be significant and underexplored. The novelty lies more in systematic synthesis and resource creation than in a new biological hypothesis."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The plan is credible at a high level, but insufficient technical detail is given on metadata standards, workflow reproducibility, provenance, and database update procedures."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is ambitious for curation, reanalysis, database deposition, and portal integration. A staged plan with a smaller high-quality first release would be more convincing."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is an exemplary synthesis-focused proposal: it uses existing data, standardizes it, reprocesses it, integrates it with other public resources, and creates reusable community outputs."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal clearly identifies PRIDE as the major source and discusses the need to triage approximately one thousand crosslinking-related datasets. It should, however, provide a preliminary accession list or at least a more formal dataset discovery protocol."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to redeposit standardized datasets in PRIDE and integrate evidence into the EBI Complex Portal is very strong. If accompanied by open workflows and transparent provenance, this would represent an excellent open-science contribution.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist and interactome mapping expert"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal targets an important gap in interactome biology. Existing large-scale interaction maps such as BioPlex and huMAP have transformed our understanding of protein complexes, but many interactions remain indirect, inferred, or lacking structural support. XL-MS is a valuable orthogonal data type because it can provide direct residue-pair evidence for physical proximity in native-like cellular contexts. A systematic synthesis of public XL-MS data could therefore refine interaction networks and potentially identify interactions missed by affinity purification or co-fractionation methods. I am enthusiastic about the idea of comparing XL-MS-derived pairwise contacts with huMAP-like complex predictions and using the results to improve curated resources. However, the proposal does not yet articulate a sufficiently compelling biological question. The title promises discovery of new PPIs, but the plan is largely resource harmonization and validation of predicted complexes. Those are valuable, but the call asks for compelling scientific questions related to emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences. The proposal could be strengthened by asking, for example, whether XL-MS preferentially reveals transient interfaces, condition-specific assemblies, hub scaffolds, or network modules not recovered by other interactomics methods. The optional aim on uncharacterized proteins is potentially interesting but underdeveloped and appears tacked on. Overall, I see this as a valuable community synthesis project with strong technical rationale, but the biological discovery framework needs sharpening."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a clear limitation of current interactome maps; XL-MS provides orthogonal residue-level support for PPIs; integration with huMAP and Complex Portal could materially improve community complex annotations; the work has potential to identify transient or structurally supported interactions missed by other approaches; it fits the collaborative, public-data synthesis model."",
      ""weakness"": ""The central biological question is not well defined; emergent properties of protein networks are not explicitly analyzed; the uncharacterized-protein aim is speculative and lacks methodology; criteria for distinguishing direct binary interactions from co-complex proximity are unclear; there is limited discussion of tissue, condition, organism, or cellular-context heterogeneity across datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong resource-building concept for interactome science, but the proposal would be more competitive with a clearer biological hypothesis about emergent protein complex organization and network behavior."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein interaction networks and complexes are emergent cellular systems, but the proposal does not explicitly define or test principles of emergence. It mainly catalogs and validates interactions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic use of public XL-MS data to refine interactome maps is significant and would complement existing AP-MS and co-fractionation resources. The novelty is moderate to high at the synthesis level."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall plan is reasonable, but network analysis methods, confidence scoring, comparison to huMAP, and criteria for new interaction calls are underdefined."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed work is ambitious for fifteen months, especially if it includes dataset curation, reprocessing, structural modeling, network comparison, database integration, and functional inference."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly based on synthesis of publicly available XL-MS and interaction datasets and does not require new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and Complex Portal as relevant resources, but it does not provide enough detail on the specific XL-MS datasets, organism coverage, or how biases in public datasets will be managed."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The planned deposition and integration into public EBI resources are strong. More detail is needed on release of network confidence scores, negative or failed validations, and reproducible analysis notebooks.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior quantitative cell biologist and data-science training leader"",
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal is well aligned with the NCEMS goal of enabling collaborative synthesis projects that exceed the capacity of a single lab. It brings together mass spectrometry expertise, structural biology, public repositories, and database curation. The proposed outputs are likely to be useful to a broad molecular and cellular biology community, particularly if the reprocessed XL-MS datasets are made accessible to users who do not specialize in proteomics search algorithms. I also appreciate that the work could provide trainees with exposure to data curation, reproducible workflows, structural modeling, and biological interpretation. However, the proposal only briefly mentions training and does not provide a concrete plan for how graduate students or postdocs will participate, what skills they will acquire, or whether there will be workshops, hackathons, documentation sprints, or cross-lab mentoring. Since training the next generation of data-savvy scientists is a stated goal of the call, this is a missed opportunity. I also worry that the project is framed as largely dependent on a dedicated data scientist rather than as a broader working group process. The proposal would benefit from clearer milestones for collaborative meetings, division of labor, trainee engagement, and community-facing products. Scientifically, the project is worthwhile, but the NCEMS-specific working group dimensions could be better developed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong multidisciplinary fit involving proteomics, structural biology, informatics, and databases; clear use of public data; likely high value for the community; good rationale for NCEMS support because the work requires coordination among multiple expertise areas; potential to produce reusable workflows and training datasets; integration into established public repositories increases impact."",
      ""weakness"": ""Training plans are vague; working group structure and collaborative mechanisms are insufficiently described; reliance on one data scientist may limit broader capacity building; diversity of participants, geographic breadth, career stages, and institutional levels are not discussed in detail; the proposal lacks explicit plans for workshops, documentation, tutorials, or trainee-led analyses."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A scientifically valuable synthesis project with strong resource potential, but it needs a more explicit working group, training, and community-engagement plan to fully meet the funding call's broader goals."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is relevant to emergent protein complex organization, but the connection is implicit. It does not clearly state how the synthesized data will reveal emergent cellular behaviors or principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A standardized, accessible XL-MS meta-resource integrated with complex annotations would be significant. The project is novel in its synthesis and community-resource aspects rather than in generating a new analytical theory."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed activities are sensible but described at a relatively high level. More detail is needed on reproducible workflow design, validation standards, quality control, and trainee-accessible documentation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is aggressive but possible if tightly scoped. The optional functional annotation aim should be treated as exploratory and not allowed to compromise the main curation and integration goals."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project fully satisfies the synthesis requirement by relying on existing public XL-MS and interaction data and by focusing on standardization, integration, and reuse."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The major public data sources are identified, especially PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and the EBI Complex Portal. A stronger proposal would include a preliminary dataset table and clearer triage process."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to release reprocessed data through PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is strong. The open-science plan should also include training materials, code repositories, workflow containers, and public documentation.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::11,y1,11,Searching the crosslinking mass spectrometry universe for new protein-protein interactions,Accepted,Stephen D. Fried; Yasset Perez-Riverol; Henning Hermjakob,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"The proposal is attractive because XL-MS data provide precisely the type of spatial restraint that can bridge interactomics and structural biology. The ambition to use public XL-MS data to validate predicted complexes and improve evidence levels in the Complex Portal is well aligned with the needs of the field. However, I am less convinced by the proposed structural validation strategy as written. The proposal appears to assume that crosslinks can be compared relatively directly to AlphaFold-generated structures, including AlphaFold3 predictions, to determine whether a PPI is supported. This can work in favorable cases, but in practice crosslink interpretation requires careful consideration of linker length distributions, side-chain flexibility, peptide-level ambiguity, protein isoforms, post-translational states, oligomeric stoichiometry, conformational ensembles, membrane environments, and indirect co-complex proximity. AlphaFold models are also not experimental truth; docking confidence, interface confidence, and multimer prediction reliability vary widely. A crosslink that violates a model may indicate a wrong model, a different conformational state, an alternative assembly, or a false positive. Conversely, a model satisfying a crosslink does not by itself establish a physiological complex. The proposal would be stronger if it framed AlphaFold as one component in an integrative scoring system rather than as a main validation criterion. Despite this concern, the synthesis objective is valuable, and the team includes relevant expertise and infrastructure. I would support the project if the milestones emphasize creation of a rigorously annotated XL-MS restraint resource and only secondarily the inference of new complexes.",The proposal exploits the unique value of XL-MS as residue-level spatial evidence; integration with predicted structures and complex databases is potentially powerful; the project could generate restraints useful for integrative modeling across many systems; it uses existing data and requires a transdisciplinary team; the focus on improving evidence for human and yeast complexes is scientifically meaningful.,"The structural validation strategy is underdeveloped; AlphaFold-based confirmation may be overinterpreted; there is little discussion of conformational heterogeneity, stoichiometry, isoforms, ambiguous peptide mapping, or crosslink distance distributions; criteria for accepting or rejecting interactions are not described quantitatively; computational cost estimates and prioritization of AlphaFold runs are not sufficiently justified.",3,"A worthwhile synthesis project with high potential resource value, but the proposed use of structural prediction to validate PPIs needs a more nuanced and rigorous integrative modeling framework.",4,"The assembly of proteins into complexes and networks is directly relevant to emergent molecular function. The proposal could reveal higher-order organization from residue-level interaction data, although it does not deeply articulate mechanistic emergence questions.",4,Combining public XL-MS datasets with structure prediction and curated complex resources is significant and relatively novel as a community-scale synthesis activity. The potential impact is high if validation is done carefully.,2,"The computational structural biology component lacks necessary detail. The proposal does not adequately define model confidence thresholds, distance restraint interpretation, treatment of flexible/disordered regions, or how conflicting evidence will be represented.",3,"The proposed timeline may be feasible for resource assembly but is tight for large-scale reprocessing, structural modeling, validation, curation, and biological interpretation. The optional uncharacterized-protein aim should be deprioritized.",5,"The project is entirely centered on reusing and integrating existing public data, including PRIDE datasets, huMAP-like predictions, AlphaFold models or predictions, and Complex Portal records.",3,"The proposal identifies PRIDE as the data source and mentions selection criteria such as proteome-wide design and cleavable crosslinkers, but it lacks a concrete inventory of datasets, expected organisms, metadata fields, and exclusion rules.",4,"Deposition into PRIDE and the Complex Portal is strong. The proposal would benefit from clearer plans for releasing modeling scripts, scoring criteria, failed or ambiguous cases, and provenance metadata.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:19:48.293895,0,success,,"This proposal aims to utilize public cross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) data to validate predicted protein complexes and enhance the Complex Portal. While the integration of interactomics with structural biology is highly valuable, the proposed structural validation strategy relies too heavily on direct comparisons with AlphaFold models without fully addressing the complexities of XL-MS data or the limitations of predicted structures. Overall, the project represents a scientifically meaningful synthesis effort that would be significantly strengthened by treating AlphaFold predictions as part of a broader integrative scoring system rather than as standalone validation.",The project effectively leverages the unique spatial evidence provided by XL-MS data to validate protein-protein interactions and create restraints useful for integrative modeling. It is supported by a capable transdisciplinary team utilizing existing data to generate biologically meaningful insights for human and yeast complexes.,"The structural validation approach is underdeveloped and risks overinterpreting AlphaFold predictions without accounting for conformational heterogeneity, mapping ambiguities, or alternative stoichiometries. Additionally, the proposal lacks quantitative criteria for evaluating interactions and insufficiently justifies the associated computational costs."
baseline::y1::11::gpt-5.5::003,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,3,baseline::y1::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:45:59.346186,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior investigator in crosslinking mass spectrometry and structural proteomics"",
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal addresses a highly timely and real bottleneck in the crosslinking mass spectrometry field: substantial amounts of XL-MS data have been deposited in PRIDE, but differences in search engines, FDR strategies, crosslinker chemistries, database construction, metadata quality, and reporting formats severely limit reuse. The plan to reannotate and reprocess large-scale XL-MS datasets using contemporary algorithms and standardized formats is directly responsive to the call's emphasis on synthesis of public data. The proposed integration of residue-level crosslinks with protein complex resources could substantially improve the evidentiary basis for known and predicted protein-protein interactions. I view the project as scientifically significant and community enabling. The principal concern is that the proposal underestimates the technical heterogeneity of legacy XL-MS datasets. Reprocessing data across studies requires detailed knowledge of sample preparation, crosslinker chemistry, fragmentation method, enrichment strategy, search space, isotope labeling, and instrument settings. The proposal names Scout but does not sufficiently justify why one search pipeline will be appropriate across diverse datasets or how inter-study FDR and comparability will be handled. The target of roughly one hundred large-scale datasets in six months is ambitious, especially if manual metadata curation is required. Nonetheless, the team appears unusually well positioned because it combines XL-MS expertise with PRIDE and Complex Portal infrastructure. With clearer triage criteria, a more explicit quality-control plan, and a realistic prioritization strategy, this could become an important reference resource for the field."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a genuine and important data-reuse problem in XL-MS; it relies entirely on public data; it would create standardized reprocessed datasets with direct community value; integration with PRIDE and the EBI Complex Portal provides a credible route to dissemination; the residue-level nature of XL-MS gives the project scientific value beyond generic PPI cataloging; the work requires expertise from multiple groups and is unlikely to be accomplished by a single laboratory."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides insufficient detail on crosslink-specific reprocessing parameters, FDR control, treatment of different crosslinker chemistries, and comparability across datasets; the number of datasets proposed for reanalysis may be unrealistic; criteria for what constitutes a validated new interaction are not fully specified; the plan for AlphaFold-based validation may be oversimplified for transient, flexible, or context-dependent complexes; trainee training plans are only implicit."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and highly relevant synthesis proposal that could substantially improve the usability of public XL-MS data, though it needs a more rigorous and realistic implementation plan for heterogeneous legacy datasets."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein complexes and interaction networks are central examples of emergent molecular organization, and XL-MS offers residue-level evidence for how proteins assemble into higher-order systems. The proposal could illuminate emergent organization of interactomes, although the framing is more resource- and interaction-discovery oriented than explicitly focused on emergence principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The individual components are not entirely new, but the large-scale synthesis of public XL-MS data, reprocessing with modern methods, and integration into complex databases is novel and significant for structural proteomics."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad workflow is sound, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on quality control, search parameter harmonization, FDR estimation across datasets, treatment of ambiguous crosslinks, and validation thresholds for database deposition."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The fifteen-month timeline is plausible for a prioritized pilot but optimistic for reprocessing and manually curating approximately one hundred heterogeneous large-scale datasets plus structural modeling and database integration."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort using existing public XL-MS data and integrating it with public complex and structural prediction resources. It does not propose new experimental data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""PRIDE is clearly identified as the primary source, and the proposal recognizes that many nominal XL-MS datasets will require filtering. However, the exact dataset list, inclusion criteria, and metadata requirements need further specification."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to redeposit standardized datasets to PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is a strong open-science commitment. More explicit statements about public workflows, code, containers, and versioning would strengthen this aspect.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational structural biologist specializing in integrative modeling and protein complex prediction"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal is attractive because XL-MS data provide precisely the type of spatial restraint that can bridge interactomics and structural biology. The ambition to use public XL-MS data to validate predicted complexes and improve evidence levels in the Complex Portal is well aligned with the needs of the field. However, I am less convinced by the proposed structural validation strategy as written. The proposal appears to assume that crosslinks can be compared relatively directly to AlphaFold-generated structures, including AlphaFold3 predictions, to determine whether a PPI is supported. This can work in favorable cases, but in practice crosslink interpretation requires careful consideration of linker length distributions, side-chain flexibility, peptide-level ambiguity, protein isoforms, post-translational states, oligomeric stoichiometry, conformational ensembles, membrane environments, and indirect co-complex proximity. AlphaFold models are also not experimental truth; docking confidence, interface confidence, and multimer prediction reliability vary widely. A crosslink that violates a model may indicate a wrong model, a different conformational state, an alternative assembly, or a false positive. Conversely, a model satisfying a crosslink does not by itself establish a physiological complex. The proposal would be stronger if it framed AlphaFold as one component in an integrative scoring system rather than as a main validation criterion. Despite this concern, the synthesis objective is valuable, and the team includes relevant expertise and infrastructure. I would support the project if the milestones emphasize creation of a rigorously annotated XL-MS restraint resource and only secondarily the inference of new complexes."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal exploits the unique value of XL-MS as residue-level spatial evidence; integration with predicted structures and complex databases is potentially powerful; the project could generate restraints useful for integrative modeling across many systems; it uses existing data and requires a transdisciplinary team; the focus on improving evidence for human and yeast complexes is scientifically meaningful."",
      ""weakness"": ""The structural validation strategy is underdeveloped; AlphaFold-based confirmation may be overinterpreted; there is little discussion of conformational heterogeneity, stoichiometry, isoforms, ambiguous peptide mapping, or crosslink distance distributions; criteria for accepting or rejecting interactions are not described quantitatively; computational cost estimates and prioritization of AlphaFold runs are not sufficiently justified."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A worthwhile synthesis project with high potential resource value, but the proposed use of structural prediction to validate PPIs needs a more nuanced and rigorous integrative modeling framework."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The assembly of proteins into complexes and networks is directly relevant to emergent molecular function. The proposal could reveal higher-order organization from residue-level interaction data, although it does not deeply articulate mechanistic emergence questions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining public XL-MS datasets with structure prediction and curated complex resources is significant and relatively novel as a community-scale synthesis activity. The potential impact is high if validation is done carefully."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational structural biology component lacks necessary detail. The proposal does not adequately define model confidence thresholds, distance restraint interpretation, treatment of flexible/disordered regions, or how conflicting evidence will be represented."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed timeline may be feasible for resource assembly but is tight for large-scale reprocessing, structural modeling, validation, curation, and biological interpretation. The optional uncharacterized-protein aim should be deprioritized."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely centered on reusing and integrating existing public data, including PRIDE datasets, huMAP-like predictions, AlphaFold models or predictions, and Complex Portal records."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE as the data source and mentions selection criteria such as proteome-wide design and cleavable crosslinkers, but it lacks a concrete inventory of datasets, expected organisms, metadata fields, and exclusion rules."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Deposition into PRIDE and the Complex Portal is strong. The proposal would benefit from clearer plans for releasing modeling scripts, scoring criteria, failed or ambiguous cases, and provenance metadata.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior bioinformatics infrastructure and biological database scientist"",
      ""review_text"": ""From a data-infrastructure perspective, this proposal is highly responsive to the call. The central problem is not a lack of XL-MS data but lack of harmonization, metadata completeness, standardized processed outputs, and integration with authoritative resources. The proposed collaboration with EBI, PRIDE, and the Complex Portal is a major strength because it provides a realistic path for community adoption. I particularly appreciate the plan to link reprocessed submissions to original accessions, redeposit standardized outputs, and make the results available to non-specialists. These are exactly the kinds of synthesis activities that NCEMS is designed to support. However, the proposal is thin on details that determine long-term data value: which standards will be used, how metadata will be represented, how provenance will be tracked, whether the pipeline will be containerized, how versioning will be handled, and how updates to protein sequence databases, AlphaFold models, huMAP predictions, and ontology terms will be managed. There is also a risk that the project becomes a one-time reanalysis rather than a sustainable resource. The proposal should include governance for curation decisions, community feedback mechanisms, and explicit alignment with existing proteomics standards such as mzIdentML/mzTab variants or crosslink-specific reporting schemas. Overall, I find this to be a strong proposal with a clear need for collaborative infrastructure, but the data stewardship plan should be made more explicit."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent fit to public-data synthesis; strong connection to PRIDE and EBI Complex Portal; clear community need for standardized XL-MS metadata and processed results; likely to benefit both specialists and non-specialists; the work is beyond a single laboratory because it requires database engineering, proteomics expertise, and biological curation; the outcomes are concrete and disseminable."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not specify metadata schemas, controlled vocabularies, provenance models, software release plans, persistent identifiers, versioning, or long-term maintenance; no clear community governance or user-feedback plan is described; the training component is not well developed; sustainability beyond the fifteen-month project is uncertain."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling data-synthesis and infrastructure proposal with strong institutional positioning, but it requires a more mature data-management and sustainability plan."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal supports future studies of emergent protein complex organization, but the immediate deliverable is primarily a harmonized data resource rather than a sharply defined emergent-phenomena analysis."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The harmonization of XL-MS datasets and their integration into major community databases would be significant and underexplored. The novelty lies more in systematic synthesis and resource creation than in a new biological hypothesis."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The plan is credible at a high level, but insufficient technical detail is given on metadata standards, workflow reproducibility, provenance, and database update procedures."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is ambitious for curation, reanalysis, database deposition, and portal integration. A staged plan with a smaller high-quality first release would be more convincing."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is an exemplary synthesis-focused proposal: it uses existing data, standardizes it, reprocesses it, integrates it with other public resources, and creates reusable community outputs."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal clearly identifies PRIDE as the major source and discusses the need to triage approximately one thousand crosslinking-related datasets. It should, however, provide a preliminary accession list or at least a more formal dataset discovery protocol."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to redeposit standardized datasets in PRIDE and integrate evidence into the EBI Complex Portal is very strong. If accompanied by open workflows and transparent provenance, this would represent an excellent open-science contribution.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist and interactome mapping expert"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal targets an important gap in interactome biology. Existing large-scale interaction maps such as BioPlex and huMAP have transformed our understanding of protein complexes, but many interactions remain indirect, inferred, or lacking structural support. XL-MS is a valuable orthogonal data type because it can provide direct residue-pair evidence for physical proximity in native-like cellular contexts. A systematic synthesis of public XL-MS data could therefore refine interaction networks and potentially identify interactions missed by affinity purification or co-fractionation methods. I am enthusiastic about the idea of comparing XL-MS-derived pairwise contacts with huMAP-like complex predictions and using the results to improve curated resources. However, the proposal does not yet articulate a sufficiently compelling biological question. The title promises discovery of new PPIs, but the plan is largely resource harmonization and validation of predicted complexes. Those are valuable, but the call asks for compelling scientific questions related to emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences. The proposal could be strengthened by asking, for example, whether XL-MS preferentially reveals transient interfaces, condition-specific assemblies, hub scaffolds, or network modules not recovered by other interactomics methods. The optional aim on uncharacterized proteins is potentially interesting but underdeveloped and appears tacked on. Overall, I see this as a valuable community synthesis project with strong technical rationale, but the biological discovery framework needs sharpening."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a clear limitation of current interactome maps; XL-MS provides orthogonal residue-level support for PPIs; integration with huMAP and Complex Portal could materially improve community complex annotations; the work has potential to identify transient or structurally supported interactions missed by other approaches; it fits the collaborative, public-data synthesis model."",
      ""weakness"": ""The central biological question is not well defined; emergent properties of protein networks are not explicitly analyzed; the uncharacterized-protein aim is speculative and lacks methodology; criteria for distinguishing direct binary interactions from co-complex proximity are unclear; there is limited discussion of tissue, condition, organism, or cellular-context heterogeneity across datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong resource-building concept for interactome science, but the proposal would be more competitive with a clearer biological hypothesis about emergent protein complex organization and network behavior."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein interaction networks and complexes are emergent cellular systems, but the proposal does not explicitly define or test principles of emergence. It mainly catalogs and validates interactions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic use of public XL-MS data to refine interactome maps is significant and would complement existing AP-MS and co-fractionation resources. The novelty is moderate to high at the synthesis level."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall plan is reasonable, but network analysis methods, confidence scoring, comparison to huMAP, and criteria for new interaction calls are underdefined."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed work is ambitious for fifteen months, especially if it includes dataset curation, reprocessing, structural modeling, network comparison, database integration, and functional inference."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly based on synthesis of publicly available XL-MS and interaction datasets and does not require new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and Complex Portal as relevant resources, but it does not provide enough detail on the specific XL-MS datasets, organism coverage, or how biases in public datasets will be managed."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The planned deposition and integration into public EBI resources are strong. More detail is needed on release of network confidence scores, negative or failed validations, and reproducible analysis notebooks.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior quantitative cell biologist and data-science training leader"",
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal is well aligned with the NCEMS goal of enabling collaborative synthesis projects that exceed the capacity of a single lab. It brings together mass spectrometry expertise, structural biology, public repositories, and database curation. The proposed outputs are likely to be useful to a broad molecular and cellular biology community, particularly if the reprocessed XL-MS datasets are made accessible to users who do not specialize in proteomics search algorithms. I also appreciate that the work could provide trainees with exposure to data curation, reproducible workflows, structural modeling, and biological interpretation. However, the proposal only briefly mentions training and does not provide a concrete plan for how graduate students or postdocs will participate, what skills they will acquire, or whether there will be workshops, hackathons, documentation sprints, or cross-lab mentoring. Since training the next generation of data-savvy scientists is a stated goal of the call, this is a missed opportunity. I also worry that the project is framed as largely dependent on a dedicated data scientist rather than as a broader working group process. The proposal would benefit from clearer milestones for collaborative meetings, division of labor, trainee engagement, and community-facing products. Scientifically, the project is worthwhile, but the NCEMS-specific working group dimensions could be better developed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong multidisciplinary fit involving proteomics, structural biology, informatics, and databases; clear use of public data; likely high value for the community; good rationale for NCEMS support because the work requires coordination among multiple expertise areas; potential to produce reusable workflows and training datasets; integration into established public repositories increases impact."",
      ""weakness"": ""Training plans are vague; working group structure and collaborative mechanisms are insufficiently described; reliance on one data scientist may limit broader capacity building; diversity of participants, geographic breadth, career stages, and institutional levels are not discussed in detail; the proposal lacks explicit plans for workshops, documentation, tutorials, or trainee-led analyses."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A scientifically valuable synthesis project with strong resource potential, but it needs a more explicit working group, training, and community-engagement plan to fully meet the funding call's broader goals."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is relevant to emergent protein complex organization, but the connection is implicit. It does not clearly state how the synthesized data will reveal emergent cellular behaviors or principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A standardized, accessible XL-MS meta-resource integrated with complex annotations would be significant. The project is novel in its synthesis and community-resource aspects rather than in generating a new analytical theory."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed activities are sensible but described at a relatively high level. More detail is needed on reproducible workflow design, validation standards, quality control, and trainee-accessible documentation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is aggressive but possible if tightly scoped. The optional functional annotation aim should be treated as exploratory and not allowed to compromise the main curation and integration goals."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project fully satisfies the synthesis requirement by relying on existing public XL-MS and interaction data and by focusing on standardization, integration, and reuse."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The major public data sources are identified, especially PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and the EBI Complex Portal. A stronger proposal would include a preliminary dataset table and clearer triage process."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to release reprocessed data through PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is strong. The open-science plan should also include training materials, code repositories, workflow containers, and public documentation.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::11,y1,11,Searching the crosslinking mass spectrometry universe for new protein-protein interactions,Accepted,Stephen D. Fried; Yasset Perez-Riverol; Henning Hermjakob,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"From a data-infrastructure perspective, this proposal is highly responsive to the call. The central problem is not a lack of XL-MS data but lack of harmonization, metadata completeness, standardized processed outputs, and integration with authoritative resources. The proposed collaboration with EBI, PRIDE, and the Complex Portal is a major strength because it provides a realistic path for community adoption. I particularly appreciate the plan to link reprocessed submissions to original accessions, redeposit standardized outputs, and make the results available to non-specialists. These are exactly the kinds of synthesis activities that NCEMS is designed to support. However, the proposal is thin on details that determine long-term data value: which standards will be used, how metadata will be represented, how provenance will be tracked, whether the pipeline will be containerized, how versioning will be handled, and how updates to protein sequence databases, AlphaFold models, huMAP predictions, and ontology terms will be managed. There is also a risk that the project becomes a one-time reanalysis rather than a sustainable resource. The proposal should include governance for curation decisions, community feedback mechanisms, and explicit alignment with existing proteomics standards such as mzIdentML/mzTab variants or crosslink-specific reporting schemas. Overall, I find this to be a strong proposal with a clear need for collaborative infrastructure, but the data stewardship plan should be made more explicit.","Excellent fit to public-data synthesis; strong connection to PRIDE and EBI Complex Portal; clear community need for standardized XL-MS metadata and processed results; likely to benefit both specialists and non-specialists; the work is beyond a single laboratory because it requires database engineering, proteomics expertise, and biological curation; the outcomes are concrete and disseminable.","The proposal does not specify metadata schemas, controlled vocabularies, provenance models, software release plans, persistent identifiers, versioning, or long-term maintenance; no clear community governance or user-feedback plan is described; the training component is not well developed; sustainability beyond the fifteen-month project is uncertain.",4,"A compelling data-synthesis and infrastructure proposal with strong institutional positioning, but it requires a more mature data-management and sustainability plan.",3,"The proposal supports future studies of emergent protein complex organization, but the immediate deliverable is primarily a harmonized data resource rather than a sharply defined emergent-phenomena analysis.",4,The harmonization of XL-MS datasets and their integration into major community databases would be significant and underexplored. The novelty lies more in systematic synthesis and resource creation than in a new biological hypothesis.,3,"The plan is credible at a high level, but insufficient technical detail is given on metadata standards, workflow reproducibility, provenance, and database update procedures.",3,"The timeline is ambitious for curation, reanalysis, database deposition, and portal integration. A staged plan with a smaller high-quality first release would be more convincing.",5,"This is an exemplary synthesis-focused proposal: it uses existing data, standardizes it, reprocesses it, integrates it with other public resources, and creates reusable community outputs.",4,"The proposal clearly identifies PRIDE as the major source and discusses the need to triage approximately one thousand crosslinking-related datasets. It should, however, provide a preliminary accession list or at least a more formal dataset discovery protocol.",5,"The commitment to redeposit standardized datasets in PRIDE and integrate evidence into the EBI Complex Portal is very strong. If accompanied by open workflows and transparent provenance, this would represent an excellent open-science contribution.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:19:58.779309,0,success,,"This proposal aligns well with the call by addressing the need for harmonized data, complete metadata, and standardized outputs in XL-MS research. The planned collaboration with established resources like PRIDE and the EBI Complex Portal provides a strong foundation for community adoption and broad accessibility. However, the proposal lacks crucial details regarding long-term data stewardship, technical standards, and sustainability. Overall, it is a strong concept that requires a more explicit data management and governance plan to ensure its viability as a long-term resource.","The project effectively targets a clear community need for standardized XL-MS metadata and integrated, processed results. Furthermore, partnerships with major repositories ensure that the outcomes will be concrete, disseminable, and beneficial to both specialists and non-specialists.","The proposal omits critical technical details regarding metadata schemas, provenance tracking, software versioning, and alignment with existing proteomics standards. Additionally, the training component is underdeveloped, and there are significant concerns regarding community governance and the project's sustainability beyond the initial fifteen-month period."
baseline::y1::11::gpt-5.5::004,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,4,baseline::y1::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:45:59.346186,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior investigator in crosslinking mass spectrometry and structural proteomics"",
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal addresses a highly timely and real bottleneck in the crosslinking mass spectrometry field: substantial amounts of XL-MS data have been deposited in PRIDE, but differences in search engines, FDR strategies, crosslinker chemistries, database construction, metadata quality, and reporting formats severely limit reuse. The plan to reannotate and reprocess large-scale XL-MS datasets using contemporary algorithms and standardized formats is directly responsive to the call's emphasis on synthesis of public data. The proposed integration of residue-level crosslinks with protein complex resources could substantially improve the evidentiary basis for known and predicted protein-protein interactions. I view the project as scientifically significant and community enabling. The principal concern is that the proposal underestimates the technical heterogeneity of legacy XL-MS datasets. Reprocessing data across studies requires detailed knowledge of sample preparation, crosslinker chemistry, fragmentation method, enrichment strategy, search space, isotope labeling, and instrument settings. The proposal names Scout but does not sufficiently justify why one search pipeline will be appropriate across diverse datasets or how inter-study FDR and comparability will be handled. The target of roughly one hundred large-scale datasets in six months is ambitious, especially if manual metadata curation is required. Nonetheless, the team appears unusually well positioned because it combines XL-MS expertise with PRIDE and Complex Portal infrastructure. With clearer triage criteria, a more explicit quality-control plan, and a realistic prioritization strategy, this could become an important reference resource for the field."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a genuine and important data-reuse problem in XL-MS; it relies entirely on public data; it would create standardized reprocessed datasets with direct community value; integration with PRIDE and the EBI Complex Portal provides a credible route to dissemination; the residue-level nature of XL-MS gives the project scientific value beyond generic PPI cataloging; the work requires expertise from multiple groups and is unlikely to be accomplished by a single laboratory."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides insufficient detail on crosslink-specific reprocessing parameters, FDR control, treatment of different crosslinker chemistries, and comparability across datasets; the number of datasets proposed for reanalysis may be unrealistic; criteria for what constitutes a validated new interaction are not fully specified; the plan for AlphaFold-based validation may be oversimplified for transient, flexible, or context-dependent complexes; trainee training plans are only implicit."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and highly relevant synthesis proposal that could substantially improve the usability of public XL-MS data, though it needs a more rigorous and realistic implementation plan for heterogeneous legacy datasets."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein complexes and interaction networks are central examples of emergent molecular organization, and XL-MS offers residue-level evidence for how proteins assemble into higher-order systems. The proposal could illuminate emergent organization of interactomes, although the framing is more resource- and interaction-discovery oriented than explicitly focused on emergence principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The individual components are not entirely new, but the large-scale synthesis of public XL-MS data, reprocessing with modern methods, and integration into complex databases is novel and significant for structural proteomics."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad workflow is sound, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on quality control, search parameter harmonization, FDR estimation across datasets, treatment of ambiguous crosslinks, and validation thresholds for database deposition."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The fifteen-month timeline is plausible for a prioritized pilot but optimistic for reprocessing and manually curating approximately one hundred heterogeneous large-scale datasets plus structural modeling and database integration."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort using existing public XL-MS data and integrating it with public complex and structural prediction resources. It does not propose new experimental data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""PRIDE is clearly identified as the primary source, and the proposal recognizes that many nominal XL-MS datasets will require filtering. However, the exact dataset list, inclusion criteria, and metadata requirements need further specification."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to redeposit standardized datasets to PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is a strong open-science commitment. More explicit statements about public workflows, code, containers, and versioning would strengthen this aspect.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational structural biologist specializing in integrative modeling and protein complex prediction"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal is attractive because XL-MS data provide precisely the type of spatial restraint that can bridge interactomics and structural biology. The ambition to use public XL-MS data to validate predicted complexes and improve evidence levels in the Complex Portal is well aligned with the needs of the field. However, I am less convinced by the proposed structural validation strategy as written. The proposal appears to assume that crosslinks can be compared relatively directly to AlphaFold-generated structures, including AlphaFold3 predictions, to determine whether a PPI is supported. This can work in favorable cases, but in practice crosslink interpretation requires careful consideration of linker length distributions, side-chain flexibility, peptide-level ambiguity, protein isoforms, post-translational states, oligomeric stoichiometry, conformational ensembles, membrane environments, and indirect co-complex proximity. AlphaFold models are also not experimental truth; docking confidence, interface confidence, and multimer prediction reliability vary widely. A crosslink that violates a model may indicate a wrong model, a different conformational state, an alternative assembly, or a false positive. Conversely, a model satisfying a crosslink does not by itself establish a physiological complex. The proposal would be stronger if it framed AlphaFold as one component in an integrative scoring system rather than as a main validation criterion. Despite this concern, the synthesis objective is valuable, and the team includes relevant expertise and infrastructure. I would support the project if the milestones emphasize creation of a rigorously annotated XL-MS restraint resource and only secondarily the inference of new complexes."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal exploits the unique value of XL-MS as residue-level spatial evidence; integration with predicted structures and complex databases is potentially powerful; the project could generate restraints useful for integrative modeling across many systems; it uses existing data and requires a transdisciplinary team; the focus on improving evidence for human and yeast complexes is scientifically meaningful."",
      ""weakness"": ""The structural validation strategy is underdeveloped; AlphaFold-based confirmation may be overinterpreted; there is little discussion of conformational heterogeneity, stoichiometry, isoforms, ambiguous peptide mapping, or crosslink distance distributions; criteria for accepting or rejecting interactions are not described quantitatively; computational cost estimates and prioritization of AlphaFold runs are not sufficiently justified."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A worthwhile synthesis project with high potential resource value, but the proposed use of structural prediction to validate PPIs needs a more nuanced and rigorous integrative modeling framework."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The assembly of proteins into complexes and networks is directly relevant to emergent molecular function. The proposal could reveal higher-order organization from residue-level interaction data, although it does not deeply articulate mechanistic emergence questions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining public XL-MS datasets with structure prediction and curated complex resources is significant and relatively novel as a community-scale synthesis activity. The potential impact is high if validation is done carefully."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational structural biology component lacks necessary detail. The proposal does not adequately define model confidence thresholds, distance restraint interpretation, treatment of flexible/disordered regions, or how conflicting evidence will be represented."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed timeline may be feasible for resource assembly but is tight for large-scale reprocessing, structural modeling, validation, curation, and biological interpretation. The optional uncharacterized-protein aim should be deprioritized."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely centered on reusing and integrating existing public data, including PRIDE datasets, huMAP-like predictions, AlphaFold models or predictions, and Complex Portal records."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE as the data source and mentions selection criteria such as proteome-wide design and cleavable crosslinkers, but it lacks a concrete inventory of datasets, expected organisms, metadata fields, and exclusion rules."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Deposition into PRIDE and the Complex Portal is strong. The proposal would benefit from clearer plans for releasing modeling scripts, scoring criteria, failed or ambiguous cases, and provenance metadata.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior bioinformatics infrastructure and biological database scientist"",
      ""review_text"": ""From a data-infrastructure perspective, this proposal is highly responsive to the call. The central problem is not a lack of XL-MS data but lack of harmonization, metadata completeness, standardized processed outputs, and integration with authoritative resources. The proposed collaboration with EBI, PRIDE, and the Complex Portal is a major strength because it provides a realistic path for community adoption. I particularly appreciate the plan to link reprocessed submissions to original accessions, redeposit standardized outputs, and make the results available to non-specialists. These are exactly the kinds of synthesis activities that NCEMS is designed to support. However, the proposal is thin on details that determine long-term data value: which standards will be used, how metadata will be represented, how provenance will be tracked, whether the pipeline will be containerized, how versioning will be handled, and how updates to protein sequence databases, AlphaFold models, huMAP predictions, and ontology terms will be managed. There is also a risk that the project becomes a one-time reanalysis rather than a sustainable resource. The proposal should include governance for curation decisions, community feedback mechanisms, and explicit alignment with existing proteomics standards such as mzIdentML/mzTab variants or crosslink-specific reporting schemas. Overall, I find this to be a strong proposal with a clear need for collaborative infrastructure, but the data stewardship plan should be made more explicit."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent fit to public-data synthesis; strong connection to PRIDE and EBI Complex Portal; clear community need for standardized XL-MS metadata and processed results; likely to benefit both specialists and non-specialists; the work is beyond a single laboratory because it requires database engineering, proteomics expertise, and biological curation; the outcomes are concrete and disseminable."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not specify metadata schemas, controlled vocabularies, provenance models, software release plans, persistent identifiers, versioning, or long-term maintenance; no clear community governance or user-feedback plan is described; the training component is not well developed; sustainability beyond the fifteen-month project is uncertain."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling data-synthesis and infrastructure proposal with strong institutional positioning, but it requires a more mature data-management and sustainability plan."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal supports future studies of emergent protein complex organization, but the immediate deliverable is primarily a harmonized data resource rather than a sharply defined emergent-phenomena analysis."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The harmonization of XL-MS datasets and their integration into major community databases would be significant and underexplored. The novelty lies more in systematic synthesis and resource creation than in a new biological hypothesis."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The plan is credible at a high level, but insufficient technical detail is given on metadata standards, workflow reproducibility, provenance, and database update procedures."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is ambitious for curation, reanalysis, database deposition, and portal integration. A staged plan with a smaller high-quality first release would be more convincing."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is an exemplary synthesis-focused proposal: it uses existing data, standardizes it, reprocesses it, integrates it with other public resources, and creates reusable community outputs."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal clearly identifies PRIDE as the major source and discusses the need to triage approximately one thousand crosslinking-related datasets. It should, however, provide a preliminary accession list or at least a more formal dataset discovery protocol."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to redeposit standardized datasets in PRIDE and integrate evidence into the EBI Complex Portal is very strong. If accompanied by open workflows and transparent provenance, this would represent an excellent open-science contribution.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist and interactome mapping expert"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal targets an important gap in interactome biology. Existing large-scale interaction maps such as BioPlex and huMAP have transformed our understanding of protein complexes, but many interactions remain indirect, inferred, or lacking structural support. XL-MS is a valuable orthogonal data type because it can provide direct residue-pair evidence for physical proximity in native-like cellular contexts. A systematic synthesis of public XL-MS data could therefore refine interaction networks and potentially identify interactions missed by affinity purification or co-fractionation methods. I am enthusiastic about the idea of comparing XL-MS-derived pairwise contacts with huMAP-like complex predictions and using the results to improve curated resources. However, the proposal does not yet articulate a sufficiently compelling biological question. The title promises discovery of new PPIs, but the plan is largely resource harmonization and validation of predicted complexes. Those are valuable, but the call asks for compelling scientific questions related to emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences. The proposal could be strengthened by asking, for example, whether XL-MS preferentially reveals transient interfaces, condition-specific assemblies, hub scaffolds, or network modules not recovered by other interactomics methods. The optional aim on uncharacterized proteins is potentially interesting but underdeveloped and appears tacked on. Overall, I see this as a valuable community synthesis project with strong technical rationale, but the biological discovery framework needs sharpening."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a clear limitation of current interactome maps; XL-MS provides orthogonal residue-level support for PPIs; integration with huMAP and Complex Portal could materially improve community complex annotations; the work has potential to identify transient or structurally supported interactions missed by other approaches; it fits the collaborative, public-data synthesis model."",
      ""weakness"": ""The central biological question is not well defined; emergent properties of protein networks are not explicitly analyzed; the uncharacterized-protein aim is speculative and lacks methodology; criteria for distinguishing direct binary interactions from co-complex proximity are unclear; there is limited discussion of tissue, condition, organism, or cellular-context heterogeneity across datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong resource-building concept for interactome science, but the proposal would be more competitive with a clearer biological hypothesis about emergent protein complex organization and network behavior."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein interaction networks and complexes are emergent cellular systems, but the proposal does not explicitly define or test principles of emergence. It mainly catalogs and validates interactions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic use of public XL-MS data to refine interactome maps is significant and would complement existing AP-MS and co-fractionation resources. The novelty is moderate to high at the synthesis level."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall plan is reasonable, but network analysis methods, confidence scoring, comparison to huMAP, and criteria for new interaction calls are underdefined."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed work is ambitious for fifteen months, especially if it includes dataset curation, reprocessing, structural modeling, network comparison, database integration, and functional inference."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly based on synthesis of publicly available XL-MS and interaction datasets and does not require new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and Complex Portal as relevant resources, but it does not provide enough detail on the specific XL-MS datasets, organism coverage, or how biases in public datasets will be managed."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The planned deposition and integration into public EBI resources are strong. More detail is needed on release of network confidence scores, negative or failed validations, and reproducible analysis notebooks.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior quantitative cell biologist and data-science training leader"",
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal is well aligned with the NCEMS goal of enabling collaborative synthesis projects that exceed the capacity of a single lab. It brings together mass spectrometry expertise, structural biology, public repositories, and database curation. The proposed outputs are likely to be useful to a broad molecular and cellular biology community, particularly if the reprocessed XL-MS datasets are made accessible to users who do not specialize in proteomics search algorithms. I also appreciate that the work could provide trainees with exposure to data curation, reproducible workflows, structural modeling, and biological interpretation. However, the proposal only briefly mentions training and does not provide a concrete plan for how graduate students or postdocs will participate, what skills they will acquire, or whether there will be workshops, hackathons, documentation sprints, or cross-lab mentoring. Since training the next generation of data-savvy scientists is a stated goal of the call, this is a missed opportunity. I also worry that the project is framed as largely dependent on a dedicated data scientist rather than as a broader working group process. The proposal would benefit from clearer milestones for collaborative meetings, division of labor, trainee engagement, and community-facing products. Scientifically, the project is worthwhile, but the NCEMS-specific working group dimensions could be better developed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong multidisciplinary fit involving proteomics, structural biology, informatics, and databases; clear use of public data; likely high value for the community; good rationale for NCEMS support because the work requires coordination among multiple expertise areas; potential to produce reusable workflows and training datasets; integration into established public repositories increases impact."",
      ""weakness"": ""Training plans are vague; working group structure and collaborative mechanisms are insufficiently described; reliance on one data scientist may limit broader capacity building; diversity of participants, geographic breadth, career stages, and institutional levels are not discussed in detail; the proposal lacks explicit plans for workshops, documentation, tutorials, or trainee-led analyses."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A scientifically valuable synthesis project with strong resource potential, but it needs a more explicit working group, training, and community-engagement plan to fully meet the funding call's broader goals."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is relevant to emergent protein complex organization, but the connection is implicit. It does not clearly state how the synthesized data will reveal emergent cellular behaviors or principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A standardized, accessible XL-MS meta-resource integrated with complex annotations would be significant. The project is novel in its synthesis and community-resource aspects rather than in generating a new analytical theory."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed activities are sensible but described at a relatively high level. More detail is needed on reproducible workflow design, validation standards, quality control, and trainee-accessible documentation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is aggressive but possible if tightly scoped. The optional functional annotation aim should be treated as exploratory and not allowed to compromise the main curation and integration goals."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project fully satisfies the synthesis requirement by relying on existing public XL-MS and interaction data and by focusing on standardization, integration, and reuse."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The major public data sources are identified, especially PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and the EBI Complex Portal. A stronger proposal would include a preliminary dataset table and clearer triage process."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to release reprocessed data through PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is strong. The open-science plan should also include training materials, code repositories, workflow containers, and public documentation.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::11,y1,11,Searching the crosslinking mass spectrometry universe for new protein-protein interactions,Accepted,Stephen D. Fried; Yasset Perez-Riverol; Henning Hermjakob,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"The proposal targets an important gap in interactome biology. Existing large-scale interaction maps such as BioPlex and huMAP have transformed our understanding of protein complexes, but many interactions remain indirect, inferred, or lacking structural support. XL-MS is a valuable orthogonal data type because it can provide direct residue-pair evidence for physical proximity in native-like cellular contexts. A systematic synthesis of public XL-MS data could therefore refine interaction networks and potentially identify interactions missed by affinity purification or co-fractionation methods. I am enthusiastic about the idea of comparing XL-MS-derived pairwise contacts with huMAP-like complex predictions and using the results to improve curated resources. However, the proposal does not yet articulate a sufficiently compelling biological question. The title promises discovery of new PPIs, but the plan is largely resource harmonization and validation of predicted complexes. Those are valuable, but the call asks for compelling scientific questions related to emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences. The proposal could be strengthened by asking, for example, whether XL-MS preferentially reveals transient interfaces, condition-specific assemblies, hub scaffolds, or network modules not recovered by other interactomics methods. The optional aim on uncharacterized proteins is potentially interesting but underdeveloped and appears tacked on. Overall, I see this as a valuable community synthesis project with strong technical rationale, but the biological discovery framework needs sharpening.","The proposal addresses a clear limitation of current interactome maps; XL-MS provides orthogonal residue-level support for PPIs; integration with huMAP and Complex Portal could materially improve community complex annotations; the work has potential to identify transient or structurally supported interactions missed by other approaches; it fits the collaborative, public-data synthesis model.","The central biological question is not well defined; emergent properties of protein networks are not explicitly analyzed; the uncharacterized-protein aim is speculative and lacks methodology; criteria for distinguishing direct binary interactions from co-complex proximity are unclear; there is limited discussion of tissue, condition, organism, or cellular-context heterogeneity across datasets.",3,"A strong resource-building concept for interactome science, but the proposal would be more competitive with a clearer biological hypothesis about emergent protein complex organization and network behavior.",3,"Protein interaction networks and complexes are emergent cellular systems, but the proposal does not explicitly define or test principles of emergence. It mainly catalogs and validates interactions.",4,The systematic use of public XL-MS data to refine interactome maps is significant and would complement existing AP-MS and co-fractionation resources. The novelty is moderate to high at the synthesis level.,3,"The overall plan is reasonable, but network analysis methods, confidence scoring, comparison to huMAP, and criteria for new interaction calls are underdefined.",3,"The proposed work is ambitious for fifteen months, especially if it includes dataset curation, reprocessing, structural modeling, network comparison, database integration, and functional inference.",5,The project is clearly based on synthesis of publicly available XL-MS and interaction datasets and does not require new experimental data.,3,"The proposal identifies PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and Complex Portal as relevant resources, but it does not provide enough detail on the specific XL-MS datasets, organism coverage, or how biases in public datasets will be managed.",4,"The planned deposition and integration into public EBI resources are strong. More detail is needed on release of network confidence scores, negative or failed validations, and reproducible analysis notebooks.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:20:08.950730,0,success,,"The proposal outlines a valuable public-data synthesis project that aims to integrate cross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) data to refine large-scale protein interaction maps. While the technical rationale is strong and offers a useful orthogonal approach for structural validation, the proposal lacks a compelling, well-defined biological question aligned with the funding call. Enhancing the focus on specific biological discoveries, rather than solely on resource harmonization, would significantly improve the submission.","The project addresses a significant limitation in current interactome maps by utilizing XL-MS data to provide orthogonal, residue-level support for protein-protein interactions. Integrating this data with existing resources offers substantial value for improving community annotations and discovering transient interactions missed by other approaches.","The central biological question regarding emergent network properties is poorly defined, and the optional aim concerning uncharacterized proteins lacks methodological detail. Additionally, the proposal does not clearly distinguish between direct binary interactions and co-complex proximity, nor does it adequately address dataset heterogeneity across varying cellular contexts."
baseline::y1::11::gpt-5.5::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,5,baseline::y1::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:45:59.346186,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior investigator in crosslinking mass spectrometry and structural proteomics"",
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal addresses a highly timely and real bottleneck in the crosslinking mass spectrometry field: substantial amounts of XL-MS data have been deposited in PRIDE, but differences in search engines, FDR strategies, crosslinker chemistries, database construction, metadata quality, and reporting formats severely limit reuse. The plan to reannotate and reprocess large-scale XL-MS datasets using contemporary algorithms and standardized formats is directly responsive to the call's emphasis on synthesis of public data. The proposed integration of residue-level crosslinks with protein complex resources could substantially improve the evidentiary basis for known and predicted protein-protein interactions. I view the project as scientifically significant and community enabling. The principal concern is that the proposal underestimates the technical heterogeneity of legacy XL-MS datasets. Reprocessing data across studies requires detailed knowledge of sample preparation, crosslinker chemistry, fragmentation method, enrichment strategy, search space, isotope labeling, and instrument settings. The proposal names Scout but does not sufficiently justify why one search pipeline will be appropriate across diverse datasets or how inter-study FDR and comparability will be handled. The target of roughly one hundred large-scale datasets in six months is ambitious, especially if manual metadata curation is required. Nonetheless, the team appears unusually well positioned because it combines XL-MS expertise with PRIDE and Complex Portal infrastructure. With clearer triage criteria, a more explicit quality-control plan, and a realistic prioritization strategy, this could become an important reference resource for the field."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a genuine and important data-reuse problem in XL-MS; it relies entirely on public data; it would create standardized reprocessed datasets with direct community value; integration with PRIDE and the EBI Complex Portal provides a credible route to dissemination; the residue-level nature of XL-MS gives the project scientific value beyond generic PPI cataloging; the work requires expertise from multiple groups and is unlikely to be accomplished by a single laboratory."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal provides insufficient detail on crosslink-specific reprocessing parameters, FDR control, treatment of different crosslinker chemistries, and comparability across datasets; the number of datasets proposed for reanalysis may be unrealistic; criteria for what constitutes a validated new interaction are not fully specified; the plan for AlphaFold-based validation may be oversimplified for transient, flexible, or context-dependent complexes; trainee training plans are only implicit."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and highly relevant synthesis proposal that could substantially improve the usability of public XL-MS data, though it needs a more rigorous and realistic implementation plan for heterogeneous legacy datasets."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein complexes and interaction networks are central examples of emergent molecular organization, and XL-MS offers residue-level evidence for how proteins assemble into higher-order systems. The proposal could illuminate emergent organization of interactomes, although the framing is more resource- and interaction-discovery oriented than explicitly focused on emergence principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The individual components are not entirely new, but the large-scale synthesis of public XL-MS data, reprocessing with modern methods, and integration into complex databases is novel and significant for structural proteomics."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The broad workflow is sound, but the proposal lacks sufficient detail on quality control, search parameter harmonization, FDR estimation across datasets, treatment of ambiguous crosslinks, and validation thresholds for database deposition."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The fifteen-month timeline is plausible for a prioritized pilot but optimistic for reprocessing and manually curating approximately one hundred heterogeneous large-scale datasets plus structural modeling and database integration."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is fundamentally a synthesis effort using existing public XL-MS data and integrating it with public complex and structural prediction resources. It does not propose new experimental data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""PRIDE is clearly identified as the primary source, and the proposal recognizes that many nominal XL-MS datasets will require filtering. However, the exact dataset list, inclusion criteria, and metadata requirements need further specification."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to redeposit standardized datasets to PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is a strong open-science commitment. More explicit statements about public workflows, code, containers, and versioning would strengthen this aspect.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior computational structural biologist specializing in integrative modeling and protein complex prediction"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal is attractive because XL-MS data provide precisely the type of spatial restraint that can bridge interactomics and structural biology. The ambition to use public XL-MS data to validate predicted complexes and improve evidence levels in the Complex Portal is well aligned with the needs of the field. However, I am less convinced by the proposed structural validation strategy as written. The proposal appears to assume that crosslinks can be compared relatively directly to AlphaFold-generated structures, including AlphaFold3 predictions, to determine whether a PPI is supported. This can work in favorable cases, but in practice crosslink interpretation requires careful consideration of linker length distributions, side-chain flexibility, peptide-level ambiguity, protein isoforms, post-translational states, oligomeric stoichiometry, conformational ensembles, membrane environments, and indirect co-complex proximity. AlphaFold models are also not experimental truth; docking confidence, interface confidence, and multimer prediction reliability vary widely. A crosslink that violates a model may indicate a wrong model, a different conformational state, an alternative assembly, or a false positive. Conversely, a model satisfying a crosslink does not by itself establish a physiological complex. The proposal would be stronger if it framed AlphaFold as one component in an integrative scoring system rather than as a main validation criterion. Despite this concern, the synthesis objective is valuable, and the team includes relevant expertise and infrastructure. I would support the project if the milestones emphasize creation of a rigorously annotated XL-MS restraint resource and only secondarily the inference of new complexes."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal exploits the unique value of XL-MS as residue-level spatial evidence; integration with predicted structures and complex databases is potentially powerful; the project could generate restraints useful for integrative modeling across many systems; it uses existing data and requires a transdisciplinary team; the focus on improving evidence for human and yeast complexes is scientifically meaningful."",
      ""weakness"": ""The structural validation strategy is underdeveloped; AlphaFold-based confirmation may be overinterpreted; there is little discussion of conformational heterogeneity, stoichiometry, isoforms, ambiguous peptide mapping, or crosslink distance distributions; criteria for accepting or rejecting interactions are not described quantitatively; computational cost estimates and prioritization of AlphaFold runs are not sufficiently justified."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A worthwhile synthesis project with high potential resource value, but the proposed use of structural prediction to validate PPIs needs a more nuanced and rigorous integrative modeling framework."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The assembly of proteins into complexes and networks is directly relevant to emergent molecular function. The proposal could reveal higher-order organization from residue-level interaction data, although it does not deeply articulate mechanistic emergence questions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining public XL-MS datasets with structure prediction and curated complex resources is significant and relatively novel as a community-scale synthesis activity. The potential impact is high if validation is done carefully."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational structural biology component lacks necessary detail. The proposal does not adequately define model confidence thresholds, distance restraint interpretation, treatment of flexible/disordered regions, or how conflicting evidence will be represented."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed timeline may be feasible for resource assembly but is tight for large-scale reprocessing, structural modeling, validation, curation, and biological interpretation. The optional uncharacterized-protein aim should be deprioritized."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is entirely centered on reusing and integrating existing public data, including PRIDE datasets, huMAP-like predictions, AlphaFold models or predictions, and Complex Portal records."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE as the data source and mentions selection criteria such as proteome-wide design and cleavable crosslinkers, but it lacks a concrete inventory of datasets, expected organisms, metadata fields, and exclusion rules."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Deposition into PRIDE and the Complex Portal is strong. The proposal would benefit from clearer plans for releasing modeling scripts, scoring criteria, failed or ambiguous cases, and provenance metadata.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior bioinformatics infrastructure and biological database scientist"",
      ""review_text"": ""From a data-infrastructure perspective, this proposal is highly responsive to the call. The central problem is not a lack of XL-MS data but lack of harmonization, metadata completeness, standardized processed outputs, and integration with authoritative resources. The proposed collaboration with EBI, PRIDE, and the Complex Portal is a major strength because it provides a realistic path for community adoption. I particularly appreciate the plan to link reprocessed submissions to original accessions, redeposit standardized outputs, and make the results available to non-specialists. These are exactly the kinds of synthesis activities that NCEMS is designed to support. However, the proposal is thin on details that determine long-term data value: which standards will be used, how metadata will be represented, how provenance will be tracked, whether the pipeline will be containerized, how versioning will be handled, and how updates to protein sequence databases, AlphaFold models, huMAP predictions, and ontology terms will be managed. There is also a risk that the project becomes a one-time reanalysis rather than a sustainable resource. The proposal should include governance for curation decisions, community feedback mechanisms, and explicit alignment with existing proteomics standards such as mzIdentML/mzTab variants or crosslink-specific reporting schemas. Overall, I find this to be a strong proposal with a clear need for collaborative infrastructure, but the data stewardship plan should be made more explicit."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent fit to public-data synthesis; strong connection to PRIDE and EBI Complex Portal; clear community need for standardized XL-MS metadata and processed results; likely to benefit both specialists and non-specialists; the work is beyond a single laboratory because it requires database engineering, proteomics expertise, and biological curation; the outcomes are concrete and disseminable."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal does not specify metadata schemas, controlled vocabularies, provenance models, software release plans, persistent identifiers, versioning, or long-term maintenance; no clear community governance or user-feedback plan is described; the training component is not well developed; sustainability beyond the fifteen-month project is uncertain."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling data-synthesis and infrastructure proposal with strong institutional positioning, but it requires a more mature data-management and sustainability plan."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal supports future studies of emergent protein complex organization, but the immediate deliverable is primarily a harmonized data resource rather than a sharply defined emergent-phenomena analysis."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The harmonization of XL-MS datasets and their integration into major community databases would be significant and underexplored. The novelty lies more in systematic synthesis and resource creation than in a new biological hypothesis."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The plan is credible at a high level, but insufficient technical detail is given on metadata standards, workflow reproducibility, provenance, and database update procedures."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is ambitious for curation, reanalysis, database deposition, and portal integration. A staged plan with a smaller high-quality first release would be more convincing."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is an exemplary synthesis-focused proposal: it uses existing data, standardizes it, reprocesses it, integrates it with other public resources, and creates reusable community outputs."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal clearly identifies PRIDE as the major source and discusses the need to triage approximately one thousand crosslinking-related datasets. It should, however, provide a preliminary accession list or at least a more formal dataset discovery protocol."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to redeposit standardized datasets in PRIDE and integrate evidence into the EBI Complex Portal is very strong. If accompanied by open workflows and transparent provenance, this would represent an excellent open-science contribution.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior systems biologist and interactome mapping expert"",
      ""review_text"": ""The proposal targets an important gap in interactome biology. Existing large-scale interaction maps such as BioPlex and huMAP have transformed our understanding of protein complexes, but many interactions remain indirect, inferred, or lacking structural support. XL-MS is a valuable orthogonal data type because it can provide direct residue-pair evidence for physical proximity in native-like cellular contexts. A systematic synthesis of public XL-MS data could therefore refine interaction networks and potentially identify interactions missed by affinity purification or co-fractionation methods. I am enthusiastic about the idea of comparing XL-MS-derived pairwise contacts with huMAP-like complex predictions and using the results to improve curated resources. However, the proposal does not yet articulate a sufficiently compelling biological question. The title promises discovery of new PPIs, but the plan is largely resource harmonization and validation of predicted complexes. Those are valuable, but the call asks for compelling scientific questions related to emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences. The proposal could be strengthened by asking, for example, whether XL-MS preferentially reveals transient interfaces, condition-specific assemblies, hub scaffolds, or network modules not recovered by other interactomics methods. The optional aim on uncharacterized proteins is potentially interesting but underdeveloped and appears tacked on. Overall, I see this as a valuable community synthesis project with strong technical rationale, but the biological discovery framework needs sharpening."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a clear limitation of current interactome maps; XL-MS provides orthogonal residue-level support for PPIs; integration with huMAP and Complex Portal could materially improve community complex annotations; the work has potential to identify transient or structurally supported interactions missed by other approaches; it fits the collaborative, public-data synthesis model."",
      ""weakness"": ""The central biological question is not well defined; emergent properties of protein networks are not explicitly analyzed; the uncharacterized-protein aim is speculative and lacks methodology; criteria for distinguishing direct binary interactions from co-complex proximity are unclear; there is limited discussion of tissue, condition, organism, or cellular-context heterogeneity across datasets."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong resource-building concept for interactome science, but the proposal would be more competitive with a clearer biological hypothesis about emergent protein complex organization and network behavior."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein interaction networks and complexes are emergent cellular systems, but the proposal does not explicitly define or test principles of emergence. It mainly catalogs and validates interactions."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic use of public XL-MS data to refine interactome maps is significant and would complement existing AP-MS and co-fractionation resources. The novelty is moderate to high at the synthesis level."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall plan is reasonable, but network analysis methods, confidence scoring, comparison to huMAP, and criteria for new interaction calls are underdefined."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed work is ambitious for fifteen months, especially if it includes dataset curation, reprocessing, structural modeling, network comparison, database integration, and functional inference."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly based on synthesis of publicly available XL-MS and interaction datasets and does not require new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and Complex Portal as relevant resources, but it does not provide enough detail on the specific XL-MS datasets, organism coverage, or how biases in public datasets will be managed."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The planned deposition and integration into public EBI resources are strong. More detail is needed on release of network confidence scores, negative or failed validations, and reproducible analysis notebooks.""
    },
    {
      ""reviewer"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior quantitative cell biologist and data-science training leader"",
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal is well aligned with the NCEMS goal of enabling collaborative synthesis projects that exceed the capacity of a single lab. It brings together mass spectrometry expertise, structural biology, public repositories, and database curation. The proposed outputs are likely to be useful to a broad molecular and cellular biology community, particularly if the reprocessed XL-MS datasets are made accessible to users who do not specialize in proteomics search algorithms. I also appreciate that the work could provide trainees with exposure to data curation, reproducible workflows, structural modeling, and biological interpretation. However, the proposal only briefly mentions training and does not provide a concrete plan for how graduate students or postdocs will participate, what skills they will acquire, or whether there will be workshops, hackathons, documentation sprints, or cross-lab mentoring. Since training the next generation of data-savvy scientists is a stated goal of the call, this is a missed opportunity. I also worry that the project is framed as largely dependent on a dedicated data scientist rather than as a broader working group process. The proposal would benefit from clearer milestones for collaborative meetings, division of labor, trainee engagement, and community-facing products. Scientifically, the project is worthwhile, but the NCEMS-specific working group dimensions could be better developed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong multidisciplinary fit involving proteomics, structural biology, informatics, and databases; clear use of public data; likely high value for the community; good rationale for NCEMS support because the work requires coordination among multiple expertise areas; potential to produce reusable workflows and training datasets; integration into established public repositories increases impact."",
      ""weakness"": ""Training plans are vague; working group structure and collaborative mechanisms are insufficiently described; reliance on one data scientist may limit broader capacity building; diversity of participants, geographic breadth, career stages, and institutional levels are not discussed in detail; the proposal lacks explicit plans for workshops, documentation, tutorials, or trainee-led analyses."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A scientifically valuable synthesis project with strong resource potential, but it needs a more explicit working group, training, and community-engagement plan to fully meet the funding call's broader goals."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is relevant to emergent protein complex organization, but the connection is implicit. It does not clearly state how the synthesized data will reveal emergent cellular behaviors or principles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A standardized, accessible XL-MS meta-resource integrated with complex annotations would be significant. The project is novel in its synthesis and community-resource aspects rather than in generating a new analytical theory."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed activities are sensible but described at a relatively high level. More detail is needed on reproducible workflow design, validation standards, quality control, and trainee-accessible documentation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is aggressive but possible if tightly scoped. The optional functional annotation aim should be treated as exploratory and not allowed to compromise the main curation and integration goals."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project fully satisfies the synthesis requirement by relying on existing public XL-MS and interaction data and by focusing on standardization, integration, and reuse."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The major public data sources are identified, especially PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and the EBI Complex Portal. A stronger proposal would include a preliminary dataset table and clearer triage process."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The plan to release reprocessed data through PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is strong. The open-science plan should also include training materials, code repositories, workflow containers, and public documentation.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::11,y1,11,Searching the crosslinking mass spectrometry universe for new protein-protein interactions,Accepted,Stephen D. Fried; Yasset Perez-Riverol; Henning Hermjakob,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"This proposal is well aligned with the NCEMS goal of enabling collaborative synthesis projects that exceed the capacity of a single lab. It brings together mass spectrometry expertise, structural biology, public repositories, and database curation. The proposed outputs are likely to be useful to a broad molecular and cellular biology community, particularly if the reprocessed XL-MS datasets are made accessible to users who do not specialize in proteomics search algorithms. I also appreciate that the work could provide trainees with exposure to data curation, reproducible workflows, structural modeling, and biological interpretation. However, the proposal only briefly mentions training and does not provide a concrete plan for how graduate students or postdocs will participate, what skills they will acquire, or whether there will be workshops, hackathons, documentation sprints, or cross-lab mentoring. Since training the next generation of data-savvy scientists is a stated goal of the call, this is a missed opportunity. I also worry that the project is framed as largely dependent on a dedicated data scientist rather than as a broader working group process. The proposal would benefit from clearer milestones for collaborative meetings, division of labor, trainee engagement, and community-facing products. Scientifically, the project is worthwhile, but the NCEMS-specific working group dimensions could be better developed.","Strong multidisciplinary fit involving proteomics, structural biology, informatics, and databases; clear use of public data; likely high value for the community; good rationale for NCEMS support because the work requires coordination among multiple expertise areas; potential to produce reusable workflows and training datasets; integration into established public repositories increases impact.","Training plans are vague; working group structure and collaborative mechanisms are insufficiently described; reliance on one data scientist may limit broader capacity building; diversity of participants, geographic breadth, career stages, and institutional levels are not discussed in detail; the proposal lacks explicit plans for workshops, documentation, tutorials, or trainee-led analyses.",3,"A scientifically valuable synthesis project with strong resource potential, but it needs a more explicit working group, training, and community-engagement plan to fully meet the funding call's broader goals.",3,"The proposal is relevant to emergent protein complex organization, but the connection is implicit. It does not clearly state how the synthesized data will reveal emergent cellular behaviors or principles.",4,"A standardized, accessible XL-MS meta-resource integrated with complex annotations would be significant. The project is novel in its synthesis and community-resource aspects rather than in generating a new analytical theory.",3,"The proposed activities are sensible but described at a relatively high level. More detail is needed on reproducible workflow design, validation standards, quality control, and trainee-accessible documentation.",3,The timeline is aggressive but possible if tightly scoped. The optional functional annotation aim should be treated as exploratory and not allowed to compromise the main curation and integration goals.,5,"The project fully satisfies the synthesis requirement by relying on existing public XL-MS and interaction data and by focusing on standardization, integration, and reuse.",4,"The major public data sources are identified, especially PRIDE, huMAP, AlphaFold, and the EBI Complex Portal. A stronger proposal would include a preliminary dataset table and clearer triage process.",4,"The plan to release reprocessed data through PRIDE and integrate results into the Complex Portal is strong. The open-science plan should also include training materials, code repositories, workflow containers, and public documentation.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:20:18.934727,0,success,,"This proposal aligns well with NCEMS objectives by proposing a multidisciplinary synthesis project that integrates mass spectrometry, structural biology, and database curation. The scientific objectives are valuable and have the potential to produce broadly accessible outputs for the molecular and cellular biology communities. However, the proposal lacks concrete plans for working group logistics, division of labor, and trainee engagement, appearing overly reliant on a single data scientist. Developing these collaborative and educational dimensions would significantly strengthen the overall project.","The project demonstrates a strong multidisciplinary fit that successfully integrates public data, proteomics, structural biology, and informatics. The anticipated outputs, including reusable workflows and integrated public repositories, offer high value and accessibility to the broader scientific community. Furthermore, the proposal provides a compelling rationale for NCEMS support by highlighting the necessity of coordinated, multi-domain expertise.","The proposal lacks a detailed training plan, providing insufficient information on trainee engagement, educational workshops, and tutorials. Additionally, the working group structure and collaboration mechanisms are poorly defined, with an over-reliance on a single data scientist rather than a distributed team effort. There is also limited discussion regarding the diversity of the participants across geographic regions, career stages, and institution types."
baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,1,baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:51:56.823435,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal addresses a genuine and well-known pain point in the proteomics community: the poor state of metadata in public repositories like PRIDE. As a computational proteomics researcher, I appreciate the technical grounding—use of SDRF-Proteomics, Param-Medic for instrument parameter extraction, open modification searching for PTM/enzyme inference, and Nextflow for pipeline orchestration are all sensible, established choices. The team clearly has deep operational knowledge of PRIDE and the proteomics data ecosystem. However, I have concerns about how well this fits the RFP's core mandate. This is fundamentally a tool/infrastructure development project rather than a synthesis project aimed at answering a scientific question about emergent phenomena in molecular/cellular biology. The 'science' here is about data curation methodology, not about biological insight into emergence. The downstream applications (AI training, reanalysis) are mentioned but not concretely pursued within the proposal itself."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong technical team with clear expertise in proteomics data standards and repository management; realistic, staged technical plan; strong existing infrastructure (PRIDE, lesSDRF, ProteomicsML) to build upon; concrete deliverables with clear ownership."",
      ""weakness"": ""Does not directly address a molecular/cellular emergent phenomenon; the scientific question being answered is more of an engineering/informatics challenge than a biological puzzle; benchmarking relies on a private test set whose curation methodology is underspecified; ambitious scope (raw data extraction + NLP + ontology harmonization + community challenge) within two years."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Technically solid infrastructure proposal but weak alignment with the RFP's emphasis on synthesizing data to elucidate emergent biological phenomena."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is about metadata infrastructure that could someday enable studies of emergent phenomena, but it does not itself investigate any emergent biological process; the connection is indirect and speculative."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Automated metadata harmonization using LLMs is a timely and useful innovation, but similar efforts (e.g., MetaSRA, other FAIRification pipelines) exist in adjacent omics fields, limiting novelty."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The technical pipeline is well thought out, leveraging established tools and a staged validation strategy against gold-standard and independent test sets."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is aggressive given the breadth of activities (raw data extraction, NLP pipeline, community challenge, retrospective curation, and API development)."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project synthesizes metadata across many existing datasets, which fits the 'no new data generation' criterion, but is more of a data engineering synthesis than a scientific insight synthesis."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Data sources (PRIDE datasets, associated publications) are clearly identified and appropriate for the proposed methodology."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong plan to release code, tutorials, and preprints, and to integrate results into open community tools like PRIDE and ProteomicsML.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From an NLP and machine learning standpoint, this proposal has an interesting core: extracting structured biological metadata from unstructured scientific text using LLMs, combined with raw-data-derived technical metadata. The idea of ensemble cross-checking among LLMs and self-verification to reduce hallucination is a reasonable mitigation strategy, though details on how disagreement will be resolved, or how false negatives (missing metadata) will be handled are sparse. The community ML challenge is a nice mechanism to crowdsource innovation and is well-aligned with the 'diverse new talent' goal of the RFP. That said, the proposal underspecifies the NLP methodology: what LLM architectures will be fine-tuned, how much annotated corpus is available for fine-tuning, and how domain-specific hallucination will be evaluated beyond precision on a private test set. There's also no discussion of computational cost-efficiency trade-offs of running ensemble LLMs at scale across the entire PRIDE repository."",
      ""strengths"": ""Innovative combination of raw-file-derived and literature-derived metadata; community challenge to crowdsource algorithmic innovation; explicit self-verification/ensemble strategy to increase reliability of LLM-extracted metadata; strong training and outreach components via ProteomicsML."",
      ""weakness"": ""Insufficient methodological detail on model architecture, fine-tuning corpus size/composition, and evaluation metrics beyond precision; ensemble LLM approach may be computationally expensive at PRIDE's scale (requested GPU hours seem modest for full-repository retrospective curation); risk of NLP errors propagating unnoticed into repository metadata without human-in-the-loop verification described in detail."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A methodologically interesting but underdeveloped NLP/ML plan; strong on vision, less so on technical specificity and evaluation rigor."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal's aims center on data infrastructure rather than uncovering or explaining any emergent molecular/cellular phenomenon."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining raw MS metadata extraction with LLM-based literature mining is a reasonably novel integration, though each component individually is not groundbreaking."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The general pipeline is described, but key technical details (LLM choice, fine-tuning data provenance, quantitative evaluation metrics beyond precision) are missing."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Combining model development, benchmarking, a community challenge, and full deployment within PRIDE in two years is ambitious for the level of technical detail provided."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project synthesizes metadata across many public datasets and publications, consistent with the synthesis mandate, though the 'insight' generated is procedural rather than scientific."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
        ""data_identification_justification"": ""Public proteomics datasets and their associated open-access publications are clearly and appropriately identified as the data sources."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Commitment to releasing workflows, models, and challenge outputs as open-source, plus educational materials, aligns well with open science principles.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a systems/molecular biologist interested in emergent phenomena—how molecular interactions give rise to higher-order cellular behavior—I find this proposal only tangentially related to the funding call's central theme. The work is valuable for the proteomics community as an enabling technology, but it does not pose or attempt to answer any specific biological question about emergence. There is no hypothesis about protein network dynamics, cellular states, or any emergent property being investigated through data synthesis; rather, the 'synthesis' here is of metadata records, not of biological insight. I would have liked to see, even as a downstream case study, an application of the enhanced metadata infrastructure to answer a concrete biological question (e.g., cross-study meta-analysis of proteostasis or stress-response networks) to ground the proposal in the call's scientific aims."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addresses a widely acknowledged bottleneck (metadata quality) that indirectly benefits nearly all downstream proteomics-based biological research; strong practical utility and community demand; team has direct connection to the PRIDE repository ensuring real-world impact."",
      ""weakness"": ""No concrete biological question or emergent phenomenon is addressed; the proposal reads as an infrastructure/tool grant rather than a hypothesis-driven synthesis study; lacks any case study demonstrating how improved metadata will translate into new biological insight."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Valuable infrastructure work, but a poor fit for an RFP focused on scientific questions about emergent phenomena; the proposal would be better suited to a data resource/repository grant mechanism."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 1,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal does not investigate any specific emergent molecular or cellular phenomenon; it is purely about metadata infrastructure."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Significant for data management practice in proteomics, but not scientifically novel in terms of biological discovery."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The technical plan for metadata extraction and validation is rigorous and grounded in established informatics practices."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Reasonable scope for an infrastructure project, though ambitious multi-workflow integration within two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""While it synthesizes metadata across datasets, it does not synthesize data to generate new biological knowledge as intended by the call."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Uses well-defined, existing public proteomics data and literature sources appropriately."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong, explicit commitment to open-source code, preprints, and educational resources.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a data science/FAIR-data infrastructure specialist, I see substantial merit in this proposal's ambition to systematically improve metadata quality at scale—an issue that plagues not just proteomics but omics science broadly. The plan to build a scalable template extending beyond proteomics to metabolomics is a strong long-term vision that fits the working group model well: it clearly requires cross-disciplinary expertise (bioinformatics, NLP, repository science, proteomics domain knowledge) beyond what any single lab could offer. The requested NCEMS resources (staff scientists, CyVerse compute, GPU hours) are reasonably scoped and justified relative to the scale of PRIDE. My main critique is that the proposal doesn't articulate concrete success metrics for 'reusability' and 'discoverability' beyond metadata extraction precision—e.g., will they measure downstream reuse of enhanced datasets, user engagement with new query features, or actual uptake by AI training pipelines?"",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear vision for scalable, cross-omics metadata infrastructure; strong justification for working-group-level collaboration involving PRIDE curators, ML/NLP experts, and proteomics scientists; reasonable and specific resource requests; multi-pronged validation strategy using gold-standard and independent test sets."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lacks concrete downstream metrics of success (e.g., actual reuse rates, dataset discoverability improvements); insufficient discussion of how metadata quality control will be sustained after the two-year funding period ends; limited discussion of ethical/privacy issues around mining unpublished or restricted-access text corpora for NLP training."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A well-conceived and operationally strong infrastructure proposal with clear collaborative rationale, though it stretches the RFP's scientific-question mandate and lacks long-term sustainability planning."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is oriented toward data infrastructure rather than any specific biological emergent phenomenon, though improved metadata could indirectly enable future emergence-related studies."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The integration of raw-data-derived and literature-derived metadata extraction using LLM ensembles, plus a scalable cross-omics template, represents a genuinely novel and significant contribution to FAIR data practices."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Methodology is grounded in established standards (SDRF, controlled vocabularies) with a staged validation plan using both public gold-standard and private test sets."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope (raw data + NLP + community challenge + retrospective curation + querying tools) is broad for two years, risking incomplete delivery of some components."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Strongly synthesizes existing public datasets and literature at scale, consistent with the RFP's requirement to use only existing data without new experiments."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Data sources (PRIDE datasets, open-access literature, gold-standard SDRF sets) are precisely and comprehensively identified."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Exemplary commitment to open-source code release, preprints, community challenge outputs, and educational tutorials via ProteomicsML.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a career-stage/team-science evaluator familiar with NCEMS's working group model, I evaluate this proposal favorably on collaborative structure and training opportunities, though with reservations about scientific framing. The team composition—spanning bioinformaticians, ML/NLP experts, proteomics scientists, and repository curators—matches the RFP's call for diverse expertise and institutional roles. The community ML challenge is an excellent mechanism to tap new talent, including early-career researchers and non-traditional contributors, and aligns with the 'diverse new talent' and 'train the future workforce' goals explicitly. However, the proposal does not explicitly describe how graduate students and postdocs will be embedded in the working group's day-to-day activities, mentorship structure, or authorship opportunities—these are just implied rather than detailed. Additionally, while the project requires cross-lab collaboration, one could argue that some components (e.g., literature-mining NLP pipeline) could plausibly be handled by a single well-resourced bioinformatics core, weakening the claim that this absolutely requires multi-lab, multi-institution collaboration beyond existing capacity."",
      ""strengths"": ""Diverse, complementary team spanning multiple domains and institutions (PRIDE curators, proteomics scientists, ML/NLP specialists); community challenge is an effective vehicle for engaging new and diverse talent; clear plans for open-source dissemination and educational tutorials that support training goals."",
      ""weakness"": ""Training plan for graduate students/postdocs is not explicitly detailed (mentorship, roles, authorship); the necessity of a large-scale, multi-institution working group for some proposed components (e.g., NLP pipeline development) is not fully justified—could arguably be executed by a smaller, well-funded core team; limited discussion of career-stage diversity or under-represented researcher inclusion strategies beyond the open challenge format."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Solid collaborative team structure that fits the working-group model, but insufficiently detailed training plan and only partial justification for requiring this scale of multi-lab collaboration."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project centers on data infrastructure rather than investigating emergent cellular or molecular phenomena directly."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Novel in its integrative approach to metadata extraction, though primarily impactful for data management rather than new biological discovery."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The stepwise validation approach (gold-standard set, independent test set, precision-focused scoring) reflects careful methodological planning."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Two years is tight for developing, validating, and deploying multiple interlocking systems (raw-data extraction, NLP, challenge, PRIDE integration)."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes existing datasets and literature at scale, but the output is metadata infrastructure rather than new scientific insight, a partial fit to the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Clear and appropriate identification of PRIDE datasets and associated literature as data sources for the project."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to open-source tools, preprints, and community-facing educational resources, though sustainability of open resources post-funding is unaddressed.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::12,y1,12,Intelligent metadata compilation to enhance the reusability and discoverability of mass spectrometry-based proteomics data,Accepted,Wout Bittremieux; Iddo Friedberg,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"This proposal addresses a genuine and well-known pain point in the proteomics community: the poor state of metadata in public repositories like PRIDE. As a computational proteomics researcher, I appreciate the technical grounding—use of SDRF-Proteomics, Param-Medic for instrument parameter extraction, open modification searching for PTM/enzyme inference, and Nextflow for pipeline orchestration are all sensible, established choices. The team clearly has deep operational knowledge of PRIDE and the proteomics data ecosystem. However, I have concerns about how well this fits the RFP's core mandate. This is fundamentally a tool/infrastructure development project rather than a synthesis project aimed at answering a scientific question about emergent phenomena in molecular/cellular biology. The 'science' here is about data curation methodology, not about biological insight into emergence. The downstream applications (AI training, reanalysis) are mentioned but not concretely pursued within the proposal itself.","Strong technical team with clear expertise in proteomics data standards and repository management; realistic, staged technical plan; strong existing infrastructure (PRIDE, lesSDRF, ProteomicsML) to build upon; concrete deliverables with clear ownership.",Does not directly address a molecular/cellular emergent phenomenon; the scientific question being answered is more of an engineering/informatics challenge than a biological puzzle; benchmarking relies on a private test set whose curation methodology is underspecified; ambitious scope (raw data extraction + NLP + ontology harmonization + community challenge) within two years.,3,Technically solid infrastructure proposal but weak alignment with the RFP's emphasis on synthesizing data to elucidate emergent biological phenomena.,2,"The proposal is about metadata infrastructure that could someday enable studies of emergent phenomena, but it does not itself investigate any emergent biological process; the connection is indirect and speculative.",3,"Automated metadata harmonization using LLMs is a timely and useful innovation, but similar efforts (e.g., MetaSRA, other FAIRification pipelines) exist in adjacent omics fields, limiting novelty.",4,"The technical pipeline is well thought out, leveraging established tools and a staged validation strategy against gold-standard and independent test sets.",3,"The two-year timeline is aggressive given the breadth of activities (raw data extraction, NLP pipeline, community challenge, retrospective curation, and API development).",3,"The project synthesizes metadata across many existing datasets, which fits the 'no new data generation' criterion, but is more of a data engineering synthesis than a scientific insight synthesis.",4,"Data sources (PRIDE datasets, associated publications) are clearly identified and appropriate for the proposed methodology.",4,"Strong plan to release code, tutorials, and preprints, and to integrate results into open community tools like PRIDE and ProteomicsML.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:20:29.323119,0,success,,"This proposal aims to improve metadata quality in public proteomics repositories using established informatics tools and data standards. While the technical plan is robust and the team demonstrates significant expertise, the project aligns more closely with infrastructure development than the funding call's focus on biological synthesis. Consequently, the proposal centers on data curation methodologies rather than investigating emergent phenomena in molecular or cellular biology.","The proposal is supported by a highly qualified team with extensive expertise in proteomics data standards and repository management. It features a realistic, staged technical plan that leverages existing infrastructure to produce concrete deliverables with clear ownership.","The project misaligns with the grant's mandate to study biological emergent phenomena, as it fundamentally addresses an informatics challenge rather than a biological question. Furthermore, the benchmarking relies on an underspecified private test set, and the extensive scope of work appears overly ambitious for a two-year timeline."
baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::002,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,2,baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:51:56.823435,True,,"{
