}",0,y1::12,y1,12,Intelligent metadata compilation to enhance the reusability and discoverability of mass spectrometry-based proteomics data,Accepted,Wout Bittremieux; Iddo Friedberg,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"As a systems/molecular biologist interested in emergent phenomena—how molecular interactions give rise to higher-order cellular behavior—I find this proposal only tangentially related to the funding call's central theme. The work is valuable for the proteomics community as an enabling technology, but it does not pose or attempt to answer any specific biological question about emergence. There is no hypothesis about protein network dynamics, cellular states, or any emergent property being investigated through data synthesis; rather, the 'synthesis' here is of metadata records, not of biological insight. I would have liked to see, even as a downstream case study, an application of the enhanced metadata infrastructure to answer a concrete biological question (e.g., cross-study meta-analysis of proteostasis or stress-response networks) to ground the proposal in the call's scientific aims.",Addresses a widely acknowledged bottleneck (metadata quality) that indirectly benefits nearly all downstream proteomics-based biological research; strong practical utility and community demand; team has direct connection to the PRIDE repository ensuring real-world impact.,No concrete biological question or emergent phenomenon is addressed; the proposal reads as an infrastructure/tool grant rather than a hypothesis-driven synthesis study; lacks any case study demonstrating how improved metadata will translate into new biological insight.,2,"Valuable infrastructure work, but a poor fit for an RFP focused on scientific questions about emergent phenomena; the proposal would be better suited to a data resource/repository grant mechanism.",1,The proposal does not investigate any specific emergent molecular or cellular phenomenon; it is purely about metadata infrastructure.,3,"Significant for data management practice in proteomics, but not scientifically novel in terms of biological discovery.",4,The technical plan for metadata extraction and validation is rigorous and grounded in established informatics practices.,3,"Reasonable scope for an infrastructure project, though ambitious multi-workflow integration within two years.",2,"While it synthesizes metadata across datasets, it does not synthesize data to generate new biological knowledge as intended by the call.",4,"Uses well-defined, existing public proteomics data and literature sources appropriately.",4,"Strong, explicit commitment to open-source code, preprints, and educational resources.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:20:47.819460,0,success,,"This proposal presents a valuable enabling technology for the proteomics community by seeking to improve metadata infrastructure. However, the project does not align strongly with the funding call's core focus on emergent biological phenomena and lacks a hypothesis-driven investigation. The proposal functions primarily as a tool-building project and would benefit significantly from a concrete case study demonstrating the application of these updates to generate actionable biological insights.","The project addresses critical bottlenecks in metadata quality, offering strong practical utility and addressing high community demand. Furthermore, the research team's direct connection to the PRIDE repository ensures the proposed infrastructure will have tangible, real-world impact on downstream proteomics research.","The proposal does not investigate a specific biological question, hypothesis, or emergent phenomenon, rendering it an infrastructure grant rather than a synthesis study. Additionally, it lacks a concrete biological case study to illustrate how the enhanced metadata will translate into novel scientific discoveries."
baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::004,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,4,baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:51:56.823435,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal addresses a genuine and well-known pain point in the proteomics community: the poor state of metadata in public repositories like PRIDE. As a computational proteomics researcher, I appreciate the technical grounding—use of SDRF-Proteomics, Param-Medic for instrument parameter extraction, open modification searching for PTM/enzyme inference, and Nextflow for pipeline orchestration are all sensible, established choices. The team clearly has deep operational knowledge of PRIDE and the proteomics data ecosystem. However, I have concerns about how well this fits the RFP's core mandate. This is fundamentally a tool/infrastructure development project rather than a synthesis project aimed at answering a scientific question about emergent phenomena in molecular/cellular biology. The 'science' here is about data curation methodology, not about biological insight into emergence. The downstream applications (AI training, reanalysis) are mentioned but not concretely pursued within the proposal itself."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong technical team with clear expertise in proteomics data standards and repository management; realistic, staged technical plan; strong existing infrastructure (PRIDE, lesSDRF, ProteomicsML) to build upon; concrete deliverables with clear ownership."",
      ""weakness"": ""Does not directly address a molecular/cellular emergent phenomenon; the scientific question being answered is more of an engineering/informatics challenge than a biological puzzle; benchmarking relies on a private test set whose curation methodology is underspecified; ambitious scope (raw data extraction + NLP + ontology harmonization + community challenge) within two years."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Technically solid infrastructure proposal but weak alignment with the RFP's emphasis on synthesizing data to elucidate emergent biological phenomena."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is about metadata infrastructure that could someday enable studies of emergent phenomena, but it does not itself investigate any emergent biological process; the connection is indirect and speculative."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Automated metadata harmonization using LLMs is a timely and useful innovation, but similar efforts (e.g., MetaSRA, other FAIRification pipelines) exist in adjacent omics fields, limiting novelty."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The technical pipeline is well thought out, leveraging established tools and a staged validation strategy against gold-standard and independent test sets."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is aggressive given the breadth of activities (raw data extraction, NLP pipeline, community challenge, retrospective curation, and API development)."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project synthesizes metadata across many existing datasets, which fits the 'no new data generation' criterion, but is more of a data engineering synthesis than a scientific insight synthesis."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Data sources (PRIDE datasets, associated publications) are clearly identified and appropriate for the proposed methodology."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong plan to release code, tutorials, and preprints, and to integrate results into open community tools like PRIDE and ProteomicsML.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From an NLP and machine learning standpoint, this proposal has an interesting core: extracting structured biological metadata from unstructured scientific text using LLMs, combined with raw-data-derived technical metadata. The idea of ensemble cross-checking among LLMs and self-verification to reduce hallucination is a reasonable mitigation strategy, though details on how disagreement will be resolved, or how false negatives (missing metadata) will be handled are sparse. The community ML challenge is a nice mechanism to crowdsource innovation and is well-aligned with the 'diverse new talent' goal of the RFP. That said, the proposal underspecifies the NLP methodology: what LLM architectures will be fine-tuned, how much annotated corpus is available for fine-tuning, and how domain-specific hallucination will be evaluated beyond precision on a private test set. There's also no discussion of computational cost-efficiency trade-offs of running ensemble LLMs at scale across the entire PRIDE repository."",
      ""strengths"": ""Innovative combination of raw-file-derived and literature-derived metadata; community challenge to crowdsource algorithmic innovation; explicit self-verification/ensemble strategy to increase reliability of LLM-extracted metadata; strong training and outreach components via ProteomicsML."",
      ""weakness"": ""Insufficient methodological detail on model architecture, fine-tuning corpus size/composition, and evaluation metrics beyond precision; ensemble LLM approach may be computationally expensive at PRIDE's scale (requested GPU hours seem modest for full-repository retrospective curation); risk of NLP errors propagating unnoticed into repository metadata without human-in-the-loop verification described in detail."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A methodologically interesting but underdeveloped NLP/ML plan; strong on vision, less so on technical specificity and evaluation rigor."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal's aims center on data infrastructure rather than uncovering or explaining any emergent molecular/cellular phenomenon."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining raw MS metadata extraction with LLM-based literature mining is a reasonably novel integration, though each component individually is not groundbreaking."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The general pipeline is described, but key technical details (LLM choice, fine-tuning data provenance, quantitative evaluation metrics beyond precision) are missing."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Combining model development, benchmarking, a community challenge, and full deployment within PRIDE in two years is ambitious for the level of technical detail provided."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project synthesizes metadata across many public datasets and publications, consistent with the synthesis mandate, though the 'insight' generated is procedural rather than scientific."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
        ""data_identification_justification"": ""Public proteomics datasets and their associated open-access publications are clearly and appropriately identified as the data sources."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Commitment to releasing workflows, models, and challenge outputs as open-source, plus educational materials, aligns well with open science principles.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a systems/molecular biologist interested in emergent phenomena—how molecular interactions give rise to higher-order cellular behavior—I find this proposal only tangentially related to the funding call's central theme. The work is valuable for the proteomics community as an enabling technology, but it does not pose or attempt to answer any specific biological question about emergence. There is no hypothesis about protein network dynamics, cellular states, or any emergent property being investigated through data synthesis; rather, the 'synthesis' here is of metadata records, not of biological insight. I would have liked to see, even as a downstream case study, an application of the enhanced metadata infrastructure to answer a concrete biological question (e.g., cross-study meta-analysis of proteostasis or stress-response networks) to ground the proposal in the call's scientific aims."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addresses a widely acknowledged bottleneck (metadata quality) that indirectly benefits nearly all downstream proteomics-based biological research; strong practical utility and community demand; team has direct connection to the PRIDE repository ensuring real-world impact."",
      ""weakness"": ""No concrete biological question or emergent phenomenon is addressed; the proposal reads as an infrastructure/tool grant rather than a hypothesis-driven synthesis study; lacks any case study demonstrating how improved metadata will translate into new biological insight."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Valuable infrastructure work, but a poor fit for an RFP focused on scientific questions about emergent phenomena; the proposal would be better suited to a data resource/repository grant mechanism."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 1,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal does not investigate any specific emergent molecular or cellular phenomenon; it is purely about metadata infrastructure."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Significant for data management practice in proteomics, but not scientifically novel in terms of biological discovery."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The technical plan for metadata extraction and validation is rigorous and grounded in established informatics practices."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Reasonable scope for an infrastructure project, though ambitious multi-workflow integration within two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""While it synthesizes metadata across datasets, it does not synthesize data to generate new biological knowledge as intended by the call."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Uses well-defined, existing public proteomics data and literature sources appropriately."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong, explicit commitment to open-source code, preprints, and educational resources.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a data science/FAIR-data infrastructure specialist, I see substantial merit in this proposal's ambition to systematically improve metadata quality at scale—an issue that plagues not just proteomics but omics science broadly. The plan to build a scalable template extending beyond proteomics to metabolomics is a strong long-term vision that fits the working group model well: it clearly requires cross-disciplinary expertise (bioinformatics, NLP, repository science, proteomics domain knowledge) beyond what any single lab could offer. The requested NCEMS resources (staff scientists, CyVerse compute, GPU hours) are reasonably scoped and justified relative to the scale of PRIDE. My main critique is that the proposal doesn't articulate concrete success metrics for 'reusability' and 'discoverability' beyond metadata extraction precision—e.g., will they measure downstream reuse of enhanced datasets, user engagement with new query features, or actual uptake by AI training pipelines?"",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear vision for scalable, cross-omics metadata infrastructure; strong justification for working-group-level collaboration involving PRIDE curators, ML/NLP experts, and proteomics scientists; reasonable and specific resource requests; multi-pronged validation strategy using gold-standard and independent test sets."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lacks concrete downstream metrics of success (e.g., actual reuse rates, dataset discoverability improvements); insufficient discussion of how metadata quality control will be sustained after the two-year funding period ends; limited discussion of ethical/privacy issues around mining unpublished or restricted-access text corpora for NLP training."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A well-conceived and operationally strong infrastructure proposal with clear collaborative rationale, though it stretches the RFP's scientific-question mandate and lacks long-term sustainability planning."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal is oriented toward data infrastructure rather than any specific biological emergent phenomenon, though improved metadata could indirectly enable future emergence-related studies."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The integration of raw-data-derived and literature-derived metadata extraction using LLM ensembles, plus a scalable cross-omics template, represents a genuinely novel and significant contribution to FAIR data practices."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Methodology is grounded in established standards (SDRF, controlled vocabularies) with a staged validation plan using both public gold-standard and private test sets."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope (raw data + NLP + community challenge + retrospective curation + querying tools) is broad for two years, risking incomplete delivery of some components."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Strongly synthesizes existing public datasets and literature at scale, consistent with the RFP's requirement to use only existing data without new experiments."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Data sources (PRIDE datasets, open-access literature, gold-standard SDRF sets) are precisely and comprehensively identified."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Exemplary commitment to open-source code release, preprints, community challenge outputs, and educational tutorials via ProteomicsML.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a career-stage/team-science evaluator familiar with NCEMS's working group model, I evaluate this proposal favorably on collaborative structure and training opportunities, though with reservations about scientific framing. The team composition—spanning bioinformaticians, ML/NLP experts, proteomics scientists, and repository curators—matches the RFP's call for diverse expertise and institutional roles. The community ML challenge is an excellent mechanism to tap new talent, including early-career researchers and non-traditional contributors, and aligns with the 'diverse new talent' and 'train the future workforce' goals explicitly. However, the proposal does not explicitly describe how graduate students and postdocs will be embedded in the working group's day-to-day activities, mentorship structure, or authorship opportunities—these are just implied rather than detailed. Additionally, while the project requires cross-lab collaboration, one could argue that some components (e.g., literature-mining NLP pipeline) could plausibly be handled by a single well-resourced bioinformatics core, weakening the claim that this absolutely requires multi-lab, multi-institution collaboration beyond existing capacity."",
      ""strengths"": ""Diverse, complementary team spanning multiple domains and institutions (PRIDE curators, proteomics scientists, ML/NLP specialists); community challenge is an effective vehicle for engaging new and diverse talent; clear plans for open-source dissemination and educational tutorials that support training goals."",
      ""weakness"": ""Training plan for graduate students/postdocs is not explicitly detailed (mentorship, roles, authorship); the necessity of a large-scale, multi-institution working group for some proposed components (e.g., NLP pipeline development) is not fully justified—could arguably be executed by a smaller, well-funded core team; limited discussion of career-stage diversity or under-represented researcher inclusion strategies beyond the open challenge format."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Solid collaborative team structure that fits the working-group model, but insufficiently detailed training plan and only partial justification for requiring this scale of multi-lab collaboration."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project centers on data infrastructure rather than investigating emergent cellular or molecular phenomena directly."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Novel in its integrative approach to metadata extraction, though primarily impactful for data management rather than new biological discovery."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The stepwise validation approach (gold-standard set, independent test set, precision-focused scoring) reflects careful methodological planning."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Two years is tight for developing, validating, and deploying multiple interlocking systems (raw-data extraction, NLP, challenge, PRIDE integration)."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes existing datasets and literature at scale, but the output is metadata infrastructure rather than new scientific insight, a partial fit to the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Clear and appropriate identification of PRIDE datasets and associated literature as data sources for the project."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to open-source tools, preprints, and community-facing educational resources, though sustainability of open resources post-funding is unaddressed.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::12,y1,12,Intelligent metadata compilation to enhance the reusability and discoverability of mass spectrometry-based proteomics data,Accepted,Wout Bittremieux; Iddo Friedberg,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"As a data science/FAIR-data infrastructure specialist, I see substantial merit in this proposal's ambition to systematically improve metadata quality at scale—an issue that plagues not just proteomics but omics science broadly. The plan to build a scalable template extending beyond proteomics to metabolomics is a strong long-term vision that fits the working group model well: it clearly requires cross-disciplinary expertise (bioinformatics, NLP, repository science, proteomics domain knowledge) beyond what any single lab could offer. The requested NCEMS resources (staff scientists, CyVerse compute, GPU hours) are reasonably scoped and justified relative to the scale of PRIDE. My main critique is that the proposal doesn't articulate concrete success metrics for 'reusability' and 'discoverability' beyond metadata extraction precision—e.g., will they measure downstream reuse of enhanced datasets, user engagement with new query features, or actual uptake by AI training pipelines?","Clear vision for scalable, cross-omics metadata infrastructure; strong justification for working-group-level collaboration involving PRIDE curators, ML/NLP experts, and proteomics scientists; reasonable and specific resource requests; multi-pronged validation strategy using gold-standard and independent test sets.","Lacks concrete downstream metrics of success (e.g., actual reuse rates, dataset discoverability improvements); insufficient discussion of how metadata quality control will be sustained after the two-year funding period ends; limited discussion of ethical/privacy issues around mining unpublished or restricted-access text corpora for NLP training.",4,"A well-conceived and operationally strong infrastructure proposal with clear collaborative rationale, though it stretches the RFP's scientific-question mandate and lacks long-term sustainability planning.",2,"The proposal is oriented toward data infrastructure rather than any specific biological emergent phenomenon, though improved metadata could indirectly enable future emergence-related studies.",4,"The integration of raw-data-derived and literature-derived metadata extraction using LLM ensembles, plus a scalable cross-omics template, represents a genuinely novel and significant contribution to FAIR data practices.",4,"Methodology is grounded in established standards (SDRF, controlled vocabularies) with a staged validation plan using both public gold-standard and private test sets.",3,"The scope (raw data + NLP + community challenge + retrospective curation + querying tools) is broad for two years, risking incomplete delivery of some components.",4,"Strongly synthesizes existing public datasets and literature at scale, consistent with the RFP's requirement to use only existing data without new experiments.",5,"Data sources (PRIDE datasets, open-access literature, gold-standard SDRF sets) are precisely and comprehensively identified.",5,"Exemplary commitment to open-source code release, preprints, community challenge outputs, and educational tutorials via ProteomicsML.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:20:58.039416,0,success,,"This proposal presents a highly relevant plan to systematically improve metadata quality at scale across proteomics and metabolomics. The collaborative, cross-disciplinary approach is well-suited for a working group model, and the requested computational and personnel resources are appropriately scoped. However, the application would benefit from more concrete success metrics regarding dataset reusability and discoverability, as well as a stronger plan for long-term sustainability and data privacy.","The proposal offers a clear, scalable vision for cross-omics metadata infrastructure with a well-justified need for multidisciplinary collaboration. It includes appropriately scoped resource requests and a robust validation strategy utilizing both gold-standard and independent test sets.","The project lacks concrete downstream success metrics for evaluating actual dataset reusability and discoverability improvements. Furthermore, it provides insufficient details regarding the post-funding sustainability of metadata quality control and overlooks potential ethical or privacy concerns related to mining restricted-access text corpora."
baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::005,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,5,baseline::y1::12::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T14:51:56.823435,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal addresses a genuine and well-known pain point in the proteomics community: the poor state of metadata in public repositories like PRIDE. As a computational proteomics researcher, I appreciate the technical grounding—use of SDRF-Proteomics, Param-Medic for instrument parameter extraction, open modification searching for PTM/enzyme inference, and Nextflow for pipeline orchestration are all sensible, established choices. The team clearly has deep operational knowledge of PRIDE and the proteomics data ecosystem. However, I have concerns about how well this fits the RFP's core mandate. This is fundamentally a tool/infrastructure development project rather than a synthesis project aimed at answering a scientific question about emergent phenomena in molecular/cellular biology. The 'science' here is about data curation methodology, not about biological insight into emergence. The downstream applications (AI training, reanalysis) are mentioned but not concretely pursued within the proposal itself."",
