      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is realistic, but the computational scope (GPU hours) might be insufficient for large-scale ensemble NLP inference across all PRIDE publications."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Excellent synthesis of text-based and binary (mzML) data into a unified, harmonized metadata structure."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Clear identification of PRIDE datasets, literature corpora, and metadata standards."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""All outputs will be open-source, and the primary goal is making existing open data more accessible and reusable.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this from the perspective of a data repository manager and FAIR data advocate, I find this proposal outstanding. Public repositories are currently data dumps rather than organized databases because researchers are poor at annotating metadata during submission. By developing tools to automatically generate SDRF-compliant metadata, this working group directly addresses a long-standing puzzle in data science: how to scale curation. The proposal perfectly aligns with the NCEMS call to 'Tap diverse new talent' and 'Train the future data-savvy workforce' through the machine learning challenge and ProteomicsML tutorials. The necessity of a Working Group is well justified, as this requires PRIDE team members, ontologists, proteomics scientists, and NLP experts. This project embodies the spirit of community-scale synthesis by creating the structural foundation required for any future cross-study comparisons."",
      ""strengths"": ""Directly tackles the core issue of FAIR data in proteomics. The machine learning challenge is an excellent mechanism for community engagement and training. Perfect team composition and rationale."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal focuses heavily on retroactive metadata generation. While lesSDRF integration is mentioned, a bit more emphasis on stopping poor metadata at the point of submission would be beneficial."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A structurally vital project for the proteomics community that fulfills the methodological, training, and open science mandates of the RFP, creating the necessary foundation for future biological synthesis."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Does not address emergent phenomena directly, but builds the semantic web required for complex, multi-scale biological systems analysis."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Automated curation at the repository scale using AI is a significant leap forward from manual curation workflows."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 5,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Uses established standards (SDRF-Proteomics) and well-validated gold standard test sets to ensure high-fidelity metadata generation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Sensible stepwise approach starting with standard bottom-up proteomics before moving to complex modifications."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes disconnected data modalities (raw files, papers, ontologies) into a unified, queryable database."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Flawless reliance on existing, publicly available data, with no new experimental data generated."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The entire premise of the grant is to advance Open Science by enforcing FAIR principles on existing public data.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational biologist who frequently works with large-scale ML modeling, I am intrigued by the proposed machine-learning challenge to crowdsource metadata extraction techniques. This is a highly effective way to stimulate cross-disciplinary collaboration and tap new talent. The proposal addresses the 'Train the Next Generation' requirement beautifully via ProteomicsML. However, I have concerns about the evaluation metric. The proposal says 'Scoring will emphasize precision to ensure high-confidence results', but in NLP and metadata extraction, recall is often just as critical to prevent missing confounding variables in experimental design. Furthermore, to truly demonstrate the value of this methodological development for molecular and cellular sciences, the team should have proposed one specific biological synthesis use-case—for example, automatically downloading all datasets identified with 'hypoxia' to find an emergent protein interaction network. Without a biological application, it reads a bit too much like an IT infrastructure grant."",
      ""strengths"": ""The ML challenge is a fantastic community-building and algorithmic optimization strategy. Excellent use of ProteomicsML for educational outreach. Clear need for NCEMS computational support."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lack of a biological synthesis use-case to demonstrate the pipeline's real-world utility in discovering emergent cellular properties. Evaluation metrics (precision vs. recall) need more nuance."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A very strong data science and community-building proposal that successfully addresses the methodological and training aspects of the RFP, but falls short on delivering direct biological insights into emergent phenomena."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Methodological in nature. Does not propose a research question related to the emergence of complex biological traits or cellular phenomena."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The crowdsourcing ML challenge for proteomics metadata is a novel approach to a very stagnant and significant problem in the field."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Generally rigorous, but the sole emphasis on precision over recall in the ML challenge could result in models that miss critical but subtly stated experimental context in literature."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Two years is a tight but manageable timeframe for running a community ML challenge and subsequently integrating the winning models into a production environment (PRIDE)."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes data context (metadata) rather than primary biological data. It facilitates future synthesis but does not perform a biological synthesis itself."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Strictly adheres to the requirement of using only existing publicly available proteomics data."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to open science, utilizing open data, open standards (SDRF), and open-source code dissemination.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::12,y1,12,Intelligent metadata compilation to enhance the reusability and discoverability of mass spectrometry-based proteomics data,Accepted,Wout Bittremieux; Iddo Friedberg,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"As a bioinformatics and natural language processing (NLP) researcher, I evaluated the technical feasibility and innovation of the proposed pipelines. The concept of utilizing fine-tuned Large Language Models (LLMs) alongside traditional bioinformatics tools (like Nextflow and Param-Medic) to reconstruct missing metadata is very strong. The applicants recognize the risks of LLM hallucinations by proposing self-verification and ensemble cross-checking among different LLMs. However, performing ensemble cross-checking at scale on scientific literature can be computationally expensive. The request for 500 GPU hours for 'local fine-tuning' seems a bit low if they also plan to run large-scale inference across thousands of PRIDE-associated publications, unless they plan to use API-based models for inference, which isn't explicitly detailed. Nonetheless, the architecture is sound, and connecting unstructured text mining to structured ontological frameworks (SDRF) is exactly the kind of methodological development this funding call supports.",Sophisticated and technically sound hybrid extraction pipeline (raw data + NLP). Smart use of ensemble LLMs to reduce hallucination. Strong integration plan with lesSDRF and PRIDE.,Computational budget for NLP tasks (500 GPU hours) may be underestimated for the scale of PRIDE. Lacks detail on exactly which LLM architectures will be utilized.,4,"A robust and highly technical proposal that leverages cutting-edge NLP to solve a critical data science problem in biology, though it lacks a specific biological discovery aim.",3,"Indirect relevance. The tool will enable the discovery of emergent phenomena, but the project itself focuses on data structure and semantics.",4,Applying fine-tuned LLMs to scientific literature for strict ontology mapping (SDRF) is highly innovative and significant for biological data science.,4,"Technically rigorous, particularly the use of LLM cross-checking and a gold-standard curated dataset to evaluate precision.",3,"The timeline is realistic, but the computational scope (GPU hours) might be insufficient for large-scale ensemble NLP inference across all PRIDE publications.",4,"Excellent synthesis of text-based and binary (mzML) data into a unified, harmonized metadata structure.",5,"Clear identification of PRIDE datasets, literature corpora, and metadata standards.",5,"All outputs will be open-source, and the primary goal is making existing open data more accessible and reusable.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:21:33.430629,0,success,,"The proposal outlines a highly relevant and methodologically sound approach that combines traditional bioinformatics tools with fine-tuned Large Language Models (LLMs) to reconstruct missing metadata. The architecture effectively addresses potential LLM hallucinations through self-verification and ensemble cross-checking, aligning well with the goals of the funding call. However, the computational budget and specific model architectures require further clarification to ensure feasibility at scale.",The project features a sophisticated hybrid extraction pipeline that integrates raw data processing with natural language processing. It effectively employs ensemble LLMs to mitigate hallucination risks and demonstrates a strong integration plan with lesSDRF and PRIDE.,"The requested computational budget of 500 GPU hours may be insufficient for large-scale inference across the PRIDE database. Additionally, the proposal lacks specific details regarding the exact LLM architectures to be implemented or whether API-based models will be utilized."
baseline::y1::12::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::004,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,4,baseline::y1::12::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T14:51:08.254051,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a cellular biologist focused on emergent biological phenomena, I evaluated this proposal based on its ability to answer fundamental biological questions through data synthesis. While the technical approach to metadata extraction is highly sophisticated, the proposal fundamentally lacks a driving biological question. The RFP explicitly requests projects that 'address compelling scientific questions' and 'solve long-standing puzzles in the molecular and cellular sciences' related to emergence phenomena. This proposal is purely a methodological and infrastructure-building exercise. It aims to make PRIDE data more FAIR and usable, which is undeniably valuable, but it does not actually use that data to synthesize a novel biological insight during the funding period. The applicants state this will 'facilitate secondary analyses,' essentially outsourcing the actual discovery of emergent phenomena to future researchers. While the RFP allows for methodological developments, it should ideally be coupled with a biological use-case that demonstrates its utility in answering a specific question about cellular emergence."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a massive bottleneck in the omics field—poor metadata quality. The team proposes a comprehensive, multi-modal approach combining raw file extraction with LLM-based literature mining. Strong commitment to open science."",
      ""weakness"": ""Complete absence of a biological research question related to emergent phenomena. The proposal builds a tool but does not synthesize data to answer a molecular or cellular puzzle."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A highly capable technical team proposing an infrastructure project that, while valuable to the proteomics community, completely misses the call's primary mandate to address fundamental questions related to emergence phenomena."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 1,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal does not investigate or identify any emergent phenomena in molecular and cellular biosciences. It merely builds the metadata infrastructure that might allow others to do so in the future."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The application of fine-tuned LLMs for proteomics metadata extraction is novel, but the lack of a biological discovery reduces its overall scientific significance within the context of this specific RFP."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The methodology is highly rigorous, employing ensemble cross-checking among LLMs, gold standard SDRF datasets for benchmarking, and robust bioinformatic pipelines like Nextflow."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is realistic for building and validating the pipeline, launching a community challenge, and integrating with PRIDE."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project synthesizes metadata rather than synthesizing molecular/cellular data to gain deeper biological insights. It misses the 'community-scale synthesis' definition intended by the RFP."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Excellent identification of data sources, leveraging raw mass spectrometry files, PRIDE repository data, and open-access publications."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Perfect alignment with open science principles. Uses ProteomicsML, GitHub, PRIDE integration, preprints, and open-source licensing.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From my perspective as an expert in mass spectrometry and proteomics, this is an absolutely critical proposal. The 'dark data' problem in proteomics is severe; we have petabytes of data in PRIDE that cannot be effectively reused because the metadata is buried in PDFs or simply missing. I interpret the RFP's allowance for 'methodological developments that advance molecular and cellular sciences through synthesis research' as a perfect fit for this project. Without harmonized metadata, community-scale synthesis of proteomics data is virtually impossible. By automating the SDRF-Proteomics annotation using NLP and raw file parsing, this working group will unlock massive datasets for the entire field. The inclusion of a machine learning challenge is a brilliant way to tap diverse new talent (RFP Goal 4) and train a data-savvy workforce (RFP Goal 5). The need for NCEMS computational resources and cross-disciplinary collaboration (proteomics experts + NLP data scientists) is clearly justified."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addresses the single biggest hurdle to proteomics data synthesis. Excellent integration with PRIDE. Outstanding plan for community engagement via a machine learning challenge."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal could be strengthened by including a 'demonstrator' biological analysis—a small-scale synthesis of the newly annotated data to prove it can uncover new biological insights."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 5,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An exceptional, highly necessary methodological proposal that will remove the primary barrier to large-scale data synthesis in proteomics, perfectly fulfilling the RFP's goals for methodological development and workforce training."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""While it does not directly study a specific phenomenon, it provides the essential methodological framework required to study emergent properties across large-scale proteomics datasets."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Automating metadata extraction using LLMs and integrating it retroactively into PRIDE is a highly novel and deeply significant advancement for the field."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 5,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The tiered approach (raw data parsing via Param-Medic + literature mining via fine-tuned LLMs) is robust. The use of manual curation as a test set ensures high precision."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 5,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope is well-defined, starting with bottom-up proteomics and expanding only if time permits. Resource requests are highly appropriate."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""It is a synthesis of metadata from disparate sources (raw files, literature, databases) that uniquely enables biological data synthesis."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Identifies the exact public data required and relies entirely on existing, publicly available PRIDE datasets."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Deeply embedded in open science, aiming to improve FAIR standards globally for the proteomics community.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a bioinformatics and natural language processing (NLP) researcher, I evaluated the technical feasibility and innovation of the proposed pipelines. The concept of utilizing fine-tuned Large Language Models (LLMs) alongside traditional bioinformatics tools (like Nextflow and Param-Medic) to reconstruct missing metadata is very strong. The applicants recognize the risks of LLM hallucinations by proposing self-verification and ensemble cross-checking among different LLMs. However, performing ensemble cross-checking at scale on scientific literature can be computationally expensive. The request for 500 GPU hours for 'local fine-tuning' seems a bit low if they also plan to run large-scale inference across thousands of PRIDE-associated publications, unless they plan to use API-based models for inference, which isn't explicitly detailed. Nonetheless, the architecture is sound, and connecting unstructured text mining to structured ontological frameworks (SDRF) is exactly the kind of methodological development this funding call supports."",
      ""strengths"": ""Sophisticated and technically sound hybrid extraction pipeline (raw data + NLP). Smart use of ensemble LLMs to reduce hallucination. Strong integration plan with lesSDRF and PRIDE."",
      ""weakness"": ""Computational budget for NLP tasks (500 GPU hours) may be underestimated for the scale of PRIDE. Lacks detail on exactly which LLM architectures will be utilized."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A robust and highly technical proposal that leverages cutting-edge NLP to solve a critical data science problem in biology, though it lacks a specific biological discovery aim."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Indirect relevance. The tool will enable the discovery of emergent phenomena, but the project itself focuses on data structure and semantics."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Applying fine-tuned LLMs to scientific literature for strict ontology mapping (SDRF) is highly innovative and significant for biological data science."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Technically rigorous, particularly the use of LLM cross-checking and a gold-standard curated dataset to evaluate precision."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is realistic, but the computational scope (GPU hours) might be insufficient for large-scale ensemble NLP inference across all PRIDE publications."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Excellent synthesis of text-based and binary (mzML) data into a unified, harmonized metadata structure."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Clear identification of PRIDE datasets, literature corpora, and metadata standards."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""All outputs will be open-source, and the primary goal is making existing open data more accessible and reusable.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this from the perspective of a data repository manager and FAIR data advocate, I find this proposal outstanding. Public repositories are currently data dumps rather than organized databases because researchers are poor at annotating metadata during submission. By developing tools to automatically generate SDRF-compliant metadata, this working group directly addresses a long-standing puzzle in data science: how to scale curation. The proposal perfectly aligns with the NCEMS call to 'Tap diverse new talent' and 'Train the future data-savvy workforce' through the machine learning challenge and ProteomicsML tutorials. The necessity of a Working Group is well justified, as this requires PRIDE team members, ontologists, proteomics scientists, and NLP experts. This project embodies the spirit of community-scale synthesis by creating the structural foundation required for any future cross-study comparisons."",
      ""strengths"": ""Directly tackles the core issue of FAIR data in proteomics. The machine learning challenge is an excellent mechanism for community engagement and training. Perfect team composition and rationale."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal focuses heavily on retroactive metadata generation. While lesSDRF integration is mentioned, a bit more emphasis on stopping poor metadata at the point of submission would be beneficial."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A structurally vital project for the proteomics community that fulfills the methodological, training, and open science mandates of the RFP, creating the necessary foundation for future biological synthesis."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Does not address emergent phenomena directly, but builds the semantic web required for complex, multi-scale biological systems analysis."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Automated curation at the repository scale using AI is a significant leap forward from manual curation workflows."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 5,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Uses established standards (SDRF-Proteomics) and well-validated gold standard test sets to ensure high-fidelity metadata generation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Sensible stepwise approach starting with standard bottom-up proteomics before moving to complex modifications."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes disconnected data modalities (raw files, papers, ontologies) into a unified, queryable database."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Flawless reliance on existing, publicly available data, with no new experimental data generated."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The entire premise of the grant is to advance Open Science by enforcing FAIR principles on existing public data.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational biologist who frequently works with large-scale ML modeling, I am intrigued by the proposed machine-learning challenge to crowdsource metadata extraction techniques. This is a highly effective way to stimulate cross-disciplinary collaboration and tap new talent. The proposal addresses the 'Train the Next Generation' requirement beautifully via ProteomicsML. However, I have concerns about the evaluation metric. The proposal says 'Scoring will emphasize precision to ensure high-confidence results', but in NLP and metadata extraction, recall is often just as critical to prevent missing confounding variables in experimental design. Furthermore, to truly demonstrate the value of this methodological development for molecular and cellular sciences, the team should have proposed one specific biological synthesis use-case—for example, automatically downloading all datasets identified with 'hypoxia' to find an emergent protein interaction network. Without a biological application, it reads a bit too much like an IT infrastructure grant."",
      ""strengths"": ""The ML challenge is a fantastic community-building and algorithmic optimization strategy. Excellent use of ProteomicsML for educational outreach. Clear need for NCEMS computational support."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lack of a biological synthesis use-case to demonstrate the pipeline's real-world utility in discovering emergent cellular properties. Evaluation metrics (precision vs. recall) need more nuance."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A very strong data science and community-building proposal that successfully addresses the methodological and training aspects of the RFP, but falls short on delivering direct biological insights into emergent phenomena."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 2,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Methodological in nature. Does not propose a research question related to the emergence of complex biological traits or cellular phenomena."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The crowdsourcing ML challenge for proteomics metadata is a novel approach to a very stagnant and significant problem in the field."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Generally rigorous, but the sole emphasis on precision over recall in the ML challenge could result in models that miss critical but subtly stated experimental context in literature."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Two years is a tight but manageable timeframe for running a community ML challenge and subsequently integrating the winning models into a production environment (PRIDE)."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes data context (metadata) rather than primary biological data. It facilitates future synthesis but does not perform a biological synthesis itself."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Strictly adheres to the requirement of using only existing publicly available proteomics data."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to open science, utilizing open data, open standards (SDRF), and open-source code dissemination.""
    }
