}",0,y1::12,y1,12,Intelligent metadata compilation to enhance the reusability and discoverability of mass spectrometry-based proteomics data,Accepted,Wout Bittremieux; Iddo Friedberg,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewing this from the perspective of a data repository manager and FAIR data advocate, I find this proposal outstanding. Public repositories are currently data dumps rather than organized databases because researchers are poor at annotating metadata during submission. By developing tools to automatically generate SDRF-compliant metadata, this working group directly addresses a long-standing puzzle in data science: how to scale curation. The proposal perfectly aligns with the NCEMS call to 'Tap diverse new talent' and 'Train the future data-savvy workforce' through the machine learning challenge and ProteomicsML tutorials. The necessity of a Working Group is well justified, as this requires PRIDE team members, ontologists, proteomics scientists, and NLP experts. This project embodies the spirit of community-scale synthesis by creating the structural foundation required for any future cross-study comparisons.",Directly tackles the core issue of FAIR data in proteomics. The machine learning challenge is an excellent mechanism for community engagement and training. Perfect team composition and rationale.,"The proposal focuses heavily on retroactive metadata generation. While lesSDRF integration is mentioned, a bit more emphasis on stopping poor metadata at the point of submission would be beneficial.",4,"A structurally vital project for the proteomics community that fulfills the methodological, training, and open science mandates of the RFP, creating the necessary foundation for future biological synthesis.",3,"Does not address emergent phenomena directly, but builds the semantic web required for complex, multi-scale biological systems analysis.",4,Automated curation at the repository scale using AI is a significant leap forward from manual curation workflows.,5,Uses established standards (SDRF-Proteomics) and well-validated gold standard test sets to ensure high-fidelity metadata generation.,4,Sensible stepwise approach starting with standard bottom-up proteomics before moving to complex modifications.,4,"Synthesizes disconnected data modalities (raw files, papers, ontologies) into a unified, queryable database.",5,"Flawless reliance on existing, publicly available data, with no new experimental data generated.",5,The entire premise of the grant is to advance Open Science by enforcing FAIR principles on existing public data.,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:21:41.857293,0,success,,"The proposal addresses the critical challenge of scaling data curation by developing tools for automatic, SDRF-compliant metadata generation. It aligns well with funding priorities by incorporating community engagement and training through a machine learning challenge and ProteomicsML tutorials. Furthermore, the formation of a Working Group is well-justified, bringing together the necessary multidisciplinary expertise to establish a foundation for future cross-study comparisons.","The project directly addresses the core challenges of FAIR data implementation in proteomics with a well-composed, multidisciplinary team. Additionally, the inclusion of a machine learning challenge provides an effective mechanism for community engagement and workforce training.",The project predominantly focuses on retroactive metadata generation rather than preventative measures. Greater emphasis on integrating tools or strategies to ensure high-quality metadata at the initial point of data submission would strengthen the overall approach.
baseline::y1::12::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,5,baseline::y1::12::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T14:51:08.254051,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a cellular biologist focused on emergent biological phenomena, I evaluated this proposal based on its ability to answer fundamental biological questions through data synthesis. While the technical approach to metadata extraction is highly sophisticated, the proposal fundamentally lacks a driving biological question. The RFP explicitly requests projects that 'address compelling scientific questions' and 'solve long-standing puzzles in the molecular and cellular sciences' related to emergence phenomena. This proposal is purely a methodological and infrastructure-building exercise. It aims to make PRIDE data more FAIR and usable, which is undeniably valuable, but it does not actually use that data to synthesize a novel biological insight during the funding period. The applicants state this will 'facilitate secondary analyses,' essentially outsourcing the actual discovery of emergent phenomena to future researchers. While the RFP allows for methodological developments, it should ideally be coupled with a biological use-case that demonstrates its utility in answering a specific question about cellular emergence."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a massive bottleneck in the omics field—poor metadata quality. The team proposes a comprehensive, multi-modal approach combining raw file extraction with LLM-based literature mining. Strong commitment to open science."",
