}",0,y1::8,y1,8,Multimodel single-cell frameworks for cell lines with extensive multi-omics data,Accepted,Elizabeth Brunk; Hyebin Song; Ferhat Ay; Vasant Honavar; William Noble,5,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewing this as a Machine Learning Expert and Data Scientist, this proposal outlines exactly the kind of foundational work needed to train next-generation AI models in biology. Current multimodal ML models (like single-cell variational autoencoders) struggle because they are trained on datasets with massive donor-to-donor batch effects and confounding variables. By focusing on 7 homogenous cell lines with dense, multi-modal data (including imaging and Perturb-seq), the authors will create the 'MNIST' or 'ImageNet' equivalent for single-cell multi-omics. This benchmarking resource will be transformative for algorithmic development. However, the proposal is very light on the actual ML methodologies. Will they use graph neural networks? Transformer-based architectures? How will they align imaging data with sequencing data in a unified latent space? Furthermore, the training of a data-savvy workforce is a key RFP requirement that is completely ignored in this text.","Brilliant strategy to create a clean, high-density benchmark dataset for ML models; leverages very high-value perturbation data; clear justification for NCEMS computational support.",No specific machine learning architectures or data wrangling strategies are proposed; completely ignores the RFP requirement to train the next generation of researchers.,4,"An excellent proposal for creating a desperately needed ML benchmark for multi-omics, though it suffers from a lack of technical detail and missing workforce training plans.",4,"Developing unified latent spaces across DNA, RNA, and protein is the exact mathematical representation needed to discover emergent biological interactions.",5,"Creating a multi-omic, multi-modal (including imaging) benchmark for isolated cell lines is highly novel and will heavily impact the computational biology field.",3,Lacks mathematical and algorithmic rigor in the proposal text; needs to define how 'precise alignment across modalities' will actually be achieved.,2,"The scope of integrating imaging with sequencing for 7 cell lines is massive, yet no timeline or feasibility assessment is provided.",5,Outstanding synthesis project that requires the exact kind of cross-disciplinary collaboration (ML + genomics) the call is looking for.,5,Great identification of diverse data types. Integrating imaging with single-cell sequencing is a particularly strong point.,2,The proposal entirely misses the RFP requirement to 'Train the Next Generation'. It also lacks explicit commitments to FAIR principles or specific open-source repositories.,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:38:59.677543,0,success,,"This proposal aims to develop a foundational benchmark dataset for training advanced machine learning models in biology by utilizing seven homogeneous cell lines with dense, multi-modal data. The resulting resource is expected to significantly benefit algorithmic development by minimizing batch effects and confounding variables. However, the proposal lacks detailed descriptions of the specific machine learning methodologies to be employed and does not address the targeted request for proposals (RFP) requirement regarding workforce training.","The project outlines a strong strategy to generate a clean, high-density benchmark dataset utilizing high-value perturbation data. Additionally, it provides a clear justification for computational support.","The proposal does not specify the machine learning architectures or data integration strategies that will be utilized. Furthermore, it completely omits the RFP requirement to include a training plan for future researchers."
