}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"As a cell biologist focused on high-content imaging and organelle interactomics, I find the biological applications of this computational proposal to be highly compelling. The idea of predicting the location and shape of 'missing' organelles based on the spatial constraints of visible ones (e.g., inferring centrosome/microtubule states from just nuclear/tubulin data) would be a game-changer for secondary analysis of massive, incomplete imaging repositories. The inclusion of pathological states like HGPS (progeria) adds significant biological value. However, the proposal glosses over the immense difficulty of batch effects and varying resolutions in public imaging datasets. Biological images are noisy, and staining protocols differ wildly between labs. If the transfer learning model is trained on disparate datasets, it may simply learn to predict batch artifacts rather than true biological interdependencies. A more robust plan for data harmonization prior to GW alignment is necessary.",Highly relevant biological test cases that build in complexity. The goal of predicting missing components could revolutionize how we mine legacy image datasets. Great inclusion of disease models (HGPS) to test generalizability.,"Fails to adequately address image harmonization, batch effects, and resolution discrepancies across public datasets, which are the primary bottlenecks in multi-source image synthesis.",4,A biologically profound synthesis proposal that could unlock massive value in existing public image repositories. The working group must carefully manage data harmonization to ensure their models learn biology rather than technical artifacts.,4,"Addresses emergence by focusing on how the interactions of organelles (nucleus, mitochondria, centrosomes) dictate the overall spatial organization of the cell.",5,The ability to perform 'holistic systems-level predictions' on incomplete image sets using transfer learning is a massive conceptual leap for cell biology.,4,"The biological design is highly rigorous, moving stepwise through increasingly complex test cases. However, image pre-processing rigor is lacking.",4,"The phased approach (months 1-6 for 2-component, remainder for 3-component) is sensible and well-structured.",5,Perfect alignment with the call's mandate to use purely existing data to solve long-standing puzzles via transdisciplinary collaboration.,3,"While biological targets are identified, the proposal admits 'limitations in dataset availability... will necessitate further data scouting,' which is a risk for a purely synthesis-driven project.",5,"Strong alignment with NCEMS goals, heavy reliance on open datasets, and a commitment to producing an open-source data integration workflow.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:41:14.754681,0,success,,"The proposal presents a compelling computational approach to predict the spatial characteristics of unobserved organelles based on visible structures, offering significant potential for analyzing incomplete imaging datasets. The application of this method to disease models, such as Hutchinson-Gilford Progeria Syndrome, adds substantial biological relevance. However, the methodology lacks a rigorous strategy for data harmonization prior to alignment. Addressing batch effects, resolution variations, and staining protocol discrepancies across disparate public datasets is necessary to prevent the model from learning artifactual associations.","The proposal features highly relevant biological test cases and incorporates disease models to evaluate generalizability. Furthermore, the approach of predicting missing cellular components has the potential to significantly enhance the utility of legacy imaging repositories.","The proposal does not adequately address the challenges of image harmonization, resolution discrepancies, and batch effects inherent in multi-source public datasets. Without a robust preprocessing and harmonization plan, the transfer learning model is at risk of learning batch artifacts rather than true biological interdependencies."
baseline::y1::9::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::004,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,4,baseline::y1::9::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T15:20:12.429066,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational biologist specializing in machine learning and topological data analysis, I find this proposal highly innovative. The core methodological premise—using Gromov-Wasserstein (GW) distances to align disparate cellular feature spaces—is an elegant solution to the perennial problem of integrating multi-modal, highly dimensional biological data. The authors propose to leverage GW to train transfer learning models and Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) that can infer missing components in incomplete datasets. While theoretically sound, aligning high-dimensional image data with lower-dimensional in silico and in vitro outputs is notoriously difficult. The proposal lacks sufficient detail on how the GANs will avoid 'hallucinating' non-biological artifacts when predicting missing structures. Additionally, estimating 10,000 GPU hours for training across three highly heterogeneous test cases is likely an underestimate, given the known convergence issues with GW-GANs in biological contexts."",
      ""strengths"": ""Pioneering application of metric geometry (GW distances) to bridge distinct morphometric and physical data spaces. Excellent conceptual framing of the computational problem. Clear, well-justified request for NCEMS computational and storage resources."",
      ""weakness"": ""Limited technical specifics on the GAN architecture and strategies to mitigate mode collapse or artifact generation during mutual prediction. Computation time and infrastructure requirements might be severely underestimated."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A computationally ambitious and methodologically innovative proposal that pushes the boundaries of transfer learning in cell biology. While carrying high technical risk regarding deep learning convergence, the potential payoff for mesoscale biology is massive."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Directly tackles emergent mesoscale organization by computationally decoding how collective cellular properties emerge from interactions among structural components."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Using GW distances for cross-space learning in molecular/cellular datasets is a highly novel application that could solve long-standing data integration puzzles."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The theoretical framework is rigorous, but practical implementation details for the deep learning models (GANs) and strategies for validating 'missing component' predictions are sparse."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is extremely tight for developing a novel GW-based GAN framework, integrating multiple datasets, and testing three complex biological systems."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Exemplifies data synthesis by entirely relying on integrating existing image, in vitro, and in silico datasets to generate new insights without generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Broad classes of data are identified, though the proposal acknowledges the need for further 'scouting' which adds some risk."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to open-source workflows (TensorFlow, PyTorch, POT library) and providing a generalizable analytical framework for the community.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From the perspective of a biophysicist studying soft matter and biomolecular condensates, this proposal hits all the right notes regarding the physical principles of cellular organization. The investigators are asking profound questions about how intrinsic physical properties (like surface tension and fluidity) compare to context-dependent interactions in driving mesoscale architecture. The test cases are brilliantly chosen, particularly the progression from the nucleolus (a classic phase-separated condensate) to mitochondrial nucleoids. My main concern lies in the translation from low-dimensional physical models back to image features. Agent-based models are mentioned as a way to integrate the findings, but the bridge between a GW-distance-derived correlation matrix and physical interaction parameters in an agent-based simulation is glossed over. The physical constraints must be strictly enforced in the model, or the transfer learning will yield physically impossible cellular geometries."",
