    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a systems biologist focused on multiscale modeling, I am highly impressed by this working group's vision. The transition from reductionist biology to systems-level understanding requires exactly this kind of transdisciplinary synthesis. By bridging in vitro minimal systems (which give us baseline physical properties) with complex in vivo cellular imaging and in silico simulations, the authors are constructing a true multiscale pipeline. The idea of using the nucleus as the 'anchor point' across all three test cases is a brilliant strategic choice that provides a common biological coordinate system. My primary critique is the lack of clarity on how dynamic, time-series data (live-cell imaging) will be temporally aligned with static data spaces using Gromov-Wasserstein distances. Temporal dynamics are crucial for understanding emergence, but the methodology heavily biases towards spatial and morphological alignments."",
      ""strengths"": ""Visionary approach to multiscale biology, integrating minimal in vitro systems with complex cellular environments. The choice of the nucleus as a biological 'anchor' is excellent. Truly transdisciplinary team and methodology."",
      ""weakness"": ""The integration of dynamic, live-cell data with static images using GW distances is mentioned but not thoroughly explained. Temporal dynamics are critical to systems biology and require specialized handling."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A highly impressive, synthesis-driven proposal that models the future of systems biology by integrating cross-scale data. If the team can successfully align temporal dynamics with their spatial models, the impact will be transformative."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Examines emergent phenomena by explicitly comparing isolated components (minimal systems) with the integrated cellular context to identify what behaviors emerge only upon interaction."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The multiscale synthesis of in vitro, in silico, and in vivo data using advanced topological machine learning is a major paradigm shift."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall logic is extremely rigorous, though the specific mathematical handling of time-series/dynamic data within the GW framework needs more explicit formulation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Ambitious but manageable, largely due to the smart constraint of anchoring all studies around the well-characterized nucleus."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Flawless execution of the synthesis mandate. Integrates distinct data types and multiple disciplines to generate models that no single lab could achieve."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Well-identified data types and strong rationale for the specific biological systems, though dependent on public repository quality."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Good alignment with FAIR principles and reproducible science, with clear plans to make the resulting analytical framework publicly available.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewing this from the perspective of data infrastructure, open science, and FAIR data principles, this proposal heavily leverages the requested NCEMS and CYVERSE resources, which is exactly what this call intends. The request for 50TB of storage and 10,000 GPU hours is well-justified for the scale of deep learning and multi-modal integration proposed. However, I have significant concerns regarding the data ingestion and curation pipeline. The proposal mentions that 'systematic literature synthesis methods' will be used to scout and pool smaller datasets. In a two-year project, manual or semi-automated data curation from literature can easily consume 75% of the timeline. Without a dedicated data steward or highly automated ingestion pipeline, the team may struggle to assemble the 'large, diverse corpus' required to train their GW-GAN models effectively in the allotted time.","Excellent justification for NCEMS support, clearly articulating the need for GPUs, high-capacity storage, and IT specialist collaboration. Strong commitment to open science and community tool-building.","Underestimates the time and effort required for 'data scouting' and data harmonization. The lack of a pre-identified, clean, and consolidated dataset poses a massive timeline risk for a 2-year grant.",3,"A technically brilliant proposal with a high reliance on NCEMS infrastructure, fitting the spirit of the call perfectly. However, the project's success is threatened by the daunting task of harmonizing fragmented, uncurated public datasets within a strict 2-year window.",4,"Clearly links data integration efforts to the discovery of emergent cellular properties, though the focus of the methodology is highly computational.",4,Proposing a universal data integration workflow for cell images and simulations is highly significant and would be a major asset to the open science community.,3,"The computational approach is sound, but the data curation approach relies too heavily on undefined 'systematic literature synthesis methods' that lack rigorous detail.",2,"Two years is highly optimistic for a project that still needs to scout, curate, harmonize, and align multi-modal data before extensive model training can even begin.",5,"The entire premise is built on synthesizing existing, fragmented data into a cohesive, predictive framework.",2,"The proposal explicitly admits that data findability and quality are limitations and relies on future scouting. For a synthesis proposal, core datasets should ideally be secured beforehand.",5,"Exceptional commitment to building tools for the community, utilizing open libraries, and directly partnering with NCEMS/CYVERSE for data management.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:41:23.905722,0,success,,"The proposal effectively justifies the need for requested computational resources, including storage and GPU hours, to support its deep learning and multi-modal integration objectives. The project also demonstrates a strong commitment to open science and community tool development. However, the reliance on manual or semi-automated data curation from literature presents a significant feasibility concern. Without a dedicated data steward or an automated ingestion pipeline, assembling the necessary training corpus may severely delay the two-year project timeline.","The proposal provides strong justification for the requested computational resources, high-capacity storage, and IT support. It also demonstrates a clear commitment to open science, FAIR data principles, and the development of community tools.","The project underestimates the time and effort required for data ingestion, scouting, and harmonization. The absence of a pre-identified, clean dataset and a dedicated data steward introduces a significant timeline risk for a two-year grant."
baseline::y1::9::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,5,baseline::y1::9::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T15:20:12.429066,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational biologist specializing in machine learning and topological data analysis, I find this proposal highly innovative. The core methodological premise—using Gromov-Wasserstein (GW) distances to align disparate cellular feature spaces—is an elegant solution to the perennial problem of integrating multi-modal, highly dimensional biological data. The authors propose to leverage GW to train transfer learning models and Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) that can infer missing components in incomplete datasets. While theoretically sound, aligning high-dimensional image data with lower-dimensional in silico and in vitro outputs is notoriously difficult. The proposal lacks sufficient detail on how the GANs will avoid 'hallucinating' non-biological artifacts when predicting missing structures. Additionally, estimating 10,000 GPU hours for training across three highly heterogeneous test cases is likely an underestimate, given the known convergence issues with GW-GANs in biological contexts."",
      ""strengths"": ""Pioneering application of metric geometry (GW distances) to bridge distinct morphometric and physical data spaces. Excellent conceptual framing of the computational problem. Clear, well-justified request for NCEMS computational and storage resources."",
      ""weakness"": ""Limited technical specifics on the GAN architecture and strategies to mitigate mode collapse or artifact generation during mutual prediction. Computation time and infrastructure requirements might be severely underestimated."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A computationally ambitious and methodologically innovative proposal that pushes the boundaries of transfer learning in cell biology. While carrying high technical risk regarding deep learning convergence, the potential payoff for mesoscale biology is massive."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Directly tackles emergent mesoscale organization by computationally decoding how collective cellular properties emerge from interactions among structural components."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Using GW distances for cross-space learning in molecular/cellular datasets is a highly novel application that could solve long-standing data integration puzzles."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The theoretical framework is rigorous, but practical implementation details for the deep learning models (GANs) and strategies for validating 'missing component' predictions are sparse."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is extremely tight for developing a novel GW-based GAN framework, integrating multiple datasets, and testing three complex biological systems."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Exemplifies data synthesis by entirely relying on integrating existing image, in vitro, and in silico datasets to generate new insights without generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Broad classes of data are identified, though the proposal acknowledges the need for further 'scouting' which adds some risk."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to open-source workflows (TensorFlow, PyTorch, POT library) and providing a generalizable analytical framework for the community.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From the perspective of a biophysicist studying soft matter and biomolecular condensates, this proposal hits all the right notes regarding the physical principles of cellular organization. The investigators are asking profound questions about how intrinsic physical properties (like surface tension and fluidity) compare to context-dependent interactions in driving mesoscale architecture. The test cases are brilliantly chosen, particularly the progression from the nucleolus (a classic phase-separated condensate) to mitochondrial nucleoids. My main concern lies in the translation from low-dimensional physical models back to image features. Agent-based models are mentioned as a way to integrate the findings, but the bridge between a GW-distance-derived correlation matrix and physical interaction parameters in an agent-based simulation is glossed over. The physical constraints must be strictly enforced in the model, or the transfer learning will yield physically impossible cellular geometries."",
      ""strengths"": ""Exceptional framing of the balance between intrinsic physical properties and cellular environmental factors. Test cases are perfectly suited to interrogate condensate biophysics. Strong transdisciplinary integration of in vitro minimal systems with whole-cell images."",
      ""weakness"": ""The pipeline connecting deep learning outputs to 'standard granular agent-based models' is conceptually weak. It is unclear how statistical correlations will be rigorously converted into thermodynamic or kinetic parameters."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A deeply fascinating proposal that successfully bridges physical sciences and cell biology. Despite some vagueness in how machine learning outputs will inform physical agent-based models, the conceptual synthesis of morphometric and physical data is outstanding."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The fundamental question of the proposal is literally how mesoscale cellular architecture emerges from physical forces and geometric constraints."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combines state-of-the-art physical principles (condensate biophysics) with cutting-edge computational data synthesis. Highly significant for structural cell biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""While the machine learning approach is sophisticated, the methodological rigor regarding the generation of biophysical agent-based models from statistical image features is insufficiently detailed."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Scaling from 2-component to 3-component systems over two years is a logical and realistic progression, provided the data is as accessible as claimed."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Integrates an impressive array of data modalities (in silico, in vitro, cellular imaging) to synthesize a holistic understanding of cellular organization."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The use of specific, well-characterized systems (like HGPS patient fibroblasts) shows good foresight, though some in vitro dataset availability remains vague."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Clearly outlines tools and open-source intentions, though specific data sharing repositories (beyond general NCEMS storage) are not explicitly named.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a cell biologist focused on high-content imaging and organelle interactomics, I find the biological applications of this computational proposal to be highly compelling. The idea of predicting the location and shape of 'missing' organelles based on the spatial constraints of visible ones (e.g., inferring centrosome/microtubule states from just nuclear/tubulin data) would be a game-changer for secondary analysis of massive, incomplete imaging repositories. The inclusion of pathological states like HGPS (progeria) adds significant biological value. However, the proposal glosses over the immense difficulty of batch effects and varying resolutions in public imaging datasets. Biological images are noisy, and staining protocols differ wildly between labs. If the transfer learning model is trained on disparate datasets, it may simply learn to predict batch artifacts rather than true biological interdependencies. A more robust plan for data harmonization prior to GW alignment is necessary."",
      ""strengths"": ""Highly relevant biological test cases that build in complexity. The goal of predicting missing components could revolutionize how we mine legacy image datasets. Great inclusion of disease models (HGPS) to test generalizability."",
      ""weakness"": ""Fails to adequately address image harmonization, batch effects, and resolution discrepancies across public datasets, which are the primary bottlenecks in multi-source image synthesis."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically profound synthesis proposal that could unlock massive value in existing public image repositories. The working group must carefully manage data harmonization to ensure their models learn biology rather than technical artifacts."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Addresses emergence by focusing on how the interactions of organelles (nucleus, mitochondria, centrosomes) dictate the overall spatial organization of the cell."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The ability to perform 'holistic systems-level predictions' on incomplete image sets using transfer learning is a massive conceptual leap for cell biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The biological design is highly rigorous, moving stepwise through increasingly complex test cases. However, image pre-processing rigor is lacking."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The phased approach (months 1-6 for 2-component, remainder for 3-component) is sensible and well-structured."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Perfect alignment with the call's mandate to use purely existing data to solve long-standing puzzles via transdisciplinary collaboration."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""While biological targets are identified, the proposal admits 'limitations in dataset availability... will necessitate further data scouting,' which is a risk for a purely synthesis-driven project."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong alignment with NCEMS goals, heavy reliance on open datasets, and a commitment to producing an open-source data integration workflow.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this from the perspective of data infrastructure, open science, and FAIR data principles, this proposal heavily leverages the requested NCEMS and CYVERSE resources, which is exactly what this call intends. The request for 50TB of storage and 10,000 GPU hours is well-justified for the scale of deep learning and multi-modal integration proposed. However, I have significant concerns regarding the data ingestion and curation pipeline. The proposal mentions that 'systematic literature synthesis methods' will be used to scout and pool smaller datasets. In a two-year project, manual or semi-automated data curation from literature can easily consume 75% of the timeline. Without a dedicated data steward or highly automated ingestion pipeline, the team may struggle to assemble the 'large, diverse corpus' required to train their GW-GAN models effectively in the allotted time."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent justification for NCEMS support, clearly articulating the need for GPUs, high-capacity storage, and IT specialist collaboration. Strong commitment to open science and community tool-building."",
      ""weakness"": ""Underestimates the time and effort required for 'data scouting' and data harmonization. The lack of a pre-identified, clean, and consolidated dataset poses a massive timeline risk for a 2-year grant."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A technically brilliant proposal with a high reliance on NCEMS infrastructure, fitting the spirit of the call perfectly. However, the project's success is threatened by the daunting task of harmonizing fragmented, uncurated public datasets within a strict 2-year window."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Clearly links data integration efforts to the discovery of emergent cellular properties, though the focus of the methodology is highly computational."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Proposing a universal data integration workflow for cell images and simulations is highly significant and would be a major asset to the open science community."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational approach is sound, but the data curation approach relies too heavily on undefined 'systematic literature synthesis methods' that lack rigorous detail."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Two years is highly optimistic for a project that still needs to scout, curate, harmonize, and align multi-modal data before extensive model training can even begin."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The entire premise is built on synthesizing existing, fragmented data into a cohesive, predictive framework."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 2,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal explicitly admits that data findability and quality are limitations and relies on future scouting. For a synthesis proposal, core datasets should ideally be secured beforehand."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Exceptional commitment to building tools for the community, utilizing open libraries, and directly partnering with NCEMS/CYVERSE for data management.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a systems biologist focused on multiscale modeling, I am highly impressed by this working group's vision. The transition from reductionist biology to systems-level understanding requires exactly this kind of transdisciplinary synthesis. By bridging in vitro minimal systems (which give us baseline physical properties) with complex in vivo cellular imaging and in silico simulations, the authors are constructing a true multiscale pipeline. The idea of using the nucleus as the 'anchor point' across all three test cases is a brilliant strategic choice that provides a common biological coordinate system. My primary critique is the lack of clarity on how dynamic, time-series data (live-cell imaging) will be temporally aligned with static data spaces using Gromov-Wasserstein distances. Temporal dynamics are crucial for understanding emergence, but the methodology heavily biases towards spatial and morphological alignments."",
      ""strengths"": ""Visionary approach to multiscale biology, integrating minimal in vitro systems with complex cellular environments. The choice of the nucleus as a biological 'anchor' is excellent. Truly transdisciplinary team and methodology."",
      ""weakness"": ""The integration of dynamic, live-cell data with static images using GW distances is mentioned but not thoroughly explained. Temporal dynamics are critical to systems biology and require specialized handling."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A highly impressive, synthesis-driven proposal that models the future of systems biology by integrating cross-scale data. If the team can successfully align temporal dynamics with their spatial models, the impact will be transformative."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Examines emergent phenomena by explicitly comparing isolated components (minimal systems) with the integrated cellular context to identify what behaviors emerge only upon interaction."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The multiscale synthesis of in vitro, in silico, and in vivo data using advanced topological machine learning is a major paradigm shift."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall logic is extremely rigorous, though the specific mathematical handling of time-series/dynamic data within the GW framework needs more explicit formulation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Ambitious but manageable, largely due to the smart constraint of anchoring all studies around the well-characterized nucleus."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Flawless execution of the synthesis mandate. Integrates distinct data types and multiple disciplines to generate models that no single lab could achieve."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Well-identified data types and strong rationale for the specific biological systems, though dependent on public repository quality."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Good alignment with FAIR principles and reproducible science, with clear plans to make the resulting analytical framework publicly available.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"As a systems biologist focused on multiscale modeling, I am highly impressed by this working group's vision. The transition from reductionist biology to systems-level understanding requires exactly this kind of transdisciplinary synthesis. By bridging in vitro minimal systems (which give us baseline physical properties) with complex in vivo cellular imaging and in silico simulations, the authors are constructing a true multiscale pipeline. The idea of using the nucleus as the 'anchor point' across all three test cases is a brilliant strategic choice that provides a common biological coordinate system. My primary critique is the lack of clarity on how dynamic, time-series data (live-cell imaging) will be temporally aligned with static data spaces using Gromov-Wasserstein distances. Temporal dynamics are crucial for understanding emergence, but the methodology heavily biases towards spatial and morphological alignments.","Visionary approach to multiscale biology, integrating minimal in vitro systems with complex cellular environments. The choice of the nucleus as a biological 'anchor' is excellent. Truly transdisciplinary team and methodology.","The integration of dynamic, live-cell data with static images using GW distances is mentioned but not thoroughly explained. Temporal dynamics are critical to systems biology and require specialized handling.",4,"A highly impressive, synthesis-driven proposal that models the future of systems biology by integrating cross-scale data. If the team can successfully align temporal dynamics with their spatial models, the impact will be transformative.",5,Examines emergent phenomena by explicitly comparing isolated components (minimal systems) with the integrated cellular context to identify what behaviors emerge only upon interaction.,5,"The multiscale synthesis of in vitro, in silico, and in vivo data using advanced topological machine learning is a major paradigm shift.",4,"The overall logic is extremely rigorous, though the specific mathematical handling of time-series/dynamic data within the GW framework needs more explicit formulation.",4,"Ambitious but manageable, largely due to the smart constraint of anchoring all studies around the well-characterized nucleus.",5,Flawless execution of the synthesis mandate. Integrates distinct data types and multiple disciplines to generate models that no single lab could achieve.,4,"Well-identified data types and strong rationale for the specific biological systems, though dependent on public repository quality.",4,"Good alignment with FAIR principles and reproducible science, with clear plans to make the resulting analytical framework publicly available.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:41:33.493612,0,success,,"The proposal outlines a strong multiscale modeling pipeline that effectively bridges in vitro minimal systems, in vivo cellular imaging, and in silico simulations. Selecting the nucleus as a biological anchor point provides a highly effective common coordinate system for this transdisciplinary approach. However, the methodology heavily favors spatial alignments and lacks clarity regarding the temporal alignment of dynamic live-cell data with static data spaces.","The project features a transdisciplinary methodology that effectively connects minimal in vitro systems with complex cellular environments. Additionally, selecting the nucleus as a biological anchor provides an excellent, standardized coordinate system across the test cases.","The proposal lacks a clear explanation of how dynamic time-series data will be temporally aligned with static images using Gromov-Wasserstein distances. The current methodology is biased toward spatial and morphological alignments, failing to adequately address the specialized handling required for temporal dynamics."
baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,1,baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:18:29.243218,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior cell biologist specializing in organelle organization and nuclear architecture. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: whether principles of mesoscale cellular organization can be inferred by integrating existing multicomponent imaging, in vitro reconstitution, and simulation datasets. The focus on nucleus-centered systems is scientifically coherent, and the three test cases are biologically meaningful. I found the biological motivation compelling, particularly the attempt to move beyond gene- or protein-centric descriptions toward geometry, physical constraints, and inter-organelle relationships. The proposal is well aligned with the call in its emphasis on emergent cellular organization and community-scale synthesis. However, the biological hypotheses remain somewhat diffuse. The three systems are each complex, and it is not fully clear what concrete biological conclusions would constitute success beyond demonstrating a transfer-learning pipeline. The proposal would be stronger if it articulated more explicit expected biological readouts, validation benchmarks, and decision points for distinguishing intrinsic physical properties from interaction-driven organization. I am also concerned that the publicly available datasets needed for some comparisons, especially matched image, in vitro, and simulation datasets, may not exist in sufficient quantity or compatibility. Overall, this is a high-potential project with strong relevance to mesoscale emergence, but it needs sharper biological framing and more concrete dataset specification."",""strengths"":""The proposal tackles a central problem in cell biology: how cellular-scale organization emerges from interactions among organelles, cytoskeletal structures, condensates, and physical constraints. The nucleus-centered design provides a unifying biological anchor across three increasingly complex test cases. The proposed integration of imaging, in vitro biophysics, and simulations is ambitious and well suited to a multidisciplinary working group. The project is clearly beyond a single laboratory and requires expertise in cell biology, image analysis, machine learning, physical modeling, and data curation. The work has the potential to generate broadly useful tools and new insights into nuclear, nucleolar, mitochondrial, and cytoskeletal organization."",""weakness"":""The biological questions are broad and sometimes under-specified. The proposal does not sufficiently define what specific hypotheses will be tested for each system or what criteria will establish that a mesoscale principle has been discovered. Dataset availability is acknowledged as a limitation but not resolved with a sufficiently detailed inventory of public datasets, metadata, or compatibility constraints. The integration of in vitro and simulation data with cellular imaging is conceptually appealing but technically and biologically underspecified. Validation strategies are described only generally, and it is unclear how inferred missing features will be biologically validated using only existing data. The project may become method development rather than synthesis-driven discovery unless the biological endpoints are made more explicit."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong and highly relevant proposal that addresses emergent mesoscale organization using a multidisciplinary synthesis strategy. Its main weaknesses are insufficiently explicit biological hypotheses, uncertain dataset readiness, and an ambitious integration plan that may be difficult to complete in two years."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly targets mesoscale cellular organization as an emergent property of interactions among physical, structural, and chemical components. The chosen systems, especially nucleolus-nucleus, nucleus-microtubule-centrosome, and nucleus-mitochondria-condensate relationships, are highly appropriate for studying emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of multicomponent imaging, minimal systems, simulations, and transfer learning is novel and potentially significant. The shift from component-specific analysis to cross-component prediction could produce important conceptual advances. The novelty is somewhat tempered by lack of specificity about the new biological principles expected from each test case."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach uses plausible modern tools, including feature extraction, Gromov-Wasserstein distances, and transfer learning. However, the proposal lacks detailed benchmarking, validation, uncertainty quantification, and criteria for interpreting learned feature relationships biologically. The deep learning component is described at a high level rather than as a rigorous analysis plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering workflow development, three biological systems, three-component inference, incorporation of in vitro and simulation data, and integration into a larger nucleus-centered model. The timeline is plausible only for a limited proof of concept, not the full set of stated outcomes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly synthesis-focused and explicitly relies on existing public image datasets, in vitro data, simulations, and literature-derived knowledge. It does not propose new experimental data generation, although the phrase 'new simulations will be developed' raises a point needing clarification within the call's constraints."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies broad classes of data and some biological systems, but it does not provide a sufficiently concrete list of public datasets, repositories, accession numbers, metadata standards, or criteria for inclusion. Dataset availability is treated as a known risk, especially for in vitro and in silico data."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to an open-source workflow, integrated datasets, and public tools, which aligns well with NCEMS principles. More detail is needed on licenses, workflow reproducibility, containerization, data provenance, documentation, and community governance.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist and machine learning researcher focused on multimodal biological data integration. The proposal presents an ambitious machine learning framework for integrating heterogeneous cellular datasets using Gromov-Wasserstein distances, transfer learning, and mutual prediction. I appreciate the recognition that many biological datasets do not inhabit a shared feature space and that cross-space alignment methods may be useful for cell image synthesis. The use of complete datasets to learn relationships that can then be applied to incomplete datasets is a strong conceptual match to the data synthesis mission. However, from a computational perspective, the approach is not yet sufficiently specified to assess feasibility. GW alignment, GANs, and transfer learning are mentioned, but the proposal does not define model architectures, training objectives, sample size requirements, baselines, evaluation metrics, or failure modes. There is also a risk of overinterpreting predictive relationships as mechanistic or causal relationships. I would have liked to see a clearer computational workplan: what features will be extracted, how missing modalities will be predicted, what held-out datasets will be used, how uncertainty will be quantified, and how domain shift across cell types, imaging platforms, perturbations, and laboratories will be handled. The proposal is exciting and well suited to NCEMS if strengthened by a more rigorous data science plan."",""strengths"":""The computational concept is timely and potentially powerful. Gromov-Wasserstein methods are appropriate for comparing datasets in different feature spaces, and transfer learning from complete to incomplete datasets addresses a common limitation of public cell imaging resources. The proposal recognizes the need for high-performance computing and data management infrastructure. If successful, the pipeline could be widely reusable for multimodal biological synthesis beyond the three examples. The working group format is justified by the need to combine mathematical, computational, cell biological, and biophysical expertise."",""weakness"":""The machine learning plan is too high-level. The proposal does not specify the precise prediction tasks, model architectures, evaluation criteria, baselines, cross-validation design, or statistical treatment of uncertainty. It invokes deep learning, GANs, GW distances, and agent-based modeling without clearly explaining how these components will interact in a reproducible workflow. There is insufficient attention to domain shift, batch effects, imaging modality differences, segmentation errors, class imbalance, and data leakage. The computational resource request of at least 10,000 GPU hours is not justified by a concrete model training plan. The proposal does not sufficiently distinguish correlation, prediction, and mechanism."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually exciting computational synthesis proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS, but the analytical plan is underdeveloped. The proposal needs clearer prediction tasks, benchmarks, validation strategies, and safeguards against overinterpretation before it can be considered fully rigorous."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal is relevant to emergent phenomena because it aims to infer how mesoscale organization arises from relationships among cellular components. However, the current computational framing risks emphasizing prediction over mechanistic emergence unless stronger links to physical and biological principles are established."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Applying GW-based transfer learning to heterogeneous cell imaging, in vitro, and simulation datasets is innovative. The significance could be high because incomplete public datasets are common and underused. The significance depends on whether the method produces interpretable and validated biological insights rather than only technical demonstrations."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks sufficient methodological detail. There are no clearly stated loss functions, model evaluation metrics, baselines such as canonical correlation analysis or contrastive learning, negative controls, held-out validation schemes, or uncertainty measures. The proposal also does not address key practical issues in image-based ML, including segmentation quality, batch effects, and domain adaptation."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""Developing a robust multimodal transfer learning pipeline and applying it to three complex biological systems in two years is ambitious. A narrower initial scope, perhaps one well-curated two-component system followed by one three-component extension, would be more realistic."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly centered on synthesis of existing public data and reuse of incomplete datasets. The strategy of extracting new information from already generated imaging and simulation resources is well aligned with the funding call."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal names general data types and biological systems but does not identify enough specific datasets to evaluate feasibility. It should include candidate repositories, dataset sizes, imaging channels, metadata, perturbations, and whether the same components are measured in compatible cellular contexts."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The plan to produce an open-source pipeline and integrated datasets is positive. More detail is needed on reproducible ML practices, including versioned data releases, containers, workflow managers, trained model deposition, model cards, and documentation for reuse by the broader community.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior biophysicist specializing in phase separation, cytoskeletal self-organization, and physical modeling of cells. I strongly support the premise that mesoscale cellular organization cannot be understood solely from molecular inventories and requires integration of geometry, physical forces, material properties, and cellular context. The proposal is especially interesting in its inclusion of minimal systems and simulations as comparators to cellular images. The nucleolus and mitochondrial nucleoid cases are well suited to questions about condensate material properties, while the centrosome-microtubule system brings in active cytoskeletal organization. Nevertheless, the proposal currently does not present a sufficiently rigorous physical modeling framework. It speaks broadly of surface tension, fluidity, morphology, physical feature spaces, and agent-based models, but does not define which physical quantities will be extracted, how comparable they are across images, reconstitutions, and simulations, or how competing physical models will be evaluated. I am also concerned by the statement that new simulations will be developed; this may be outside the spirit of a synthesis-only call unless these are analyses of previously published models or clearly framed as computational reanalysis rather than new data generation. The proposal has strong conceptual fit but needs sharper physical hypotheses and better integration between machine learning and mechanistic modeling."",""strengths"":""The project asks important physical questions about the relative contributions of intrinsic component properties and inter-component interactions to cellular organization. The inclusion of minimal systems and simulations is a major strength because it could help distinguish what can arise from intrinsic material properties versus cellular context. The three chosen systems span condensates, membranes, cytoskeleton, and organelles, providing a rich test bed for principles of mesoscale organization. The focus on the nucleus as a common anchor is biologically and physically sensible. The project would benefit from the interdisciplinary working group environment described in the call."",""weakness"":""The physical modeling component is not yet adequately formulated. The proposal does not specify the physical observables, nondimensional parameters, model classes, or tests that will distinguish alternative mechanisms. There is a danger that morphological similarity metrics will be treated as evidence of shared physics without sufficient mechanistic validation. The integration of in vitro and in silico data with cell images is conceptually promising but operationally vague. Dataset compatibility across different experimental conditions, length scales, and measurement modalities is a major unresolved issue. The plan to develop new simulations needs clarification to ensure compliance with a no-new-data synthesis call."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually strong and biologically exciting synthesis project that could advance physical principles of mesoscale cellular organization. The main limitations are vague physical modeling details and uncertainty about how mechanistic conclusions will be validated from heterogeneous existing data."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""This proposal is highly relevant to emergence. It explicitly examines how mesoscale organization arises from interactions among condensates, organelles, cytoskeletal structures, and physical constraints, which is at the heart of emergent molecular and cellular phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":5,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed synthesis of cellular imaging with minimal-system biophysics and simulations to infer principles of mesoscale organization is highly novel and significant. If executed well, it could provide a new paradigm for linking image phenotypes to physical mechanisms."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal contains promising methodological ideas but lacks detailed physical hypotheses, model selection criteria, quantitative observables, and validation strategies. The link between GW-based feature alignment and mechanistic biophysical inference remains underdeveloped."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is broad, covering three biological systems, multiple data types, physical modeling, machine learning, and literature synthesis. Two years may be sufficient for a prototype and one or two focused discoveries, but not for the full integrated nucleus-centered model envisioned."",""synthesis_focus_score"":4,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is largely synthesis-focused and based on existing datasets. However, the mention of developing new simulations introduces ambiguity. If these simulations are new computational experiments producing new data, the team should clarify whether this is permissible within NCEMS expectations or reframe the activity as reanalysis of existing simulation outputs and models."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies relevant categories of imaging, reconstitution, and simulation data but does not provide enough detail on specific public sources, measurement compatibility, or metadata sufficiency. The acknowledged need for further data scouting is appropriate but weakens feasibility."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source tools and integrated datasets is strong. Additional detail is needed about how physical models, parameter files, simulation-derived features, and literature-curated data will be shared in reproducible formats.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior bioimage informatics and data infrastructure scientist specializing in public imaging repositories, metadata, and reproducible workflows. This proposal is very well matched to the growing opportunity in public bioimage data reuse. The authors correctly identify that large image datasets are underutilized for secondary synthesis and that incomplete multicomponent datasets create an opportunity for cross-dataset prediction. The project would clearly require infrastructure support for storage, compute, curation, and workflow optimization, all of which fit the NCEMS model. However, as a bioimage data proposal, it currently underestimates the importance of metadata harmonization, image preprocessing, segmentation quality, feature standardization, and provenance tracking. The data plan is not specific enough. The proposal mentions high-resolution imaging datasets, live-cell data, fibroblasts, HGPS patients, in vitro data, and simulations, but it does not list the principal public datasets, their formats, channels, sample sizes, licenses, image resolutions, perturbations, or associated metadata. The success of transfer learning across public imaging datasets will depend heavily on these practical details. I am enthusiastic about the concept, but I would require a much more concrete data inventory and curation plan before judging the project fully feasible."",""strengths"":""The proposal has a strong synthesis orientation and recognizes the need to integrate public image datasets that are currently difficult to compare. It makes a compelling case for NCEMS support in compute, storage, data transfer, and curation. The focus on open-source workflows and reusable integrated datasets could have substantial community value. The project brings together image analysis, machine learning, biophysics, and cell biology in a way that is appropriate for a working group. The use of incomplete datasets is particularly relevant because many public bioimage resources contain only subsets of the components needed for systems-level analysis."",""weakness"":""The data identification and curation plan is underdeveloped. There is no detailed inventory of datasets, repositories, file formats, metadata fields, image acquisition conditions, segmentation annotations, quality control procedures, or licensing constraints. The proposal does not explain how batch effects, microscope differences, staining protocols, cell line differences, and cell state heterogeneity will be normalized or modeled. It is unclear who will perform the substantial curation labor and how trainees will be trained in these practices. The open science plan is positive but generic and lacks specific workflow, metadata, and FAIR data implementation details."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is highly appropriate for data synthesis and could produce valuable community resources, but feasibility depends on bioimage metadata and curation details that are currently missing. The proposal should be strengthened with a concrete dataset inventory, QC plan, and reproducible workflow architecture."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The biological focus on mesoscale cellular organization is relevant to emergent phenomena. From the data infrastructure perspective, the emergent biology is compelling, though the proposal needs clearer operational definitions of the emergent features to be measured from images."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Using public imaging datasets for cross-component prediction and mesoscale synthesis is novel and significant. The potential community impact is high if the workflow becomes reusable for other systems. The significance is limited by uncertainty about whether the public data are sufficiently standardized for the intended analyses."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposed computational methods are promising, but the rigor of the bioimage analysis pipeline is not fully described. The proposal needs more detail on segmentation, feature extraction, QC, metadata harmonization, batch correction, provenance, and benchmark datasets."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The proposed storage and compute needs are plausible for large imaging data, but the two-year plan does not fully account for the time required to identify, download, curate, harmonize, and document heterogeneous public datasets before model development can proceed."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""This is a strong synthesis proposal. It explicitly leverages existing public image, in vitro, simulation, and literature-derived data and seeks to create new value through integration rather than new experiments."",""data_identification_score"":2,""data_identification_justification"":""This is the weakest area. The proposal identifies broad classes of data but not specific datasets or repositories in enough detail to assess feasibility. A successful bioimage synthesis project requires a much more explicit data table with dataset sources, components imaged, metadata availability, quality, scale, licenses, and anticipated preprocessing needs."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The open-source commitment is encouraging, but the plan is generic. The proposal should specify FAIR metadata standards, repository choices, workflow systems, containerization, persistent identifiers, versioning, documentation, and policies for sharing derived features and trained models.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior systems cell biologist and team-science reviewer with experience in collaborative synthesis centers and trainee training. This proposal fits the spirit of the call in several important ways: it addresses a broad scientific question that cannot be solved by a single lab, relies on existing public data, requires a multidisciplinary team, and aims to produce reusable computational workflows. I was particularly pleased to see attention to systematic literature synthesis and to the possibility of pooling smaller datasets, which are often overlooked in data synthesis proposals. The working group justification is credible, and the project could provide excellent training opportunities in data science, image analysis, biophysics, and collaborative research. That said, the proposal does not provide enough detail about the actual working group structure, trainee roles, governance, meeting cadence, division of labor, or community engagement. It also lacks a clear training plan despite the call's emphasis on developing the future data-savvy workforce. Scientifically, the proposal is ambitious and exciting, but it would benefit from clearer milestones, deliverables, and go/no-go criteria. I view this as a strong but somewhat under-organized proposal for a working group."",""strengths"":""The project clearly requires multiple domains of expertise and is appropriate for a collaborative working group. It addresses an important and broadly significant question in cell organization and emergence. The planned outputs, including a generalizable framework, predictive models, integrated datasets, and open-source workflows, are well aligned with NCEMS priorities. The proposal recognizes the need for community-scale curation and synthesis, including smaller literature-derived datasets. The project could provide rich interdisciplinary training opportunities if formalized."",""weakness"":""The working group plan is not sufficiently developed. The proposal does not describe team composition in detail, governance, how conflicts or technical decisions will be managed, how trainees will be integrated, or how the broader community will be engaged. The training component is implicit rather than explicit. Milestones are broad, and the two-year timeline lacks concrete deliverables by quarter or by test case. The rationale for NCEMS support is reasonable but could better articulate why existing collaborations or institutional resources cannot accomplish the work. The project risks spreading effort across too many biological systems and computational methods without adequate prioritization."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A compelling synthesis and team-science proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS goals, but it needs more concrete project management, training, and milestone plans. Its scientific ambition is high, and success would depend on disciplined prioritization and strong coordination."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly addresses emergent cellular architecture and how collective interactions among components generate mesoscale organization. This is highly responsive to the emergence-focused mission of the call."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed integration of public imaging, in vitro, simulation, and literature data through transfer learning is innovative and could significantly influence how cell organization is studied. The significance is high, though the proposal could better define the specific long-standing puzzles it will solve."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The general approach is credible, but the proposal lacks detailed milestones, validation metrics, and decision criteria. The methodological breadth is impressive but not yet organized into a fully rigorous execution plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":2,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is very large for two years. Developing a general computational framework, applying it to three complex systems, integrating multiple data modalities, conducting literature synthesis, and building a larger nucleus-centered model may exceed the capacity of a working group unless the scope is narrowed and staged."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly focused on synthesis of existing data and explicitly avoids new experimental data generation. It makes good use of public datasets and aims to create new value through integration, curation, and secondary analysis."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies data types and biological domains but does not provide enough concrete information about the datasets to be synthesized. Further data scouting is acknowledged, but the feasibility of the full plan cannot be assessed without more detail."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source methods, public datasets, and reproducible workflows is well aligned with the call. However, the proposal should include a more specific open science implementation plan and a clearer description of trainee involvement in reproducible research practices.""}]}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 1: Senior cell biologist specializing in organelle organization and nuclear architecture. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: whether principles of mesoscale cellular organization can be inferred by integrating existing multicomponent imaging, in vitro reconstitution, and simulation datasets. The focus on nucleus-centered systems is scientifically coherent, and the three test cases are biologically meaningful. I found the biological motivation compelling, particularly the attempt to move beyond gene- or protein-centric descriptions toward geometry, physical constraints, and inter-organelle relationships. The proposal is well aligned with the call in its emphasis on emergent cellular organization and community-scale synthesis. However, the biological hypotheses remain somewhat diffuse. The three systems are each complex, and it is not fully clear what concrete biological conclusions would constitute success beyond demonstrating a transfer-learning pipeline. The proposal would be stronger if it articulated more explicit expected biological readouts, validation benchmarks, and decision points for distinguishing intrinsic physical properties from interaction-driven organization. I am also concerned that the publicly available datasets needed for some comparisons, especially matched image, in vitro, and simulation datasets, may not exist in sufficient quantity or compatibility. Overall, this is a high-potential project with strong relevance to mesoscale emergence, but it needs sharper biological framing and more concrete dataset specification.","The proposal tackles a central problem in cell biology: how cellular-scale organization emerges from interactions among organelles, cytoskeletal structures, condensates, and physical constraints. The nucleus-centered design provides a unifying biological anchor across three increasingly complex test cases. The proposed integration of imaging, in vitro biophysics, and simulations is ambitious and well suited to a multidisciplinary working group. The project is clearly beyond a single laboratory and requires expertise in cell biology, image analysis, machine learning, physical modeling, and data curation. The work has the potential to generate broadly useful tools and new insights into nuclear, nucleolar, mitochondrial, and cytoskeletal organization.","The biological questions are broad and sometimes under-specified. The proposal does not sufficiently define what specific hypotheses will be tested for each system or what criteria will establish that a mesoscale principle has been discovered. Dataset availability is acknowledged as a limitation but not resolved with a sufficiently detailed inventory of public datasets, metadata, or compatibility constraints. The integration of in vitro and simulation data with cellular imaging is conceptually appealing but technically and biologically underspecified. Validation strategies are described only generally, and it is unclear how inferred missing features will be biologically validated using only existing data. The project may become method development rather than synthesis-driven discovery unless the biological endpoints are made more explicit.",4,"A strong and highly relevant proposal that addresses emergent mesoscale organization using a multidisciplinary synthesis strategy. Its main weaknesses are insufficiently explicit biological hypotheses, uncertain dataset readiness, and an ambitious integration plan that may be difficult to complete in two years.",5,"The proposal directly targets mesoscale cellular organization as an emergent property of interactions among physical, structural, and chemical components. The chosen systems, especially nucleolus-nucleus, nucleus-microtubule-centrosome, and nucleus-mitochondria-condensate relationships, are highly appropriate for studying emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences.",4,"The integration of multicomponent imaging, minimal systems, simulations, and transfer learning is novel and potentially significant. The shift from component-specific analysis to cross-component prediction could produce important conceptual advances. The novelty is somewhat tempered by lack of specificity about the new biological principles expected from each test case.",3,"The approach uses plausible modern tools, including feature extraction, Gromov-Wasserstein distances, and transfer learning. However, the proposal lacks detailed benchmarking, validation, uncertainty quantification, and criteria for interpreting learned feature relationships biologically. The deep learning component is described at a high level rather than as a rigorous analysis plan.",3,"The scope is ambitious for two years, covering workflow development, three biological systems, three-component inference, incorporation of in vitro and simulation data, and integration into a larger nucleus-centered model. The timeline is plausible only for a limited proof of concept, not the full set of stated outcomes.",5,"The proposal is strongly synthesis-focused and explicitly relies on existing public image datasets, in vitro data, simulations, and literature-derived knowledge. It does not propose new experimental data generation, although the phrase 'new simulations will be developed' raises a point needing clarification within the call's constraints.",3,"The proposal identifies broad classes of data and some biological systems, but it does not provide a sufficiently concrete list of public datasets, repositories, accession numbers, metadata standards, or criteria for inclusion. Dataset availability is treated as a known risk, especially for in vitro and in silico data.",4,"The proposal commits to an open-source workflow, integrated datasets, and public tools, which aligns well with NCEMS principles. More detail is needed on licenses, workflow reproducibility, containerization, data provenance, documentation, and community governance.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:41:41.987670,0,success,,"This proposal presents a highly relevant and ambitious approach to understanding mesoscale cellular organization by integrating multicomponent imaging, in vitro reconstitution, and simulation datasets around nucleus-centered systems. While the scientific motivation and multidisciplinary focus align well with the funding call, the specific biological hypotheses and expected outcomes remain somewhat diffuse. To maximize its potential for synthesis-driven discovery, the project requires clearer biological framing, more explicit validation benchmarks, and a concrete specification of available datasets.","The project addresses a fundamental question in cell biology regarding the emergence of cellular organization using a conceptually unified, nucleus-centered design. The ambitious integration of imaging, biophysics, and physical modeling is well-suited for a multidisciplinary working group. Furthermore, the work demonstrates strong potential to yield broadly applicable tools and novel insights into organelle and cytoskeletal architecture.","The core biological hypotheses and criteria for successfully identifying mesoscale principles are overly broad and under-specified. Additionally, the proposal lacks a detailed inventory confirming the availability and compatibility of the necessary public datasets for imaging, in vitro, and simulation comparisons. Finally, the technical integration and validation strategies are vaguely defined, raising the risk that the project will lean toward method development rather than biological discovery."
baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::002,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,2,baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:18:29.243218,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior cell biologist specializing in organelle organization and nuclear architecture. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: whether principles of mesoscale cellular organization can be inferred by integrating existing multicomponent imaging, in vitro reconstitution, and simulation datasets. The focus on nucleus-centered systems is scientifically coherent, and the three test cases are biologically meaningful. I found the biological motivation compelling, particularly the attempt to move beyond gene- or protein-centric descriptions toward geometry, physical constraints, and inter-organelle relationships. The proposal is well aligned with the call in its emphasis on emergent cellular organization and community-scale synthesis. However, the biological hypotheses remain somewhat diffuse. The three systems are each complex, and it is not fully clear what concrete biological conclusions would constitute success beyond demonstrating a transfer-learning pipeline. The proposal would be stronger if it articulated more explicit expected biological readouts, validation benchmarks, and decision points for distinguishing intrinsic physical properties from interaction-driven organization. I am also concerned that the publicly available datasets needed for some comparisons, especially matched image, in vitro, and simulation datasets, may not exist in sufficient quantity or compatibility. Overall, this is a high-potential project with strong relevance to mesoscale emergence, but it needs sharper biological framing and more concrete dataset specification."",""strengths"":""The proposal tackles a central problem in cell biology: how cellular-scale organization emerges from interactions among organelles, cytoskeletal structures, condensates, and physical constraints. The nucleus-centered design provides a unifying biological anchor across three increasingly complex test cases. The proposed integration of imaging, in vitro biophysics, and simulations is ambitious and well suited to a multidisciplinary working group. The project is clearly beyond a single laboratory and requires expertise in cell biology, image analysis, machine learning, physical modeling, and data curation. The work has the potential to generate broadly useful tools and new insights into nuclear, nucleolar, mitochondrial, and cytoskeletal organization."",""weakness"":""The biological questions are broad and sometimes under-specified. The proposal does not sufficiently define what specific hypotheses will be tested for each system or what criteria will establish that a mesoscale principle has been discovered. Dataset availability is acknowledged as a limitation but not resolved with a sufficiently detailed inventory of public datasets, metadata, or compatibility constraints. The integration of in vitro and simulation data with cellular imaging is conceptually appealing but technically and biologically underspecified. Validation strategies are described only generally, and it is unclear how inferred missing features will be biologically validated using only existing data. The project may become method development rather than synthesis-driven discovery unless the biological endpoints are made more explicit."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong and highly relevant proposal that addresses emergent mesoscale organization using a multidisciplinary synthesis strategy. Its main weaknesses are insufficiently explicit biological hypotheses, uncertain dataset readiness, and an ambitious integration plan that may be difficult to complete in two years."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly targets mesoscale cellular organization as an emergent property of interactions among physical, structural, and chemical components. The chosen systems, especially nucleolus-nucleus, nucleus-microtubule-centrosome, and nucleus-mitochondria-condensate relationships, are highly appropriate for studying emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of multicomponent imaging, minimal systems, simulations, and transfer learning is novel and potentially significant. The shift from component-specific analysis to cross-component prediction could produce important conceptual advances. The novelty is somewhat tempered by lack of specificity about the new biological principles expected from each test case."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach uses plausible modern tools, including feature extraction, Gromov-Wasserstein distances, and transfer learning. However, the proposal lacks detailed benchmarking, validation, uncertainty quantification, and criteria for interpreting learned feature relationships biologically. The deep learning component is described at a high level rather than as a rigorous analysis plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering workflow development, three biological systems, three-component inference, incorporation of in vitro and simulation data, and integration into a larger nucleus-centered model. The timeline is plausible only for a limited proof of concept, not the full set of stated outcomes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly synthesis-focused and explicitly relies on existing public image datasets, in vitro data, simulations, and literature-derived knowledge. It does not propose new experimental data generation, although the phrase 'new simulations will be developed' raises a point needing clarification within the call's constraints."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies broad classes of data and some biological systems, but it does not provide a sufficiently concrete list of public datasets, repositories, accession numbers, metadata standards, or criteria for inclusion. Dataset availability is treated as a known risk, especially for in vitro and in silico data."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to an open-source workflow, integrated datasets, and public tools, which aligns well with NCEMS principles. More detail is needed on licenses, workflow reproducibility, containerization, data provenance, documentation, and community governance.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist and machine learning researcher focused on multimodal biological data integration. The proposal presents an ambitious machine learning framework for integrating heterogeneous cellular datasets using Gromov-Wasserstein distances, transfer learning, and mutual prediction. I appreciate the recognition that many biological datasets do not inhabit a shared feature space and that cross-space alignment methods may be useful for cell image synthesis. The use of complete datasets to learn relationships that can then be applied to incomplete datasets is a strong conceptual match to the data synthesis mission. However, from a computational perspective, the approach is not yet sufficiently specified to assess feasibility. GW alignment, GANs, and transfer learning are mentioned, but the proposal does not define model architectures, training objectives, sample size requirements, baselines, evaluation metrics, or failure modes. There is also a risk of overinterpreting predictive relationships as mechanistic or causal relationships. I would have liked to see a clearer computational workplan: what features will be extracted, how missing modalities will be predicted, what held-out datasets will be used, how uncertainty will be quantified, and how domain shift across cell types, imaging platforms, perturbations, and laboratories will be handled. The proposal is exciting and well suited to NCEMS if strengthened by a more rigorous data science plan."",""strengths"":""The computational concept is timely and potentially powerful. Gromov-Wasserstein methods are appropriate for comparing datasets in different feature spaces, and transfer learning from complete to incomplete datasets addresses a common limitation of public cell imaging resources. The proposal recognizes the need for high-performance computing and data management infrastructure. If successful, the pipeline could be widely reusable for multimodal biological synthesis beyond the three examples. The working group format is justified by the need to combine mathematical, computational, cell biological, and biophysical expertise."",""weakness"":""The machine learning plan is too high-level. The proposal does not specify the precise prediction tasks, model architectures, evaluation criteria, baselines, cross-validation design, or statistical treatment of uncertainty. It invokes deep learning, GANs, GW distances, and agent-based modeling without clearly explaining how these components will interact in a reproducible workflow. There is insufficient attention to domain shift, batch effects, imaging modality differences, segmentation errors, class imbalance, and data leakage. The computational resource request of at least 10,000 GPU hours is not justified by a concrete model training plan. The proposal does not sufficiently distinguish correlation, prediction, and mechanism."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually exciting computational synthesis proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS, but the analytical plan is underdeveloped. The proposal needs clearer prediction tasks, benchmarks, validation strategies, and safeguards against overinterpretation before it can be considered fully rigorous."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal is relevant to emergent phenomena because it aims to infer how mesoscale organization arises from relationships among cellular components. However, the current computational framing risks emphasizing prediction over mechanistic emergence unless stronger links to physical and biological principles are established."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Applying GW-based transfer learning to heterogeneous cell imaging, in vitro, and simulation datasets is innovative. The significance could be high because incomplete public datasets are common and underused. The significance depends on whether the method produces interpretable and validated biological insights rather than only technical demonstrations."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks sufficient methodological detail. There are no clearly stated loss functions, model evaluation metrics, baselines such as canonical correlation analysis or contrastive learning, negative controls, held-out validation schemes, or uncertainty measures. The proposal also does not address key practical issues in image-based ML, including segmentation quality, batch effects, and domain adaptation."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""Developing a robust multimodal transfer learning pipeline and applying it to three complex biological systems in two years is ambitious. A narrower initial scope, perhaps one well-curated two-component system followed by one three-component extension, would be more realistic."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly centered on synthesis of existing public data and reuse of incomplete datasets. The strategy of extracting new information from already generated imaging and simulation resources is well aligned with the funding call."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal names general data types and biological systems but does not identify enough specific datasets to evaluate feasibility. It should include candidate repositories, dataset sizes, imaging channels, metadata, perturbations, and whether the same components are measured in compatible cellular contexts."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The plan to produce an open-source pipeline and integrated datasets is positive. More detail is needed on reproducible ML practices, including versioned data releases, containers, workflow managers, trained model deposition, model cards, and documentation for reuse by the broader community.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior biophysicist specializing in phase separation, cytoskeletal self-organization, and physical modeling of cells. I strongly support the premise that mesoscale cellular organization cannot be understood solely from molecular inventories and requires integration of geometry, physical forces, material properties, and cellular context. The proposal is especially interesting in its inclusion of minimal systems and simulations as comparators to cellular images. The nucleolus and mitochondrial nucleoid cases are well suited to questions about condensate material properties, while the centrosome-microtubule system brings in active cytoskeletal organization. Nevertheless, the proposal currently does not present a sufficiently rigorous physical modeling framework. It speaks broadly of surface tension, fluidity, morphology, physical feature spaces, and agent-based models, but does not define which physical quantities will be extracted, how comparable they are across images, reconstitutions, and simulations, or how competing physical models will be evaluated. I am also concerned by the statement that new simulations will be developed; this may be outside the spirit of a synthesis-only call unless these are analyses of previously published models or clearly framed as computational reanalysis rather than new data generation. The proposal has strong conceptual fit but needs sharper physical hypotheses and better integration between machine learning and mechanistic modeling."",""strengths"":""The project asks important physical questions about the relative contributions of intrinsic component properties and inter-component interactions to cellular organization. The inclusion of minimal systems and simulations is a major strength because it could help distinguish what can arise from intrinsic material properties versus cellular context. The three chosen systems span condensates, membranes, cytoskeleton, and organelles, providing a rich test bed for principles of mesoscale organization. The focus on the nucleus as a common anchor is biologically and physically sensible. The project would benefit from the interdisciplinary working group environment described in the call."",""weakness"":""The physical modeling component is not yet adequately formulated. The proposal does not specify the physical observables, nondimensional parameters, model classes, or tests that will distinguish alternative mechanisms. There is a danger that morphological similarity metrics will be treated as evidence of shared physics without sufficient mechanistic validation. The integration of in vitro and in silico data with cell images is conceptually promising but operationally vague. Dataset compatibility across different experimental conditions, length scales, and measurement modalities is a major unresolved issue. The plan to develop new simulations needs clarification to ensure compliance with a no-new-data synthesis call."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually strong and biologically exciting synthesis project that could advance physical principles of mesoscale cellular organization. The main limitations are vague physical modeling details and uncertainty about how mechanistic conclusions will be validated from heterogeneous existing data."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""This proposal is highly relevant to emergence. It explicitly examines how mesoscale organization arises from interactions among condensates, organelles, cytoskeletal structures, and physical constraints, which is at the heart of emergent molecular and cellular phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":5,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed synthesis of cellular imaging with minimal-system biophysics and simulations to infer principles of mesoscale organization is highly novel and significant. If executed well, it could provide a new paradigm for linking image phenotypes to physical mechanisms."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal contains promising methodological ideas but lacks detailed physical hypotheses, model selection criteria, quantitative observables, and validation strategies. The link between GW-based feature alignment and mechanistic biophysical inference remains underdeveloped."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is broad, covering three biological systems, multiple data types, physical modeling, machine learning, and literature synthesis. Two years may be sufficient for a prototype and one or two focused discoveries, but not for the full integrated nucleus-centered model envisioned."",""synthesis_focus_score"":4,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is largely synthesis-focused and based on existing datasets. However, the mention of developing new simulations introduces ambiguity. If these simulations are new computational experiments producing new data, the team should clarify whether this is permissible within NCEMS expectations or reframe the activity as reanalysis of existing simulation outputs and models."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies relevant categories of imaging, reconstitution, and simulation data but does not provide enough detail on specific public sources, measurement compatibility, or metadata sufficiency. The acknowledged need for further data scouting is appropriate but weakens feasibility."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source tools and integrated datasets is strong. Additional detail is needed about how physical models, parameter files, simulation-derived features, and literature-curated data will be shared in reproducible formats.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior bioimage informatics and data infrastructure scientist specializing in public imaging repositories, metadata, and reproducible workflows. This proposal is very well matched to the growing opportunity in public bioimage data reuse. The authors correctly identify that large image datasets are underutilized for secondary synthesis and that incomplete multicomponent datasets create an opportunity for cross-dataset prediction. The project would clearly require infrastructure support for storage, compute, curation, and workflow optimization, all of which fit the NCEMS model. However, as a bioimage data proposal, it currently underestimates the importance of metadata harmonization, image preprocessing, segmentation quality, feature standardization, and provenance tracking. The data plan is not specific enough. The proposal mentions high-resolution imaging datasets, live-cell data, fibroblasts, HGPS patients, in vitro data, and simulations, but it does not list the principal public datasets, their formats, channels, sample sizes, licenses, image resolutions, perturbations, or associated metadata. The success of transfer learning across public imaging datasets will depend heavily on these practical details. I am enthusiastic about the concept, but I would require a much more concrete data inventory and curation plan before judging the project fully feasible."",""strengths"":""The proposal has a strong synthesis orientation and recognizes the need to integrate public image datasets that are currently difficult to compare. It makes a compelling case for NCEMS support in compute, storage, data transfer, and curation. The focus on open-source workflows and reusable integrated datasets could have substantial community value. The project brings together image analysis, machine learning, biophysics, and cell biology in a way that is appropriate for a working group. The use of incomplete datasets is particularly relevant because many public bioimage resources contain only subsets of the components needed for systems-level analysis."",""weakness"":""The data identification and curation plan is underdeveloped. There is no detailed inventory of datasets, repositories, file formats, metadata fields, image acquisition conditions, segmentation annotations, quality control procedures, or licensing constraints. The proposal does not explain how batch effects, microscope differences, staining protocols, cell line differences, and cell state heterogeneity will be normalized or modeled. It is unclear who will perform the substantial curation labor and how trainees will be trained in these practices. The open science plan is positive but generic and lacks specific workflow, metadata, and FAIR data implementation details."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is highly appropriate for data synthesis and could produce valuable community resources, but feasibility depends on bioimage metadata and curation details that are currently missing. The proposal should be strengthened with a concrete dataset inventory, QC plan, and reproducible workflow architecture."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The biological focus on mesoscale cellular organization is relevant to emergent phenomena. From the data infrastructure perspective, the emergent biology is compelling, though the proposal needs clearer operational definitions of the emergent features to be measured from images."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Using public imaging datasets for cross-component prediction and mesoscale synthesis is novel and significant. The potential community impact is high if the workflow becomes reusable for other systems. The significance is limited by uncertainty about whether the public data are sufficiently standardized for the intended analyses."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposed computational methods are promising, but the rigor of the bioimage analysis pipeline is not fully described. The proposal needs more detail on segmentation, feature extraction, QC, metadata harmonization, batch correction, provenance, and benchmark datasets."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The proposed storage and compute needs are plausible for large imaging data, but the two-year plan does not fully account for the time required to identify, download, curate, harmonize, and document heterogeneous public datasets before model development can proceed."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""This is a strong synthesis proposal. It explicitly leverages existing public image, in vitro, simulation, and literature-derived data and seeks to create new value through integration rather than new experiments."",""data_identification_score"":2,""data_identification_justification"":""This is the weakest area. The proposal identifies broad classes of data but not specific datasets or repositories in enough detail to assess feasibility. A successful bioimage synthesis project requires a much more explicit data table with dataset sources, components imaged, metadata availability, quality, scale, licenses, and anticipated preprocessing needs."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The open-source commitment is encouraging, but the plan is generic. The proposal should specify FAIR metadata standards, repository choices, workflow systems, containerization, persistent identifiers, versioning, documentation, and policies for sharing derived features and trained models.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior systems cell biologist and team-science reviewer with experience in collaborative synthesis centers and trainee training. This proposal fits the spirit of the call in several important ways: it addresses a broad scientific question that cannot be solved by a single lab, relies on existing public data, requires a multidisciplinary team, and aims to produce reusable computational workflows. I was particularly pleased to see attention to systematic literature synthesis and to the possibility of pooling smaller datasets, which are often overlooked in data synthesis proposals. The working group justification is credible, and the project could provide excellent training opportunities in data science, image analysis, biophysics, and collaborative research. That said, the proposal does not provide enough detail about the actual working group structure, trainee roles, governance, meeting cadence, division of labor, or community engagement. It also lacks a clear training plan despite the call's emphasis on developing the future data-savvy workforce. Scientifically, the proposal is ambitious and exciting, but it would benefit from clearer milestones, deliverables, and go/no-go criteria. I view this as a strong but somewhat under-organized proposal for a working group."",""strengths"":""The project clearly requires multiple domains of expertise and is appropriate for a collaborative working group. It addresses an important and broadly significant question in cell organization and emergence. The planned outputs, including a generalizable framework, predictive models, integrated datasets, and open-source workflows, are well aligned with NCEMS priorities. The proposal recognizes the need for community-scale curation and synthesis, including smaller literature-derived datasets. The project could provide rich interdisciplinary training opportunities if formalized."",""weakness"":""The working group plan is not sufficiently developed. The proposal does not describe team composition in detail, governance, how conflicts or technical decisions will be managed, how trainees will be integrated, or how the broader community will be engaged. The training component is implicit rather than explicit. Milestones are broad, and the two-year timeline lacks concrete deliverables by quarter or by test case. The rationale for NCEMS support is reasonable but could better articulate why existing collaborations or institutional resources cannot accomplish the work. The project risks spreading effort across too many biological systems and computational methods without adequate prioritization."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A compelling synthesis and team-science proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS goals, but it needs more concrete project management, training, and milestone plans. Its scientific ambition is high, and success would depend on disciplined prioritization and strong coordination."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly addresses emergent cellular architecture and how collective interactions among components generate mesoscale organization. This is highly responsive to the emergence-focused mission of the call."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed integration of public imaging, in vitro, simulation, and literature data through transfer learning is innovative and could significantly influence how cell organization is studied. The significance is high, though the proposal could better define the specific long-standing puzzles it will solve."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The general approach is credible, but the proposal lacks detailed milestones, validation metrics, and decision criteria. The methodological breadth is impressive but not yet organized into a fully rigorous execution plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":2,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is very large for two years. Developing a general computational framework, applying it to three complex systems, integrating multiple data modalities, conducting literature synthesis, and building a larger nucleus-centered model may exceed the capacity of a working group unless the scope is narrowed and staged."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly focused on synthesis of existing data and explicitly avoids new experimental data generation. It makes good use of public datasets and aims to create new value through integration, curation, and secondary analysis."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies data types and biological domains but does not provide enough concrete information about the datasets to be synthesized. Further data scouting is acknowledged, but the feasibility of the full plan cannot be assessed without more detail."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source methods, public datasets, and reproducible workflows is well aligned with the call. However, the proposal should include a more specific open science implementation plan and a clearer description of trainee involvement in reproducible research practices.""}]}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist and machine learning researcher focused on multimodal biological data integration. The proposal presents an ambitious machine learning framework for integrating heterogeneous cellular datasets using Gromov-Wasserstein distances, transfer learning, and mutual prediction. I appreciate the recognition that many biological datasets do not inhabit a shared feature space and that cross-space alignment methods may be useful for cell image synthesis. The use of complete datasets to learn relationships that can then be applied to incomplete datasets is a strong conceptual match to the data synthesis mission. However, from a computational perspective, the approach is not yet sufficiently specified to assess feasibility. GW alignment, GANs, and transfer learning are mentioned, but the proposal does not define model architectures, training objectives, sample size requirements, baselines, evaluation metrics, or failure modes. There is also a risk of overinterpreting predictive relationships as mechanistic or causal relationships. I would have liked to see a clearer computational workplan: what features will be extracted, how missing modalities will be predicted, what held-out datasets will be used, how uncertainty will be quantified, and how domain shift across cell types, imaging platforms, perturbations, and laboratories will be handled. The proposal is exciting and well suited to NCEMS if strengthened by a more rigorous data science plan.","The computational concept is timely and potentially powerful. Gromov-Wasserstein methods are appropriate for comparing datasets in different feature spaces, and transfer learning from complete to incomplete datasets addresses a common limitation of public cell imaging resources. The proposal recognizes the need for high-performance computing and data management infrastructure. If successful, the pipeline could be widely reusable for multimodal biological synthesis beyond the three examples. The working group format is justified by the need to combine mathematical, computational, cell biological, and biophysical expertise.","The machine learning plan is too high-level. The proposal does not specify the precise prediction tasks, model architectures, evaluation criteria, baselines, cross-validation design, or statistical treatment of uncertainty. It invokes deep learning, GANs, GW distances, and agent-based modeling without clearly explaining how these components will interact in a reproducible workflow. There is insufficient attention to domain shift, batch effects, imaging modality differences, segmentation errors, class imbalance, and data leakage. The computational resource request of at least 10,000 GPU hours is not justified by a concrete model training plan. The proposal does not sufficiently distinguish correlation, prediction, and mechanism.",3,"A conceptually exciting computational synthesis proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS, but the analytical plan is underdeveloped. The proposal needs clearer prediction tasks, benchmarks, validation strategies, and safeguards against overinterpretation before it can be considered fully rigorous.",4,"The proposal is relevant to emergent phenomena because it aims to infer how mesoscale organization arises from relationships among cellular components. However, the current computational framing risks emphasizing prediction over mechanistic emergence unless stronger links to physical and biological principles are established.",4,"Applying GW-based transfer learning to heterogeneous cell imaging, in vitro, and simulation datasets is innovative. The significance could be high because incomplete public datasets are common and underused. The significance depends on whether the method produces interpretable and validated biological insights rather than only technical demonstrations.",2,"The approach lacks sufficient methodological detail. There are no clearly stated loss functions, model evaluation metrics, baselines such as canonical correlation analysis or contrastive learning, negative controls, held-out validation schemes, or uncertainty measures. The proposal also does not address key practical issues in image-based ML, including segmentation quality, batch effects, and domain adaptation.",3,"Developing a robust multimodal transfer learning pipeline and applying it to three complex biological systems in two years is ambitious. A narrower initial scope, perhaps one well-curated two-component system followed by one three-component extension, would be more realistic.",5,The proposal is clearly centered on synthesis of existing public data and reuse of incomplete datasets. The strategy of extracting new information from already generated imaging and simulation resources is well aligned with the funding call.,3,"The proposal names general data types and biological systems but does not identify enough specific datasets to evaluate feasibility. It should include candidate repositories, dataset sizes, imaging channels, metadata, perturbations, and whether the same components are measured in compatible cellular contexts.",4,"The plan to produce an open-source pipeline and integrated datasets is positive. More detail is needed on reproducible ML practices, including versioned data releases, containers, workflow managers, trained model deposition, model cards, and documentation for reuse by the broader community.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:41:52.331118,0,success,,"The proposal outlines an ambitious machine learning framework that leverages Gromov-Wasserstein distances and transfer learning to integrate heterogeneous cellular datasets. While the conceptual approach strongly aligns with the goal of data synthesis, the computational methodology lacks the specificity required to fully assess its feasibility. To be compelling, the proposal needs a more rigorous data science plan detailing model architectures, evaluation metrics, uncertainty quantification, and strategies for managing domain shifts.","The proposed use of Gromov-Wasserstein methods and transfer learning offers a timely and appropriate solution for comparing datasets across different feature spaces. Additionally, the project features a well-justified working group and recognizes necessary high-performance computing infrastructure, promising a pipeline with broad reusability for multimodal biological data integration.","The machine learning methodology is insufficiently detailed, lacking specific model architectures, training objectives, evaluation metrics, and plans for handling batch effects or data leakage. Furthermore, the requested computational resources are not adequately justified, and the proposal risks conflating predictive correlations with causal or mechanistic relationships."
baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::003,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,3,baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:18:29.243218,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior cell biologist specializing in organelle organization and nuclear architecture. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: whether principles of mesoscale cellular organization can be inferred by integrating existing multicomponent imaging, in vitro reconstitution, and simulation datasets. The focus on nucleus-centered systems is scientifically coherent, and the three test cases are biologically meaningful. I found the biological motivation compelling, particularly the attempt to move beyond gene- or protein-centric descriptions toward geometry, physical constraints, and inter-organelle relationships. The proposal is well aligned with the call in its emphasis on emergent cellular organization and community-scale synthesis. However, the biological hypotheses remain somewhat diffuse. The three systems are each complex, and it is not fully clear what concrete biological conclusions would constitute success beyond demonstrating a transfer-learning pipeline. The proposal would be stronger if it articulated more explicit expected biological readouts, validation benchmarks, and decision points for distinguishing intrinsic physical properties from interaction-driven organization. I am also concerned that the publicly available datasets needed for some comparisons, especially matched image, in vitro, and simulation datasets, may not exist in sufficient quantity or compatibility. Overall, this is a high-potential project with strong relevance to mesoscale emergence, but it needs sharper biological framing and more concrete dataset specification."",""strengths"":""The proposal tackles a central problem in cell biology: how cellular-scale organization emerges from interactions among organelles, cytoskeletal structures, condensates, and physical constraints. The nucleus-centered design provides a unifying biological anchor across three increasingly complex test cases. The proposed integration of imaging, in vitro biophysics, and simulations is ambitious and well suited to a multidisciplinary working group. The project is clearly beyond a single laboratory and requires expertise in cell biology, image analysis, machine learning, physical modeling, and data curation. The work has the potential to generate broadly useful tools and new insights into nuclear, nucleolar, mitochondrial, and cytoskeletal organization."",""weakness"":""The biological questions are broad and sometimes under-specified. The proposal does not sufficiently define what specific hypotheses will be tested for each system or what criteria will establish that a mesoscale principle has been discovered. Dataset availability is acknowledged as a limitation but not resolved with a sufficiently detailed inventory of public datasets, metadata, or compatibility constraints. The integration of in vitro and simulation data with cellular imaging is conceptually appealing but technically and biologically underspecified. Validation strategies are described only generally, and it is unclear how inferred missing features will be biologically validated using only existing data. The project may become method development rather than synthesis-driven discovery unless the biological endpoints are made more explicit."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong and highly relevant proposal that addresses emergent mesoscale organization using a multidisciplinary synthesis strategy. Its main weaknesses are insufficiently explicit biological hypotheses, uncertain dataset readiness, and an ambitious integration plan that may be difficult to complete in two years."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly targets mesoscale cellular organization as an emergent property of interactions among physical, structural, and chemical components. The chosen systems, especially nucleolus-nucleus, nucleus-microtubule-centrosome, and nucleus-mitochondria-condensate relationships, are highly appropriate for studying emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of multicomponent imaging, minimal systems, simulations, and transfer learning is novel and potentially significant. The shift from component-specific analysis to cross-component prediction could produce important conceptual advances. The novelty is somewhat tempered by lack of specificity about the new biological principles expected from each test case."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach uses plausible modern tools, including feature extraction, Gromov-Wasserstein distances, and transfer learning. However, the proposal lacks detailed benchmarking, validation, uncertainty quantification, and criteria for interpreting learned feature relationships biologically. The deep learning component is described at a high level rather than as a rigorous analysis plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering workflow development, three biological systems, three-component inference, incorporation of in vitro and simulation data, and integration into a larger nucleus-centered model. The timeline is plausible only for a limited proof of concept, not the full set of stated outcomes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly synthesis-focused and explicitly relies on existing public image datasets, in vitro data, simulations, and literature-derived knowledge. It does not propose new experimental data generation, although the phrase 'new simulations will be developed' raises a point needing clarification within the call's constraints."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies broad classes of data and some biological systems, but it does not provide a sufficiently concrete list of public datasets, repositories, accession numbers, metadata standards, or criteria for inclusion. Dataset availability is treated as a known risk, especially for in vitro and in silico data."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to an open-source workflow, integrated datasets, and public tools, which aligns well with NCEMS principles. More detail is needed on licenses, workflow reproducibility, containerization, data provenance, documentation, and community governance.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist and machine learning researcher focused on multimodal biological data integration. The proposal presents an ambitious machine learning framework for integrating heterogeneous cellular datasets using Gromov-Wasserstein distances, transfer learning, and mutual prediction. I appreciate the recognition that many biological datasets do not inhabit a shared feature space and that cross-space alignment methods may be useful for cell image synthesis. The use of complete datasets to learn relationships that can then be applied to incomplete datasets is a strong conceptual match to the data synthesis mission. However, from a computational perspective, the approach is not yet sufficiently specified to assess feasibility. GW alignment, GANs, and transfer learning are mentioned, but the proposal does not define model architectures, training objectives, sample size requirements, baselines, evaluation metrics, or failure modes. There is also a risk of overinterpreting predictive relationships as mechanistic or causal relationships. I would have liked to see a clearer computational workplan: what features will be extracted, how missing modalities will be predicted, what held-out datasets will be used, how uncertainty will be quantified, and how domain shift across cell types, imaging platforms, perturbations, and laboratories will be handled. The proposal is exciting and well suited to NCEMS if strengthened by a more rigorous data science plan."",""strengths"":""The computational concept is timely and potentially powerful. Gromov-Wasserstein methods are appropriate for comparing datasets in different feature spaces, and transfer learning from complete to incomplete datasets addresses a common limitation of public cell imaging resources. The proposal recognizes the need for high-performance computing and data management infrastructure. If successful, the pipeline could be widely reusable for multimodal biological synthesis beyond the three examples. The working group format is justified by the need to combine mathematical, computational, cell biological, and biophysical expertise."",""weakness"":""The machine learning plan is too high-level. The proposal does not specify the precise prediction tasks, model architectures, evaluation criteria, baselines, cross-validation design, or statistical treatment of uncertainty. It invokes deep learning, GANs, GW distances, and agent-based modeling without clearly explaining how these components will interact in a reproducible workflow. There is insufficient attention to domain shift, batch effects, imaging modality differences, segmentation errors, class imbalance, and data leakage. The computational resource request of at least 10,000 GPU hours is not justified by a concrete model training plan. The proposal does not sufficiently distinguish correlation, prediction, and mechanism."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually exciting computational synthesis proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS, but the analytical plan is underdeveloped. The proposal needs clearer prediction tasks, benchmarks, validation strategies, and safeguards against overinterpretation before it can be considered fully rigorous."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal is relevant to emergent phenomena because it aims to infer how mesoscale organization arises from relationships among cellular components. However, the current computational framing risks emphasizing prediction over mechanistic emergence unless stronger links to physical and biological principles are established."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Applying GW-based transfer learning to heterogeneous cell imaging, in vitro, and simulation datasets is innovative. The significance could be high because incomplete public datasets are common and underused. The significance depends on whether the method produces interpretable and validated biological insights rather than only technical demonstrations."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks sufficient methodological detail. There are no clearly stated loss functions, model evaluation metrics, baselines such as canonical correlation analysis or contrastive learning, negative controls, held-out validation schemes, or uncertainty measures. The proposal also does not address key practical issues in image-based ML, including segmentation quality, batch effects, and domain adaptation."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""Developing a robust multimodal transfer learning pipeline and applying it to three complex biological systems in two years is ambitious. A narrower initial scope, perhaps one well-curated two-component system followed by one three-component extension, would be more realistic."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly centered on synthesis of existing public data and reuse of incomplete datasets. The strategy of extracting new information from already generated imaging and simulation resources is well aligned with the funding call."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal names general data types and biological systems but does not identify enough specific datasets to evaluate feasibility. It should include candidate repositories, dataset sizes, imaging channels, metadata, perturbations, and whether the same components are measured in compatible cellular contexts."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The plan to produce an open-source pipeline and integrated datasets is positive. More detail is needed on reproducible ML practices, including versioned data releases, containers, workflow managers, trained model deposition, model cards, and documentation for reuse by the broader community.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior biophysicist specializing in phase separation, cytoskeletal self-organization, and physical modeling of cells. I strongly support the premise that mesoscale cellular organization cannot be understood solely from molecular inventories and requires integration of geometry, physical forces, material properties, and cellular context. The proposal is especially interesting in its inclusion of minimal systems and simulations as comparators to cellular images. The nucleolus and mitochondrial nucleoid cases are well suited to questions about condensate material properties, while the centrosome-microtubule system brings in active cytoskeletal organization. Nevertheless, the proposal currently does not present a sufficiently rigorous physical modeling framework. It speaks broadly of surface tension, fluidity, morphology, physical feature spaces, and agent-based models, but does not define which physical quantities will be extracted, how comparable they are across images, reconstitutions, and simulations, or how competing physical models will be evaluated. I am also concerned by the statement that new simulations will be developed; this may be outside the spirit of a synthesis-only call unless these are analyses of previously published models or clearly framed as computational reanalysis rather than new data generation. The proposal has strong conceptual fit but needs sharper physical hypotheses and better integration between machine learning and mechanistic modeling."",""strengths"":""The project asks important physical questions about the relative contributions of intrinsic component properties and inter-component interactions to cellular organization. The inclusion of minimal systems and simulations is a major strength because it could help distinguish what can arise from intrinsic material properties versus cellular context. The three chosen systems span condensates, membranes, cytoskeleton, and organelles, providing a rich test bed for principles of mesoscale organization. The focus on the nucleus as a common anchor is biologically and physically sensible. The project would benefit from the interdisciplinary working group environment described in the call."",""weakness"":""The physical modeling component is not yet adequately formulated. The proposal does not specify the physical observables, nondimensional parameters, model classes, or tests that will distinguish alternative mechanisms. There is a danger that morphological similarity metrics will be treated as evidence of shared physics without sufficient mechanistic validation. The integration of in vitro and in silico data with cell images is conceptually promising but operationally vague. Dataset compatibility across different experimental conditions, length scales, and measurement modalities is a major unresolved issue. The plan to develop new simulations needs clarification to ensure compliance with a no-new-data synthesis call."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually strong and biologically exciting synthesis project that could advance physical principles of mesoscale cellular organization. The main limitations are vague physical modeling details and uncertainty about how mechanistic conclusions will be validated from heterogeneous existing data."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""This proposal is highly relevant to emergence. It explicitly examines how mesoscale organization arises from interactions among condensates, organelles, cytoskeletal structures, and physical constraints, which is at the heart of emergent molecular and cellular phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":5,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed synthesis of cellular imaging with minimal-system biophysics and simulations to infer principles of mesoscale organization is highly novel and significant. If executed well, it could provide a new paradigm for linking image phenotypes to physical mechanisms."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal contains promising methodological ideas but lacks detailed physical hypotheses, model selection criteria, quantitative observables, and validation strategies. The link between GW-based feature alignment and mechanistic biophysical inference remains underdeveloped."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is broad, covering three biological systems, multiple data types, physical modeling, machine learning, and literature synthesis. Two years may be sufficient for a prototype and one or two focused discoveries, but not for the full integrated nucleus-centered model envisioned."",""synthesis_focus_score"":4,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is largely synthesis-focused and based on existing datasets. However, the mention of developing new simulations introduces ambiguity. If these simulations are new computational experiments producing new data, the team should clarify whether this is permissible within NCEMS expectations or reframe the activity as reanalysis of existing simulation outputs and models."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies relevant categories of imaging, reconstitution, and simulation data but does not provide enough detail on specific public sources, measurement compatibility, or metadata sufficiency. The acknowledged need for further data scouting is appropriate but weakens feasibility."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source tools and integrated datasets is strong. Additional detail is needed about how physical models, parameter files, simulation-derived features, and literature-curated data will be shared in reproducible formats.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior bioimage informatics and data infrastructure scientist specializing in public imaging repositories, metadata, and reproducible workflows. This proposal is very well matched to the growing opportunity in public bioimage data reuse. The authors correctly identify that large image datasets are underutilized for secondary synthesis and that incomplete multicomponent datasets create an opportunity for cross-dataset prediction. The project would clearly require infrastructure support for storage, compute, curation, and workflow optimization, all of which fit the NCEMS model. However, as a bioimage data proposal, it currently underestimates the importance of metadata harmonization, image preprocessing, segmentation quality, feature standardization, and provenance tracking. The data plan is not specific enough. The proposal mentions high-resolution imaging datasets, live-cell data, fibroblasts, HGPS patients, in vitro data, and simulations, but it does not list the principal public datasets, their formats, channels, sample sizes, licenses, image resolutions, perturbations, or associated metadata. The success of transfer learning across public imaging datasets will depend heavily on these practical details. I am enthusiastic about the concept, but I would require a much more concrete data inventory and curation plan before judging the project fully feasible."",""strengths"":""The proposal has a strong synthesis orientation and recognizes the need to integrate public image datasets that are currently difficult to compare. It makes a compelling case for NCEMS support in compute, storage, data transfer, and curation. The focus on open-source workflows and reusable integrated datasets could have substantial community value. The project brings together image analysis, machine learning, biophysics, and cell biology in a way that is appropriate for a working group. The use of incomplete datasets is particularly relevant because many public bioimage resources contain only subsets of the components needed for systems-level analysis."",""weakness"":""The data identification and curation plan is underdeveloped. There is no detailed inventory of datasets, repositories, file formats, metadata fields, image acquisition conditions, segmentation annotations, quality control procedures, or licensing constraints. The proposal does not explain how batch effects, microscope differences, staining protocols, cell line differences, and cell state heterogeneity will be normalized or modeled. It is unclear who will perform the substantial curation labor and how trainees will be trained in these practices. The open science plan is positive but generic and lacks specific workflow, metadata, and FAIR data implementation details."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is highly appropriate for data synthesis and could produce valuable community resources, but feasibility depends on bioimage metadata and curation details that are currently missing. The proposal should be strengthened with a concrete dataset inventory, QC plan, and reproducible workflow architecture."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The biological focus on mesoscale cellular organization is relevant to emergent phenomena. From the data infrastructure perspective, the emergent biology is compelling, though the proposal needs clearer operational definitions of the emergent features to be measured from images."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Using public imaging datasets for cross-component prediction and mesoscale synthesis is novel and significant. The potential community impact is high if the workflow becomes reusable for other systems. The significance is limited by uncertainty about whether the public data are sufficiently standardized for the intended analyses."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposed computational methods are promising, but the rigor of the bioimage analysis pipeline is not fully described. The proposal needs more detail on segmentation, feature extraction, QC, metadata harmonization, batch correction, provenance, and benchmark datasets."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The proposed storage and compute needs are plausible for large imaging data, but the two-year plan does not fully account for the time required to identify, download, curate, harmonize, and document heterogeneous public datasets before model development can proceed."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""This is a strong synthesis proposal. It explicitly leverages existing public image, in vitro, simulation, and literature-derived data and seeks to create new value through integration rather than new experiments."",""data_identification_score"":2,""data_identification_justification"":""This is the weakest area. The proposal identifies broad classes of data but not specific datasets or repositories in enough detail to assess feasibility. A successful bioimage synthesis project requires a much more explicit data table with dataset sources, components imaged, metadata availability, quality, scale, licenses, and anticipated preprocessing needs."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The open-source commitment is encouraging, but the plan is generic. The proposal should specify FAIR metadata standards, repository choices, workflow systems, containerization, persistent identifiers, versioning, documentation, and policies for sharing derived features and trained models.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior systems cell biologist and team-science reviewer with experience in collaborative synthesis centers and trainee training. This proposal fits the spirit of the call in several important ways: it addresses a broad scientific question that cannot be solved by a single lab, relies on existing public data, requires a multidisciplinary team, and aims to produce reusable computational workflows. I was particularly pleased to see attention to systematic literature synthesis and to the possibility of pooling smaller datasets, which are often overlooked in data synthesis proposals. The working group justification is credible, and the project could provide excellent training opportunities in data science, image analysis, biophysics, and collaborative research. That said, the proposal does not provide enough detail about the actual working group structure, trainee roles, governance, meeting cadence, division of labor, or community engagement. It also lacks a clear training plan despite the call's emphasis on developing the future data-savvy workforce. Scientifically, the proposal is ambitious and exciting, but it would benefit from clearer milestones, deliverables, and go/no-go criteria. I view this as a strong but somewhat under-organized proposal for a working group."",""strengths"":""The project clearly requires multiple domains of expertise and is appropriate for a collaborative working group. It addresses an important and broadly significant question in cell organization and emergence. The planned outputs, including a generalizable framework, predictive models, integrated datasets, and open-source workflows, are well aligned with NCEMS priorities. The proposal recognizes the need for community-scale curation and synthesis, including smaller literature-derived datasets. The project could provide rich interdisciplinary training opportunities if formalized."",""weakness"":""The working group plan is not sufficiently developed. The proposal does not describe team composition in detail, governance, how conflicts or technical decisions will be managed, how trainees will be integrated, or how the broader community will be engaged. The training component is implicit rather than explicit. Milestones are broad, and the two-year timeline lacks concrete deliverables by quarter or by test case. The rationale for NCEMS support is reasonable but could better articulate why existing collaborations or institutional resources cannot accomplish the work. The project risks spreading effort across too many biological systems and computational methods without adequate prioritization."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A compelling synthesis and team-science proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS goals, but it needs more concrete project management, training, and milestone plans. Its scientific ambition is high, and success would depend on disciplined prioritization and strong coordination."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly addresses emergent cellular architecture and how collective interactions among components generate mesoscale organization. This is highly responsive to the emergence-focused mission of the call."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed integration of public imaging, in vitro, simulation, and literature data through transfer learning is innovative and could significantly influence how cell organization is studied. The significance is high, though the proposal could better define the specific long-standing puzzles it will solve."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The general approach is credible, but the proposal lacks detailed milestones, validation metrics, and decision criteria. The methodological breadth is impressive but not yet organized into a fully rigorous execution plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":2,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is very large for two years. Developing a general computational framework, applying it to three complex systems, integrating multiple data modalities, conducting literature synthesis, and building a larger nucleus-centered model may exceed the capacity of a working group unless the scope is narrowed and staged."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly focused on synthesis of existing data and explicitly avoids new experimental data generation. It makes good use of public datasets and aims to create new value through integration, curation, and secondary analysis."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies data types and biological domains but does not provide enough concrete information about the datasets to be synthesized. Further data scouting is acknowledged, but the feasibility of the full plan cannot be assessed without more detail."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source methods, public datasets, and reproducible workflows is well aligned with the call. However, the proposal should include a more specific open science implementation plan and a clearer description of trainee involvement in reproducible research practices.""}]}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 3: Senior biophysicist specializing in phase separation, cytoskeletal self-organization, and physical modeling of cells. I strongly support the premise that mesoscale cellular organization cannot be understood solely from molecular inventories and requires integration of geometry, physical forces, material properties, and cellular context. The proposal is especially interesting in its inclusion of minimal systems and simulations as comparators to cellular images. The nucleolus and mitochondrial nucleoid cases are well suited to questions about condensate material properties, while the centrosome-microtubule system brings in active cytoskeletal organization. Nevertheless, the proposal currently does not present a sufficiently rigorous physical modeling framework. It speaks broadly of surface tension, fluidity, morphology, physical feature spaces, and agent-based models, but does not define which physical quantities will be extracted, how comparable they are across images, reconstitutions, and simulations, or how competing physical models will be evaluated. I am also concerned by the statement that new simulations will be developed; this may be outside the spirit of a synthesis-only call unless these are analyses of previously published models or clearly framed as computational reanalysis rather than new data generation. The proposal has strong conceptual fit but needs sharper physical hypotheses and better integration between machine learning and mechanistic modeling.","The project asks important physical questions about the relative contributions of intrinsic component properties and inter-component interactions to cellular organization. The inclusion of minimal systems and simulations is a major strength because it could help distinguish what can arise from intrinsic material properties versus cellular context. The three chosen systems span condensates, membranes, cytoskeleton, and organelles, providing a rich test bed for principles of mesoscale organization. The focus on the nucleus as a common anchor is biologically and physically sensible. The project would benefit from the interdisciplinary working group environment described in the call.","The physical modeling component is not yet adequately formulated. The proposal does not specify the physical observables, nondimensional parameters, model classes, or tests that will distinguish alternative mechanisms. There is a danger that morphological similarity metrics will be treated as evidence of shared physics without sufficient mechanistic validation. The integration of in vitro and in silico data with cell images is conceptually promising but operationally vague. Dataset compatibility across different experimental conditions, length scales, and measurement modalities is a major unresolved issue. The plan to develop new simulations needs clarification to ensure compliance with a no-new-data synthesis call.",4,A conceptually strong and biologically exciting synthesis project that could advance physical principles of mesoscale cellular organization. The main limitations are vague physical modeling details and uncertainty about how mechanistic conclusions will be validated from heterogeneous existing data.,5,"This proposal is highly relevant to emergence. It explicitly examines how mesoscale organization arises from interactions among condensates, organelles, cytoskeletal structures, and physical constraints, which is at the heart of emergent molecular and cellular phenomena.",5,"The proposed synthesis of cellular imaging with minimal-system biophysics and simulations to infer principles of mesoscale organization is highly novel and significant. If executed well, it could provide a new paradigm for linking image phenotypes to physical mechanisms.",3,"The proposal contains promising methodological ideas but lacks detailed physical hypotheses, model selection criteria, quantitative observables, and validation strategies. The link between GW-based feature alignment and mechanistic biophysical inference remains underdeveloped.",3,"The project is broad, covering three biological systems, multiple data types, physical modeling, machine learning, and literature synthesis. Two years may be sufficient for a prototype and one or two focused discoveries, but not for the full integrated nucleus-centered model envisioned.",4,"The proposal is largely synthesis-focused and based on existing datasets. However, the mention of developing new simulations introduces ambiguity. If these simulations are new computational experiments producing new data, the team should clarify whether this is permissible within NCEMS expectations or reframe the activity as reanalysis of existing simulation outputs and models.",3,"The proposal identifies relevant categories of imaging, reconstitution, and simulation data but does not provide enough detail on specific public sources, measurement compatibility, or metadata sufficiency. The acknowledged need for further data scouting is appropriate but weakens feasibility.",4,"The commitment to open-source tools and integrated datasets is strong. Additional detail is needed about how physical models, parameter files, simulation-derived features, and literature-curated data will be shared in reproducible formats.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:42:02.102761,0,success,,"The proposal presents a strong conceptual framework for investigating mesoscale cellular organization by integrating geometry, physical forces, and material properties across diverse biological systems. However, the physical modeling approach lacks necessary rigor, as specific observables and evaluation metrics are not clearly defined. Additionally, the planned development of new simulations requires clarification to ensure alignment with the synthesis-only constraints of the funding call. Overall, the project requires sharper physical hypotheses and improved integration between machine learning and mechanistic modeling.","The project addresses critical questions regarding cellular organization using a well-chosen, diverse set of biological test cases anchored by the nucleus. The proposed integration of minimal systems and simulations is a notable advantage, allowing for the differentiation between intrinsic material properties and cellular context effects.","The physical modeling component is inadequately detailed, lacking specific observables, parameters, and mechanistic validation strategies to prevent conflating morphological similarity with shared underlying physics. Operational methods for integrating diverse datasets are vague, with unresolved issues regarding compatibility across varying scales and modalities. Furthermore, the intention to develop new simulations may violate the specific constraints of a synthesis-only call."
baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::004,20260709_143810,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,4,baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:18:29.243218,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior cell biologist specializing in organelle organization and nuclear architecture. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: whether principles of mesoscale cellular organization can be inferred by integrating existing multicomponent imaging, in vitro reconstitution, and simulation datasets. The focus on nucleus-centered systems is scientifically coherent, and the three test cases are biologically meaningful. I found the biological motivation compelling, particularly the attempt to move beyond gene- or protein-centric descriptions toward geometry, physical constraints, and inter-organelle relationships. The proposal is well aligned with the call in its emphasis on emergent cellular organization and community-scale synthesis. However, the biological hypotheses remain somewhat diffuse. The three systems are each complex, and it is not fully clear what concrete biological conclusions would constitute success beyond demonstrating a transfer-learning pipeline. The proposal would be stronger if it articulated more explicit expected biological readouts, validation benchmarks, and decision points for distinguishing intrinsic physical properties from interaction-driven organization. I am also concerned that the publicly available datasets needed for some comparisons, especially matched image, in vitro, and simulation datasets, may not exist in sufficient quantity or compatibility. Overall, this is a high-potential project with strong relevance to mesoscale emergence, but it needs sharper biological framing and more concrete dataset specification."",""strengths"":""The proposal tackles a central problem in cell biology: how cellular-scale organization emerges from interactions among organelles, cytoskeletal structures, condensates, and physical constraints. The nucleus-centered design provides a unifying biological anchor across three increasingly complex test cases. The proposed integration of imaging, in vitro biophysics, and simulations is ambitious and well suited to a multidisciplinary working group. The project is clearly beyond a single laboratory and requires expertise in cell biology, image analysis, machine learning, physical modeling, and data curation. The work has the potential to generate broadly useful tools and new insights into nuclear, nucleolar, mitochondrial, and cytoskeletal organization."",""weakness"":""The biological questions are broad and sometimes under-specified. The proposal does not sufficiently define what specific hypotheses will be tested for each system or what criteria will establish that a mesoscale principle has been discovered. Dataset availability is acknowledged as a limitation but not resolved with a sufficiently detailed inventory of public datasets, metadata, or compatibility constraints. The integration of in vitro and simulation data with cellular imaging is conceptually appealing but technically and biologically underspecified. Validation strategies are described only generally, and it is unclear how inferred missing features will be biologically validated using only existing data. The project may become method development rather than synthesis-driven discovery unless the biological endpoints are made more explicit."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong and highly relevant proposal that addresses emergent mesoscale organization using a multidisciplinary synthesis strategy. Its main weaknesses are insufficiently explicit biological hypotheses, uncertain dataset readiness, and an ambitious integration plan that may be difficult to complete in two years."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly targets mesoscale cellular organization as an emergent property of interactions among physical, structural, and chemical components. The chosen systems, especially nucleolus-nucleus, nucleus-microtubule-centrosome, and nucleus-mitochondria-condensate relationships, are highly appropriate for studying emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of multicomponent imaging, minimal systems, simulations, and transfer learning is novel and potentially significant. The shift from component-specific analysis to cross-component prediction could produce important conceptual advances. The novelty is somewhat tempered by lack of specificity about the new biological principles expected from each test case."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach uses plausible modern tools, including feature extraction, Gromov-Wasserstein distances, and transfer learning. However, the proposal lacks detailed benchmarking, validation, uncertainty quantification, and criteria for interpreting learned feature relationships biologically. The deep learning component is described at a high level rather than as a rigorous analysis plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering workflow development, three biological systems, three-component inference, incorporation of in vitro and simulation data, and integration into a larger nucleus-centered model. The timeline is plausible only for a limited proof of concept, not the full set of stated outcomes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly synthesis-focused and explicitly relies on existing public image datasets, in vitro data, simulations, and literature-derived knowledge. It does not propose new experimental data generation, although the phrase 'new simulations will be developed' raises a point needing clarification within the call's constraints."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies broad classes of data and some biological systems, but it does not provide a sufficiently concrete list of public datasets, repositories, accession numbers, metadata standards, or criteria for inclusion. Dataset availability is treated as a known risk, especially for in vitro and in silico data."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to an open-source workflow, integrated datasets, and public tools, which aligns well with NCEMS principles. More detail is needed on licenses, workflow reproducibility, containerization, data provenance, documentation, and community governance.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist and machine learning researcher focused on multimodal biological data integration. The proposal presents an ambitious machine learning framework for integrating heterogeneous cellular datasets using Gromov-Wasserstein distances, transfer learning, and mutual prediction. I appreciate the recognition that many biological datasets do not inhabit a shared feature space and that cross-space alignment methods may be useful for cell image synthesis. The use of complete datasets to learn relationships that can then be applied to incomplete datasets is a strong conceptual match to the data synthesis mission. However, from a computational perspective, the approach is not yet sufficiently specified to assess feasibility. GW alignment, GANs, and transfer learning are mentioned, but the proposal does not define model architectures, training objectives, sample size requirements, baselines, evaluation metrics, or failure modes. There is also a risk of overinterpreting predictive relationships as mechanistic or causal relationships. I would have liked to see a clearer computational workplan: what features will be extracted, how missing modalities will be predicted, what held-out datasets will be used, how uncertainty will be quantified, and how domain shift across cell types, imaging platforms, perturbations, and laboratories will be handled. The proposal is exciting and well suited to NCEMS if strengthened by a more rigorous data science plan."",""strengths"":""The computational concept is timely and potentially powerful. Gromov-Wasserstein methods are appropriate for comparing datasets in different feature spaces, and transfer learning from complete to incomplete datasets addresses a common limitation of public cell imaging resources. The proposal recognizes the need for high-performance computing and data management infrastructure. If successful, the pipeline could be widely reusable for multimodal biological synthesis beyond the three examples. The working group format is justified by the need to combine mathematical, computational, cell biological, and biophysical expertise."",""weakness"":""The machine learning plan is too high-level. The proposal does not specify the precise prediction tasks, model architectures, evaluation criteria, baselines, cross-validation design, or statistical treatment of uncertainty. It invokes deep learning, GANs, GW distances, and agent-based modeling without clearly explaining how these components will interact in a reproducible workflow. There is insufficient attention to domain shift, batch effects, imaging modality differences, segmentation errors, class imbalance, and data leakage. The computational resource request of at least 10,000 GPU hours is not justified by a concrete model training plan. The proposal does not sufficiently distinguish correlation, prediction, and mechanism."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually exciting computational synthesis proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS, but the analytical plan is underdeveloped. The proposal needs clearer prediction tasks, benchmarks, validation strategies, and safeguards against overinterpretation before it can be considered fully rigorous."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal is relevant to emergent phenomena because it aims to infer how mesoscale organization arises from relationships among cellular components. However, the current computational framing risks emphasizing prediction over mechanistic emergence unless stronger links to physical and biological principles are established."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Applying GW-based transfer learning to heterogeneous cell imaging, in vitro, and simulation datasets is innovative. The significance could be high because incomplete public datasets are common and underused. The significance depends on whether the method produces interpretable and validated biological insights rather than only technical demonstrations."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks sufficient methodological detail. There are no clearly stated loss functions, model evaluation metrics, baselines such as canonical correlation analysis or contrastive learning, negative controls, held-out validation schemes, or uncertainty measures. The proposal also does not address key practical issues in image-based ML, including segmentation quality, batch effects, and domain adaptation."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""Developing a robust multimodal transfer learning pipeline and applying it to three complex biological systems in two years is ambitious. A narrower initial scope, perhaps one well-curated two-component system followed by one three-component extension, would be more realistic."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly centered on synthesis of existing public data and reuse of incomplete datasets. The strategy of extracting new information from already generated imaging and simulation resources is well aligned with the funding call."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal names general data types and biological systems but does not identify enough specific datasets to evaluate feasibility. It should include candidate repositories, dataset sizes, imaging channels, metadata, perturbations, and whether the same components are measured in compatible cellular contexts."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The plan to produce an open-source pipeline and integrated datasets is positive. More detail is needed on reproducible ML practices, including versioned data releases, containers, workflow managers, trained model deposition, model cards, and documentation for reuse by the broader community.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior biophysicist specializing in phase separation, cytoskeletal self-organization, and physical modeling of cells. I strongly support the premise that mesoscale cellular organization cannot be understood solely from molecular inventories and requires integration of geometry, physical forces, material properties, and cellular context. The proposal is especially interesting in its inclusion of minimal systems and simulations as comparators to cellular images. The nucleolus and mitochondrial nucleoid cases are well suited to questions about condensate material properties, while the centrosome-microtubule system brings in active cytoskeletal organization. Nevertheless, the proposal currently does not present a sufficiently rigorous physical modeling framework. It speaks broadly of surface tension, fluidity, morphology, physical feature spaces, and agent-based models, but does not define which physical quantities will be extracted, how comparable they are across images, reconstitutions, and simulations, or how competing physical models will be evaluated. I am also concerned by the statement that new simulations will be developed; this may be outside the spirit of a synthesis-only call unless these are analyses of previously published models or clearly framed as computational reanalysis rather than new data generation. The proposal has strong conceptual fit but needs sharper physical hypotheses and better integration between machine learning and mechanistic modeling."",""strengths"":""The project asks important physical questions about the relative contributions of intrinsic component properties and inter-component interactions to cellular organization. The inclusion of minimal systems and simulations is a major strength because it could help distinguish what can arise from intrinsic material properties versus cellular context. The three chosen systems span condensates, membranes, cytoskeleton, and organelles, providing a rich test bed for principles of mesoscale organization. The focus on the nucleus as a common anchor is biologically and physically sensible. The project would benefit from the interdisciplinary working group environment described in the call."",""weakness"":""The physical modeling component is not yet adequately formulated. The proposal does not specify the physical observables, nondimensional parameters, model classes, or tests that will distinguish alternative mechanisms. There is a danger that morphological similarity metrics will be treated as evidence of shared physics without sufficient mechanistic validation. The integration of in vitro and in silico data with cell images is conceptually promising but operationally vague. Dataset compatibility across different experimental conditions, length scales, and measurement modalities is a major unresolved issue. The plan to develop new simulations needs clarification to ensure compliance with a no-new-data synthesis call."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually strong and biologically exciting synthesis project that could advance physical principles of mesoscale cellular organization. The main limitations are vague physical modeling details and uncertainty about how mechanistic conclusions will be validated from heterogeneous existing data."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""This proposal is highly relevant to emergence. It explicitly examines how mesoscale organization arises from interactions among condensates, organelles, cytoskeletal structures, and physical constraints, which is at the heart of emergent molecular and cellular phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":5,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed synthesis of cellular imaging with minimal-system biophysics and simulations to infer principles of mesoscale organization is highly novel and significant. If executed well, it could provide a new paradigm for linking image phenotypes to physical mechanisms."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal contains promising methodological ideas but lacks detailed physical hypotheses, model selection criteria, quantitative observables, and validation strategies. The link between GW-based feature alignment and mechanistic biophysical inference remains underdeveloped."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is broad, covering three biological systems, multiple data types, physical modeling, machine learning, and literature synthesis. Two years may be sufficient for a prototype and one or two focused discoveries, but not for the full integrated nucleus-centered model envisioned."",""synthesis_focus_score"":4,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is largely synthesis-focused and based on existing datasets. However, the mention of developing new simulations introduces ambiguity. If these simulations are new computational experiments producing new data, the team should clarify whether this is permissible within NCEMS expectations or reframe the activity as reanalysis of existing simulation outputs and models."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies relevant categories of imaging, reconstitution, and simulation data but does not provide enough detail on specific public sources, measurement compatibility, or metadata sufficiency. The acknowledged need for further data scouting is appropriate but weakens feasibility."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source tools and integrated datasets is strong. Additional detail is needed about how physical models, parameter files, simulation-derived features, and literature-curated data will be shared in reproducible formats.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior bioimage informatics and data infrastructure scientist specializing in public imaging repositories, metadata, and reproducible workflows. This proposal is very well matched to the growing opportunity in public bioimage data reuse. The authors correctly identify that large image datasets are underutilized for secondary synthesis and that incomplete multicomponent datasets create an opportunity for cross-dataset prediction. The project would clearly require infrastructure support for storage, compute, curation, and workflow optimization, all of which fit the NCEMS model. However, as a bioimage data proposal, it currently underestimates the importance of metadata harmonization, image preprocessing, segmentation quality, feature standardization, and provenance tracking. The data plan is not specific enough. The proposal mentions high-resolution imaging datasets, live-cell data, fibroblasts, HGPS patients, in vitro data, and simulations, but it does not list the principal public datasets, their formats, channels, sample sizes, licenses, image resolutions, perturbations, or associated metadata. The success of transfer learning across public imaging datasets will depend heavily on these practical details. I am enthusiastic about the concept, but I would require a much more concrete data inventory and curation plan before judging the project fully feasible."",""strengths"":""The proposal has a strong synthesis orientation and recognizes the need to integrate public image datasets that are currently difficult to compare. It makes a compelling case for NCEMS support in compute, storage, data transfer, and curation. The focus on open-source workflows and reusable integrated datasets could have substantial community value. The project brings together image analysis, machine learning, biophysics, and cell biology in a way that is appropriate for a working group. The use of incomplete datasets is particularly relevant because many public bioimage resources contain only subsets of the components needed for systems-level analysis."",""weakness"":""The data identification and curation plan is underdeveloped. There is no detailed inventory of datasets, repositories, file formats, metadata fields, image acquisition conditions, segmentation annotations, quality control procedures, or licensing constraints. The proposal does not explain how batch effects, microscope differences, staining protocols, cell line differences, and cell state heterogeneity will be normalized or modeled. It is unclear who will perform the substantial curation labor and how trainees will be trained in these practices. The open science plan is positive but generic and lacks specific workflow, metadata, and FAIR data implementation details."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is highly appropriate for data synthesis and could produce valuable community resources, but feasibility depends on bioimage metadata and curation details that are currently missing. The proposal should be strengthened with a concrete dataset inventory, QC plan, and reproducible workflow architecture."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The biological focus on mesoscale cellular organization is relevant to emergent phenomena. From the data infrastructure perspective, the emergent biology is compelling, though the proposal needs clearer operational definitions of the emergent features to be measured from images."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Using public imaging datasets for cross-component prediction and mesoscale synthesis is novel and significant. The potential community impact is high if the workflow becomes reusable for other systems. The significance is limited by uncertainty about whether the public data are sufficiently standardized for the intended analyses."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposed computational methods are promising, but the rigor of the bioimage analysis pipeline is not fully described. The proposal needs more detail on segmentation, feature extraction, QC, metadata harmonization, batch correction, provenance, and benchmark datasets."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The proposed storage and compute needs are plausible for large imaging data, but the two-year plan does not fully account for the time required to identify, download, curate, harmonize, and document heterogeneous public datasets before model development can proceed."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""This is a strong synthesis proposal. It explicitly leverages existing public image, in vitro, simulation, and literature-derived data and seeks to create new value through integration rather than new experiments."",""data_identification_score"":2,""data_identification_justification"":""This is the weakest area. The proposal identifies broad classes of data but not specific datasets or repositories in enough detail to assess feasibility. A successful bioimage synthesis project requires a much more explicit data table with dataset sources, components imaged, metadata availability, quality, scale, licenses, and anticipated preprocessing needs."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The open-source commitment is encouraging, but the plan is generic. The proposal should specify FAIR metadata standards, repository choices, workflow systems, containerization, persistent identifiers, versioning, documentation, and policies for sharing derived features and trained models.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior systems cell biologist and team-science reviewer with experience in collaborative synthesis centers and trainee training. This proposal fits the spirit of the call in several important ways: it addresses a broad scientific question that cannot be solved by a single lab, relies on existing public data, requires a multidisciplinary team, and aims to produce reusable computational workflows. I was particularly pleased to see attention to systematic literature synthesis and to the possibility of pooling smaller datasets, which are often overlooked in data synthesis proposals. The working group justification is credible, and the project could provide excellent training opportunities in data science, image analysis, biophysics, and collaborative research. That said, the proposal does not provide enough detail about the actual working group structure, trainee roles, governance, meeting cadence, division of labor, or community engagement. It also lacks a clear training plan despite the call's emphasis on developing the future data-savvy workforce. Scientifically, the proposal is ambitious and exciting, but it would benefit from clearer milestones, deliverables, and go/no-go criteria. I view this as a strong but somewhat under-organized proposal for a working group."",""strengths"":""The project clearly requires multiple domains of expertise and is appropriate for a collaborative working group. It addresses an important and broadly significant question in cell organization and emergence. The planned outputs, including a generalizable framework, predictive models, integrated datasets, and open-source workflows, are well aligned with NCEMS priorities. The proposal recognizes the need for community-scale curation and synthesis, including smaller literature-derived datasets. The project could provide rich interdisciplinary training opportunities if formalized."",""weakness"":""The working group plan is not sufficiently developed. The proposal does not describe team composition in detail, governance, how conflicts or technical decisions will be managed, how trainees will be integrated, or how the broader community will be engaged. The training component is implicit rather than explicit. Milestones are broad, and the two-year timeline lacks concrete deliverables by quarter or by test case. The rationale for NCEMS support is reasonable but could better articulate why existing collaborations or institutional resources cannot accomplish the work. The project risks spreading effort across too many biological systems and computational methods without adequate prioritization."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A compelling synthesis and team-science proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS goals, but it needs more concrete project management, training, and milestone plans. Its scientific ambition is high, and success would depend on disciplined prioritization and strong coordination."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly addresses emergent cellular architecture and how collective interactions among components generate mesoscale organization. This is highly responsive to the emergence-focused mission of the call."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed integration of public imaging, in vitro, simulation, and literature data through transfer learning is innovative and could significantly influence how cell organization is studied. The significance is high, though the proposal could better define the specific long-standing puzzles it will solve."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The general approach is credible, but the proposal lacks detailed milestones, validation metrics, and decision criteria. The methodological breadth is impressive but not yet organized into a fully rigorous execution plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":2,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is very large for two years. Developing a general computational framework, applying it to three complex systems, integrating multiple data modalities, conducting literature synthesis, and building a larger nucleus-centered model may exceed the capacity of a working group unless the scope is narrowed and staged."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly focused on synthesis of existing data and explicitly avoids new experimental data generation. It makes good use of public datasets and aims to create new value through integration, curation, and secondary analysis."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies data types and biological domains but does not provide enough concrete information about the datasets to be synthesized. Further data scouting is acknowledged, but the feasibility of the full plan cannot be assessed without more detail."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source methods, public datasets, and reproducible workflows is well aligned with the call. However, the proposal should include a more specific open science implementation plan and a clearer description of trainee involvement in reproducible research practices.""}]}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 4: Senior bioimage informatics and data infrastructure scientist specializing in public imaging repositories, metadata, and reproducible workflows. This proposal is very well matched to the growing opportunity in public bioimage data reuse. The authors correctly identify that large image datasets are underutilized for secondary synthesis and that incomplete multicomponent datasets create an opportunity for cross-dataset prediction. The project would clearly require infrastructure support for storage, compute, curation, and workflow optimization, all of which fit the NCEMS model. However, as a bioimage data proposal, it currently underestimates the importance of metadata harmonization, image preprocessing, segmentation quality, feature standardization, and provenance tracking. The data plan is not specific enough. The proposal mentions high-resolution imaging datasets, live-cell data, fibroblasts, HGPS patients, in vitro data, and simulations, but it does not list the principal public datasets, their formats, channels, sample sizes, licenses, image resolutions, perturbations, or associated metadata. The success of transfer learning across public imaging datasets will depend heavily on these practical details. I am enthusiastic about the concept, but I would require a much more concrete data inventory and curation plan before judging the project fully feasible.","The proposal has a strong synthesis orientation and recognizes the need to integrate public image datasets that are currently difficult to compare. It makes a compelling case for NCEMS support in compute, storage, data transfer, and curation. The focus on open-source workflows and reusable integrated datasets could have substantial community value. The project brings together image analysis, machine learning, biophysics, and cell biology in a way that is appropriate for a working group. The use of incomplete datasets is particularly relevant because many public bioimage resources contain only subsets of the components needed for systems-level analysis.","The data identification and curation plan is underdeveloped. There is no detailed inventory of datasets, repositories, file formats, metadata fields, image acquisition conditions, segmentation annotations, quality control procedures, or licensing constraints. The proposal does not explain how batch effects, microscope differences, staining protocols, cell line differences, and cell state heterogeneity will be normalized or modeled. It is unclear who will perform the substantial curation labor and how trainees will be trained in these practices. The open science plan is positive but generic and lacks specific workflow, metadata, and FAIR data implementation details.",3,"The project is highly appropriate for data synthesis and could produce valuable community resources, but feasibility depends on bioimage metadata and curation details that are currently missing. The proposal should be strengthened with a concrete dataset inventory, QC plan, and reproducible workflow architecture.",4,"The biological focus on mesoscale cellular organization is relevant to emergent phenomena. From the data infrastructure perspective, the emergent biology is compelling, though the proposal needs clearer operational definitions of the emergent features to be measured from images.",4,Using public imaging datasets for cross-component prediction and mesoscale synthesis is novel and significant. The potential community impact is high if the workflow becomes reusable for other systems. The significance is limited by uncertainty about whether the public data are sufficiently standardized for the intended analyses.,3,"The proposed computational methods are promising, but the rigor of the bioimage analysis pipeline is not fully described. The proposal needs more detail on segmentation, feature extraction, QC, metadata harmonization, batch correction, provenance, and benchmark datasets.",3,"The proposed storage and compute needs are plausible for large imaging data, but the two-year plan does not fully account for the time required to identify, download, curate, harmonize, and document heterogeneous public datasets before model development can proceed.",5,"This is a strong synthesis proposal. It explicitly leverages existing public image, in vitro, simulation, and literature-derived data and seeks to create new value through integration rather than new experiments.",2,"This is the weakest area. The proposal identifies broad classes of data but not specific datasets or repositories in enough detail to assess feasibility. A successful bioimage synthesis project requires a much more explicit data table with dataset sources, components imaged, metadata availability, quality, scale, licenses, and anticipated preprocessing needs.",3,"The open-source commitment is encouraging, but the plan is generic. The proposal should specify FAIR metadata standards, repository choices, workflow systems, containerization, persistent identifiers, versioning, documentation, and policies for sharing derived features and trained models.",,,,,,,,,,,
baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,5,baseline::y1::9::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:18:29.243218,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior cell biologist specializing in organelle organization and nuclear architecture. This proposal addresses an important and timely question: whether principles of mesoscale cellular organization can be inferred by integrating existing multicomponent imaging, in vitro reconstitution, and simulation datasets. The focus on nucleus-centered systems is scientifically coherent, and the three test cases are biologically meaningful. I found the biological motivation compelling, particularly the attempt to move beyond gene- or protein-centric descriptions toward geometry, physical constraints, and inter-organelle relationships. The proposal is well aligned with the call in its emphasis on emergent cellular organization and community-scale synthesis. However, the biological hypotheses remain somewhat diffuse. The three systems are each complex, and it is not fully clear what concrete biological conclusions would constitute success beyond demonstrating a transfer-learning pipeline. The proposal would be stronger if it articulated more explicit expected biological readouts, validation benchmarks, and decision points for distinguishing intrinsic physical properties from interaction-driven organization. I am also concerned that the publicly available datasets needed for some comparisons, especially matched image, in vitro, and simulation datasets, may not exist in sufficient quantity or compatibility. Overall, this is a high-potential project with strong relevance to mesoscale emergence, but it needs sharper biological framing and more concrete dataset specification."",""strengths"":""The proposal tackles a central problem in cell biology: how cellular-scale organization emerges from interactions among organelles, cytoskeletal structures, condensates, and physical constraints. The nucleus-centered design provides a unifying biological anchor across three increasingly complex test cases. The proposed integration of imaging, in vitro biophysics, and simulations is ambitious and well suited to a multidisciplinary working group. The project is clearly beyond a single laboratory and requires expertise in cell biology, image analysis, machine learning, physical modeling, and data curation. The work has the potential to generate broadly useful tools and new insights into nuclear, nucleolar, mitochondrial, and cytoskeletal organization."",""weakness"":""The biological questions are broad and sometimes under-specified. The proposal does not sufficiently define what specific hypotheses will be tested for each system or what criteria will establish that a mesoscale principle has been discovered. Dataset availability is acknowledged as a limitation but not resolved with a sufficiently detailed inventory of public datasets, metadata, or compatibility constraints. The integration of in vitro and simulation data with cellular imaging is conceptually appealing but technically and biologically underspecified. Validation strategies are described only generally, and it is unclear how inferred missing features will be biologically validated using only existing data. The project may become method development rather than synthesis-driven discovery unless the biological endpoints are made more explicit."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong and highly relevant proposal that addresses emergent mesoscale organization using a multidisciplinary synthesis strategy. Its main weaknesses are insufficiently explicit biological hypotheses, uncertain dataset readiness, and an ambitious integration plan that may be difficult to complete in two years."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly targets mesoscale cellular organization as an emergent property of interactions among physical, structural, and chemical components. The chosen systems, especially nucleolus-nucleus, nucleus-microtubule-centrosome, and nucleus-mitochondria-condensate relationships, are highly appropriate for studying emergence in molecular and cellular biosciences."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of multicomponent imaging, minimal systems, simulations, and transfer learning is novel and potentially significant. The shift from component-specific analysis to cross-component prediction could produce important conceptual advances. The novelty is somewhat tempered by lack of specificity about the new biological principles expected from each test case."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach uses plausible modern tools, including feature extraction, Gromov-Wasserstein distances, and transfer learning. However, the proposal lacks detailed benchmarking, validation, uncertainty quantification, and criteria for interpreting learned feature relationships biologically. The deep learning component is described at a high level rather than as a rigorous analysis plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering workflow development, three biological systems, three-component inference, incorporation of in vitro and simulation data, and integration into a larger nucleus-centered model. The timeline is plausible only for a limited proof of concept, not the full set of stated outcomes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly synthesis-focused and explicitly relies on existing public image datasets, in vitro data, simulations, and literature-derived knowledge. It does not propose new experimental data generation, although the phrase 'new simulations will be developed' raises a point needing clarification within the call's constraints."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies broad classes of data and some biological systems, but it does not provide a sufficiently concrete list of public datasets, repositories, accession numbers, metadata standards, or criteria for inclusion. Dataset availability is treated as a known risk, especially for in vitro and in silico data."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to an open-source workflow, integrated datasets, and public tools, which aligns well with NCEMS principles. More detail is needed on licenses, workflow reproducibility, containerization, data provenance, documentation, and community governance.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior computational biologist and machine learning researcher focused on multimodal biological data integration. The proposal presents an ambitious machine learning framework for integrating heterogeneous cellular datasets using Gromov-Wasserstein distances, transfer learning, and mutual prediction. I appreciate the recognition that many biological datasets do not inhabit a shared feature space and that cross-space alignment methods may be useful for cell image synthesis. The use of complete datasets to learn relationships that can then be applied to incomplete datasets is a strong conceptual match to the data synthesis mission. However, from a computational perspective, the approach is not yet sufficiently specified to assess feasibility. GW alignment, GANs, and transfer learning are mentioned, but the proposal does not define model architectures, training objectives, sample size requirements, baselines, evaluation metrics, or failure modes. There is also a risk of overinterpreting predictive relationships as mechanistic or causal relationships. I would have liked to see a clearer computational workplan: what features will be extracted, how missing modalities will be predicted, what held-out datasets will be used, how uncertainty will be quantified, and how domain shift across cell types, imaging platforms, perturbations, and laboratories will be handled. The proposal is exciting and well suited to NCEMS if strengthened by a more rigorous data science plan."",""strengths"":""The computational concept is timely and potentially powerful. Gromov-Wasserstein methods are appropriate for comparing datasets in different feature spaces, and transfer learning from complete to incomplete datasets addresses a common limitation of public cell imaging resources. The proposal recognizes the need for high-performance computing and data management infrastructure. If successful, the pipeline could be widely reusable for multimodal biological synthesis beyond the three examples. The working group format is justified by the need to combine mathematical, computational, cell biological, and biophysical expertise."",""weakness"":""The machine learning plan is too high-level. The proposal does not specify the precise prediction tasks, model architectures, evaluation criteria, baselines, cross-validation design, or statistical treatment of uncertainty. It invokes deep learning, GANs, GW distances, and agent-based modeling without clearly explaining how these components will interact in a reproducible workflow. There is insufficient attention to domain shift, batch effects, imaging modality differences, segmentation errors, class imbalance, and data leakage. The computational resource request of at least 10,000 GPU hours is not justified by a concrete model training plan. The proposal does not sufficiently distinguish correlation, prediction, and mechanism."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually exciting computational synthesis proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS, but the analytical plan is underdeveloped. The proposal needs clearer prediction tasks, benchmarks, validation strategies, and safeguards against overinterpretation before it can be considered fully rigorous."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal is relevant to emergent phenomena because it aims to infer how mesoscale organization arises from relationships among cellular components. However, the current computational framing risks emphasizing prediction over mechanistic emergence unless stronger links to physical and biological principles are established."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Applying GW-based transfer learning to heterogeneous cell imaging, in vitro, and simulation datasets is innovative. The significance could be high because incomplete public datasets are common and underused. The significance depends on whether the method produces interpretable and validated biological insights rather than only technical demonstrations."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks sufficient methodological detail. There are no clearly stated loss functions, model evaluation metrics, baselines such as canonical correlation analysis or contrastive learning, negative controls, held-out validation schemes, or uncertainty measures. The proposal also does not address key practical issues in image-based ML, including segmentation quality, batch effects, and domain adaptation."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""Developing a robust multimodal transfer learning pipeline and applying it to three complex biological systems in two years is ambitious. A narrower initial scope, perhaps one well-curated two-component system followed by one three-component extension, would be more realistic."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly centered on synthesis of existing public data and reuse of incomplete datasets. The strategy of extracting new information from already generated imaging and simulation resources is well aligned with the funding call."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal names general data types and biological systems but does not identify enough specific datasets to evaluate feasibility. It should include candidate repositories, dataset sizes, imaging channels, metadata, perturbations, and whether the same components are measured in compatible cellular contexts."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The plan to produce an open-source pipeline and integrated datasets is positive. More detail is needed on reproducible ML practices, including versioned data releases, containers, workflow managers, trained model deposition, model cards, and documentation for reuse by the broader community.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior biophysicist specializing in phase separation, cytoskeletal self-organization, and physical modeling of cells. I strongly support the premise that mesoscale cellular organization cannot be understood solely from molecular inventories and requires integration of geometry, physical forces, material properties, and cellular context. The proposal is especially interesting in its inclusion of minimal systems and simulations as comparators to cellular images. The nucleolus and mitochondrial nucleoid cases are well suited to questions about condensate material properties, while the centrosome-microtubule system brings in active cytoskeletal organization. Nevertheless, the proposal currently does not present a sufficiently rigorous physical modeling framework. It speaks broadly of surface tension, fluidity, morphology, physical feature spaces, and agent-based models, but does not define which physical quantities will be extracted, how comparable they are across images, reconstitutions, and simulations, or how competing physical models will be evaluated. I am also concerned by the statement that new simulations will be developed; this may be outside the spirit of a synthesis-only call unless these are analyses of previously published models or clearly framed as computational reanalysis rather than new data generation. The proposal has strong conceptual fit but needs sharper physical hypotheses and better integration between machine learning and mechanistic modeling."",""strengths"":""The project asks important physical questions about the relative contributions of intrinsic component properties and inter-component interactions to cellular organization. The inclusion of minimal systems and simulations is a major strength because it could help distinguish what can arise from intrinsic material properties versus cellular context. The three chosen systems span condensates, membranes, cytoskeleton, and organelles, providing a rich test bed for principles of mesoscale organization. The focus on the nucleus as a common anchor is biologically and physically sensible. The project would benefit from the interdisciplinary working group environment described in the call."",""weakness"":""The physical modeling component is not yet adequately formulated. The proposal does not specify the physical observables, nondimensional parameters, model classes, or tests that will distinguish alternative mechanisms. There is a danger that morphological similarity metrics will be treated as evidence of shared physics without sufficient mechanistic validation. The integration of in vitro and in silico data with cell images is conceptually promising but operationally vague. Dataset compatibility across different experimental conditions, length scales, and measurement modalities is a major unresolved issue. The plan to develop new simulations needs clarification to ensure compliance with a no-new-data synthesis call."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A conceptually strong and biologically exciting synthesis project that could advance physical principles of mesoscale cellular organization. The main limitations are vague physical modeling details and uncertainty about how mechanistic conclusions will be validated from heterogeneous existing data."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""This proposal is highly relevant to emergence. It explicitly examines how mesoscale organization arises from interactions among condensates, organelles, cytoskeletal structures, and physical constraints, which is at the heart of emergent molecular and cellular phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":5,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed synthesis of cellular imaging with minimal-system biophysics and simulations to infer principles of mesoscale organization is highly novel and significant. If executed well, it could provide a new paradigm for linking image phenotypes to physical mechanisms."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal contains promising methodological ideas but lacks detailed physical hypotheses, model selection criteria, quantitative observables, and validation strategies. The link between GW-based feature alignment and mechanistic biophysical inference remains underdeveloped."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is broad, covering three biological systems, multiple data types, physical modeling, machine learning, and literature synthesis. Two years may be sufficient for a prototype and one or two focused discoveries, but not for the full integrated nucleus-centered model envisioned."",""synthesis_focus_score"":4,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is largely synthesis-focused and based on existing datasets. However, the mention of developing new simulations introduces ambiguity. If these simulations are new computational experiments producing new data, the team should clarify whether this is permissible within NCEMS expectations or reframe the activity as reanalysis of existing simulation outputs and models."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies relevant categories of imaging, reconstitution, and simulation data but does not provide enough detail on specific public sources, measurement compatibility, or metadata sufficiency. The acknowledged need for further data scouting is appropriate but weakens feasibility."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source tools and integrated datasets is strong. Additional detail is needed about how physical models, parameter files, simulation-derived features, and literature-curated data will be shared in reproducible formats.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior bioimage informatics and data infrastructure scientist specializing in public imaging repositories, metadata, and reproducible workflows. This proposal is very well matched to the growing opportunity in public bioimage data reuse. The authors correctly identify that large image datasets are underutilized for secondary synthesis and that incomplete multicomponent datasets create an opportunity for cross-dataset prediction. The project would clearly require infrastructure support for storage, compute, curation, and workflow optimization, all of which fit the NCEMS model. However, as a bioimage data proposal, it currently underestimates the importance of metadata harmonization, image preprocessing, segmentation quality, feature standardization, and provenance tracking. The data plan is not specific enough. The proposal mentions high-resolution imaging datasets, live-cell data, fibroblasts, HGPS patients, in vitro data, and simulations, but it does not list the principal public datasets, their formats, channels, sample sizes, licenses, image resolutions, perturbations, or associated metadata. The success of transfer learning across public imaging datasets will depend heavily on these practical details. I am enthusiastic about the concept, but I would require a much more concrete data inventory and curation plan before judging the project fully feasible."",""strengths"":""The proposal has a strong synthesis orientation and recognizes the need to integrate public image datasets that are currently difficult to compare. It makes a compelling case for NCEMS support in compute, storage, data transfer, and curation. The focus on open-source workflows and reusable integrated datasets could have substantial community value. The project brings together image analysis, machine learning, biophysics, and cell biology in a way that is appropriate for a working group. The use of incomplete datasets is particularly relevant because many public bioimage resources contain only subsets of the components needed for systems-level analysis."",""weakness"":""The data identification and curation plan is underdeveloped. There is no detailed inventory of datasets, repositories, file formats, metadata fields, image acquisition conditions, segmentation annotations, quality control procedures, or licensing constraints. The proposal does not explain how batch effects, microscope differences, staining protocols, cell line differences, and cell state heterogeneity will be normalized or modeled. It is unclear who will perform the substantial curation labor and how trainees will be trained in these practices. The open science plan is positive but generic and lacks specific workflow, metadata, and FAIR data implementation details."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is highly appropriate for data synthesis and could produce valuable community resources, but feasibility depends on bioimage metadata and curation details that are currently missing. The proposal should be strengthened with a concrete dataset inventory, QC plan, and reproducible workflow architecture."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The biological focus on mesoscale cellular organization is relevant to emergent phenomena. From the data infrastructure perspective, the emergent biology is compelling, though the proposal needs clearer operational definitions of the emergent features to be measured from images."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Using public imaging datasets for cross-component prediction and mesoscale synthesis is novel and significant. The potential community impact is high if the workflow becomes reusable for other systems. The significance is limited by uncertainty about whether the public data are sufficiently standardized for the intended analyses."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposed computational methods are promising, but the rigor of the bioimage analysis pipeline is not fully described. The proposal needs more detail on segmentation, feature extraction, QC, metadata harmonization, batch correction, provenance, and benchmark datasets."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The proposed storage and compute needs are plausible for large imaging data, but the two-year plan does not fully account for the time required to identify, download, curate, harmonize, and document heterogeneous public datasets before model development can proceed."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""This is a strong synthesis proposal. It explicitly leverages existing public image, in vitro, simulation, and literature-derived data and seeks to create new value through integration rather than new experiments."",""data_identification_score"":2,""data_identification_justification"":""This is the weakest area. The proposal identifies broad classes of data but not specific datasets or repositories in enough detail to assess feasibility. A successful bioimage synthesis project requires a much more explicit data table with dataset sources, components imaged, metadata availability, quality, scale, licenses, and anticipated preprocessing needs."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The open-source commitment is encouraging, but the plan is generic. The proposal should specify FAIR metadata standards, repository choices, workflow systems, containerization, persistent identifiers, versioning, documentation, and policies for sharing derived features and trained models.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior systems cell biologist and team-science reviewer with experience in collaborative synthesis centers and trainee training. This proposal fits the spirit of the call in several important ways: it addresses a broad scientific question that cannot be solved by a single lab, relies on existing public data, requires a multidisciplinary team, and aims to produce reusable computational workflows. I was particularly pleased to see attention to systematic literature synthesis and to the possibility of pooling smaller datasets, which are often overlooked in data synthesis proposals. The working group justification is credible, and the project could provide excellent training opportunities in data science, image analysis, biophysics, and collaborative research. That said, the proposal does not provide enough detail about the actual working group structure, trainee roles, governance, meeting cadence, division of labor, or community engagement. It also lacks a clear training plan despite the call's emphasis on developing the future data-savvy workforce. Scientifically, the proposal is ambitious and exciting, but it would benefit from clearer milestones, deliverables, and go/no-go criteria. I view this as a strong but somewhat under-organized proposal for a working group."",""strengths"":""The project clearly requires multiple domains of expertise and is appropriate for a collaborative working group. It addresses an important and broadly significant question in cell organization and emergence. The planned outputs, including a generalizable framework, predictive models, integrated datasets, and open-source workflows, are well aligned with NCEMS priorities. The proposal recognizes the need for community-scale curation and synthesis, including smaller literature-derived datasets. The project could provide rich interdisciplinary training opportunities if formalized."",""weakness"":""The working group plan is not sufficiently developed. The proposal does not describe team composition in detail, governance, how conflicts or technical decisions will be managed, how trainees will be integrated, or how the broader community will be engaged. The training component is implicit rather than explicit. Milestones are broad, and the two-year timeline lacks concrete deliverables by quarter or by test case. The rationale for NCEMS support is reasonable but could better articulate why existing collaborations or institutional resources cannot accomplish the work. The project risks spreading effort across too many biological systems and computational methods without adequate prioritization."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A compelling synthesis and team-science proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS goals, but it needs more concrete project management, training, and milestone plans. Its scientific ambition is high, and success would depend on disciplined prioritization and strong coordination."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal directly addresses emergent cellular architecture and how collective interactions among components generate mesoscale organization. This is highly responsive to the emergence-focused mission of the call."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The proposed integration of public imaging, in vitro, simulation, and literature data through transfer learning is innovative and could significantly influence how cell organization is studied. The significance is high, though the proposal could better define the specific long-standing puzzles it will solve."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The general approach is credible, but the proposal lacks detailed milestones, validation metrics, and decision criteria. The methodological breadth is impressive but not yet organized into a fully rigorous execution plan."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":2,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is very large for two years. Developing a general computational framework, applying it to three complex systems, integrating multiple data modalities, conducting literature synthesis, and building a larger nucleus-centered model may exceed the capacity of a working group unless the scope is narrowed and staged."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is strongly focused on synthesis of existing data and explicitly avoids new experimental data generation. It makes good use of public datasets and aims to create new value through integration, curation, and secondary analysis."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies data types and biological domains but does not provide enough concrete information about the datasets to be synthesized. Further data scouting is acknowledged, but the feasibility of the full plan cannot be assessed without more detail."",""open_science_commitment_score"":4,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to open-source methods, public datasets, and reproducible workflows is well aligned with the call. However, the proposal should include a more specific open science implementation plan and a clearer description of trainee involvement in reproducible research practices.""}]}",0,y1::9,y1,9,From image data and biophysical simulations to principles of mesoscale organization through integrated transfer learning,Rejected,Mary Mirvis; Adriana Dawes,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y1.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 5: Senior systems cell biologist and team-science reviewer with experience in collaborative synthesis centers and trainee training. This proposal fits the spirit of the call in several important ways: it addresses a broad scientific question that cannot be solved by a single lab, relies on existing public data, requires a multidisciplinary team, and aims to produce reusable computational workflows. I was particularly pleased to see attention to systematic literature synthesis and to the possibility of pooling smaller datasets, which are often overlooked in data synthesis proposals. The working group justification is credible, and the project could provide excellent training opportunities in data science, image analysis, biophysics, and collaborative research. That said, the proposal does not provide enough detail about the actual working group structure, trainee roles, governance, meeting cadence, division of labor, or community engagement. It also lacks a clear training plan despite the call's emphasis on developing the future data-savvy workforce. Scientifically, the proposal is ambitious and exciting, but it would benefit from clearer milestones, deliverables, and go/no-go criteria. I view this as a strong but somewhat under-organized proposal for a working group.","The project clearly requires multiple domains of expertise and is appropriate for a collaborative working group. It addresses an important and broadly significant question in cell organization and emergence. The planned outputs, including a generalizable framework, predictive models, integrated datasets, and open-source workflows, are well aligned with NCEMS priorities. The proposal recognizes the need for community-scale curation and synthesis, including smaller literature-derived datasets. The project could provide rich interdisciplinary training opportunities if formalized.","The working group plan is not sufficiently developed. The proposal does not describe team composition in detail, governance, how conflicts or technical decisions will be managed, how trainees will be integrated, or how the broader community will be engaged. The training component is implicit rather than explicit. Milestones are broad, and the two-year timeline lacks concrete deliverables by quarter or by test case. The rationale for NCEMS support is reasonable but could better articulate why existing collaborations or institutional resources cannot accomplish the work. The project risks spreading effort across too many biological systems and computational methods without adequate prioritization.",4,"A compelling synthesis and team-science proposal with strong alignment to NCEMS goals, but it needs more concrete project management, training, and milestone plans. Its scientific ambition is high, and success would depend on disciplined prioritization and strong coordination.",5,The proposal directly addresses emergent cellular architecture and how collective interactions among components generate mesoscale organization. This is highly responsive to the emergence-focused mission of the call.,4,"The proposed integration of public imaging, in vitro, simulation, and literature data through transfer learning is innovative and could significantly influence how cell organization is studied. The significance is high, though the proposal could better define the specific long-standing puzzles it will solve.",3,"The general approach is credible, but the proposal lacks detailed milestones, validation metrics, and decision criteria. The methodological breadth is impressive but not yet organized into a fully rigorous execution plan.",2,"The scope is very large for two years. Developing a general computational framework, applying it to three complex systems, integrating multiple data modalities, conducting literature synthesis, and building a larger nucleus-centered model may exceed the capacity of a working group unless the scope is narrowed and staged.",5,"The proposal is strongly focused on synthesis of existing data and explicitly avoids new experimental data generation. It makes good use of public datasets and aims to create new value through integration, curation, and secondary analysis.",3,"The proposal identifies data types and biological domains but does not provide enough concrete information about the datasets to be synthesized. Further data scouting is acknowledged, but the feasibility of the full plan cannot be assessed without more detail.",4,"The commitment to open-source methods, public datasets, and reproducible workflows is well aligned with the call. However, the proposal should include a more specific open science implementation plan and a clearer description of trainee involvement in reproducible research practices.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:42:22.149843,0,success,,"This proposal aligns well with the funding call by addressing a broad, multidisciplinary scientific question using existing public datasets and open-source workflows. While the scientific premise is ambitious and offers strong potential for literature synthesis and interdisciplinary training, the project's organizational structure remains underdeveloped. Specifically, the proposal lacks crucial details regarding working group governance, an explicit trainee training plan, and concrete project milestones.","The proposal targets an important scientific question in cell organization that necessitates a collaborative, multidisciplinary approach. Proposed deliverables, including predictive models, open-source computational workflows, and integrated datasets, strongly align with institutional priorities. Additionally, the project's focus on systematic literature synthesis and pooling smaller datasets presents a robust framework for community-scale curation.","The proposal lacks organizational detail, particularly regarding team composition, project governance, trainee integration, and community engagement. Furthermore, the two-year timeline is overly broad, lacking concrete, prioritized deliverables and clear go/no-go criteria for the various biological systems and computational methods proposed. Finally, the training component remains implicit rather than formal, and the justification for specific institutional funding over existing resources requires stronger articulation."
baseline::y2::1::claude-sonnet-5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,1,baseline::y2::1::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:24:14.597208,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational structural biologist working on protein dynamics, I find the physics-informed framing of this proposal compelling. The idea of deriving continuous, physically interpretable order parameters (coupling strength, directionality, timescale) to replace qualitative allostery classification is a genuine conceptual advance, and grounding this in correlation functions and fluctuation-response theory is well-motivated by the dynamic allostery literature (Cooper/Dryden, McLeish, Nussinov). My primary concern is methodological rigor around the plan to generate new MD trajectories via BioEmu and Folding@home for the 18 proteins lacking public MD data. This is effectively new simulation data generation, which sits uneasily with the RFP's core requirement that no new data be generated. The proposal should have restricted scope to the 10 systems with existing MD data, or explicitly justified why generating structural models/trajectories for missing systems still qualifies as 'synthesis.' Additionally, the linear-response formalism for propagation is reasonable but somewhat oversimplified for capturing anharmonic or allosteric transitions involving larger conformational shifts; more justification of assumptions is needed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong theoretical grounding in dynamic allostery physics; clear articulation of a gap (lack of quantitative, comparable allostery metrics); reasonable choice of well-studied systems (KRAS, EGFR, BTK, SRC)."",
      ""weakness"": ""Plan to generate new MD trajectories conflicts with 'no new data' mandate; linear-response assumptions may not generalize to large conformational allostery; limited discussion of validation against ground-truth allosteric mechanisms beyond DMS proxies."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong and physically grounded synthesis idea, but partially undermined by scope creep into new simulation and experimental data generation that conflicts with the call's core synthesis-only mandate."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Allostery is a textbook emergent phenomenon arising from collective residue dynamics rather than any single interaction, making this an excellent fit for the emergence theme."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A quantitative, transferable 'allostery score' would be a significant advance over existing heuristic network methods, though similar dynamic-coupling metrics have been proposed piecemeal in prior literature."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The correlation-function/linear-response framework is reasonable but likely oversimplified for allostery involving large conformational transitions; validation strategy relies heavily on indirect DMS correlations."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Combining descriptor development, ML training, evolutionary analysis, and experimental design/validation within 24 months is highly ambitious given the complexity of MD-based analyses alone."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The plan to generate new MD trajectories for 18 of 28 proteins and to perform new experimental validation directly contradicts the 'no new data' principle central to this call."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Specific, appropriate public repositories (MaveDB, MDRepo, MDVerse) are identified with a concrete protein table, showing solid initial data scouting."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""An open-source tool (AllosteryMap) is promised, but there is little detail on data standards, licensing, or workflow reproducibility practices.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From a machine learning and AI methodology standpoint, this proposal has an appealing goal—learning a low-dimensional, physically interpretable embedding of allosteric behavior—but the ML plan itself is underspecified. The proposal mentions diffusion maps and manifold learning for constructing an 'allosteric order parameter' space, and separately mentions training models to predict how mutations shift these order parameters, but does not specify architectures, training/validation splits, or how heterogeneous data modalities (MD trajectories, DMS fitness values, phylogenetic features) will be harmonized into comparable feature vectors across proteins of different lengths and folds. This cross-protein feature alignment problem is nontrivial and is the crux of whether the framework will actually generalize, yet it receives only qualitative treatment. The claim that unrelated proteins with similar coupling architecture will 'cluster together' in the learned space is an interesting testable hypothesis, but the criteria for success (e.g., quantitative benchmarks, cross-validation across protein families) are not defined. I would want to see benchmark datasets and explicit metrics before funding."",
      ""strengths"": ""Ambitious and well-motivated goal of building a transferable, physically interpretable embedding space for allostery; interesting three-way integration of MD, DMS, and evolutionary features."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lack of specificity on ML architecture, training/validation methodology, and feature harmonization across heterogeneous protein systems; no clear quantitative success metrics for generalization claims."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An intellectually exciting AI framework whose methodological details need substantial elaboration to be convincing as a rigorous, generalizable data science approach."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The attempt to define emergent regulatory behavior via low-dimensional order parameters aligns well with the emergence theme, though the ML framing is somewhat abstracted from the underlying biology."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Constructing a unified, cross-protein allostery embedding is novel relative to existing single-protein or heuristic network approaches."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Model architecture, feature normalization across differently sized/folded proteins, and validation metrics are not adequately specified, raising concerns about reproducibility and generalizability claims."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Descriptor development, model training, and design/validation are compressed into overlapping phases without clear contingency for the technical difficulty of feature harmonization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Data synthesis of DMS, MD, and evolutionary datasets is central, but generation of new simulations and experimental testing dilutes the pure synthesis focus."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Good identification of MaveDB, MDRepo, and MDVerse as data sources, though it is unclear how data heterogeneity across these sources will be reconciled computationally."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Promise of open-source tools is stated but no detail on model/code versioning, benchmark release, or adherence to FAIR ML practices is provided.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As an evolutionary biologist, I appreciate the ambition to trace how allosteric network architecture changes 'from primitive to complex organisms,' but this component of the proposal is the weakest scientifically. The hypothesis that allosteric networks are optimized for information transmission with increasing organismal complexity is stated without a clear phylogenetic framework, appropriate orthology controls, or statistical treatment of confounds (e.g., differences in protein family, functional context, or selection regime unrelated to complexity per se). The framing of a fruit fly to human comparison as a proxy for 'complexity' is scientifically outdated and risks conflating organismal complexity with evolutionary distance in a way that could produce spurious correlations. There is also no team member with explicit expertise in molecular evolution, phylogenetics, or comparative genomics, which is a notable gap given the significance placed on this evolutionary analysis in Goal 3 and the final project phase."",
      ""strengths"": ""Interesting integration of coevolutionary/conservation signals with dynamics-derived networks; potential to generate hypotheses about how allosteric robustness evolves."",
      ""weakness"": ""No dedicated evolutionary biology expertise on the team; conflation of phylogenetic distance with 'organismal complexity'; lack of rigorous phylogenetic/statistical methodology for cross-species comparisons."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising but underdeveloped evolutionary component that would benefit substantially from added expertise and a more rigorous comparative framework."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Evolution of network-level communication properties is a good example of emergent systems-level phenomena, fitting the call's scope."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The idea of tracking allosteric network evolution is intriguing but not fully novel; comparative dynamics studies exist, and the proposal doesn't clearly differentiate its approach from them."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The evolutionary hypothesis lacks a defined phylogenetic comparative method, appropriate controls, and statistical framework, undermining confidence in interpretability of results."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The evolutionary analysis is relegated to the final 6 months, which seems insufficient given its conceptual complexity and reliance on earlier-stage descriptor development being completed on schedule."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Integrating evolutionary sequence data with dynamics and DMS data across taxa is a genuine multi-source synthesis effort appropriate to the call."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Table 1 proteins and DMS sources are identified, but public sequence/phylogenetic databases to be used for the evolutionary analysis are not explicitly named."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""General commitment to releasing curated datasets is stated, but no specifics on phylogenetic data provenance or evolutionary analysis reproducibility are given.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this from a data synthesis and open science infrastructure perspective, I have mixed feelings. On one hand, the proposal identifies concrete, appropriate public data sources (MaveDB, MDRepo, MDVerse) and a defined protein list, which is commendable NCEMS-style data scouting. On the other hand, the proposal's plan to generate new MD trajectories with BioEmu/Folding@home for systems lacking existing data, and to experimentally validate designed mutations in a wet lab, moves substantially beyond the community-scale synthesis mandate into primary data generation. This is a recurring tension throughout the proposal and needs to be resolved—either by scoping the project strictly to available MD/DMS data (a smaller but cleaner synthesis project) or by clearly firewalling the 'new data generation' components as a downstream, non-NCEMS-funded validation step. The requested GPU resources (25,000 GPU hours) suggest substantial new computation rather than pure data synthesis, which raises questions about whether NCEMS support is being appropriately targeted. The open science plan (AllosteryMap tool, curated datasets) is a positive gesture, but lacks specifics on repository choice, licensing, and workflow documentation standards (e.g., FAIR, CODATA)."",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear identification of specific public datasets and repositories; genuine multi-lab collaboration structure; concrete deliverable (AllosteryMap) promised as an open resource."",
      ""weakness"": ""Significant scope creep into new simulation and experimental data generation, conflicting with the synthesis-only mandate; open science plan lacks concrete standards or repository commitments; heavy computational resource request without clear boundary between synthesis and novel computation."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""While data identification is solid, the proposal's substantial reliance on new simulation and experimental data generation is a fundamental misalignment with the community-scale synthesis mandate of this call."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Allosteric communication as an emergent, network-level property of proteins is squarely within the scope of molecular/cellular emergence phenomena."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A cross-protein, physically grounded allostery metric would be significant if achieved, addressing a genuine long-standing gap in the field."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Individual technical components (correlation functions, dimensionality reduction) are reasonable, but the overall pipeline's rigor is undermined by unclear data provenance boundaries between existing and newly generated data."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is unrealistic for the combined scope of MD generation for 18 systems, ML model development, evolutionary analysis, and wet-lab validation."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""A substantial fraction of proposed work (new MD simulations, experimental mutagenesis) is not synthesis of existing public data, directly conflicting with a core defining criterion of this RFP."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Specific public repositories and a defined protein table demonstrate genuine engagement with data scouting, a strength relative to many synthesis proposals."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Intent to release tools and datasets is stated but lacks concrete repository, licensing, or documentation commitments expected under NCEMS open science policy.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As an experimental biochemist working on transcription factor regulation, I find the proposed experimental validation loop with the Okafor lab appealing in principle—it grounds the computational predictions in a testable biological system with clear functional readouts (DNA binding, gene expression). However, from a feasibility standpoint, the proposal provides very little detail on which specific transcription factors will be targeted for mutational redesign, what biophysical assays will be used, or how success/failure of designed variants will be quantitatively benchmarked against the AI-predicted order parameter shifts. Additionally, the inclusion of wet-lab mutagenesis and biophysical characterization as a core deliverable is inconsistent with the RFP's explicit 'no new experimental data' criterion; this should either be reframed as optional exploratory work outside the NCEMS-supported scope, or removed to keep the project within the synthesis boundary. I also note the proposal does not explicitly describe trainee roles (graduate students, postdocs) or structured training opportunities within the working group, despite this being a stated priority of the call."",
      ""strengths"": ""Well-chosen model system (allosteric transcription factors) with clear, interpretable functional readouts; logical feedback loop between computational design and biological validation; diverse protein set spanning kinases, signaling proteins, and transcription factors."",
      ""weakness"": ""Wet-lab validation directly conflicts with the synthesis-only, no-new-data mandate; insufficient detail on specific TF targets, assay design, and quantitative benchmarking of designed variants; no explicit discussion of trainee involvement or training structure."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A biologically sound validation concept undermined by insufficient experimental detail and a structural conflict with the call's synthesis-only mandate, plus a missing explicit training plan for early-career researchers."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Allosteric regulation of transcription factors is a clear molecular-scale emergent phenomenon relevant to the call's focus on molecular and cellular emergence."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Applying a generalized allostery score to engineer TF regulation is a compelling application, though the novelty rests heavily on the (unvalidated) generality of the upstream order parameter framework."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational-to-experimental feedback loop is conceptually sound but lacks operational detail (assay types, sample sizes, statistical thresholds for validating designed mutants)."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Allocating only ~7 months (months 11–18) for experimental validation and design iteration is tight given typical mutagenesis-expression-assay cycle times."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The explicit plan for new wet-lab mutagenesis and biophysical assays is new experimental data generation, which is inconsistent with the core synthesis-only requirement of the call."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 3,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""DMS data sources for the chosen TFs are identified, but it's unclear whether sufficient public structural/dynamics data exist for the specific TFs slated for experimental redesign."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""General commitment to public release of tools/data is mentioned, but there is no explicit plan for training the next generation of researchers or documenting the experimental validation workflow openly.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::1,y2,1,Emergent Order Parameters of Allostery: An AI Framework for Predicting and Engineering Protein Regulation,Accepted,Banu Ozkan; Denise Okafor; Vincent Voelz,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"As a computational structural biologist working on protein dynamics, I find the physics-informed framing of this proposal compelling. The idea of deriving continuous, physically interpretable order parameters (coupling strength, directionality, timescale) to replace qualitative allostery classification is a genuine conceptual advance, and grounding this in correlation functions and fluctuation-response theory is well-motivated by the dynamic allostery literature (Cooper/Dryden, McLeish, Nussinov). My primary concern is methodological rigor around the plan to generate new MD trajectories via BioEmu and Folding@home for the 18 proteins lacking public MD data. This is effectively new simulation data generation, which sits uneasily with the RFP's core requirement that no new data be generated. The proposal should have restricted scope to the 10 systems with existing MD data, or explicitly justified why generating structural models/trajectories for missing systems still qualifies as 'synthesis.' Additionally, the linear-response formalism for propagation is reasonable but somewhat oversimplified for capturing anharmonic or allosteric transitions involving larger conformational shifts; more justification of assumptions is needed.","Strong theoretical grounding in dynamic allostery physics; clear articulation of a gap (lack of quantitative, comparable allostery metrics); reasonable choice of well-studied systems (KRAS, EGFR, BTK, SRC).",Plan to generate new MD trajectories conflicts with 'no new data' mandate; linear-response assumptions may not generalize to large conformational allostery; limited discussion of validation against ground-truth allosteric mechanisms beyond DMS proxies.,3,"A conceptually strong and physically grounded synthesis idea, but partially undermined by scope creep into new simulation and experimental data generation that conflicts with the call's core synthesis-only mandate.",5,"Allostery is a textbook emergent phenomenon arising from collective residue dynamics rather than any single interaction, making this an excellent fit for the emergence theme.",4,"A quantitative, transferable 'allostery score' would be a significant advance over existing heuristic network methods, though similar dynamic-coupling metrics have been proposed piecemeal in prior literature.",3,The correlation-function/linear-response framework is reasonable but likely oversimplified for allostery involving large conformational transitions; validation strategy relies heavily on indirect DMS correlations.,3,"Combining descriptor development, ML training, evolutionary analysis, and experimental design/validation within 24 months is highly ambitious given the complexity of MD-based analyses alone.",2,The plan to generate new MD trajectories for 18 of 28 proteins and to perform new experimental validation directly contradicts the 'no new data' principle central to this call.,4,"Specific, appropriate public repositories (MaveDB, MDRepo, MDVerse) are identified with a concrete protein table, showing solid initial data scouting.",3,"An open-source tool (AllosteryMap) is promised, but there is little detail on data standards, licensing, or workflow reproducibility practices.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:42:32.749884,0,success,,"The proposal presents a compelling, physics-informed approach to derive continuous order parameters for allostery, well-grounded in fluctuation-response theory. However, the plan to generate new molecular dynamics trajectories appears to violate the funding mandate against new data generation. Furthermore, the use of a linear-response formalism may be too simplistic for capturing large conformational changes without further justification.","The project features a strong theoretical foundation in dynamic allostery physics and clearly addresses the need for quantitative metrics. Furthermore, the selected protein systems are well-studied and highly appropriate for the proposed research.","The generation of new molecular dynamics trajectories directly conflicts with the stipulation prohibiting new data collection. Additionally, the linear-response assumptions may fail to capture large conformational shifts, and validation against ground-truth mechanisms is insufficiently addressed."
baseline::y2::1::claude-sonnet-5::002,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,2,baseline::y2::1::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:24:14.597208,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational structural biologist working on protein dynamics, I find the physics-informed framing of this proposal compelling. The idea of deriving continuous, physically interpretable order parameters (coupling strength, directionality, timescale) to replace qualitative allostery classification is a genuine conceptual advance, and grounding this in correlation functions and fluctuation-response theory is well-motivated by the dynamic allostery literature (Cooper/Dryden, McLeish, Nussinov). My primary concern is methodological rigor around the plan to generate new MD trajectories via BioEmu and Folding@home for the 18 proteins lacking public MD data. This is effectively new simulation data generation, which sits uneasily with the RFP's core requirement that no new data be generated. The proposal should have restricted scope to the 10 systems with existing MD data, or explicitly justified why generating structural models/trajectories for missing systems still qualifies as 'synthesis.' Additionally, the linear-response formalism for propagation is reasonable but somewhat oversimplified for capturing anharmonic or allosteric transitions involving larger conformational shifts; more justification of assumptions is needed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong theoretical grounding in dynamic allostery physics; clear articulation of a gap (lack of quantitative, comparable allostery metrics); reasonable choice of well-studied systems (KRAS, EGFR, BTK, SRC)."",
      ""weakness"": ""Plan to generate new MD trajectories conflicts with 'no new data' mandate; linear-response assumptions may not generalize to large conformational allostery; limited discussion of validation against ground-truth allosteric mechanisms beyond DMS proxies."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong and physically grounded synthesis idea, but partially undermined by scope creep into new simulation and experimental data generation that conflicts with the call's core synthesis-only mandate."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Allostery is a textbook emergent phenomenon arising from collective residue dynamics rather than any single interaction, making this an excellent fit for the emergence theme."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A quantitative, transferable 'allostery score' would be a significant advance over existing heuristic network methods, though similar dynamic-coupling metrics have been proposed piecemeal in prior literature."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The correlation-function/linear-response framework is reasonable but likely oversimplified for allostery involving large conformational transitions; validation strategy relies heavily on indirect DMS correlations."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Combining descriptor development, ML training, evolutionary analysis, and experimental design/validation within 24 months is highly ambitious given the complexity of MD-based analyses alone."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The plan to generate new MD trajectories for 18 of 28 proteins and to perform new experimental validation directly contradicts the 'no new data' principle central to this call."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Specific, appropriate public repositories (MaveDB, MDRepo, MDVerse) are identified with a concrete protein table, showing solid initial data scouting."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""An open-source tool (AllosteryMap) is promised, but there is little detail on data standards, licensing, or workflow reproducibility practices.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From a machine learning and AI methodology standpoint, this proposal has an appealing goal—learning a low-dimensional, physically interpretable embedding of allosteric behavior—but the ML plan itself is underspecified. The proposal mentions diffusion maps and manifold learning for constructing an 'allosteric order parameter' space, and separately mentions training models to predict how mutations shift these order parameters, but does not specify architectures, training/validation splits, or how heterogeneous data modalities (MD trajectories, DMS fitness values, phylogenetic features) will be harmonized into comparable feature vectors across proteins of different lengths and folds. This cross-protein feature alignment problem is nontrivial and is the crux of whether the framework will actually generalize, yet it receives only qualitative treatment. The claim that unrelated proteins with similar coupling architecture will 'cluster together' in the learned space is an interesting testable hypothesis, but the criteria for success (e.g., quantitative benchmarks, cross-validation across protein families) are not defined. I would want to see benchmark datasets and explicit metrics before funding."",
      ""strengths"": ""Ambitious and well-motivated goal of building a transferable, physically interpretable embedding space for allostery; interesting three-way integration of MD, DMS, and evolutionary features."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lack of specificity on ML architecture, training/validation methodology, and feature harmonization across heterogeneous protein systems; no clear quantitative success metrics for generalization claims."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An intellectually exciting AI framework whose methodological details need substantial elaboration to be convincing as a rigorous, generalizable data science approach."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The attempt to define emergent regulatory behavior via low-dimensional order parameters aligns well with the emergence theme, though the ML framing is somewhat abstracted from the underlying biology."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Constructing a unified, cross-protein allostery embedding is novel relative to existing single-protein or heuristic network approaches."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Model architecture, feature normalization across differently sized/folded proteins, and validation metrics are not adequately specified, raising concerns about reproducibility and generalizability claims."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Descriptor development, model training, and design/validation are compressed into overlapping phases without clear contingency for the technical difficulty of feature harmonization."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 3,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Data synthesis of DMS, MD, and evolutionary datasets is central, but generation of new simulations and experimental testing dilutes the pure synthesis focus."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Good identification of MaveDB, MDRepo, and MDVerse as data sources, though it is unclear how data heterogeneity across these sources will be reconciled computationally."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Promise of open-source tools is stated but no detail on model/code versioning, benchmark release, or adherence to FAIR ML practices is provided.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As an evolutionary biologist, I appreciate the ambition to trace how allosteric network architecture changes 'from primitive to complex organisms,' but this component of the proposal is the weakest scientifically. The hypothesis that allosteric networks are optimized for information transmission with increasing organismal complexity is stated without a clear phylogenetic framework, appropriate orthology controls, or statistical treatment of confounds (e.g., differences in protein family, functional context, or selection regime unrelated to complexity per se). The framing of a fruit fly to human comparison as a proxy for 'complexity' is scientifically outdated and risks conflating organismal complexity with evolutionary distance in a way that could produce spurious correlations. There is also no team member with explicit expertise in molecular evolution, phylogenetics, or comparative genomics, which is a notable gap given the significance placed on this evolutionary analysis in Goal 3 and the final project phase."",
      ""strengths"": ""Interesting integration of coevolutionary/conservation signals with dynamics-derived networks; potential to generate hypotheses about how allosteric robustness evolves."",
      ""weakness"": ""No dedicated evolutionary biology expertise on the team; conflation of phylogenetic distance with 'organismal complexity'; lack of rigorous phylogenetic/statistical methodology for cross-species comparisons."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising but underdeveloped evolutionary component that would benefit substantially from added expertise and a more rigorous comparative framework."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
