    }
  ]
}",0,y2::1,y2,1,Emergent Order Parameters of Allostery: An AI Framework for Predicting and Engineering Protein Regulation,Accepted,Banu Ozkan; Denise Okafor; Vincent Voelz,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"As an experimental biochemist working on transcription factor regulation, I find the proposed experimental validation loop with the Okafor lab appealing in principle—it grounds the computational predictions in a testable biological system with clear functional readouts (DNA binding, gene expression). However, from a feasibility standpoint, the proposal provides very little detail on which specific transcription factors will be targeted for mutational redesign, what biophysical assays will be used, or how success/failure of designed variants will be quantitatively benchmarked against the AI-predicted order parameter shifts. Additionally, the inclusion of wet-lab mutagenesis and biophysical characterization as a core deliverable is inconsistent with the RFP's explicit 'no new experimental data' criterion; this should either be reframed as optional exploratory work outside the NCEMS-supported scope, or removed to keep the project within the synthesis boundary. I also note the proposal does not explicitly describe trainee roles (graduate students, postdocs) or structured training opportunities within the working group, despite this being a stated priority of the call.","Well-chosen model system (allosteric transcription factors) with clear, interpretable functional readouts; logical feedback loop between computational design and biological validation; diverse protein set spanning kinases, signaling proteins, and transcription factors.","Wet-lab validation directly conflicts with the synthesis-only, no-new-data mandate; insufficient detail on specific TF targets, assay design, and quantitative benchmarking of designed variants; no explicit discussion of trainee involvement or training structure.",3,"A biologically sound validation concept undermined by insufficient experimental detail and a structural conflict with the call's synthesis-only mandate, plus a missing explicit training plan for early-career researchers.",4,Allosteric regulation of transcription factors is a clear molecular-scale emergent phenomenon relevant to the call's focus on molecular and cellular emergence.,3,"Applying a generalized allostery score to engineer TF regulation is a compelling application, though the novelty rests heavily on the (unvalidated) generality of the upstream order parameter framework.",3,"The computational-to-experimental feedback loop is conceptually sound but lacks operational detail (assay types, sample sizes, statistical thresholds for validating designed mutants).",3,Allocating only ~7 months (months 11–18) for experimental validation and design iteration is tight given typical mutagenesis-expression-assay cycle times.,2,"The explicit plan for new wet-lab mutagenesis and biophysical assays is new experimental data generation, which is inconsistent with the core synthesis-only requirement of the call.",3,"DMS data sources for the chosen TFs are identified, but it's unclear whether sufficient public structural/dynamics data exist for the specific TFs slated for experimental redesign.",3,"General commitment to public release of tools/data is mentioned, but there is no explicit plan for training the next generation of researchers or documenting the experimental validation workflow openly.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:43:06.573545,0,success,,"The proposal features a promising experimental validation loop using allosteric transcription factors, but lacks crucial detail on target selection and biophysical assays. A major concern is the inclusion of wet-lab validation, which directly conflicts with the RFP's strict mandate against generating new experimental data. Additionally, the application does not address the required trainee roles and structured training opportunities.","The proposed model system of allosteric transcription factors is well-chosen and offers clear, interpretable functional readouts. The project also establishes a logical feedback loop between computational design and biological validation across a diverse set of proteins, including kinases and signaling proteins.","The inclusion of wet-lab experimental validation fundamentally conflicts with the RFP's mandate against generating new data. Furthermore, the methodology lacks sufficient detail on specific targets, assay designs, and quantitative benchmarking. Finally, the proposal fails to explicitly outline trainee involvement and structured training opportunities."
baseline::y2::1::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::001,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,1,baseline::y2::1::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T15:23:24.456048,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational biophysicist specializing in molecular dynamics and protein energy landscapes, I find the theoretical framework of this proposal outstanding. The applicants correctly identify the limitations of classical discrete-state thermodynamic models (MWC, KNF) and propose a highly compelling, physics-informed data science approach to derive continuous 'allosteric order parameters' from dynamic ensembles. The integration of MD simulations with deep mutational scanning (DMS) to evaluate fluctuation-response relationships is a powerful idea. However, I have a major reservation regarding the proposal's compliance with the specific rules of this funding call. The RFP explicitly states that community-scale synthesis projects 'must use only existing publicly available data' and 'does not generate any new experimental data.' The proposal clearly outlines plans in Phase 3 to have the Okafor lab experimentally test computational predictions using mutagenesis and biophysical assays. Furthermore, the generation of new MD simulations via Folding@home pushes the boundary of 'synthesis of existing data,' although computational generation is sometimes a gray area. Because the experimental aims explicitly violate the RFP guidelines, I must significantly lower the synthesis focus and overall scores, despite the high scientific merit of the physical modeling."",
      ""strengths"": ""Exceptional theoretical grounding in dynamic allostery; innovative use of fluctuation-response relationships to create quantifiable order parameters; strong team with clear complementary expertise in MD and statistical mechanics."",
      ""weakness"": ""Explicitly violates the fundamental RFP constraint against generating new experimental data by proposing wet-lab mutagenesis and biophysical assays. Extensive generation of new MD simulations also stretches the definition of a pure data synthesis project."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A scientifically brilliant proposal aiming to redefine allostery through physics-based dynamic parameters, but it fundamentally runs afoul of the specific constraints of this data synthesis funding call by including de novo experimental validation."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Allostery is a quintessential emergent phenomenon at the molecular scale, and quantifying it via dynamic network responses perfectly aligns with the call's goals."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Moving from descriptive, structural allosteric mechanisms to predictive, continuous order parameters is a major paradigm shift that could solve long-standing puzzles in molecular biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational and theoretical approaches (correlation functions, diffusion maps) are highly rigorous, though the inclusion of experimental aims detracts from the methodological focus expected here."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is overly ambitious. Developing the ML framework, generating massive new MD trajectories (25,000 GPU hours requested), and attempting wet-lab validation within 24 months is unrealistic."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 1,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal fails the primary synthesis criterion: 'It does not generate any new experimental data.' The inclusion of the Okafor lab's experimental mutagenesis directly violates this."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Excellent identification of MaveDB, MDRepo, and MDVerse for existing data. The fallback to BioEmu/Folding@home for missing MD data is clever but leans away from pure synthesis."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to open science through the planned release of the AllosteryMap tools and curated datasets to public repositories.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From the perspective of an evolutionary biologist and bioinformatician, this proposal presents an exciting opportunity to study the evolution of protein communication networks. The hypothesis that allosteric networks evolve systematically, showing optimization of information transmission across phylogenetic trees, is fascinating. Mapping DMS and MD data onto evolutionary conservation offers a novel way to distinguish essential communication pathways from those that have been rewired. However, the evolutionary framing is somewhat archaic; describing a fruit fly as 'primitive' compared to a 'complex' human reflects a misunderstanding of modern evolutionary biology, which views both as highly derived endpoints of their respective lineages. Furthermore, as a reviewer evaluating against the NCEMS synthesis criteria, I must penalize the proposal for proposing new bench experiments. A synthesis project must rely solely on existing data. The team should have replaced the experimental validation aim with a deeper, purely computational evolutionary analysis across a broader set of taxa."",
      ""strengths"": ""Innovative integration of evolutionary coevolution patterns with dynamic coupling networks; strong potential to answer fundamental questions about how regulatory networks evolve."",
      ""weakness"": ""Use of outdated evolutionary terminology ('primitive' vs 'complex'); inclusion of new experimental generation violating the RFP; evolutionary analyses lack methodological detail compared to the biophysical aims."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An intriguing proposal with strong potential for evolutionary insights into allostery, but it is undermined by flawed evolutionary terminology and a fatal violation of the synthesis-only rule by proposing new experiments."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Addresses emergence by studying how local sequence changes cascade through a protein network and evolve over millions of years."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Linking atomistic MD order parameters to evolutionary fitness landscapes (via DMS) is highly novel and significant."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""While the MD/DMS integration is rigorous, the evolutionary methodology is glossed over, lacking specifics on phylogenetic reconstruction or ancestral sequence resurrection models."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope is disjointed. Trying to do deep biophysics, evolutionary biology, machine learning, and wet-lab experiments in 2 years is fundamentally unfeasible for a working group of this size."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 1,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Directly violates the mandate against generating new experimental data. The experimental testing by the Okafor lab disqualifies it as a pure synthesis project."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Good use of public DMS and MD datasets, but lacks clear identification of the specific evolutionary/phylogenetic databases (e.g., Pfam, OrthoDB) to be used."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Commits to releasing data and models, aligning well with open science principles, though specifics on licensing and platform (e.g., GitHub, Zenodo) are missing.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a machine learning scientist focused on biological systems, I am very enthusiastic about the algorithmic core of this proposal. Projecting high-dimensional molecular dynamics trajectories and mutation effects into a low-dimensional 'allosteric order-parameter' using manifold learning and diffusion maps is exactly the kind of innovative analytical strategy this call aims to support. The idea of learning a generalized 'allostery score' across diverse proteins is a fantastic application of AI. However, there are significant red flags. First, the proposal asks NCEMS for 'practical expertise/consultation about the best architectures for machine learning models'. If the PIs are proposing an 'AI Framework', they should already possess the core AI expertise; relying on the funding agency for architectural consultation suggests a critical skills gap in the proposed working group. Second, the proposal fails the synthesis test by including wet-lab experiments. The project would be much stronger if the third aim focused on in silico validation on hold-out datasets from MaveDB rather than pivoting to bench work."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent application of non-linear dimensionality reduction and manifold learning to create a unified, quantitative metric for a complex biological phenomenon."",
      ""weakness"": ""Proposes new experimental data generation; requests basic ML architecture consultation from NCEMS indicating a gap in team expertise; lacks specifics on the actual ML models (e.g., GNNs, transformers) to be employed."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A conceptually strong AI/ML proposal that seeks to create a universal allostery score, but is hampered by a lack of specified ML architectures, reliance on NCEMS for core AI expertise, and an ineligible experimental validation aim."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Perfectly captures the essence of emergent phenomena by using AI to decode how global regulatory wiring emerges from local dynamics."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A generalized, AI-driven predictive map from sequence to allosteric control would be a monumental achievement in structural biology and protein engineering."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The theoretical physics rigor is high, but the ML rigor is low. Merely stating 'build ML models' and asking NCEMS for architectural help is insufficient for an AI-focused proposal."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline allocates too little time (Months 5-12) for the complex task of integrating DMS, MD, and building/training novel ML architectures."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 2,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Uses diverse datasets to synthesize a new model, but explicitly plans to generate new experimental data, violating a core tenet of the RFP."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Excellent identification of 28 specific proteins with matching DMS (MaveDB) and MD data (MDRepo/MDVerse), showing strong preliminary data scouting."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Clear commitment to releasing the AI models and the AllosteryMap tool, which will be a valuable resource for the community.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""I am reviewing this from the perspective of a structural biologist who studies transcription factors (TFs). The premise of understanding allostery dynamically rather than through static MWC/KNF discrete states is the correct modern view of the field. Applying this to TFs is a brilliant choice, as their regulation by small molecules or distal protein-protein interactions is notoriously difficult to capture via static crystallography. The inclusion of the Okafor lab, which has strong expertise in this area, is a huge plus. However, as an evaluator for the NCEMS data synthesis call, I must follow the strict rules of the RFP. The call states: 'It does not generate any new experimental data.' The proposal's plan to have the Okafor lab experimentally test designs via mutagenesis and biophysical assays is a fatal flaw for this specific funding mechanism. The PIs should submit this to a standard NSF or NIH mechanism, where computational-experimental feedback loops are praised. Here, it disqualifies the proposal from being a true synthesis project."",
      ""strengths"": ""Focuses on a highly relevant and challenging class of proteins (transcription factors); strong theoretical shift from static to dynamic allostery; well-composed interdisciplinary team."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal fundamentally misunderstands the nature of this specific RFP by including a significant wet-lab experimental component (Phase 3)."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Scientifically, this is a 5-star proposal that beautifully marries biophysics, ML, and structural biology. Unfortunately, administratively it is a 1-star proposal because it explicitly proposes generating new experimental data in a strict data-synthesis-only call."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Allosteric regulation in transcription factors is a prime example of an emergent molecular property crucial for cellular signaling."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Creating a quantifiable 'allostery score' that allows cross-species and cross-protein comparisons is highly novel."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The biophysical approach (fluctuation-response relationships, dynamic coupling) is extremely rigorous and well-established in the PI's prior work."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The inclusion of wet-lab experimental validation makes the 2-year timeline completely unrealistic, especially considering the computational bottlenecks of MD."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 1,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Fails the synthesis requirement by proposing new experimental data generation. Furthermore, generating massive new MD datasets borders on violating the 'existing data' rule."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Good use of MaveDB and structural databases. The strategy for identifying orthologs is sound."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Promises open tools and datasets, but lacks a specific data management plan regarding how the massive 200TB of generated MD data will be hosted publicly.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a data scientist focused on open science and community-scale synthesis methodology, I evaluate how well projects integrate existing knowledge and adhere to synthesis principles. The team has done an excellent job identifying disparate databases (MaveDB, MDRepo, MDVerse) and bringing them together to answer a novel question. This is the hallmark of a great synthesis project. However, the proposal fails on the most critical eligibility criterion of the RFP: 'It does not generate any new experimental data.' Aim 3 proposes rational design and experimental testing by the Okafor lab. Additionally, requesting 25,000 GPU hours to generate *new* MD simulations using BioEmu and Folding@home directly contradicts the spirit of utilizing *existing* publicly available data. While computing is sometimes allowed in synthesis, generating 200TB of de novo simulation data shifts this from a 'data synthesis' project to a standard computational biology project. The need for NCEMS is also poorly justified; they ask for 200TB of storage and ML consulting, which sounds like they want NCEMS to act as a core facility rather than a collaborative synthesis center."",
      ""strengths"": ""Excellent initial data scouting linking 28 specific proteins across DMS and MD databases. Strong commitment to open science and creating a community tool (AllosteryMap)."",
      ""weakness"": ""Explicitly violates the no-new-experimental-data rule. Heavily relies on generating massive amounts of new computational data rather than synthesizing existing data. Weak justification for NCEMS support."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 2,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong computational biology project that is submitted to the wrong funding call. It violates the core mandates of the synthesis RFP by proposing wet-lab experiments and massive de novo simulation generation rather than relying on existing public data."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The project addresses molecular emergence by modeling how local mutations cause global regulatory shifts."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The synthesis of DMS data with dynamic descriptors to create a predictive map is highly innovative."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Data integration pipeline is well thought out, but the plan to rely on NCEMS for ML architectures reduces confidence in the team's technical rigor in AI."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 2,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Generating 200TB of new MD data and conducting experimental validation within 24 months is highly improbable."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 1,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Directly proposes experimental generation (Okafor lab) and massive computational data generation (Folding@home), violating the 'only existing publicly available data' requirement."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The preliminary work to identify 28 target proteins with overlapping DMS and MD data availability is exactly what synthesis proposals should demonstrate."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The commitment to open science is explicitly stated, aligning with community-created policies on team and reproducible science.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::1,y2,1,Emergent Order Parameters of Allostery: An AI Framework for Predicting and Engineering Protein Regulation,Accepted,Banu Ozkan; Denise Okafor; Vincent Voelz,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"As a computational biophysicist specializing in molecular dynamics and protein energy landscapes, I find the theoretical framework of this proposal outstanding. The applicants correctly identify the limitations of classical discrete-state thermodynamic models (MWC, KNF) and propose a highly compelling, physics-informed data science approach to derive continuous 'allosteric order parameters' from dynamic ensembles. The integration of MD simulations with deep mutational scanning (DMS) to evaluate fluctuation-response relationships is a powerful idea. However, I have a major reservation regarding the proposal's compliance with the specific rules of this funding call. The RFP explicitly states that community-scale synthesis projects 'must use only existing publicly available data' and 'does not generate any new experimental data.' The proposal clearly outlines plans in Phase 3 to have the Okafor lab experimentally test computational predictions using mutagenesis and biophysical assays. Furthermore, the generation of new MD simulations via Folding@home pushes the boundary of 'synthesis of existing data,' although computational generation is sometimes a gray area. Because the experimental aims explicitly violate the RFP guidelines, I must significantly lower the synthesis focus and overall scores, despite the high scientific merit of the physical modeling.",Exceptional theoretical grounding in dynamic allostery; innovative use of fluctuation-response relationships to create quantifiable order parameters; strong team with clear complementary expertise in MD and statistical mechanics.,Explicitly violates the fundamental RFP constraint against generating new experimental data by proposing wet-lab mutagenesis and biophysical assays. Extensive generation of new MD simulations also stretches the definition of a pure data synthesis project.,3,"A scientifically brilliant proposal aiming to redefine allostery through physics-based dynamic parameters, but it fundamentally runs afoul of the specific constraints of this data synthesis funding call by including de novo experimental validation.",5,"Allostery is a quintessential emergent phenomenon at the molecular scale, and quantifying it via dynamic network responses perfectly aligns with the call's goals.",5,"Moving from descriptive, structural allosteric mechanisms to predictive, continuous order parameters is a major paradigm shift that could solve long-standing puzzles in molecular biology.",4,"The computational and theoretical approaches (correlation functions, diffusion maps) are highly rigorous, though the inclusion of experimental aims detracts from the methodological focus expected here.",3,"The timeline is overly ambitious. Developing the ML framework, generating massive new MD trajectories (25,000 GPU hours requested), and attempting wet-lab validation within 24 months is unrealistic.",1,The proposal fails the primary synthesis criterion: 'It does not generate any new experimental data.' The inclusion of the Okafor lab's experimental mutagenesis directly violates this.,4,"Excellent identification of MaveDB, MDRepo, and MDVerse for existing data. The fallback to BioEmu/Folding@home for missing MD data is clever but leans away from pure synthesis.",5,Strong commitment to open science through the planned release of the AllosteryMap tools and curated datasets to public repositories.,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:43:17.261282,0,success,,"The proposal presents a strong theoretical framework for deriving continuous allosteric order parameters by integrating molecular dynamics simulations with deep mutational scanning data. However, the proposed work violates the specific requirements of the funding call by including plans to generate new experimental mutagenesis and biophysical data. Additionally, the planned generation of new computational simulations pushes the boundaries of the grant's data synthesis constraints. Despite the high scientific merit, the overall evaluation is significantly lowered due to these non-compliance issues.","The project features an exceptionally strong theoretical foundation in dynamic allostery and introduces an innovative approach to quantifying order parameters using fluctuation-response relationships. Furthermore, the research team possesses highly complementary expertise in molecular dynamics and statistical mechanics.","The inclusion of new wet-lab experiments directly violates the grant's stipulation that projects must rely exclusively on existing publicly available data. Moreover, the extensive generation of new molecular dynamics simulations stretches the definition of a pure data synthesis project."
baseline::y2::1::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::002,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,2,baseline::y2::1::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T15:23:24.456048,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
