      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Moving beyond single-organism studies to analyze organelle co-expression networks in a complex human microbiome is groundbreaking."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The statistical approaches are advanced and appropriate, though potential sparsity of transcriptomic data requires careful normalization."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 5,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The step-by-step timeline, dedicating specific months to data cleaning, harmonization, and parallel analyses, is highly realistic and well-structured."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""A true synthesis project that goes beyond the capabilities of a single lab, requiring complex data integration and team science."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The selection of the longitudinal IBD multi-omics dataset is the perfect testbed for the proposed network analyses."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to releasing workflows via established repositories (MGnify, PRIDE) and publishing reproducible frameworks.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal perfectly captures the spirit of the NCEMS synthesis call. By strictly utilizing publicly available data to solve long-standing puzzles about bacterial compartmentalization, the team demonstrates a deep understanding of data re-use. The creation of a searchable, integrated database of bacterial organelles will be a massive boon to the microbiology community, fulfilling the mandate to develop innovative research strategies and train future scientists. However, the proposal's mention of training and outreach is quite vague. It states that activities will 'engage early-career researchers', but there is no specific plan on how trainees will be integrated into the workflow, mentored, or recruited. Additionally, while open science principles are mentioned, a concrete Data Management Plan outlining metadata standards (e.g., FAIR principles) would have elevated the proposal."",
      ""strengths"": ""100% reliance on public data; addresses fundamental questions through team science; clear deliverables including a public database and predictive frameworks."",
      ""weakness"": ""The training plan for graduate students and postdocs is generic and lacks specific actionable items. Missing explicit mention of FAIR data principles."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A highly relevant and well-designed data synthesis project that will produce valuable community resources, though it could improve its explicit training and data management details."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Directly tackles emergence by studying how complex subcellular structures assemble and function within a broader ecological community."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The concept of a unified database for bacterial organelles is highly significant and fills a major gap in the community."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed working group appropriately matches the domain expertise required to rigorously evaluate the synthesized data."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 5,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project scope matches the requested NCEMS resources perfectly, with clear deliverables outlined in the final year."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Embodies the synthesis mandate: integrating massive distinct datasets to generate new insights without generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Public datasets are clearly identified, accessible, and highly appropriate for the proposed synthesis."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""While a database is promised, the lack of adherence to specific FAIR principles and a vague training plan brings this score down slightly.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::11,y2,11,Discovery and functional analysis of bacterial organelles through mining and refinement of omics data,Rejected,Y Hoang; Arash Komeili; Hualiang Pi; Markus Sutter,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"Evaluating this proposal from a systems biology and multi-omics integration standpoint, this is a stellar application. The strategy to use matched metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data from the IBD dataset (MGYS00006120) is precisely the kind of complex synthesis NCEMS is designed to support. The application of WGCNA to build co-expression networks for organelles is an innovative way to infer inter-organelle interactions and regulatory modules in a non-cultivable setting. The rationale for requiring NCEMS staff scientists and computational resources is perfectly justified, as harmonizing multi-omics data across 90 longitudinal subjects is a massive computational undertaking. One minor weakness is that WGCNA requires a relatively large sample size to build robust networks; depending on the sparsity of the specific organelle transcripts in the 90 subjects, the statistical power might be marginal.","Sophisticated use of multi-omics integration and network analysis (WGCNA). Perfect alignment with NCEMS resource offerings. Clear, testable hypotheses regarding coordinated expression.",Statistical power for WGCNA might be limited if the specific organelle transcripts are low-abundance or sparsely distributed across the 90 IBD subjects.,5,"An outstanding, highly integrative systems biology proposal that leverages NCEMS capabilities to extract deep regulatory insights from existing multi-omics datasets.",5,Coordinated multi-gene expression resulting in physical cellular compartments is a fundamental emergent bioscience phenomenon.,5,Moving beyond single-organism studies to analyze organelle co-expression networks in a complex human microbiome is groundbreaking.,4,"The statistical approaches are advanced and appropriate, though potential sparsity of transcriptomic data requires careful normalization.",5,"The step-by-step timeline, dedicating specific months to data cleaning, harmonization, and parallel analyses, is highly realistic and well-structured.",5,"A true synthesis project that goes beyond the capabilities of a single lab, requiring complex data integration and team science.",5,The selection of the longitudinal IBD multi-omics dataset is the perfect testbed for the proposed network analyses.,5,"Strong commitment to releasing workflows via established repositories (MGnify, PRIDE) and publishing reproducible frameworks.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:48:45.797225,0,success,,"This proposal presents a strong systems biology approach by integrating matched metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data from an existing inflammatory bowel disease dataset. The planned use of weighted gene co-expression network analysis (WGCNA) to infer inter-organelle interactions in a non-cultivable setting is a well-designed strategy. Furthermore, the request for computational resources and staff support is justified given the complexity of harmonizing multi-omics data across 90 longitudinal subjects.","The project features a sophisticated multi-omics integration strategy with clear, testable hypotheses regarding coordinated expression. Additionally, the proposed work aligns well with the facility's available computational resources and staff expertise.",The statistical power for the network analysis may be limited if the targeted organelle transcripts exhibit low abundance or high sparsity across the 90 study subjects.
baseline::y2::11::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,5,baseline::y2::11::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T15:30:55.609697,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational biologist specializing in metagenomics and bioinformatics pipeline development, I find this proposal highly compelling. The authors plan to utilize well-established profile HMMs to mine publicly available datasets (GMrepo, PRIDE, MGnify) for bacterial organelles. The bioinformatics workflow is generally solid and relies on validated computational methods. However, the proposal glosses over the inherent difficulty of mapping metaproteomic data in complex communities like the human gut microbiome. Proteomic data in such environments is often incredibly sparse and heavily biased towards highly abundant housekeeping proteins, making the detection of specialized organelle components challenging. Additionally, while the computational demand justifies the request for NCEMS CPU hours, a more detailed breakdown of the metaproteomic search space (e.g., handling false discovery rates in large microbiome graphs) would have strengthened the proposal."",
      ""strengths"": ""Clear, well-defined computational pipeline leveraging established HMM profiles. Excellent selection of curated public databases. Strong justification for the requested NCEMS computational resources."",
      ""weakness"": ""Underestimates the technical challenges and sparsity of metaproteomic data in complex gut microbiome samples. Lacks specific details on handling false discovery rates in metaproteomic integration."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A computationally sound and exciting synthesis project that will push the boundaries of our understanding of bacterial organelles, despite some optimistic assumptions about metaproteomics."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Cellular compartmentalization is a classic example of an emergent phenomenon at the molecular level, directly aligning with the funding call."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""While bacterial organelles are known, mining them at a community scale across human gut phenotypes is a highly novel application."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The metagenomic and metatranscriptomic approaches are robust, but the metaproteomic integration lacks the necessary technical rigor for complex microbiome communities."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is realistic, though Year 1 relies heavily on successful pipeline deployment across multiple large datasets simultaneously."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Fully relies on public data and integrates distinct omics datasets to answer novel questions, perfectly fitting the synthesis definition."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Datasets (GMrepo, IBD multi-omics) are clearly identified with accession numbers, though data quality control steps are only briefly mentioned."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Commitment to creating a public database and publishing methods is clear, but specific data sharing platforms (e.g., GitHub, Zenodo) are not explicitly named.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From a microbial ecology perspective, this proposal offers a fascinating paradigm shift. Instead of treating the microbiome as a collection of metabolic pathways, it views the community as a collection of compartmentalized machines. The plan to link the presence of organelles (like ferrosomes and encapsulins) to specific ecological disruptions (e.g., IBD, C. difficile infections) is an outstanding way to explore environmental responsiveness. The hypothesis that functionally overlapping organelles might exhibit distinct patterns of environmental occurrence is highly testable and ecologically relevant. My main concern is that comparing across so many disparate disease states (diarrhea, CF, IBS, C. diff) in a short timeframe may introduce too many confounding host factors, diluting the ecological signals. A more phased approach, starting strictly with IBD where multi-omics longitudinal data is available, might have been more prudent."",
      ""strengths"": ""Brilliant ecological framing of subcellular compartments. Excellent use of the longitudinal IBD multi-omics dataset to track environmental responsiveness over time. Strong collaborative team."",
      ""weakness"": ""The inclusion of multiple disparate disease states in the initial pilot phase may introduce overwhelming confounding variables, making ecological inferences difficult."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An ecologically innovative proposal that leverages public data to answer fundamental questions about spatial organization in microbial communities. Highly recommended with slight reservations about scope."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Investigates how environmental pressures drive the emergence and coordinated expression of complex subcellular structures in microbial communities."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Linking organelle prevalence to specific human gut phenotypes and dysbiosis is entirely novel and highly significant."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Ecological hypotheses are well-formulated, though controlling for host-specific confounding factors in the GMrepo datasets will be challenging."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope might be slightly overambitious for a 2-year project, especially considering the vast metadata harmonization required for diverse disease datasets."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is a textbook example of data synthesis, bringing together previously disconnected multi-omics data to find new biological insights."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies specific, high-quality, curated datasets (e.g., PRJEB14038, PRJNA648321) that are ideal for this ecological study."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The promise to maintain the database for at least three years is good, but long-term sustainability plans are absent.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a molecular biologist specializing in bacterial microcompartments (BMCs), I applaud the assembly of this expert team. Bringing together leaders in BMCs, ferrosomes, and condensates guarantees deep domain knowledge. The HMM strategies for BMCs and ferrosomes are sound. However, I have significant reservations regarding the identification of biomolecular condensates and encapsulins. Encapsulin shell proteins are notoriously similar to bacteriophage capsid proteins, and while the authors acknowledge this high false positive rate, they do not provide a concrete bioinformatic strategy to resolve it beyond manual curation, which isn't scalable. Furthermore, the use of 'Phaseek' to identify condensates in bacteria from sequence data alone is highly speculative. Phaseek is primarily trained on eukaryotic proteins, and bacterial IDRs (intrinsically disordered regions) behave differently; this will likely yield massive false positives."",
      ""strengths"": ""Exceptional team of domain experts. Strong foundational methods for BMC and ferrosome identification. Aims to build a much-needed comprehensive database for bacterial organelles."",
      ""weakness"": ""Bioinformatic mitigation of false positives for encapsulins (viral capsids) and condensates (eukaryotic-trained machine learning models) is inadequate and lacks mechanistic rigor."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A visionary proposal led by an excellent team, but it suffers from significant technical vulnerabilities in accurately identifying condensates and encapsulins from sequence data alone."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Organelle formation is clearly an emergent property, but the condensate aspect may not be reliably measurable with the proposed methods."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Applying these concepts to metagenomic data is novel, but risks generating a database filled with viral structural proteins misannotated as encapsulins."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The lack of a robust strategy to filter out bacteriophage capsids and the speculative use of Phaseek on bacterial metagenomes significantly weakens the methodological rigor."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is adequately planned assuming the tools work as intended, but troubleshooting false positives will likely derail the schedule."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Synthesizes existing structural knowledge with metagenomic data, though experimental validation might ultimately be needed due to bioinformatics limitations."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Pfam IDs and specific datasets are provided, showing good preparation, though some HMM thresholds will need major adjustments."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Good commitment to open science, but sharing the raw, uncurated outputs of the condensate predictions might muddy the public data waters.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Evaluating this proposal from a systems biology and multi-omics integration standpoint, this is a stellar application. The strategy to use matched metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data from the IBD dataset (MGYS00006120) is precisely the kind of complex synthesis NCEMS is designed to support. The application of WGCNA to build co-expression networks for organelles is an innovative way to infer inter-organelle interactions and regulatory modules in a non-cultivable setting. The rationale for requiring NCEMS staff scientists and computational resources is perfectly justified, as harmonizing multi-omics data across 90 longitudinal subjects is a massive computational undertaking. One minor weakness is that WGCNA requires a relatively large sample size to build robust networks; depending on the sparsity of the specific organelle transcripts in the 90 subjects, the statistical power might be marginal."",
      ""strengths"": ""Sophisticated use of multi-omics integration and network analysis (WGCNA). Perfect alignment with NCEMS resource offerings. Clear, testable hypotheses regarding coordinated expression."",
      ""weakness"": ""Statistical power for WGCNA might be limited if the specific organelle transcripts are low-abundance or sparsely distributed across the 90 IBD subjects."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 5,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An outstanding, highly integrative systems biology proposal that leverages NCEMS capabilities to extract deep regulatory insights from existing multi-omics datasets."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Coordinated multi-gene expression resulting in physical cellular compartments is a fundamental emergent bioscience phenomenon."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Moving beyond single-organism studies to analyze organelle co-expression networks in a complex human microbiome is groundbreaking."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The statistical approaches are advanced and appropriate, though potential sparsity of transcriptomic data requires careful normalization."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 5,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The step-by-step timeline, dedicating specific months to data cleaning, harmonization, and parallel analyses, is highly realistic and well-structured."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""A true synthesis project that goes beyond the capabilities of a single lab, requiring complex data integration and team science."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The selection of the longitudinal IBD multi-omics dataset is the perfect testbed for the proposed network analyses."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to releasing workflows via established repositories (MGnify, PRIDE) and publishing reproducible frameworks.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""This proposal perfectly captures the spirit of the NCEMS synthesis call. By strictly utilizing publicly available data to solve long-standing puzzles about bacterial compartmentalization, the team demonstrates a deep understanding of data re-use. The creation of a searchable, integrated database of bacterial organelles will be a massive boon to the microbiology community, fulfilling the mandate to develop innovative research strategies and train future scientists. However, the proposal's mention of training and outreach is quite vague. It states that activities will 'engage early-career researchers', but there is no specific plan on how trainees will be integrated into the workflow, mentored, or recruited. Additionally, while open science principles are mentioned, a concrete Data Management Plan outlining metadata standards (e.g., FAIR principles) would have elevated the proposal."",
      ""strengths"": ""100% reliance on public data; addresses fundamental questions through team science; clear deliverables including a public database and predictive frameworks."",
      ""weakness"": ""The training plan for graduate students and postdocs is generic and lacks specific actionable items. Missing explicit mention of FAIR data principles."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A highly relevant and well-designed data synthesis project that will produce valuable community resources, though it could improve its explicit training and data management details."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Directly tackles emergence by studying how complex subcellular structures assemble and function within a broader ecological community."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The concept of a unified database for bacterial organelles is highly significant and fills a major gap in the community."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposed working group appropriately matches the domain expertise required to rigorously evaluate the synthesized data."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 5,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project scope matches the requested NCEMS resources perfectly, with clear deliverables outlined in the final year."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Embodies the synthesis mandate: integrating massive distinct datasets to generate new insights without generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Public datasets are clearly identified, accessible, and highly appropriate for the proposed synthesis."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""While a database is promised, the lack of adherence to specific FAIR principles and a vague training plan brings this score down slightly.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::11,y2,11,Discovery and functional analysis of bacterial organelles through mining and refinement of omics data,Rejected,Y Hoang; Arash Komeili; Hualiang Pi; Markus Sutter,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"This proposal perfectly captures the spirit of the NCEMS synthesis call. By strictly utilizing publicly available data to solve long-standing puzzles about bacterial compartmentalization, the team demonstrates a deep understanding of data re-use. The creation of a searchable, integrated database of bacterial organelles will be a massive boon to the microbiology community, fulfilling the mandate to develop innovative research strategies and train future scientists. However, the proposal's mention of training and outreach is quite vague. It states that activities will 'engage early-career researchers', but there is no specific plan on how trainees will be integrated into the workflow, mentored, or recruited. Additionally, while open science principles are mentioned, a concrete Data Management Plan outlining metadata standards (e.g., FAIR principles) would have elevated the proposal.",100% reliance on public data; addresses fundamental questions through team science; clear deliverables including a public database and predictive frameworks.,The training plan for graduate students and postdocs is generic and lacks specific actionable items. Missing explicit mention of FAIR data principles.,4,"A highly relevant and well-designed data synthesis project that will produce valuable community resources, though it could improve its explicit training and data management details.",5,Directly tackles emergence by studying how complex subcellular structures assemble and function within a broader ecological community.,4,The concept of a unified database for bacterial organelles is highly significant and fills a major gap in the community.,4,The proposed working group appropriately matches the domain expertise required to rigorously evaluate the synthesized data.,5,"The project scope matches the requested NCEMS resources perfectly, with clear deliverables outlined in the final year.",5,Embodies the synthesis mandate: integrating massive distinct datasets to generate new insights without generating new experimental data.,5,"Public datasets are clearly identified, accessible, and highly appropriate for the proposed synthesis.",3,"While a database is promised, the lack of adherence to specific FAIR principles and a vague training plan brings this score down slightly.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:48:54.785458,0,success,,"This proposal effectively addresses the synthesis call by utilizing publicly available data to investigate bacterial compartmentalization. The planned development of a searchable database of bacterial organelles represents a valuable resource for the microbiology community. However, the proposal lacks a specific training plan for early-career researchers and would benefit from a detailed data management plan that includes FAIR principles.","The project relies entirely on public data to address fundamental biological questions through a collaborative science approach. Additionally, it provides clear deliverables, including a comprehensive public database and predictive frameworks.","The proposed training plan for graduate students and postdoctoral researchers lacks specific, actionable details regarding integration and mentorship. Furthermore, the proposal does not explicitly incorporate FAIR data principles into a concrete data management plan."
baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,1,baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:29:22.412842,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior bacterial cell biologist specializing in bacterial microcompartments and protein-bounded organelles. This proposal addresses an important and timely area: bacterial subcellular organization in natural microbial communities. The focus on bacterial microcompartments, encapsulins, ferrosomes, and condensates is well aligned with molecular and cellular biosciences, and the working group includes strong domain experts for several organelle classes. I am enthusiastic about the potential value of a curated, searchable resource that links organelle markers to metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic evidence. The proposal is strongest for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, where genetic markers and prior comparative frameworks exist. However, the scientific questions are somewhat diffuse, and the plan risks becoming an annotation exercise rather than a sharply hypothesis-driven synthesis project. The proposal would benefit from clearer criteria for distinguishing complete organelle systems from isolated homologs, explicit validation strategies for operon context in fragmented metagenomic assemblies, and a stronger statistical plan for testing co-occurrence and expression hypotheses. The condensate component is substantially less mature than the other organelle classes and may introduce many false positives. Overall, this is a strong but imperfect fit to the call: it uses public data, requires cross-disciplinary expertise, and could produce a valuable community resource, but the rigor of some analyses and the specificity of emergent-phenomena hypotheses need strengthening."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a compelling and underexplored question in bacterial cell biology: how organelles are distributed and regulated in complex microbial communities. The team has strong expertise in BMCs, ferrosomes, and condensates. The use of public metagenomic and multi-omics datasets is appropriate for the call. The plan to generate HMM-based detection pipelines and a searchable database could provide a lasting resource for the field. The focus on organelle co-occurrence and expression across ecological perturbations is potentially novel and significant."",""weakness"":""The hypotheses are broad and sometimes framed as exploratory cataloging rather than specific tests of biological mechanisms. The proposal does not sufficiently address false positives, incomplete assemblies, strain-level ambiguity, or the difference between detecting a marker gene and inferring a functional organelle. The condensate detection strategy relies heavily on Phaseek predictions without a clear bacterial-specific validation plan. Statistical approaches for co-occurrence, environmental responsiveness, and coordinated expression are underdeveloped. The open science and training plans are present but generic."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong proposal with high potential to create a valuable synthesis resource for bacterial organelle biology, especially for genetically marked organelles. Its impact would be improved by sharper hypotheses, clearer validation and statistical frameworks, and a more cautious treatment of condensates."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Bacterial organelles and community-level regulation of compartmentalization are relevant to emergence in molecular and cellular systems. The proposal connects molecular components to higher-order cellular structures and ecological context, although the emergent properties are not always explicitly formulated as such."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Mapping bacterial organelles across the human gut microbiome using integrated multi-omics is novel and potentially significant. Prior work has cataloged some organelles across genomes, but expression and ecological regulation in complex communities remain underexplored."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The HMM-based approach is reasonable for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, but key details are missing regarding thresholds, operon reconstruction, assembly quality, taxonomic assignment, false discovery control, and statistical hypothesis testing. Condensate prediction is particularly uncertain."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering four organelle categories, multiple disease-associated datasets, multi-omics integration, database construction, and multiple manuscripts. The timeline is plausible only if the analyses are tightly prioritized."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data and does not propose new experimental data generation. It clearly requires integration across datasets and expertise areas."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies specific public resources, including GMrepo datasets and the IBD Multi-omics Database, and names relevant accession numbers. However, it could better document sample sizes, metadata completeness, pairing across omics layers, and suitability for the proposed comparisons."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to a public database and publication of the predictive framework, but the plan lacks detail on code repositories, workflow reproducibility, licensing, metadata standards, community governance, and long-term maintenance beyond a brief three-year statement.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior microbiome systems biologist with expertise in human gut multi-omics and host-associated microbial ecology. This proposal has a compelling biological premise: bacterial organelles may be widespread and dynamically regulated in the gut microbiome, and public multi-omics data now make it possible to study these structures at community scale. The proposed use of GMrepo and the IBD longitudinal multi-omics dataset is appropriate and responsive to the NCEMS mission. I appreciate the effort to move beyond presence/absence toward expression, regulation, and potential coordination among organelle systems. However, as a microbiome proposal, it underestimates major challenges in comparing human gut datasets across studies, phenotypes, sequencing protocols, host covariates, medication exposures, diet, geography, and sample processing. The proposal lists disease states such as diarrhea, C. difficile infection, cystic fibrosis, IBS, and IBD as environmental perturbations, but it does not provide a coherent design for controlling confounders or distinguishing organelle regulation from shifts in community composition. The strongest part is the longitudinal IBD multi-omics analysis, but even there, the plan does not clearly state whether organelle expression will be normalized to taxon abundance, gene copy number, or total community transcription/protein abundance. Without these controls, claims about regulation could be confounded by changes in species abundance. I consider this a promising but methodologically underdeveloped proposal."",""strengths"":""The proposal uses relevant public microbiome resources and addresses an interesting gap: the ecological and regulatory context of bacterial organelles in human gut communities. The longitudinal IBD dataset is a strong choice for paired multi-omics analysis. The team’s organelle biology expertise is excellent, and NCEMS support could add important expertise in multi-omics harmonization and metadata standardization. The project could generate useful hypotheses about organelle distribution across health and disease states."",""weakness"":""The microbiome study design is not sufficiently rigorous. The proposal does not define sample sizes, metadata covariates, statistical models, batch correction methods, or controls for compositionality. Disease categories are treated as environmental perturbations without detailed attention to medication, diet, inflammation, antibiotics, age, geography, sequencing methods, or study-specific biases. It is unclear how the team will distinguish differential expression of organelle genes from changes in the abundance of organisms carrying those genes. The metaproteomic component may be technically difficult and less complete than implied."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is well aligned with public-data synthesis and has a compelling biological question, but the microbiome comparative framework requires substantial strengthening before robust conclusions about organelle regulation or disease-associated dynamics can be drawn."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses how subcellular structures emerge and function within organisms embedded in complex communities. The concept of organelle-level regulation across community perturbations fits the call, though the proposal could more explicitly connect community-level patterns to emergent cellular mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Studying bacterial organelles through human gut multi-omics is novel and could be significant. The ability to connect organelle repertoires to microbial ecology and disease-associated states would advance both microbiome science and bacterial cell biology."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal lacks sufficient detail on microbiome statistical analysis, compositional data handling, longitudinal modeling, batch effects, and normalization of expression relative to organism abundance. These omissions threaten interpretability of the key conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but possible for a descriptive analysis. However, robust cross-study harmonization, longitudinal modeling, metaproteomic analysis, and database deployment within the proposed period may be difficult, especially with multiple organelle classes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is strongly synthesis-focused and relies entirely on public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data. It is a good example of a question that benefits from integrating existing datasets across studies."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies several specific public datasets and databases. It would be stronger if it provided a table of sample counts, omics modalities, metadata fields, and data quality metrics for each dataset."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to a public database is positive, but the proposal gives limited detail on reproducible workflows, containerization, provenance tracking, FAIR metadata, or community standards for updates and reuse.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior computational metagenomics and bioinformatics researcher specializing in genome-resolved microbiome analysis. The proposal is responsive to the call in its exclusive reliance on public data and its need for computational synthesis across large-scale omics datasets. The idea of building a reusable pipeline to detect bacterial organelle systems in metagenomic and multi-omics data is worthwhile. Nonetheless, I have concerns about computational rigor and feasibility. The proposed workflow says metagenomic data will be translated into protein sequences and scanned using organelle-specific HMMs, but it is not clear whether this will be done on reads, contigs, metagenome-assembled genomes, gene catalogs, or isolate genomes. This distinction is critical for operon-based detection, co-occurrence within organisms, and claims about taxonomic distribution. The requested resources mention 100 CPU hours per genome assembly and raw data per isolate, which is incongruent with a metagenomic synthesis project and suggests that the computational plan has not been fully specified. Inferring that two organelles coexist within the same organism requires genome-resolved binning or robust linkage; detecting both markers in a sample is not sufficient. Similarly, gene neighborhood analyses are difficult in fragmented assemblies. The proposal would be much stronger with explicit benchmarking against known genomes, positive and negative controls, reproducible workflow design, confidence tiers for organelle calls, and clear plans for MAG quality assessment and dereplication. I see high potential, but at present the computational strategy is too high-level."",""strengths"":""The proposal identifies a tractable set of organelle marker systems and uses widely accepted tools such as HMMER, Pfam, InterProScan, IUPred2A, MGnify workflows, and WGCNA. It proposes to synthesize public multi-omics datasets and produce reusable computational resources. NCEMS staff support for harmonization, quality control, and statistical analysis is well justified. The proposed database could be valuable if organelle calls are carefully benchmarked and annotated with confidence scores."",""weakness"":""The computational workflow is underspecified. It does not clearly define whether analyses will occur at the read, contig, MAG, species, or sample level. It lacks benchmarking, false-positive controls, assembly/binning strategies, quality thresholds, taxonomic classification methods, confidence scoring, and statistical models. Co-occurrence within organisms cannot be inferred from sample-level marker detection. The resource request includes confusing language about isolates and genome assembly that does not match the stated public metagenomic data synthesis focus."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A potentially impactful synthesis project whose success depends on computational implementation. The current proposal is promising but needs a much clearer genome-resolved and statistically rigorous analysis plan to support its biological claims."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":3,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The project concerns emergence of cellular compartmentalization and possible coordinated organelle expression. However, much of the proposed work is marker detection and cataloging, and the computational plan does not yet convincingly connect the data to emergent mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A public, genome-resolved catalog of bacterial organelle systems in the gut microbiome would be novel and significant. The multi-omics expression component could add an important layer beyond existing genome surveys."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks key computational details required for robust metagenomic inference, including assembly strategy, MAG recovery, gene linkage, benchmarking, thresholds, error rates, and statistical testing. These are central to the proposal’s conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The computational scope is large but potentially feasible with NCEMS support if narrowed and better specified. Building pipelines, processing large multi-omics datasets, and deploying databases within two years will be challenging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly based on synthesis of public data and integration of multiple omics types. No new experimental data are proposed."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal lists relevant datasets and accessions, but it does not adequately specify data types, sample numbers, read depths, assembly availability, MAG availability, or whether datasets are suitable for organism-level co-occurrence and expression analyses."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database commitment is useful, but a computational synthesis project should provide more detail on versioned code, workflow languages, containers, continuous integration, data provenance, documentation, and standards for reproducibility.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior molecular biophysicist studying biomolecular condensates and phase separation in bacteria. I am pleased to see bacterial condensates included alongside more classical genetically encoded organelles, since condensates are an important frontier in bacterial cell organization. However, from the condensate perspective, this proposal is not yet rigorous. Unlike BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, condensates generally lack simple operonic signatures, and the proposal acknowledges this. The proposed solution is to apply Phaseek to predicted proteins, followed by co-expression networks and disorder/domain analyses. This may be useful for candidate discovery but is insufficient to identify condensates or infer their regulation in the gut microbiome. Condensate propensity predictors are trained under particular assumptions, often using eukaryotic or curated protein sets, and are prone to bias from protein length, low-complexity sequence, charge patterning, nucleic-acid-binding domains, and taxonomic composition. In metagenomic data, fragmented genes and annotation errors will exacerbate this problem. The proposal also does not define which bacterial condensate systems are known, how predictions will be benchmarked against them, or how false positives will be controlled. I would support the project more strongly if condensates were treated as a focused pilot with strict confidence tiers rather than as an equivalent organelle class. The broader proposal remains compelling, especially for genetically marked organelles, but the condensate component currently overpromises."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a conceptually important area: the diversity of bacterial compartmentalization mechanisms. Including condensates broadens the project beyond operon-defined organelles and could stimulate productive interaction between bacterial cell biology, biophysics, and computational biology. The planned integration of disorder prediction, domain annotation, gene neighborhood analysis, and expression data could generate useful candidate lists. The team includes investigators with relevant condensate expertise."",""weakness"":""The condensate identification strategy is weak relative to the claims. Phaseek predictions alone cannot establish condensate formation, and the proposal lacks bacterial-specific benchmarking, negative controls, orthogonal sequence features, and clear criteria for high-confidence calls. Co-expression networks do not demonstrate physical condensate interaction or phase separation. The proposal risks conflating intrinsically disordered or low-complexity proteins with bona fide condensate-forming systems. The integration of condensates with other organelle classes is conceptually interesting but underdeveloped."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The overall project is worthwhile, but the condensate component needs substantial refinement. If the proposal limited condensate analyses to candidate generation while emphasizing stronger marker-based organelle classes, it would be more convincing."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Biomolecular condensates and bacterial organelles are direct examples of emergent cellular organization arising from molecular interactions. The proposal’s focus on coordinated expression and compartmentalization is highly relevant to emergence phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A cross-organelle comparison of bacterial compartmentalization in gut communities is novel. The condensate aspect could be significant if handled carefully, but the current plan may not yield reliable biological conclusions."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""For condensates, the rigor is limited by reliance on a machine-learning predictor without adequate validation, benchmarking, or false-positive control. The approaches for genetically marked organelles are more solid, but the proposal presents all classes together without sufficiently acknowledging differences in evidentiary strength."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is ambitious, especially because condensates require distinct analysis logic from operon-defined compartments. The timeline may be feasible if condensates are scoped as exploratory candidate discovery rather than definitive cataloging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is entirely based on mining and integrating public omics datasets, making it a strong fit for synthesis research."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies useful gut microbiome datasets and public annotation tools. For condensates specifically, it does not identify appropriate training, benchmark, or validation datasets of known bacterial condensate proteins."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database and publication plans are positive, but the proposal should provide more explicit plans for releasing prediction scores, confidence categories, training assumptions, code, and reproducible notebooks so users can evaluate condensate calls critically.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior open-science data infrastructure and molecular systems biology researcher. This proposal is a good thematic fit for a synthesis center: it brings together multiple labs, uses only public data, and aims to create an integrated resource that would be difficult for a single laboratory to produce. The proposed database of bacterial organelles, if well implemented, could be a valuable community asset. I also appreciate the request for NCEMS support in data harmonization, metadata standardization, and multi-omics integration; these are precisely the areas where a synthesis center can add value. However, the proposal’s open science and community-resource plans are underdeveloped relative to the ambition of the project. It states that the database will be publicly accessible and maintained for at least three years, but it does not specify governance, repository choices, data models, APIs, update cycles, documentation, licensing, persistent identifiers, provenance, or mechanisms for community contribution. The training plan is also brief and generic, mentioning open science and bioinformatics training but not defining trainee roles, learning objectives, products, or mentoring structures. As a working group proposal, the team has strong scientific expertise, but the plan would benefit from clearer evidence of diversity across institutions, career stages, computational roles, and community engagement. The scientific topic is compelling, but the project’s lasting impact will depend heavily on whether the outputs are reusable, reproducible, and trusted by the community."",""strengths"":""The proposal aligns well with the NCEMS model by synthesizing public multi-omics data and requiring collaboration among organelle experts, microbiome analysts, and NCEMS staff. It identifies specific public datasets and proposes a reusable searchable database. The project has the potential to train early-career researchers in bioinformatics and multi-omics synthesis. The intended outputs, including databases, pipelines, and manuscripts, are appropriate for an open synthesis project."",""weakness"":""The open science implementation is too vague. The proposal does not specify code repositories, workflow systems, containerization, data standards, database schema, licensing, APIs, documentation, versioning, provenance, or long-term sustainability beyond three years. Training and outreach are mentioned but not described in detail. The role of NCEMS staff is important but somewhat broad, and the division of labor between NCEMS and the investigators is not fully defined. The database maintenance plan may be insufficient for a community-scale resource."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""This is a strong synthesis-oriented proposal with a potentially valuable community resource, but its open science, reproducibility, training, and data-infrastructure plans need more specificity to fully meet the expectations of a community-scale NCEMS working group."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses emergence of bacterial subcellular organization and potential coordinated regulation of organelles in microbial communities. It is clearly relevant, although the proposal could frame the emergent systems questions more explicitly."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of bacterial organelle biology with public gut microbiome multi-omics is novel and likely to produce useful resources and hypotheses. The significance depends on the quality and usability of the resulting database and workflows."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The analytical approach uses reasonable tools, but the proposal lacks enough detail on reproducible workflow implementation, validation, statistical modeling, and quality control. These issues are especially important for a public resource."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":4,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but generally plausible with NCEMS support, especially if the team prioritizes a pilot dataset, pipeline refinement, IBD multi-omics integration, and database release. The number of promised manuscripts and database features may need prioritization."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is an excellent fit for synthesis-focused work: it uses only existing public data, integrates multiple data types, and requires expertise beyond a single lab."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies GMrepo, several accessions, MGnify resources, and the IBD Multi-omics Database. It would be improved by a structured data inventory with sample counts, metadata fields, omics pairing, quality measures, and reuse constraints."",""open_science_commitment_score"":2,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to public outputs but does not provide a sufficiently detailed open science plan. A community-scale synthesis proposal should specify FAIR data practices, licensing, versioned repositories, executable workflows, containerized environments, public documentation, issue tracking, and long-term governance.""}]}",0,y2::11,y2,11,Discovery and functional analysis of bacterial organelles through mining and refinement of omics data,Rejected,Y Hoang; Arash Komeili; Hualiang Pi; Markus Sutter,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 1: Senior bacterial cell biologist specializing in bacterial microcompartments and protein-bounded organelles. This proposal addresses an important and timely area: bacterial subcellular organization in natural microbial communities. The focus on bacterial microcompartments, encapsulins, ferrosomes, and condensates is well aligned with molecular and cellular biosciences, and the working group includes strong domain experts for several organelle classes. I am enthusiastic about the potential value of a curated, searchable resource that links organelle markers to metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic evidence. The proposal is strongest for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, where genetic markers and prior comparative frameworks exist. However, the scientific questions are somewhat diffuse, and the plan risks becoming an annotation exercise rather than a sharply hypothesis-driven synthesis project. The proposal would benefit from clearer criteria for distinguishing complete organelle systems from isolated homologs, explicit validation strategies for operon context in fragmented metagenomic assemblies, and a stronger statistical plan for testing co-occurrence and expression hypotheses. The condensate component is substantially less mature than the other organelle classes and may introduce many false positives. Overall, this is a strong but imperfect fit to the call: it uses public data, requires cross-disciplinary expertise, and could produce a valuable community resource, but the rigor of some analyses and the specificity of emergent-phenomena hypotheses need strengthening.","The proposal addresses a compelling and underexplored question in bacterial cell biology: how organelles are distributed and regulated in complex microbial communities. The team has strong expertise in BMCs, ferrosomes, and condensates. The use of public metagenomic and multi-omics datasets is appropriate for the call. The plan to generate HMM-based detection pipelines and a searchable database could provide a lasting resource for the field. The focus on organelle co-occurrence and expression across ecological perturbations is potentially novel and significant.","The hypotheses are broad and sometimes framed as exploratory cataloging rather than specific tests of biological mechanisms. The proposal does not sufficiently address false positives, incomplete assemblies, strain-level ambiguity, or the difference between detecting a marker gene and inferring a functional organelle. The condensate detection strategy relies heavily on Phaseek predictions without a clear bacterial-specific validation plan. Statistical approaches for co-occurrence, environmental responsiveness, and coordinated expression are underdeveloped. The open science and training plans are present but generic.",4,"A strong proposal with high potential to create a valuable synthesis resource for bacterial organelle biology, especially for genetically marked organelles. Its impact would be improved by sharper hypotheses, clearer validation and statistical frameworks, and a more cautious treatment of condensates.",4,"Bacterial organelles and community-level regulation of compartmentalization are relevant to emergence in molecular and cellular systems. The proposal connects molecular components to higher-order cellular structures and ecological context, although the emergent properties are not always explicitly formulated as such.",4,"Mapping bacterial organelles across the human gut microbiome using integrated multi-omics is novel and potentially significant. Prior work has cataloged some organelles across genomes, but expression and ecological regulation in complex communities remain underexplored.",3,"The HMM-based approach is reasonable for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, but key details are missing regarding thresholds, operon reconstruction, assembly quality, taxonomic assignment, false discovery control, and statistical hypothesis testing. Condensate prediction is particularly uncertain.",3,"The scope is ambitious for two years, covering four organelle categories, multiple disease-associated datasets, multi-omics integration, database construction, and multiple manuscripts. The timeline is plausible only if the analyses are tightly prioritized.",5,"The project is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data and does not propose new experimental data generation. It clearly requires integration across datasets and expertise areas.",4,"The proposal identifies specific public resources, including GMrepo datasets and the IBD Multi-omics Database, and names relevant accession numbers. However, it could better document sample sizes, metadata completeness, pairing across omics layers, and suitability for the proposed comparisons.",3,"The proposal commits to a public database and publication of the predictive framework, but the plan lacks detail on code repositories, workflow reproducibility, licensing, metadata standards, community governance, and long-term maintenance beyond a brief three-year statement.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:49:03.205510,0,success,,"The proposal outlines a synthesis project to investigate bacterial subcellular organization within natural microbial communities using public multi-omics data. While it promises a valuable community resource for tracking bacterial microcompartments, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, the project risks becoming an exploratory annotation exercise rather than a hypothesis-driven study. Overall, the proposal represents a strong cross-disciplinary effort but requires refined hypotheses and enhanced analytical rigor to fully succeed.","The project addresses a compelling, underexplored area of bacterial cell biology using appropriate multi-omics datasets and a highly qualified team of domain experts. Furthermore, the planned development of HMM-based detection pipelines and a searchable database offers substantial potential to produce a lasting, novel community resource.","The overarching hypotheses remain overly broad, and the statistical plans for analyzing co-occurrence and environmental responsiveness are currently underdeveloped. Additionally, the proposal lacks robust strategies to mitigate false positives in condensate detection, fragmented assemblies, and strain-level ambiguities, making it difficult to distinguish true functional organelles from isolated marker genes."
baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::002,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,2,baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:29:22.412842,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior bacterial cell biologist specializing in bacterial microcompartments and protein-bounded organelles. This proposal addresses an important and timely area: bacterial subcellular organization in natural microbial communities. The focus on bacterial microcompartments, encapsulins, ferrosomes, and condensates is well aligned with molecular and cellular biosciences, and the working group includes strong domain experts for several organelle classes. I am enthusiastic about the potential value of a curated, searchable resource that links organelle markers to metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic evidence. The proposal is strongest for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, where genetic markers and prior comparative frameworks exist. However, the scientific questions are somewhat diffuse, and the plan risks becoming an annotation exercise rather than a sharply hypothesis-driven synthesis project. The proposal would benefit from clearer criteria for distinguishing complete organelle systems from isolated homologs, explicit validation strategies for operon context in fragmented metagenomic assemblies, and a stronger statistical plan for testing co-occurrence and expression hypotheses. The condensate component is substantially less mature than the other organelle classes and may introduce many false positives. Overall, this is a strong but imperfect fit to the call: it uses public data, requires cross-disciplinary expertise, and could produce a valuable community resource, but the rigor of some analyses and the specificity of emergent-phenomena hypotheses need strengthening."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a compelling and underexplored question in bacterial cell biology: how organelles are distributed and regulated in complex microbial communities. The team has strong expertise in BMCs, ferrosomes, and condensates. The use of public metagenomic and multi-omics datasets is appropriate for the call. The plan to generate HMM-based detection pipelines and a searchable database could provide a lasting resource for the field. The focus on organelle co-occurrence and expression across ecological perturbations is potentially novel and significant."",""weakness"":""The hypotheses are broad and sometimes framed as exploratory cataloging rather than specific tests of biological mechanisms. The proposal does not sufficiently address false positives, incomplete assemblies, strain-level ambiguity, or the difference between detecting a marker gene and inferring a functional organelle. The condensate detection strategy relies heavily on Phaseek predictions without a clear bacterial-specific validation plan. Statistical approaches for co-occurrence, environmental responsiveness, and coordinated expression are underdeveloped. The open science and training plans are present but generic."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong proposal with high potential to create a valuable synthesis resource for bacterial organelle biology, especially for genetically marked organelles. Its impact would be improved by sharper hypotheses, clearer validation and statistical frameworks, and a more cautious treatment of condensates."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Bacterial organelles and community-level regulation of compartmentalization are relevant to emergence in molecular and cellular systems. The proposal connects molecular components to higher-order cellular structures and ecological context, although the emergent properties are not always explicitly formulated as such."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Mapping bacterial organelles across the human gut microbiome using integrated multi-omics is novel and potentially significant. Prior work has cataloged some organelles across genomes, but expression and ecological regulation in complex communities remain underexplored."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The HMM-based approach is reasonable for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, but key details are missing regarding thresholds, operon reconstruction, assembly quality, taxonomic assignment, false discovery control, and statistical hypothesis testing. Condensate prediction is particularly uncertain."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering four organelle categories, multiple disease-associated datasets, multi-omics integration, database construction, and multiple manuscripts. The timeline is plausible only if the analyses are tightly prioritized."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data and does not propose new experimental data generation. It clearly requires integration across datasets and expertise areas."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies specific public resources, including GMrepo datasets and the IBD Multi-omics Database, and names relevant accession numbers. However, it could better document sample sizes, metadata completeness, pairing across omics layers, and suitability for the proposed comparisons."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to a public database and publication of the predictive framework, but the plan lacks detail on code repositories, workflow reproducibility, licensing, metadata standards, community governance, and long-term maintenance beyond a brief three-year statement.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior microbiome systems biologist with expertise in human gut multi-omics and host-associated microbial ecology. This proposal has a compelling biological premise: bacterial organelles may be widespread and dynamically regulated in the gut microbiome, and public multi-omics data now make it possible to study these structures at community scale. The proposed use of GMrepo and the IBD longitudinal multi-omics dataset is appropriate and responsive to the NCEMS mission. I appreciate the effort to move beyond presence/absence toward expression, regulation, and potential coordination among organelle systems. However, as a microbiome proposal, it underestimates major challenges in comparing human gut datasets across studies, phenotypes, sequencing protocols, host covariates, medication exposures, diet, geography, and sample processing. The proposal lists disease states such as diarrhea, C. difficile infection, cystic fibrosis, IBS, and IBD as environmental perturbations, but it does not provide a coherent design for controlling confounders or distinguishing organelle regulation from shifts in community composition. The strongest part is the longitudinal IBD multi-omics analysis, but even there, the plan does not clearly state whether organelle expression will be normalized to taxon abundance, gene copy number, or total community transcription/protein abundance. Without these controls, claims about regulation could be confounded by changes in species abundance. I consider this a promising but methodologically underdeveloped proposal."",""strengths"":""The proposal uses relevant public microbiome resources and addresses an interesting gap: the ecological and regulatory context of bacterial organelles in human gut communities. The longitudinal IBD dataset is a strong choice for paired multi-omics analysis. The team’s organelle biology expertise is excellent, and NCEMS support could add important expertise in multi-omics harmonization and metadata standardization. The project could generate useful hypotheses about organelle distribution across health and disease states."",""weakness"":""The microbiome study design is not sufficiently rigorous. The proposal does not define sample sizes, metadata covariates, statistical models, batch correction methods, or controls for compositionality. Disease categories are treated as environmental perturbations without detailed attention to medication, diet, inflammation, antibiotics, age, geography, sequencing methods, or study-specific biases. It is unclear how the team will distinguish differential expression of organelle genes from changes in the abundance of organisms carrying those genes. The metaproteomic component may be technically difficult and less complete than implied."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is well aligned with public-data synthesis and has a compelling biological question, but the microbiome comparative framework requires substantial strengthening before robust conclusions about organelle regulation or disease-associated dynamics can be drawn."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses how subcellular structures emerge and function within organisms embedded in complex communities. The concept of organelle-level regulation across community perturbations fits the call, though the proposal could more explicitly connect community-level patterns to emergent cellular mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Studying bacterial organelles through human gut multi-omics is novel and could be significant. The ability to connect organelle repertoires to microbial ecology and disease-associated states would advance both microbiome science and bacterial cell biology."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal lacks sufficient detail on microbiome statistical analysis, compositional data handling, longitudinal modeling, batch effects, and normalization of expression relative to organism abundance. These omissions threaten interpretability of the key conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but possible for a descriptive analysis. However, robust cross-study harmonization, longitudinal modeling, metaproteomic analysis, and database deployment within the proposed period may be difficult, especially with multiple organelle classes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is strongly synthesis-focused and relies entirely on public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data. It is a good example of a question that benefits from integrating existing datasets across studies."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies several specific public datasets and databases. It would be stronger if it provided a table of sample counts, omics modalities, metadata fields, and data quality metrics for each dataset."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to a public database is positive, but the proposal gives limited detail on reproducible workflows, containerization, provenance tracking, FAIR metadata, or community standards for updates and reuse.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior computational metagenomics and bioinformatics researcher specializing in genome-resolved microbiome analysis. The proposal is responsive to the call in its exclusive reliance on public data and its need for computational synthesis across large-scale omics datasets. The idea of building a reusable pipeline to detect bacterial organelle systems in metagenomic and multi-omics data is worthwhile. Nonetheless, I have concerns about computational rigor and feasibility. The proposed workflow says metagenomic data will be translated into protein sequences and scanned using organelle-specific HMMs, but it is not clear whether this will be done on reads, contigs, metagenome-assembled genomes, gene catalogs, or isolate genomes. This distinction is critical for operon-based detection, co-occurrence within organisms, and claims about taxonomic distribution. The requested resources mention 100 CPU hours per genome assembly and raw data per isolate, which is incongruent with a metagenomic synthesis project and suggests that the computational plan has not been fully specified. Inferring that two organelles coexist within the same organism requires genome-resolved binning or robust linkage; detecting both markers in a sample is not sufficient. Similarly, gene neighborhood analyses are difficult in fragmented assemblies. The proposal would be much stronger with explicit benchmarking against known genomes, positive and negative controls, reproducible workflow design, confidence tiers for organelle calls, and clear plans for MAG quality assessment and dereplication. I see high potential, but at present the computational strategy is too high-level."",""strengths"":""The proposal identifies a tractable set of organelle marker systems and uses widely accepted tools such as HMMER, Pfam, InterProScan, IUPred2A, MGnify workflows, and WGCNA. It proposes to synthesize public multi-omics datasets and produce reusable computational resources. NCEMS staff support for harmonization, quality control, and statistical analysis is well justified. The proposed database could be valuable if organelle calls are carefully benchmarked and annotated with confidence scores."",""weakness"":""The computational workflow is underspecified. It does not clearly define whether analyses will occur at the read, contig, MAG, species, or sample level. It lacks benchmarking, false-positive controls, assembly/binning strategies, quality thresholds, taxonomic classification methods, confidence scoring, and statistical models. Co-occurrence within organisms cannot be inferred from sample-level marker detection. The resource request includes confusing language about isolates and genome assembly that does not match the stated public metagenomic data synthesis focus."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A potentially impactful synthesis project whose success depends on computational implementation. The current proposal is promising but needs a much clearer genome-resolved and statistically rigorous analysis plan to support its biological claims."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":3,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The project concerns emergence of cellular compartmentalization and possible coordinated organelle expression. However, much of the proposed work is marker detection and cataloging, and the computational plan does not yet convincingly connect the data to emergent mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A public, genome-resolved catalog of bacterial organelle systems in the gut microbiome would be novel and significant. The multi-omics expression component could add an important layer beyond existing genome surveys."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks key computational details required for robust metagenomic inference, including assembly strategy, MAG recovery, gene linkage, benchmarking, thresholds, error rates, and statistical testing. These are central to the proposal’s conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The computational scope is large but potentially feasible with NCEMS support if narrowed and better specified. Building pipelines, processing large multi-omics datasets, and deploying databases within two years will be challenging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly based on synthesis of public data and integration of multiple omics types. No new experimental data are proposed."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal lists relevant datasets and accessions, but it does not adequately specify data types, sample numbers, read depths, assembly availability, MAG availability, or whether datasets are suitable for organism-level co-occurrence and expression analyses."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database commitment is useful, but a computational synthesis project should provide more detail on versioned code, workflow languages, containers, continuous integration, data provenance, documentation, and standards for reproducibility.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior molecular biophysicist studying biomolecular condensates and phase separation in bacteria. I am pleased to see bacterial condensates included alongside more classical genetically encoded organelles, since condensates are an important frontier in bacterial cell organization. However, from the condensate perspective, this proposal is not yet rigorous. Unlike BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, condensates generally lack simple operonic signatures, and the proposal acknowledges this. The proposed solution is to apply Phaseek to predicted proteins, followed by co-expression networks and disorder/domain analyses. This may be useful for candidate discovery but is insufficient to identify condensates or infer their regulation in the gut microbiome. Condensate propensity predictors are trained under particular assumptions, often using eukaryotic or curated protein sets, and are prone to bias from protein length, low-complexity sequence, charge patterning, nucleic-acid-binding domains, and taxonomic composition. In metagenomic data, fragmented genes and annotation errors will exacerbate this problem. The proposal also does not define which bacterial condensate systems are known, how predictions will be benchmarked against them, or how false positives will be controlled. I would support the project more strongly if condensates were treated as a focused pilot with strict confidence tiers rather than as an equivalent organelle class. The broader proposal remains compelling, especially for genetically marked organelles, but the condensate component currently overpromises."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a conceptually important area: the diversity of bacterial compartmentalization mechanisms. Including condensates broadens the project beyond operon-defined organelles and could stimulate productive interaction between bacterial cell biology, biophysics, and computational biology. The planned integration of disorder prediction, domain annotation, gene neighborhood analysis, and expression data could generate useful candidate lists. The team includes investigators with relevant condensate expertise."",""weakness"":""The condensate identification strategy is weak relative to the claims. Phaseek predictions alone cannot establish condensate formation, and the proposal lacks bacterial-specific benchmarking, negative controls, orthogonal sequence features, and clear criteria for high-confidence calls. Co-expression networks do not demonstrate physical condensate interaction or phase separation. The proposal risks conflating intrinsically disordered or low-complexity proteins with bona fide condensate-forming systems. The integration of condensates with other organelle classes is conceptually interesting but underdeveloped."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The overall project is worthwhile, but the condensate component needs substantial refinement. If the proposal limited condensate analyses to candidate generation while emphasizing stronger marker-based organelle classes, it would be more convincing."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Biomolecular condensates and bacterial organelles are direct examples of emergent cellular organization arising from molecular interactions. The proposal’s focus on coordinated expression and compartmentalization is highly relevant to emergence phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A cross-organelle comparison of bacterial compartmentalization in gut communities is novel. The condensate aspect could be significant if handled carefully, but the current plan may not yield reliable biological conclusions."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""For condensates, the rigor is limited by reliance on a machine-learning predictor without adequate validation, benchmarking, or false-positive control. The approaches for genetically marked organelles are more solid, but the proposal presents all classes together without sufficiently acknowledging differences in evidentiary strength."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is ambitious, especially because condensates require distinct analysis logic from operon-defined compartments. The timeline may be feasible if condensates are scoped as exploratory candidate discovery rather than definitive cataloging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is entirely based on mining and integrating public omics datasets, making it a strong fit for synthesis research."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies useful gut microbiome datasets and public annotation tools. For condensates specifically, it does not identify appropriate training, benchmark, or validation datasets of known bacterial condensate proteins."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database and publication plans are positive, but the proposal should provide more explicit plans for releasing prediction scores, confidence categories, training assumptions, code, and reproducible notebooks so users can evaluate condensate calls critically.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior open-science data infrastructure and molecular systems biology researcher. This proposal is a good thematic fit for a synthesis center: it brings together multiple labs, uses only public data, and aims to create an integrated resource that would be difficult for a single laboratory to produce. The proposed database of bacterial organelles, if well implemented, could be a valuable community asset. I also appreciate the request for NCEMS support in data harmonization, metadata standardization, and multi-omics integration; these are precisely the areas where a synthesis center can add value. However, the proposal’s open science and community-resource plans are underdeveloped relative to the ambition of the project. It states that the database will be publicly accessible and maintained for at least three years, but it does not specify governance, repository choices, data models, APIs, update cycles, documentation, licensing, persistent identifiers, provenance, or mechanisms for community contribution. The training plan is also brief and generic, mentioning open science and bioinformatics training but not defining trainee roles, learning objectives, products, or mentoring structures. As a working group proposal, the team has strong scientific expertise, but the plan would benefit from clearer evidence of diversity across institutions, career stages, computational roles, and community engagement. The scientific topic is compelling, but the project’s lasting impact will depend heavily on whether the outputs are reusable, reproducible, and trusted by the community."",""strengths"":""The proposal aligns well with the NCEMS model by synthesizing public multi-omics data and requiring collaboration among organelle experts, microbiome analysts, and NCEMS staff. It identifies specific public datasets and proposes a reusable searchable database. The project has the potential to train early-career researchers in bioinformatics and multi-omics synthesis. The intended outputs, including databases, pipelines, and manuscripts, are appropriate for an open synthesis project."",""weakness"":""The open science implementation is too vague. The proposal does not specify code repositories, workflow systems, containerization, data standards, database schema, licensing, APIs, documentation, versioning, provenance, or long-term sustainability beyond three years. Training and outreach are mentioned but not described in detail. The role of NCEMS staff is important but somewhat broad, and the division of labor between NCEMS and the investigators is not fully defined. The database maintenance plan may be insufficient for a community-scale resource."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""This is a strong synthesis-oriented proposal with a potentially valuable community resource, but its open science, reproducibility, training, and data-infrastructure plans need more specificity to fully meet the expectations of a community-scale NCEMS working group."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses emergence of bacterial subcellular organization and potential coordinated regulation of organelles in microbial communities. It is clearly relevant, although the proposal could frame the emergent systems questions more explicitly."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of bacterial organelle biology with public gut microbiome multi-omics is novel and likely to produce useful resources and hypotheses. The significance depends on the quality and usability of the resulting database and workflows."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The analytical approach uses reasonable tools, but the proposal lacks enough detail on reproducible workflow implementation, validation, statistical modeling, and quality control. These issues are especially important for a public resource."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":4,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but generally plausible with NCEMS support, especially if the team prioritizes a pilot dataset, pipeline refinement, IBD multi-omics integration, and database release. The number of promised manuscripts and database features may need prioritization."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is an excellent fit for synthesis-focused work: it uses only existing public data, integrates multiple data types, and requires expertise beyond a single lab."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies GMrepo, several accessions, MGnify resources, and the IBD Multi-omics Database. It would be improved by a structured data inventory with sample counts, metadata fields, omics pairing, quality measures, and reuse constraints."",""open_science_commitment_score"":2,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to public outputs but does not provide a sufficiently detailed open science plan. A community-scale synthesis proposal should specify FAIR data practices, licensing, versioned repositories, executable workflows, containerized environments, public documentation, issue tracking, and long-term governance.""}]}",0,y2::11,y2,11,Discovery and functional analysis of bacterial organelles through mining and refinement of omics data,Rejected,Y Hoang; Arash Komeili; Hualiang Pi; Markus Sutter,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 2: Senior microbiome systems biologist with expertise in human gut multi-omics and host-associated microbial ecology. This proposal has a compelling biological premise: bacterial organelles may be widespread and dynamically regulated in the gut microbiome, and public multi-omics data now make it possible to study these structures at community scale. The proposed use of GMrepo and the IBD longitudinal multi-omics dataset is appropriate and responsive to the NCEMS mission. I appreciate the effort to move beyond presence/absence toward expression, regulation, and potential coordination among organelle systems. However, as a microbiome proposal, it underestimates major challenges in comparing human gut datasets across studies, phenotypes, sequencing protocols, host covariates, medication exposures, diet, geography, and sample processing. The proposal lists disease states such as diarrhea, C. difficile infection, cystic fibrosis, IBS, and IBD as environmental perturbations, but it does not provide a coherent design for controlling confounders or distinguishing organelle regulation from shifts in community composition. The strongest part is the longitudinal IBD multi-omics analysis, but even there, the plan does not clearly state whether organelle expression will be normalized to taxon abundance, gene copy number, or total community transcription/protein abundance. Without these controls, claims about regulation could be confounded by changes in species abundance. I consider this a promising but methodologically underdeveloped proposal.","The proposal uses relevant public microbiome resources and addresses an interesting gap: the ecological and regulatory context of bacterial organelles in human gut communities. The longitudinal IBD dataset is a strong choice for paired multi-omics analysis. The team’s organelle biology expertise is excellent, and NCEMS support could add important expertise in multi-omics harmonization and metadata standardization. The project could generate useful hypotheses about organelle distribution across health and disease states.","The microbiome study design is not sufficiently rigorous. The proposal does not define sample sizes, metadata covariates, statistical models, batch correction methods, or controls for compositionality. Disease categories are treated as environmental perturbations without detailed attention to medication, diet, inflammation, antibiotics, age, geography, sequencing methods, or study-specific biases. It is unclear how the team will distinguish differential expression of organelle genes from changes in the abundance of organisms carrying those genes. The metaproteomic component may be technically difficult and less complete than implied.",3,"The project is well aligned with public-data synthesis and has a compelling biological question, but the microbiome comparative framework requires substantial strengthening before robust conclusions about organelle regulation or disease-associated dynamics can be drawn.",4,"The proposal addresses how subcellular structures emerge and function within organisms embedded in complex communities. The concept of organelle-level regulation across community perturbations fits the call, though the proposal could more explicitly connect community-level patterns to emergent cellular mechanisms.",4,Studying bacterial organelles through human gut multi-omics is novel and could be significant. The ability to connect organelle repertoires to microbial ecology and disease-associated states would advance both microbiome science and bacterial cell biology.,2,"The proposal lacks sufficient detail on microbiome statistical analysis, compositional data handling, longitudinal modeling, batch effects, and normalization of expression relative to organism abundance. These omissions threaten interpretability of the key conclusions.",3,"The timeline is ambitious but possible for a descriptive analysis. However, robust cross-study harmonization, longitudinal modeling, metaproteomic analysis, and database deployment within the proposed period may be difficult, especially with multiple organelle classes.",5,"The project is strongly synthesis-focused and relies entirely on public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data. It is a good example of a question that benefits from integrating existing datasets across studies.",4,"The proposal identifies several specific public datasets and databases. It would be stronger if it provided a table of sample counts, omics modalities, metadata fields, and data quality metrics for each dataset.",3,"The commitment to a public database is positive, but the proposal gives limited detail on reproducible workflows, containerization, provenance tracking, FAIR metadata, or community standards for updates and reuse.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:49:14.110642,0,success,,"The proposal presents a compelling biological premise to investigate bacterial organelles within the human gut microbiome using public multi-omics datasets. While the focus on expression and regulation across health and disease states is scientifically valuable, the methodological approach remains underdeveloped. Specifically, the analytical design lacks sufficient strategies to account for dataset heterogeneity, host covariates, and community composition shifts. Addressing these confounding factors and clarifying normalization methods are necessary to substantiate claims about organelle regulation.","The project addresses a novel biological gap by examining the ecological and regulatory context of bacterial organelles in human gut communities. The proposed use of relevant public resources, particularly the longitudinal IBD dataset, is highly appropriate for paired multi-omics analysis. Furthermore, the investigative team possesses excellent expertise in organelle biology, positioning them well to generate valuable hypotheses regarding organelle distribution.","The analytical design lacks sufficient rigor, omitting crucial details on sample sizes, statistical models, batch correction, and metadata covariates. A significant limitation is the lack of a clear strategy to distinguish differential gene expression from underlying shifts in microbial community composition or to control for confounding factors like diet and medication. Additionally, the metaproteomic analysis may present unacknowledged technical challenges regarding data completeness."
baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::003,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,3,baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:29:22.412842,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior bacterial cell biologist specializing in bacterial microcompartments and protein-bounded organelles. This proposal addresses an important and timely area: bacterial subcellular organization in natural microbial communities. The focus on bacterial microcompartments, encapsulins, ferrosomes, and condensates is well aligned with molecular and cellular biosciences, and the working group includes strong domain experts for several organelle classes. I am enthusiastic about the potential value of a curated, searchable resource that links organelle markers to metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic evidence. The proposal is strongest for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, where genetic markers and prior comparative frameworks exist. However, the scientific questions are somewhat diffuse, and the plan risks becoming an annotation exercise rather than a sharply hypothesis-driven synthesis project. The proposal would benefit from clearer criteria for distinguishing complete organelle systems from isolated homologs, explicit validation strategies for operon context in fragmented metagenomic assemblies, and a stronger statistical plan for testing co-occurrence and expression hypotheses. The condensate component is substantially less mature than the other organelle classes and may introduce many false positives. Overall, this is a strong but imperfect fit to the call: it uses public data, requires cross-disciplinary expertise, and could produce a valuable community resource, but the rigor of some analyses and the specificity of emergent-phenomena hypotheses need strengthening."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a compelling and underexplored question in bacterial cell biology: how organelles are distributed and regulated in complex microbial communities. The team has strong expertise in BMCs, ferrosomes, and condensates. The use of public metagenomic and multi-omics datasets is appropriate for the call. The plan to generate HMM-based detection pipelines and a searchable database could provide a lasting resource for the field. The focus on organelle co-occurrence and expression across ecological perturbations is potentially novel and significant."",""weakness"":""The hypotheses are broad and sometimes framed as exploratory cataloging rather than specific tests of biological mechanisms. The proposal does not sufficiently address false positives, incomplete assemblies, strain-level ambiguity, or the difference between detecting a marker gene and inferring a functional organelle. The condensate detection strategy relies heavily on Phaseek predictions without a clear bacterial-specific validation plan. Statistical approaches for co-occurrence, environmental responsiveness, and coordinated expression are underdeveloped. The open science and training plans are present but generic."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong proposal with high potential to create a valuable synthesis resource for bacterial organelle biology, especially for genetically marked organelles. Its impact would be improved by sharper hypotheses, clearer validation and statistical frameworks, and a more cautious treatment of condensates."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Bacterial organelles and community-level regulation of compartmentalization are relevant to emergence in molecular and cellular systems. The proposal connects molecular components to higher-order cellular structures and ecological context, although the emergent properties are not always explicitly formulated as such."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Mapping bacterial organelles across the human gut microbiome using integrated multi-omics is novel and potentially significant. Prior work has cataloged some organelles across genomes, but expression and ecological regulation in complex communities remain underexplored."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The HMM-based approach is reasonable for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, but key details are missing regarding thresholds, operon reconstruction, assembly quality, taxonomic assignment, false discovery control, and statistical hypothesis testing. Condensate prediction is particularly uncertain."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering four organelle categories, multiple disease-associated datasets, multi-omics integration, database construction, and multiple manuscripts. The timeline is plausible only if the analyses are tightly prioritized."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data and does not propose new experimental data generation. It clearly requires integration across datasets and expertise areas."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies specific public resources, including GMrepo datasets and the IBD Multi-omics Database, and names relevant accession numbers. However, it could better document sample sizes, metadata completeness, pairing across omics layers, and suitability for the proposed comparisons."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to a public database and publication of the predictive framework, but the plan lacks detail on code repositories, workflow reproducibility, licensing, metadata standards, community governance, and long-term maintenance beyond a brief three-year statement.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior microbiome systems biologist with expertise in human gut multi-omics and host-associated microbial ecology. This proposal has a compelling biological premise: bacterial organelles may be widespread and dynamically regulated in the gut microbiome, and public multi-omics data now make it possible to study these structures at community scale. The proposed use of GMrepo and the IBD longitudinal multi-omics dataset is appropriate and responsive to the NCEMS mission. I appreciate the effort to move beyond presence/absence toward expression, regulation, and potential coordination among organelle systems. However, as a microbiome proposal, it underestimates major challenges in comparing human gut datasets across studies, phenotypes, sequencing protocols, host covariates, medication exposures, diet, geography, and sample processing. The proposal lists disease states such as diarrhea, C. difficile infection, cystic fibrosis, IBS, and IBD as environmental perturbations, but it does not provide a coherent design for controlling confounders or distinguishing organelle regulation from shifts in community composition. The strongest part is the longitudinal IBD multi-omics analysis, but even there, the plan does not clearly state whether organelle expression will be normalized to taxon abundance, gene copy number, or total community transcription/protein abundance. Without these controls, claims about regulation could be confounded by changes in species abundance. I consider this a promising but methodologically underdeveloped proposal."",""strengths"":""The proposal uses relevant public microbiome resources and addresses an interesting gap: the ecological and regulatory context of bacterial organelles in human gut communities. The longitudinal IBD dataset is a strong choice for paired multi-omics analysis. The team’s organelle biology expertise is excellent, and NCEMS support could add important expertise in multi-omics harmonization and metadata standardization. The project could generate useful hypotheses about organelle distribution across health and disease states."",""weakness"":""The microbiome study design is not sufficiently rigorous. The proposal does not define sample sizes, metadata covariates, statistical models, batch correction methods, or controls for compositionality. Disease categories are treated as environmental perturbations without detailed attention to medication, diet, inflammation, antibiotics, age, geography, sequencing methods, or study-specific biases. It is unclear how the team will distinguish differential expression of organelle genes from changes in the abundance of organisms carrying those genes. The metaproteomic component may be technically difficult and less complete than implied."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is well aligned with public-data synthesis and has a compelling biological question, but the microbiome comparative framework requires substantial strengthening before robust conclusions about organelle regulation or disease-associated dynamics can be drawn."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses how subcellular structures emerge and function within organisms embedded in complex communities. The concept of organelle-level regulation across community perturbations fits the call, though the proposal could more explicitly connect community-level patterns to emergent cellular mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Studying bacterial organelles through human gut multi-omics is novel and could be significant. The ability to connect organelle repertoires to microbial ecology and disease-associated states would advance both microbiome science and bacterial cell biology."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal lacks sufficient detail on microbiome statistical analysis, compositional data handling, longitudinal modeling, batch effects, and normalization of expression relative to organism abundance. These omissions threaten interpretability of the key conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but possible for a descriptive analysis. However, robust cross-study harmonization, longitudinal modeling, metaproteomic analysis, and database deployment within the proposed period may be difficult, especially with multiple organelle classes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is strongly synthesis-focused and relies entirely on public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data. It is a good example of a question that benefits from integrating existing datasets across studies."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies several specific public datasets and databases. It would be stronger if it provided a table of sample counts, omics modalities, metadata fields, and data quality metrics for each dataset."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to a public database is positive, but the proposal gives limited detail on reproducible workflows, containerization, provenance tracking, FAIR metadata, or community standards for updates and reuse.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior computational metagenomics and bioinformatics researcher specializing in genome-resolved microbiome analysis. The proposal is responsive to the call in its exclusive reliance on public data and its need for computational synthesis across large-scale omics datasets. The idea of building a reusable pipeline to detect bacterial organelle systems in metagenomic and multi-omics data is worthwhile. Nonetheless, I have concerns about computational rigor and feasibility. The proposed workflow says metagenomic data will be translated into protein sequences and scanned using organelle-specific HMMs, but it is not clear whether this will be done on reads, contigs, metagenome-assembled genomes, gene catalogs, or isolate genomes. This distinction is critical for operon-based detection, co-occurrence within organisms, and claims about taxonomic distribution. The requested resources mention 100 CPU hours per genome assembly and raw data per isolate, which is incongruent with a metagenomic synthesis project and suggests that the computational plan has not been fully specified. Inferring that two organelles coexist within the same organism requires genome-resolved binning or robust linkage; detecting both markers in a sample is not sufficient. Similarly, gene neighborhood analyses are difficult in fragmented assemblies. The proposal would be much stronger with explicit benchmarking against known genomes, positive and negative controls, reproducible workflow design, confidence tiers for organelle calls, and clear plans for MAG quality assessment and dereplication. I see high potential, but at present the computational strategy is too high-level."",""strengths"":""The proposal identifies a tractable set of organelle marker systems and uses widely accepted tools such as HMMER, Pfam, InterProScan, IUPred2A, MGnify workflows, and WGCNA. It proposes to synthesize public multi-omics datasets and produce reusable computational resources. NCEMS staff support for harmonization, quality control, and statistical analysis is well justified. The proposed database could be valuable if organelle calls are carefully benchmarked and annotated with confidence scores."",""weakness"":""The computational workflow is underspecified. It does not clearly define whether analyses will occur at the read, contig, MAG, species, or sample level. It lacks benchmarking, false-positive controls, assembly/binning strategies, quality thresholds, taxonomic classification methods, confidence scoring, and statistical models. Co-occurrence within organisms cannot be inferred from sample-level marker detection. The resource request includes confusing language about isolates and genome assembly that does not match the stated public metagenomic data synthesis focus."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A potentially impactful synthesis project whose success depends on computational implementation. The current proposal is promising but needs a much clearer genome-resolved and statistically rigorous analysis plan to support its biological claims."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":3,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The project concerns emergence of cellular compartmentalization and possible coordinated organelle expression. However, much of the proposed work is marker detection and cataloging, and the computational plan does not yet convincingly connect the data to emergent mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A public, genome-resolved catalog of bacterial organelle systems in the gut microbiome would be novel and significant. The multi-omics expression component could add an important layer beyond existing genome surveys."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks key computational details required for robust metagenomic inference, including assembly strategy, MAG recovery, gene linkage, benchmarking, thresholds, error rates, and statistical testing. These are central to the proposal’s conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The computational scope is large but potentially feasible with NCEMS support if narrowed and better specified. Building pipelines, processing large multi-omics datasets, and deploying databases within two years will be challenging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly based on synthesis of public data and integration of multiple omics types. No new experimental data are proposed."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal lists relevant datasets and accessions, but it does not adequately specify data types, sample numbers, read depths, assembly availability, MAG availability, or whether datasets are suitable for organism-level co-occurrence and expression analyses."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database commitment is useful, but a computational synthesis project should provide more detail on versioned code, workflow languages, containers, continuous integration, data provenance, documentation, and standards for reproducibility.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior molecular biophysicist studying biomolecular condensates and phase separation in bacteria. I am pleased to see bacterial condensates included alongside more classical genetically encoded organelles, since condensates are an important frontier in bacterial cell organization. However, from the condensate perspective, this proposal is not yet rigorous. Unlike BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, condensates generally lack simple operonic signatures, and the proposal acknowledges this. The proposed solution is to apply Phaseek to predicted proteins, followed by co-expression networks and disorder/domain analyses. This may be useful for candidate discovery but is insufficient to identify condensates or infer their regulation in the gut microbiome. Condensate propensity predictors are trained under particular assumptions, often using eukaryotic or curated protein sets, and are prone to bias from protein length, low-complexity sequence, charge patterning, nucleic-acid-binding domains, and taxonomic composition. In metagenomic data, fragmented genes and annotation errors will exacerbate this problem. The proposal also does not define which bacterial condensate systems are known, how predictions will be benchmarked against them, or how false positives will be controlled. I would support the project more strongly if condensates were treated as a focused pilot with strict confidence tiers rather than as an equivalent organelle class. The broader proposal remains compelling, especially for genetically marked organelles, but the condensate component currently overpromises."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a conceptually important area: the diversity of bacterial compartmentalization mechanisms. Including condensates broadens the project beyond operon-defined organelles and could stimulate productive interaction between bacterial cell biology, biophysics, and computational biology. The planned integration of disorder prediction, domain annotation, gene neighborhood analysis, and expression data could generate useful candidate lists. The team includes investigators with relevant condensate expertise."",""weakness"":""The condensate identification strategy is weak relative to the claims. Phaseek predictions alone cannot establish condensate formation, and the proposal lacks bacterial-specific benchmarking, negative controls, orthogonal sequence features, and clear criteria for high-confidence calls. Co-expression networks do not demonstrate physical condensate interaction or phase separation. The proposal risks conflating intrinsically disordered or low-complexity proteins with bona fide condensate-forming systems. The integration of condensates with other organelle classes is conceptually interesting but underdeveloped."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The overall project is worthwhile, but the condensate component needs substantial refinement. If the proposal limited condensate analyses to candidate generation while emphasizing stronger marker-based organelle classes, it would be more convincing."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Biomolecular condensates and bacterial organelles are direct examples of emergent cellular organization arising from molecular interactions. The proposal’s focus on coordinated expression and compartmentalization is highly relevant to emergence phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A cross-organelle comparison of bacterial compartmentalization in gut communities is novel. The condensate aspect could be significant if handled carefully, but the current plan may not yield reliable biological conclusions."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""For condensates, the rigor is limited by reliance on a machine-learning predictor without adequate validation, benchmarking, or false-positive control. The approaches for genetically marked organelles are more solid, but the proposal presents all classes together without sufficiently acknowledging differences in evidentiary strength."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is ambitious, especially because condensates require distinct analysis logic from operon-defined compartments. The timeline may be feasible if condensates are scoped as exploratory candidate discovery rather than definitive cataloging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is entirely based on mining and integrating public omics datasets, making it a strong fit for synthesis research."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies useful gut microbiome datasets and public annotation tools. For condensates specifically, it does not identify appropriate training, benchmark, or validation datasets of known bacterial condensate proteins."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database and publication plans are positive, but the proposal should provide more explicit plans for releasing prediction scores, confidence categories, training assumptions, code, and reproducible notebooks so users can evaluate condensate calls critically.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior open-science data infrastructure and molecular systems biology researcher. This proposal is a good thematic fit for a synthesis center: it brings together multiple labs, uses only public data, and aims to create an integrated resource that would be difficult for a single laboratory to produce. The proposed database of bacterial organelles, if well implemented, could be a valuable community asset. I also appreciate the request for NCEMS support in data harmonization, metadata standardization, and multi-omics integration; these are precisely the areas where a synthesis center can add value. However, the proposal’s open science and community-resource plans are underdeveloped relative to the ambition of the project. It states that the database will be publicly accessible and maintained for at least three years, but it does not specify governance, repository choices, data models, APIs, update cycles, documentation, licensing, persistent identifiers, provenance, or mechanisms for community contribution. The training plan is also brief and generic, mentioning open science and bioinformatics training but not defining trainee roles, learning objectives, products, or mentoring structures. As a working group proposal, the team has strong scientific expertise, but the plan would benefit from clearer evidence of diversity across institutions, career stages, computational roles, and community engagement. The scientific topic is compelling, but the project’s lasting impact will depend heavily on whether the outputs are reusable, reproducible, and trusted by the community."",""strengths"":""The proposal aligns well with the NCEMS model by synthesizing public multi-omics data and requiring collaboration among organelle experts, microbiome analysts, and NCEMS staff. It identifies specific public datasets and proposes a reusable searchable database. The project has the potential to train early-career researchers in bioinformatics and multi-omics synthesis. The intended outputs, including databases, pipelines, and manuscripts, are appropriate for an open synthesis project."",""weakness"":""The open science implementation is too vague. The proposal does not specify code repositories, workflow systems, containerization, data standards, database schema, licensing, APIs, documentation, versioning, provenance, or long-term sustainability beyond three years. Training and outreach are mentioned but not described in detail. The role of NCEMS staff is important but somewhat broad, and the division of labor between NCEMS and the investigators is not fully defined. The database maintenance plan may be insufficient for a community-scale resource."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""This is a strong synthesis-oriented proposal with a potentially valuable community resource, but its open science, reproducibility, training, and data-infrastructure plans need more specificity to fully meet the expectations of a community-scale NCEMS working group."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses emergence of bacterial subcellular organization and potential coordinated regulation of organelles in microbial communities. It is clearly relevant, although the proposal could frame the emergent systems questions more explicitly."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of bacterial organelle biology with public gut microbiome multi-omics is novel and likely to produce useful resources and hypotheses. The significance depends on the quality and usability of the resulting database and workflows."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The analytical approach uses reasonable tools, but the proposal lacks enough detail on reproducible workflow implementation, validation, statistical modeling, and quality control. These issues are especially important for a public resource."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":4,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but generally plausible with NCEMS support, especially if the team prioritizes a pilot dataset, pipeline refinement, IBD multi-omics integration, and database release. The number of promised manuscripts and database features may need prioritization."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is an excellent fit for synthesis-focused work: it uses only existing public data, integrates multiple data types, and requires expertise beyond a single lab."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies GMrepo, several accessions, MGnify resources, and the IBD Multi-omics Database. It would be improved by a structured data inventory with sample counts, metadata fields, omics pairing, quality measures, and reuse constraints."",""open_science_commitment_score"":2,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to public outputs but does not provide a sufficiently detailed open science plan. A community-scale synthesis proposal should specify FAIR data practices, licensing, versioned repositories, executable workflows, containerized environments, public documentation, issue tracking, and long-term governance.""}]}",0,y2::11,y2,11,Discovery and functional analysis of bacterial organelles through mining and refinement of omics data,Rejected,Y Hoang; Arash Komeili; Hualiang Pi; Markus Sutter,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 3: Senior computational metagenomics and bioinformatics researcher specializing in genome-resolved microbiome analysis. The proposal is responsive to the call in its exclusive reliance on public data and its need for computational synthesis across large-scale omics datasets. The idea of building a reusable pipeline to detect bacterial organelle systems in metagenomic and multi-omics data is worthwhile. Nonetheless, I have concerns about computational rigor and feasibility. The proposed workflow says metagenomic data will be translated into protein sequences and scanned using organelle-specific HMMs, but it is not clear whether this will be done on reads, contigs, metagenome-assembled genomes, gene catalogs, or isolate genomes. This distinction is critical for operon-based detection, co-occurrence within organisms, and claims about taxonomic distribution. The requested resources mention 100 CPU hours per genome assembly and raw data per isolate, which is incongruent with a metagenomic synthesis project and suggests that the computational plan has not been fully specified. Inferring that two organelles coexist within the same organism requires genome-resolved binning or robust linkage; detecting both markers in a sample is not sufficient. Similarly, gene neighborhood analyses are difficult in fragmented assemblies. The proposal would be much stronger with explicit benchmarking against known genomes, positive and negative controls, reproducible workflow design, confidence tiers for organelle calls, and clear plans for MAG quality assessment and dereplication. I see high potential, but at present the computational strategy is too high-level.","The proposal identifies a tractable set of organelle marker systems and uses widely accepted tools such as HMMER, Pfam, InterProScan, IUPred2A, MGnify workflows, and WGCNA. It proposes to synthesize public multi-omics datasets and produce reusable computational resources. NCEMS staff support for harmonization, quality control, and statistical analysis is well justified. The proposed database could be valuable if organelle calls are carefully benchmarked and annotated with confidence scores.","The computational workflow is underspecified. It does not clearly define whether analyses will occur at the read, contig, MAG, species, or sample level. It lacks benchmarking, false-positive controls, assembly/binning strategies, quality thresholds, taxonomic classification methods, confidence scoring, and statistical models. Co-occurrence within organisms cannot be inferred from sample-level marker detection. The resource request includes confusing language about isolates and genome assembly that does not match the stated public metagenomic data synthesis focus.",3,A potentially impactful synthesis project whose success depends on computational implementation. The current proposal is promising but needs a much clearer genome-resolved and statistically rigorous analysis plan to support its biological claims.,3,"The project concerns emergence of cellular compartmentalization and possible coordinated organelle expression. However, much of the proposed work is marker detection and cataloging, and the computational plan does not yet convincingly connect the data to emergent mechanisms.",4,"A public, genome-resolved catalog of bacterial organelle systems in the gut microbiome would be novel and significant. The multi-omics expression component could add an important layer beyond existing genome surveys.",2,"The approach lacks key computational details required for robust metagenomic inference, including assembly strategy, MAG recovery, gene linkage, benchmarking, thresholds, error rates, and statistical testing. These are central to the proposal’s conclusions.",3,"The computational scope is large but potentially feasible with NCEMS support if narrowed and better specified. Building pipelines, processing large multi-omics datasets, and deploying databases within two years will be challenging.",5,The proposal is clearly based on synthesis of public data and integration of multiple omics types. No new experimental data are proposed.,3,"The proposal lists relevant datasets and accessions, but it does not adequately specify data types, sample numbers, read depths, assembly availability, MAG availability, or whether datasets are suitable for organism-level co-occurrence and expression analyses.",3,"The public database commitment is useful, but a computational synthesis project should provide more detail on versioned code, workflow languages, containers, continuous integration, data provenance, documentation, and standards for reproducibility.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:49:25.389458,0,success,,"The proposal aims to develop a reusable computational pipeline for detecting bacterial organelle systems using public multi-omics data, aligning well with the funding call. However, the computational strategy is currently too abstract and lacks necessary methodological details regarding data processing levels, benchmarking, and quality assessment. Clarifying the workflow, particularly distinguishing between sample-level and organism-level detection, is essential to ensure the feasibility and rigor of the proposed research.","The project utilizes well-established bioinformatics tools to analyze a tractable set of organelle markers across public multi-omics datasets. It has the potential to generate highly valuable, reusable computational resources and databases for the scientific community. Furthermore, the request for staff support to assist with data harmonization and statistical analysis is appropriately justified.","The computational workflow lacks critical details regarding whether analyses will target reads, contigs, or metagenome-assembled genomes, which is vital for inferring organelle co-occurrence. The methodology also omits essential benchmarking, false-positive controls, and specific quality thresholds. Additionally, the resource requests mention isolate genome assembly, which contradicts the proposal's primary focus on public metagenomic data synthesis."
baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::004,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,4,baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:29:22.412842,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior bacterial cell biologist specializing in bacterial microcompartments and protein-bounded organelles. This proposal addresses an important and timely area: bacterial subcellular organization in natural microbial communities. The focus on bacterial microcompartments, encapsulins, ferrosomes, and condensates is well aligned with molecular and cellular biosciences, and the working group includes strong domain experts for several organelle classes. I am enthusiastic about the potential value of a curated, searchable resource that links organelle markers to metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic evidence. The proposal is strongest for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, where genetic markers and prior comparative frameworks exist. However, the scientific questions are somewhat diffuse, and the plan risks becoming an annotation exercise rather than a sharply hypothesis-driven synthesis project. The proposal would benefit from clearer criteria for distinguishing complete organelle systems from isolated homologs, explicit validation strategies for operon context in fragmented metagenomic assemblies, and a stronger statistical plan for testing co-occurrence and expression hypotheses. The condensate component is substantially less mature than the other organelle classes and may introduce many false positives. Overall, this is a strong but imperfect fit to the call: it uses public data, requires cross-disciplinary expertise, and could produce a valuable community resource, but the rigor of some analyses and the specificity of emergent-phenomena hypotheses need strengthening."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a compelling and underexplored question in bacterial cell biology: how organelles are distributed and regulated in complex microbial communities. The team has strong expertise in BMCs, ferrosomes, and condensates. The use of public metagenomic and multi-omics datasets is appropriate for the call. The plan to generate HMM-based detection pipelines and a searchable database could provide a lasting resource for the field. The focus on organelle co-occurrence and expression across ecological perturbations is potentially novel and significant."",""weakness"":""The hypotheses are broad and sometimes framed as exploratory cataloging rather than specific tests of biological mechanisms. The proposal does not sufficiently address false positives, incomplete assemblies, strain-level ambiguity, or the difference between detecting a marker gene and inferring a functional organelle. The condensate detection strategy relies heavily on Phaseek predictions without a clear bacterial-specific validation plan. Statistical approaches for co-occurrence, environmental responsiveness, and coordinated expression are underdeveloped. The open science and training plans are present but generic."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong proposal with high potential to create a valuable synthesis resource for bacterial organelle biology, especially for genetically marked organelles. Its impact would be improved by sharper hypotheses, clearer validation and statistical frameworks, and a more cautious treatment of condensates."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Bacterial organelles and community-level regulation of compartmentalization are relevant to emergence in molecular and cellular systems. The proposal connects molecular components to higher-order cellular structures and ecological context, although the emergent properties are not always explicitly formulated as such."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Mapping bacterial organelles across the human gut microbiome using integrated multi-omics is novel and potentially significant. Prior work has cataloged some organelles across genomes, but expression and ecological regulation in complex communities remain underexplored."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The HMM-based approach is reasonable for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, but key details are missing regarding thresholds, operon reconstruction, assembly quality, taxonomic assignment, false discovery control, and statistical hypothesis testing. Condensate prediction is particularly uncertain."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering four organelle categories, multiple disease-associated datasets, multi-omics integration, database construction, and multiple manuscripts. The timeline is plausible only if the analyses are tightly prioritized."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data and does not propose new experimental data generation. It clearly requires integration across datasets and expertise areas."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies specific public resources, including GMrepo datasets and the IBD Multi-omics Database, and names relevant accession numbers. However, it could better document sample sizes, metadata completeness, pairing across omics layers, and suitability for the proposed comparisons."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to a public database and publication of the predictive framework, but the plan lacks detail on code repositories, workflow reproducibility, licensing, metadata standards, community governance, and long-term maintenance beyond a brief three-year statement.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior microbiome systems biologist with expertise in human gut multi-omics and host-associated microbial ecology. This proposal has a compelling biological premise: bacterial organelles may be widespread and dynamically regulated in the gut microbiome, and public multi-omics data now make it possible to study these structures at community scale. The proposed use of GMrepo and the IBD longitudinal multi-omics dataset is appropriate and responsive to the NCEMS mission. I appreciate the effort to move beyond presence/absence toward expression, regulation, and potential coordination among organelle systems. However, as a microbiome proposal, it underestimates major challenges in comparing human gut datasets across studies, phenotypes, sequencing protocols, host covariates, medication exposures, diet, geography, and sample processing. The proposal lists disease states such as diarrhea, C. difficile infection, cystic fibrosis, IBS, and IBD as environmental perturbations, but it does not provide a coherent design for controlling confounders or distinguishing organelle regulation from shifts in community composition. The strongest part is the longitudinal IBD multi-omics analysis, but even there, the plan does not clearly state whether organelle expression will be normalized to taxon abundance, gene copy number, or total community transcription/protein abundance. Without these controls, claims about regulation could be confounded by changes in species abundance. I consider this a promising but methodologically underdeveloped proposal."",""strengths"":""The proposal uses relevant public microbiome resources and addresses an interesting gap: the ecological and regulatory context of bacterial organelles in human gut communities. The longitudinal IBD dataset is a strong choice for paired multi-omics analysis. The team’s organelle biology expertise is excellent, and NCEMS support could add important expertise in multi-omics harmonization and metadata standardization. The project could generate useful hypotheses about organelle distribution across health and disease states."",""weakness"":""The microbiome study design is not sufficiently rigorous. The proposal does not define sample sizes, metadata covariates, statistical models, batch correction methods, or controls for compositionality. Disease categories are treated as environmental perturbations without detailed attention to medication, diet, inflammation, antibiotics, age, geography, sequencing methods, or study-specific biases. It is unclear how the team will distinguish differential expression of organelle genes from changes in the abundance of organisms carrying those genes. The metaproteomic component may be technically difficult and less complete than implied."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is well aligned with public-data synthesis and has a compelling biological question, but the microbiome comparative framework requires substantial strengthening before robust conclusions about organelle regulation or disease-associated dynamics can be drawn."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses how subcellular structures emerge and function within organisms embedded in complex communities. The concept of organelle-level regulation across community perturbations fits the call, though the proposal could more explicitly connect community-level patterns to emergent cellular mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Studying bacterial organelles through human gut multi-omics is novel and could be significant. The ability to connect organelle repertoires to microbial ecology and disease-associated states would advance both microbiome science and bacterial cell biology."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal lacks sufficient detail on microbiome statistical analysis, compositional data handling, longitudinal modeling, batch effects, and normalization of expression relative to organism abundance. These omissions threaten interpretability of the key conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but possible for a descriptive analysis. However, robust cross-study harmonization, longitudinal modeling, metaproteomic analysis, and database deployment within the proposed period may be difficult, especially with multiple organelle classes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is strongly synthesis-focused and relies entirely on public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data. It is a good example of a question that benefits from integrating existing datasets across studies."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies several specific public datasets and databases. It would be stronger if it provided a table of sample counts, omics modalities, metadata fields, and data quality metrics for each dataset."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to a public database is positive, but the proposal gives limited detail on reproducible workflows, containerization, provenance tracking, FAIR metadata, or community standards for updates and reuse.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior computational metagenomics and bioinformatics researcher specializing in genome-resolved microbiome analysis. The proposal is responsive to the call in its exclusive reliance on public data and its need for computational synthesis across large-scale omics datasets. The idea of building a reusable pipeline to detect bacterial organelle systems in metagenomic and multi-omics data is worthwhile. Nonetheless, I have concerns about computational rigor and feasibility. The proposed workflow says metagenomic data will be translated into protein sequences and scanned using organelle-specific HMMs, but it is not clear whether this will be done on reads, contigs, metagenome-assembled genomes, gene catalogs, or isolate genomes. This distinction is critical for operon-based detection, co-occurrence within organisms, and claims about taxonomic distribution. The requested resources mention 100 CPU hours per genome assembly and raw data per isolate, which is incongruent with a metagenomic synthesis project and suggests that the computational plan has not been fully specified. Inferring that two organelles coexist within the same organism requires genome-resolved binning or robust linkage; detecting both markers in a sample is not sufficient. Similarly, gene neighborhood analyses are difficult in fragmented assemblies. The proposal would be much stronger with explicit benchmarking against known genomes, positive and negative controls, reproducible workflow design, confidence tiers for organelle calls, and clear plans for MAG quality assessment and dereplication. I see high potential, but at present the computational strategy is too high-level."",""strengths"":""The proposal identifies a tractable set of organelle marker systems and uses widely accepted tools such as HMMER, Pfam, InterProScan, IUPred2A, MGnify workflows, and WGCNA. It proposes to synthesize public multi-omics datasets and produce reusable computational resources. NCEMS staff support for harmonization, quality control, and statistical analysis is well justified. The proposed database could be valuable if organelle calls are carefully benchmarked and annotated with confidence scores."",""weakness"":""The computational workflow is underspecified. It does not clearly define whether analyses will occur at the read, contig, MAG, species, or sample level. It lacks benchmarking, false-positive controls, assembly/binning strategies, quality thresholds, taxonomic classification methods, confidence scoring, and statistical models. Co-occurrence within organisms cannot be inferred from sample-level marker detection. The resource request includes confusing language about isolates and genome assembly that does not match the stated public metagenomic data synthesis focus."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A potentially impactful synthesis project whose success depends on computational implementation. The current proposal is promising but needs a much clearer genome-resolved and statistically rigorous analysis plan to support its biological claims."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":3,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The project concerns emergence of cellular compartmentalization and possible coordinated organelle expression. However, much of the proposed work is marker detection and cataloging, and the computational plan does not yet convincingly connect the data to emergent mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A public, genome-resolved catalog of bacterial organelle systems in the gut microbiome would be novel and significant. The multi-omics expression component could add an important layer beyond existing genome surveys."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks key computational details required for robust metagenomic inference, including assembly strategy, MAG recovery, gene linkage, benchmarking, thresholds, error rates, and statistical testing. These are central to the proposal’s conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The computational scope is large but potentially feasible with NCEMS support if narrowed and better specified. Building pipelines, processing large multi-omics datasets, and deploying databases within two years will be challenging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly based on synthesis of public data and integration of multiple omics types. No new experimental data are proposed."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal lists relevant datasets and accessions, but it does not adequately specify data types, sample numbers, read depths, assembly availability, MAG availability, or whether datasets are suitable for organism-level co-occurrence and expression analyses."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database commitment is useful, but a computational synthesis project should provide more detail on versioned code, workflow languages, containers, continuous integration, data provenance, documentation, and standards for reproducibility.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior molecular biophysicist studying biomolecular condensates and phase separation in bacteria. I am pleased to see bacterial condensates included alongside more classical genetically encoded organelles, since condensates are an important frontier in bacterial cell organization. However, from the condensate perspective, this proposal is not yet rigorous. Unlike BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, condensates generally lack simple operonic signatures, and the proposal acknowledges this. The proposed solution is to apply Phaseek to predicted proteins, followed by co-expression networks and disorder/domain analyses. This may be useful for candidate discovery but is insufficient to identify condensates or infer their regulation in the gut microbiome. Condensate propensity predictors are trained under particular assumptions, often using eukaryotic or curated protein sets, and are prone to bias from protein length, low-complexity sequence, charge patterning, nucleic-acid-binding domains, and taxonomic composition. In metagenomic data, fragmented genes and annotation errors will exacerbate this problem. The proposal also does not define which bacterial condensate systems are known, how predictions will be benchmarked against them, or how false positives will be controlled. I would support the project more strongly if condensates were treated as a focused pilot with strict confidence tiers rather than as an equivalent organelle class. The broader proposal remains compelling, especially for genetically marked organelles, but the condensate component currently overpromises."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a conceptually important area: the diversity of bacterial compartmentalization mechanisms. Including condensates broadens the project beyond operon-defined organelles and could stimulate productive interaction between bacterial cell biology, biophysics, and computational biology. The planned integration of disorder prediction, domain annotation, gene neighborhood analysis, and expression data could generate useful candidate lists. The team includes investigators with relevant condensate expertise."",""weakness"":""The condensate identification strategy is weak relative to the claims. Phaseek predictions alone cannot establish condensate formation, and the proposal lacks bacterial-specific benchmarking, negative controls, orthogonal sequence features, and clear criteria for high-confidence calls. Co-expression networks do not demonstrate physical condensate interaction or phase separation. The proposal risks conflating intrinsically disordered or low-complexity proteins with bona fide condensate-forming systems. The integration of condensates with other organelle classes is conceptually interesting but underdeveloped."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The overall project is worthwhile, but the condensate component needs substantial refinement. If the proposal limited condensate analyses to candidate generation while emphasizing stronger marker-based organelle classes, it would be more convincing."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Biomolecular condensates and bacterial organelles are direct examples of emergent cellular organization arising from molecular interactions. The proposal’s focus on coordinated expression and compartmentalization is highly relevant to emergence phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A cross-organelle comparison of bacterial compartmentalization in gut communities is novel. The condensate aspect could be significant if handled carefully, but the current plan may not yield reliable biological conclusions."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""For condensates, the rigor is limited by reliance on a machine-learning predictor without adequate validation, benchmarking, or false-positive control. The approaches for genetically marked organelles are more solid, but the proposal presents all classes together without sufficiently acknowledging differences in evidentiary strength."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is ambitious, especially because condensates require distinct analysis logic from operon-defined compartments. The timeline may be feasible if condensates are scoped as exploratory candidate discovery rather than definitive cataloging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is entirely based on mining and integrating public omics datasets, making it a strong fit for synthesis research."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies useful gut microbiome datasets and public annotation tools. For condensates specifically, it does not identify appropriate training, benchmark, or validation datasets of known bacterial condensate proteins."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database and publication plans are positive, but the proposal should provide more explicit plans for releasing prediction scores, confidence categories, training assumptions, code, and reproducible notebooks so users can evaluate condensate calls critically.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior open-science data infrastructure and molecular systems biology researcher. This proposal is a good thematic fit for a synthesis center: it brings together multiple labs, uses only public data, and aims to create an integrated resource that would be difficult for a single laboratory to produce. The proposed database of bacterial organelles, if well implemented, could be a valuable community asset. I also appreciate the request for NCEMS support in data harmonization, metadata standardization, and multi-omics integration; these are precisely the areas where a synthesis center can add value. However, the proposal’s open science and community-resource plans are underdeveloped relative to the ambition of the project. It states that the database will be publicly accessible and maintained for at least three years, but it does not specify governance, repository choices, data models, APIs, update cycles, documentation, licensing, persistent identifiers, provenance, or mechanisms for community contribution. The training plan is also brief and generic, mentioning open science and bioinformatics training but not defining trainee roles, learning objectives, products, or mentoring structures. As a working group proposal, the team has strong scientific expertise, but the plan would benefit from clearer evidence of diversity across institutions, career stages, computational roles, and community engagement. The scientific topic is compelling, but the project’s lasting impact will depend heavily on whether the outputs are reusable, reproducible, and trusted by the community."",""strengths"":""The proposal aligns well with the NCEMS model by synthesizing public multi-omics data and requiring collaboration among organelle experts, microbiome analysts, and NCEMS staff. It identifies specific public datasets and proposes a reusable searchable database. The project has the potential to train early-career researchers in bioinformatics and multi-omics synthesis. The intended outputs, including databases, pipelines, and manuscripts, are appropriate for an open synthesis project."",""weakness"":""The open science implementation is too vague. The proposal does not specify code repositories, workflow systems, containerization, data standards, database schema, licensing, APIs, documentation, versioning, provenance, or long-term sustainability beyond three years. Training and outreach are mentioned but not described in detail. The role of NCEMS staff is important but somewhat broad, and the division of labor between NCEMS and the investigators is not fully defined. The database maintenance plan may be insufficient for a community-scale resource."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""This is a strong synthesis-oriented proposal with a potentially valuable community resource, but its open science, reproducibility, training, and data-infrastructure plans need more specificity to fully meet the expectations of a community-scale NCEMS working group."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses emergence of bacterial subcellular organization and potential coordinated regulation of organelles in microbial communities. It is clearly relevant, although the proposal could frame the emergent systems questions more explicitly."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of bacterial organelle biology with public gut microbiome multi-omics is novel and likely to produce useful resources and hypotheses. The significance depends on the quality and usability of the resulting database and workflows."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The analytical approach uses reasonable tools, but the proposal lacks enough detail on reproducible workflow implementation, validation, statistical modeling, and quality control. These issues are especially important for a public resource."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":4,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but generally plausible with NCEMS support, especially if the team prioritizes a pilot dataset, pipeline refinement, IBD multi-omics integration, and database release. The number of promised manuscripts and database features may need prioritization."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is an excellent fit for synthesis-focused work: it uses only existing public data, integrates multiple data types, and requires expertise beyond a single lab."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies GMrepo, several accessions, MGnify resources, and the IBD Multi-omics Database. It would be improved by a structured data inventory with sample counts, metadata fields, omics pairing, quality measures, and reuse constraints."",""open_science_commitment_score"":2,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to public outputs but does not provide a sufficiently detailed open science plan. A community-scale synthesis proposal should specify FAIR data practices, licensing, versioned repositories, executable workflows, containerized environments, public documentation, issue tracking, and long-term governance.""}]}",0,y2::11,y2,11,Discovery and functional analysis of bacterial organelles through mining and refinement of omics data,Rejected,Y Hoang; Arash Komeili; Hualiang Pi; Markus Sutter,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 4: Senior molecular biophysicist studying biomolecular condensates and phase separation in bacteria. I am pleased to see bacterial condensates included alongside more classical genetically encoded organelles, since condensates are an important frontier in bacterial cell organization. However, from the condensate perspective, this proposal is not yet rigorous. Unlike BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, condensates generally lack simple operonic signatures, and the proposal acknowledges this. The proposed solution is to apply Phaseek to predicted proteins, followed by co-expression networks and disorder/domain analyses. This may be useful for candidate discovery but is insufficient to identify condensates or infer their regulation in the gut microbiome. Condensate propensity predictors are trained under particular assumptions, often using eukaryotic or curated protein sets, and are prone to bias from protein length, low-complexity sequence, charge patterning, nucleic-acid-binding domains, and taxonomic composition. In metagenomic data, fragmented genes and annotation errors will exacerbate this problem. The proposal also does not define which bacterial condensate systems are known, how predictions will be benchmarked against them, or how false positives will be controlled. I would support the project more strongly if condensates were treated as a focused pilot with strict confidence tiers rather than as an equivalent organelle class. The broader proposal remains compelling, especially for genetically marked organelles, but the condensate component currently overpromises.","The proposal addresses a conceptually important area: the diversity of bacterial compartmentalization mechanisms. Including condensates broadens the project beyond operon-defined organelles and could stimulate productive interaction between bacterial cell biology, biophysics, and computational biology. The planned integration of disorder prediction, domain annotation, gene neighborhood analysis, and expression data could generate useful candidate lists. The team includes investigators with relevant condensate expertise.","The condensate identification strategy is weak relative to the claims. Phaseek predictions alone cannot establish condensate formation, and the proposal lacks bacterial-specific benchmarking, negative controls, orthogonal sequence features, and clear criteria for high-confidence calls. Co-expression networks do not demonstrate physical condensate interaction or phase separation. The proposal risks conflating intrinsically disordered or low-complexity proteins with bona fide condensate-forming systems. The integration of condensates with other organelle classes is conceptually interesting but underdeveloped.",3,"The overall project is worthwhile, but the condensate component needs substantial refinement. If the proposal limited condensate analyses to candidate generation while emphasizing stronger marker-based organelle classes, it would be more convincing.",5,Biomolecular condensates and bacterial organelles are direct examples of emergent cellular organization arising from molecular interactions. The proposal’s focus on coordinated expression and compartmentalization is highly relevant to emergence phenomena.,4,"A cross-organelle comparison of bacterial compartmentalization in gut communities is novel. The condensate aspect could be significant if handled carefully, but the current plan may not yield reliable biological conclusions.",2,"For condensates, the rigor is limited by reliance on a machine-learning predictor without adequate validation, benchmarking, or false-positive control. The approaches for genetically marked organelles are more solid, but the proposal presents all classes together without sufficiently acknowledging differences in evidentiary strength.",3,"The project is ambitious, especially because condensates require distinct analysis logic from operon-defined compartments. The timeline may be feasible if condensates are scoped as exploratory candidate discovery rather than definitive cataloging.",5,"The proposal is entirely based on mining and integrating public omics datasets, making it a strong fit for synthesis research.",4,"The proposal identifies useful gut microbiome datasets and public annotation tools. For condensates specifically, it does not identify appropriate training, benchmark, or validation datasets of known bacterial condensate proteins.",3,"The public database and publication plans are positive, but the proposal should provide more explicit plans for releasing prediction scores, confidence categories, training assumptions, code, and reproducible notebooks so users can evaluate condensate calls critically.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:49:35.700033,0,success,,"The proposal presents a compelling approach to exploring bacterial compartmentalization by incorporating biomolecular condensates alongside classical genetically encoded organelles. However, the methodology for identifying condensates lacks sufficient rigor, as it relies heavily on predictive tools that are prone to bias and lacks clear benchmarking for bacterial systems. Reframing the condensate component as a focused pilot study with strict confidence tiers would significantly strengthen the overall project.","The inclusion of condensates expands the study of bacterial compartmentalization in a conceptually valuable direction. The proposed integration of disorder prediction, domain annotation, and co-expression analysis offers a solid framework for generating candidate lists, supported by a highly capable research team.","The condensate identification strategy lacks bacterial-specific benchmarking, negative controls, and robust criteria to mitigate false positives in metagenomic data. Additionally, relying on co-expression networks and broad prediction tools risks conflating intrinsically disordered proteins with bona fide phase-separating systems."
baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,5,baseline::y2::11::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:29:22.412842,True,,"{""reviews"":[{""review_text"":""Reviewer 1: Senior bacterial cell biologist specializing in bacterial microcompartments and protein-bounded organelles. This proposal addresses an important and timely area: bacterial subcellular organization in natural microbial communities. The focus on bacterial microcompartments, encapsulins, ferrosomes, and condensates is well aligned with molecular and cellular biosciences, and the working group includes strong domain experts for several organelle classes. I am enthusiastic about the potential value of a curated, searchable resource that links organelle markers to metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic evidence. The proposal is strongest for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, where genetic markers and prior comparative frameworks exist. However, the scientific questions are somewhat diffuse, and the plan risks becoming an annotation exercise rather than a sharply hypothesis-driven synthesis project. The proposal would benefit from clearer criteria for distinguishing complete organelle systems from isolated homologs, explicit validation strategies for operon context in fragmented metagenomic assemblies, and a stronger statistical plan for testing co-occurrence and expression hypotheses. The condensate component is substantially less mature than the other organelle classes and may introduce many false positives. Overall, this is a strong but imperfect fit to the call: it uses public data, requires cross-disciplinary expertise, and could produce a valuable community resource, but the rigor of some analyses and the specificity of emergent-phenomena hypotheses need strengthening."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a compelling and underexplored question in bacterial cell biology: how organelles are distributed and regulated in complex microbial communities. The team has strong expertise in BMCs, ferrosomes, and condensates. The use of public metagenomic and multi-omics datasets is appropriate for the call. The plan to generate HMM-based detection pipelines and a searchable database could provide a lasting resource for the field. The focus on organelle co-occurrence and expression across ecological perturbations is potentially novel and significant."",""weakness"":""The hypotheses are broad and sometimes framed as exploratory cataloging rather than specific tests of biological mechanisms. The proposal does not sufficiently address false positives, incomplete assemblies, strain-level ambiguity, or the difference between detecting a marker gene and inferring a functional organelle. The condensate detection strategy relies heavily on Phaseek predictions without a clear bacterial-specific validation plan. Statistical approaches for co-occurrence, environmental responsiveness, and coordinated expression are underdeveloped. The open science and training plans are present but generic."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""A strong proposal with high potential to create a valuable synthesis resource for bacterial organelle biology, especially for genetically marked organelles. Its impact would be improved by sharper hypotheses, clearer validation and statistical frameworks, and a more cautious treatment of condensates."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Bacterial organelles and community-level regulation of compartmentalization are relevant to emergence in molecular and cellular systems. The proposal connects molecular components to higher-order cellular structures and ecological context, although the emergent properties are not always explicitly formulated as such."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Mapping bacterial organelles across the human gut microbiome using integrated multi-omics is novel and potentially significant. Prior work has cataloged some organelles across genomes, but expression and ecological regulation in complex communities remain underexplored."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The HMM-based approach is reasonable for BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, but key details are missing regarding thresholds, operon reconstruction, assembly quality, taxonomic assignment, false discovery control, and statistical hypothesis testing. Condensate prediction is particularly uncertain."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The scope is ambitious for two years, covering four organelle categories, multiple disease-associated datasets, multi-omics integration, database construction, and multiple manuscripts. The timeline is plausible only if the analyses are tightly prioritized."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is explicitly based on synthesis of existing public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data and does not propose new experimental data generation. It clearly requires integration across datasets and expertise areas."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies specific public resources, including GMrepo datasets and the IBD Multi-omics Database, and names relevant accession numbers. However, it could better document sample sizes, metadata completeness, pairing across omics layers, and suitability for the proposed comparisons."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to a public database and publication of the predictive framework, but the plan lacks detail on code repositories, workflow reproducibility, licensing, metadata standards, community governance, and long-term maintenance beyond a brief three-year statement.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 2: Senior microbiome systems biologist with expertise in human gut multi-omics and host-associated microbial ecology. This proposal has a compelling biological premise: bacterial organelles may be widespread and dynamically regulated in the gut microbiome, and public multi-omics data now make it possible to study these structures at community scale. The proposed use of GMrepo and the IBD longitudinal multi-omics dataset is appropriate and responsive to the NCEMS mission. I appreciate the effort to move beyond presence/absence toward expression, regulation, and potential coordination among organelle systems. However, as a microbiome proposal, it underestimates major challenges in comparing human gut datasets across studies, phenotypes, sequencing protocols, host covariates, medication exposures, diet, geography, and sample processing. The proposal lists disease states such as diarrhea, C. difficile infection, cystic fibrosis, IBS, and IBD as environmental perturbations, but it does not provide a coherent design for controlling confounders or distinguishing organelle regulation from shifts in community composition. The strongest part is the longitudinal IBD multi-omics analysis, but even there, the plan does not clearly state whether organelle expression will be normalized to taxon abundance, gene copy number, or total community transcription/protein abundance. Without these controls, claims about regulation could be confounded by changes in species abundance. I consider this a promising but methodologically underdeveloped proposal."",""strengths"":""The proposal uses relevant public microbiome resources and addresses an interesting gap: the ecological and regulatory context of bacterial organelles in human gut communities. The longitudinal IBD dataset is a strong choice for paired multi-omics analysis. The team’s organelle biology expertise is excellent, and NCEMS support could add important expertise in multi-omics harmonization and metadata standardization. The project could generate useful hypotheses about organelle distribution across health and disease states."",""weakness"":""The microbiome study design is not sufficiently rigorous. The proposal does not define sample sizes, metadata covariates, statistical models, batch correction methods, or controls for compositionality. Disease categories are treated as environmental perturbations without detailed attention to medication, diet, inflammation, antibiotics, age, geography, sequencing methods, or study-specific biases. It is unclear how the team will distinguish differential expression of organelle genes from changes in the abundance of organisms carrying those genes. The metaproteomic component may be technically difficult and less complete than implied."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The project is well aligned with public-data synthesis and has a compelling biological question, but the microbiome comparative framework requires substantial strengthening before robust conclusions about organelle regulation or disease-associated dynamics can be drawn."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses how subcellular structures emerge and function within organisms embedded in complex communities. The concept of organelle-level regulation across community perturbations fits the call, though the proposal could more explicitly connect community-level patterns to emergent cellular mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""Studying bacterial organelles through human gut multi-omics is novel and could be significant. The ability to connect organelle repertoires to microbial ecology and disease-associated states would advance both microbiome science and bacterial cell biology."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The proposal lacks sufficient detail on microbiome statistical analysis, compositional data handling, longitudinal modeling, batch effects, and normalization of expression relative to organism abundance. These omissions threaten interpretability of the key conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but possible for a descriptive analysis. However, robust cross-study harmonization, longitudinal modeling, metaproteomic analysis, and database deployment within the proposed period may be difficult, especially with multiple organelle classes."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is strongly synthesis-focused and relies entirely on public metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, and metaproteomic data. It is a good example of a question that benefits from integrating existing datasets across studies."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies several specific public datasets and databases. It would be stronger if it provided a table of sample counts, omics modalities, metadata fields, and data quality metrics for each dataset."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The commitment to a public database is positive, but the proposal gives limited detail on reproducible workflows, containerization, provenance tracking, FAIR metadata, or community standards for updates and reuse.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 3: Senior computational metagenomics and bioinformatics researcher specializing in genome-resolved microbiome analysis. The proposal is responsive to the call in its exclusive reliance on public data and its need for computational synthesis across large-scale omics datasets. The idea of building a reusable pipeline to detect bacterial organelle systems in metagenomic and multi-omics data is worthwhile. Nonetheless, I have concerns about computational rigor and feasibility. The proposed workflow says metagenomic data will be translated into protein sequences and scanned using organelle-specific HMMs, but it is not clear whether this will be done on reads, contigs, metagenome-assembled genomes, gene catalogs, or isolate genomes. This distinction is critical for operon-based detection, co-occurrence within organisms, and claims about taxonomic distribution. The requested resources mention 100 CPU hours per genome assembly and raw data per isolate, which is incongruent with a metagenomic synthesis project and suggests that the computational plan has not been fully specified. Inferring that two organelles coexist within the same organism requires genome-resolved binning or robust linkage; detecting both markers in a sample is not sufficient. Similarly, gene neighborhood analyses are difficult in fragmented assemblies. The proposal would be much stronger with explicit benchmarking against known genomes, positive and negative controls, reproducible workflow design, confidence tiers for organelle calls, and clear plans for MAG quality assessment and dereplication. I see high potential, but at present the computational strategy is too high-level."",""strengths"":""The proposal identifies a tractable set of organelle marker systems and uses widely accepted tools such as HMMER, Pfam, InterProScan, IUPred2A, MGnify workflows, and WGCNA. It proposes to synthesize public multi-omics datasets and produce reusable computational resources. NCEMS staff support for harmonization, quality control, and statistical analysis is well justified. The proposed database could be valuable if organelle calls are carefully benchmarked and annotated with confidence scores."",""weakness"":""The computational workflow is underspecified. It does not clearly define whether analyses will occur at the read, contig, MAG, species, or sample level. It lacks benchmarking, false-positive controls, assembly/binning strategies, quality thresholds, taxonomic classification methods, confidence scoring, and statistical models. Co-occurrence within organisms cannot be inferred from sample-level marker detection. The resource request includes confusing language about isolates and genome assembly that does not match the stated public metagenomic data synthesis focus."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""A potentially impactful synthesis project whose success depends on computational implementation. The current proposal is promising but needs a much clearer genome-resolved and statistically rigorous analysis plan to support its biological claims."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":3,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The project concerns emergence of cellular compartmentalization and possible coordinated organelle expression. However, much of the proposed work is marker detection and cataloging, and the computational plan does not yet convincingly connect the data to emergent mechanisms."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A public, genome-resolved catalog of bacterial organelle systems in the gut microbiome would be novel and significant. The multi-omics expression component could add an important layer beyond existing genome surveys."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The approach lacks key computational details required for robust metagenomic inference, including assembly strategy, MAG recovery, gene linkage, benchmarking, thresholds, error rates, and statistical testing. These are central to the proposal’s conclusions."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The computational scope is large but potentially feasible with NCEMS support if narrowed and better specified. Building pipelines, processing large multi-omics datasets, and deploying databases within two years will be challenging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is clearly based on synthesis of public data and integration of multiple omics types. No new experimental data are proposed."",""data_identification_score"":3,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal lists relevant datasets and accessions, but it does not adequately specify data types, sample numbers, read depths, assembly availability, MAG availability, or whether datasets are suitable for organism-level co-occurrence and expression analyses."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database commitment is useful, but a computational synthesis project should provide more detail on versioned code, workflow languages, containers, continuous integration, data provenance, documentation, and standards for reproducibility.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 4: Senior molecular biophysicist studying biomolecular condensates and phase separation in bacteria. I am pleased to see bacterial condensates included alongside more classical genetically encoded organelles, since condensates are an important frontier in bacterial cell organization. However, from the condensate perspective, this proposal is not yet rigorous. Unlike BMCs, encapsulins, and ferrosomes, condensates generally lack simple operonic signatures, and the proposal acknowledges this. The proposed solution is to apply Phaseek to predicted proteins, followed by co-expression networks and disorder/domain analyses. This may be useful for candidate discovery but is insufficient to identify condensates or infer their regulation in the gut microbiome. Condensate propensity predictors are trained under particular assumptions, often using eukaryotic or curated protein sets, and are prone to bias from protein length, low-complexity sequence, charge patterning, nucleic-acid-binding domains, and taxonomic composition. In metagenomic data, fragmented genes and annotation errors will exacerbate this problem. The proposal also does not define which bacterial condensate systems are known, how predictions will be benchmarked against them, or how false positives will be controlled. I would support the project more strongly if condensates were treated as a focused pilot with strict confidence tiers rather than as an equivalent organelle class. The broader proposal remains compelling, especially for genetically marked organelles, but the condensate component currently overpromises."",""strengths"":""The proposal addresses a conceptually important area: the diversity of bacterial compartmentalization mechanisms. Including condensates broadens the project beyond operon-defined organelles and could stimulate productive interaction between bacterial cell biology, biophysics, and computational biology. The planned integration of disorder prediction, domain annotation, gene neighborhood analysis, and expression data could generate useful candidate lists. The team includes investigators with relevant condensate expertise."",""weakness"":""The condensate identification strategy is weak relative to the claims. Phaseek predictions alone cannot establish condensate formation, and the proposal lacks bacterial-specific benchmarking, negative controls, orthogonal sequence features, and clear criteria for high-confidence calls. Co-expression networks do not demonstrate physical condensate interaction or phase separation. The proposal risks conflating intrinsically disordered or low-complexity proteins with bona fide condensate-forming systems. The integration of condensates with other organelle classes is conceptually interesting but underdeveloped."",""overall_numeric_score"":3,""overall_summary"":""The overall project is worthwhile, but the condensate component needs substantial refinement. If the proposal limited condensate analyses to candidate generation while emphasizing stronger marker-based organelle classes, it would be more convincing."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":5,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""Biomolecular condensates and bacterial organelles are direct examples of emergent cellular organization arising from molecular interactions. The proposal’s focus on coordinated expression and compartmentalization is highly relevant to emergence phenomena."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""A cross-organelle comparison of bacterial compartmentalization in gut communities is novel. The condensate aspect could be significant if handled carefully, but the current plan may not yield reliable biological conclusions."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":2,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""For condensates, the rigor is limited by reliance on a machine-learning predictor without adequate validation, benchmarking, or false-positive control. The approaches for genetically marked organelles are more solid, but the proposal presents all classes together without sufficiently acknowledging differences in evidentiary strength."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":3,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The project is ambitious, especially because condensates require distinct analysis logic from operon-defined compartments. The timeline may be feasible if condensates are scoped as exploratory candidate discovery rather than definitive cataloging."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The proposal is entirely based on mining and integrating public omics datasets, making it a strong fit for synthesis research."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies useful gut microbiome datasets and public annotation tools. For condensates specifically, it does not identify appropriate training, benchmark, or validation datasets of known bacterial condensate proteins."",""open_science_commitment_score"":3,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The public database and publication plans are positive, but the proposal should provide more explicit plans for releasing prediction scores, confidence categories, training assumptions, code, and reproducible notebooks so users can evaluate condensate calls critically.""},{""review_text"":""Reviewer 5: Senior open-science data infrastructure and molecular systems biology researcher. This proposal is a good thematic fit for a synthesis center: it brings together multiple labs, uses only public data, and aims to create an integrated resource that would be difficult for a single laboratory to produce. The proposed database of bacterial organelles, if well implemented, could be a valuable community asset. I also appreciate the request for NCEMS support in data harmonization, metadata standardization, and multi-omics integration; these are precisely the areas where a synthesis center can add value. However, the proposal’s open science and community-resource plans are underdeveloped relative to the ambition of the project. It states that the database will be publicly accessible and maintained for at least three years, but it does not specify governance, repository choices, data models, APIs, update cycles, documentation, licensing, persistent identifiers, provenance, or mechanisms for community contribution. The training plan is also brief and generic, mentioning open science and bioinformatics training but not defining trainee roles, learning objectives, products, or mentoring structures. As a working group proposal, the team has strong scientific expertise, but the plan would benefit from clearer evidence of diversity across institutions, career stages, computational roles, and community engagement. The scientific topic is compelling, but the project’s lasting impact will depend heavily on whether the outputs are reusable, reproducible, and trusted by the community."",""strengths"":""The proposal aligns well with the NCEMS model by synthesizing public multi-omics data and requiring collaboration among organelle experts, microbiome analysts, and NCEMS staff. It identifies specific public datasets and proposes a reusable searchable database. The project has the potential to train early-career researchers in bioinformatics and multi-omics synthesis. The intended outputs, including databases, pipelines, and manuscripts, are appropriate for an open synthesis project."",""weakness"":""The open science implementation is too vague. The proposal does not specify code repositories, workflow systems, containerization, data standards, database schema, licensing, APIs, documentation, versioning, provenance, or long-term sustainability beyond three years. Training and outreach are mentioned but not described in detail. The role of NCEMS staff is important but somewhat broad, and the division of labor between NCEMS and the investigators is not fully defined. The database maintenance plan may be insufficient for a community-scale resource."",""overall_numeric_score"":4,""overall_summary"":""This is a strong synthesis-oriented proposal with a potentially valuable community resource, but its open science, reproducibility, training, and data-infrastructure plans need more specificity to fully meet the expectations of a community-scale NCEMS working group."",""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"":4,""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"":""The proposal addresses emergence of bacterial subcellular organization and potential coordinated regulation of organelles in microbial communities. It is clearly relevant, although the proposal could frame the emergent systems questions more explicitly."",""novelty_and_significance_score"":4,""novelty_and_significance_justification"":""The integration of bacterial organelle biology with public gut microbiome multi-omics is novel and likely to produce useful resources and hypotheses. The significance depends on the quality and usability of the resulting database and workflows."",""rigor_of_approach_score"":3,""rigor_of_approach_justification"":""The analytical approach uses reasonable tools, but the proposal lacks enough detail on reproducible workflow implementation, validation, statistical modeling, and quality control. These issues are especially important for a public resource."",""scope_and_timeline_score"":4,""scope_and_timeline_justification"":""The timeline is ambitious but generally plausible with NCEMS support, especially if the team prioritizes a pilot dataset, pipeline refinement, IBD multi-omics integration, and database release. The number of promised manuscripts and database features may need prioritization."",""synthesis_focus_score"":5,""synthesis_focus_justification"":""The project is an excellent fit for synthesis-focused work: it uses only existing public data, integrates multiple data types, and requires expertise beyond a single lab."",""data_identification_score"":4,""data_identification_justification"":""The proposal identifies GMrepo, several accessions, MGnify resources, and the IBD Multi-omics Database. It would be improved by a structured data inventory with sample counts, metadata fields, omics pairing, quality measures, and reuse constraints."",""open_science_commitment_score"":2,""open_science_commitment_justification"":""The proposal commits to public outputs but does not provide a sufficiently detailed open science plan. A community-scale synthesis proposal should specify FAIR data practices, licensing, versioned repositories, executable workflows, containerized environments, public documentation, issue tracking, and long-term governance.""}]}",0,y2::11,y2,11,Discovery and functional analysis of bacterial organelles through mining and refinement of omics data,Rejected,Y Hoang; Arash Komeili; Hualiang Pi; Markus Sutter,4,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 5: Senior open-science data infrastructure and molecular systems biology researcher. This proposal is a good thematic fit for a synthesis center: it brings together multiple labs, uses only public data, and aims to create an integrated resource that would be difficult for a single laboratory to produce. The proposed database of bacterial organelles, if well implemented, could be a valuable community asset. I also appreciate the request for NCEMS support in data harmonization, metadata standardization, and multi-omics integration; these are precisely the areas where a synthesis center can add value. However, the proposal’s open science and community-resource plans are underdeveloped relative to the ambition of the project. It states that the database will be publicly accessible and maintained for at least three years, but it does not specify governance, repository choices, data models, APIs, update cycles, documentation, licensing, persistent identifiers, provenance, or mechanisms for community contribution. The training plan is also brief and generic, mentioning open science and bioinformatics training but not defining trainee roles, learning objectives, products, or mentoring structures. As a working group proposal, the team has strong scientific expertise, but the plan would benefit from clearer evidence of diversity across institutions, career stages, computational roles, and community engagement. The scientific topic is compelling, but the project’s lasting impact will depend heavily on whether the outputs are reusable, reproducible, and trusted by the community.","The proposal aligns well with the NCEMS model by synthesizing public multi-omics data and requiring collaboration among organelle experts, microbiome analysts, and NCEMS staff. It identifies specific public datasets and proposes a reusable searchable database. The project has the potential to train early-career researchers in bioinformatics and multi-omics synthesis. The intended outputs, including databases, pipelines, and manuscripts, are appropriate for an open synthesis project.","The open science implementation is too vague. The proposal does not specify code repositories, workflow systems, containerization, data standards, database schema, licensing, APIs, documentation, versioning, provenance, or long-term sustainability beyond three years. Training and outreach are mentioned but not described in detail. The role of NCEMS staff is important but somewhat broad, and the division of labor between NCEMS and the investigators is not fully defined. The database maintenance plan may be insufficient for a community-scale resource.",4,"This is a strong synthesis-oriented proposal with a potentially valuable community resource, but its open science, reproducibility, training, and data-infrastructure plans need more specificity to fully meet the expectations of a community-scale NCEMS working group.",4,"The proposal addresses emergence of bacterial subcellular organization and potential coordinated regulation of organelles in microbial communities. It is clearly relevant, although the proposal could frame the emergent systems questions more explicitly.",4,The integration of bacterial organelle biology with public gut microbiome multi-omics is novel and likely to produce useful resources and hypotheses. The significance depends on the quality and usability of the resulting database and workflows.,3,"The analytical approach uses reasonable tools, but the proposal lacks enough detail on reproducible workflow implementation, validation, statistical modeling, and quality control. These issues are especially important for a public resource.",4,"The timeline is ambitious but generally plausible with NCEMS support, especially if the team prioritizes a pilot dataset, pipeline refinement, IBD multi-omics integration, and database release. The number of promised manuscripts and database features may need prioritization.",5,"The project is an excellent fit for synthesis-focused work: it uses only existing public data, integrates multiple data types, and requires expertise beyond a single lab.",4,"The proposal identifies GMrepo, several accessions, MGnify resources, and the IBD Multi-omics Database. It would be improved by a structured data inventory with sample counts, metadata fields, omics pairing, quality measures, and reuse constraints.",2,"The proposal commits to public outputs but does not provide a sufficiently detailed open science plan. A community-scale synthesis proposal should specify FAIR data practices, licensing, versioned repositories, executable workflows, containerized environments, public documentation, issue tracking, and long-term governance.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:49:45.764539,0,success,,"This proposal strongly aligns with the synthesis center model by leveraging public multi-omics data to create a valuable, integrated database of bacterial organelles. However, the project's long-term impact is hindered by underdeveloped open science practices, community resource plans, and training frameworks. Clarifying the technical implementation, sustainability, and working group diversity will be critical for success.",The project effectively leverages public datasets and multi-lab expertise to build a reusable database and analytical pipelines. It aligns perfectly with the synthesis center model and offers valuable multi-omics training opportunities for early-career researchers.,"The proposal lacks essential technical details regarding open science implementation, including data standards, licensing, APIs, and long-term sustainability. Furthermore, the training plans are overly generic, and the division of labor with center staff remains poorly defined."
baseline::y2::2::claude-sonnet-5::001,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,1,baseline::y2::2::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:35:07.914448,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a structural biologist working on protein topology and folding, I find this proposal scientifically well-grounded and timely. The team clearly has deep domain expertise in knot/lasso detection (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, AlphaLasso) and has already published foundational work in this space. The idea of pinpointing evolutionary 'turning points' where topological complexity first emerges is a genuinely open and important question that has not been systematically addressed despite the explosion of AlphaFold-based structural annotation. The choice of well-characterized knotted superfamilies (SPOUT, UCH, carbonic anhydrase, etc.) as a starting point is sensible and demonstrates methodological maturity. However, the proposal is more of a natural extension of the PI's existing computational protein-topology program than a qualitatively new field requiring a large working group; much of the sequence-structure-topology annotation pipeline seems to already exist in the team's toolkit from prior papers. I would have liked more discussion of how structural inference will be validated at ancestral branch points where no structures exist—modeling ancestral sequences with AlphaFold-type tools carries real uncertainty about whether predicted topology at a branch point reflects true ancestral folding behavior versus prediction artifacts. Overall a rigorous, if somewhat incremental, synthesis project with a clear NCEMS computational resource need."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong prior work and clear familiarity with topology annotation tools (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, LassoProt, AlphaLasso); well-defined starting set of superfamilies; concrete, quantified computational plan (GPU/CPU/storage estimates); logical staged approach from knots to more complex entanglements."",
      ""weakness"": ""Heavy reliance on structure prediction at ancestral branch points without a clear validation strategy against experimental data; some secondary questions (pathogenicity, virus-specific patterns) feel appended rather than core to design; team composition doesn't obviously reflect broad disciplinary diversity beyond structural bioinformatics and evolutionary biology."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A technically solid and well-scoped synthesis project addressing an underexplored question in protein topology evolution, though the novelty relative to the PI's existing body of work and validation of ancestral structural inference could be strengthened."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein topological complexity emerging from simpler folds across evolutionary lineages is a clear example of emergent structural complexity in molecular biosciences, directly relevant to the call's focus."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The question of evolutionary origins of entanglement is novel, but the analytical approach largely extends existing tools and prior publications from the same team rather than proposing fundamentally new methods."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational plan is detailed with concrete resource estimates, but validation of ancestral structural predictions is underspecified."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The 24-month plan with staged knot-then-lasso analysis is reasonable, though covering 29 families/superfamilies plus multiple secondary questions in two years is ambitious."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project appropriately integrates multiple independent public databases (UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold, KnotProt, OMA, TimeTree) without generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Excellent identification of specific, appropriate public databases and tools directly suited to the questions posed."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal mentions an 'open, data-driven platform' as an outcome but does not explicitly detail data/code sharing plans or reproducibility workflows.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From an evolutionary biology and phylogenetics perspective, this proposal tackles an intriguing question—when and how topological entanglement arose within protein families—but the phylogenetic methodology described is somewhat underdeveloped for the scale of inference being proposed. Reconstructing precise 'turning points' of topological change on a phylogeny requires robust ancestral sequence reconstruction and ideally ancestral structure modeling with uncertainty quantification, not just annotating extant sequences via OMA orthologous clades and TimeTree divergence estimates. The proposal does not discuss how ambiguous or convergent evolutionary origins of entanglement will be distinguished from single origins, nor how tree topology uncertainty will propagate into confidence about the location of the 'turning point.' That said, the use of established comparative genomics/evolutionary databases (OMA, TimeTree) is appropriate, and the selection of both strictly conserved and non-strictly conserved knot topology superfamilies is a thoughtful design choice to probe generality. The addition of Dr. Cheng, an evolutionary analysis expert, strengthens the team, though the proposal could better articulate specific phylogenetic methods (e.g., ancestral state reconstruction models, molecular clock choices) rather than relying on general database integration."",
      ""strengths"": ""Thoughtful selection of both conserved and variable-topology superfamilies for broader evolutionary generalizability; recruitment of an evolutionary analysis expert; use of established phylogenomic resources (OMA, TimeTree); staged design moving from simpler knotted proteins to more complex lasso topologies."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lacks detail on specific ancestral reconstruction/phylogenetic inference methods; does not address how convergent vs. single evolutionary origin of entanglement will be disambiguated; uncertainty quantification for branch-point localization is not discussed."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An evolutionarily interesting question with reasonable database choices, but the phylogenetic inference framework needs more methodological rigor and explicit uncertainty handling to support strong evolutionary claims."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Tracing emergence of a novel structural feature (entanglement) across a phylogeny is a strong fit for questions of emergent complexity in molecular evolution."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Systematic evolutionary mapping of topological emergence across many protein families is a novel contribution beyond prior single-family case studies."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Phylogenetic and ancestral reconstruction methodology is not sufficiently detailed given the centrality of 'turning point' identification to the project's claims."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Given the complexity of robust ancestral reconstruction across 29 families, the two-year timeline seems optimistic without more automated, validated pipelines."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Relies entirely on integrating existing sequence, structure, and phylogenomic databases, consistent with the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Appropriate databases identified (OMA, TimeTree, UniProt) though could include additional ancestral sequence reconstruction tools (e.g., PAML, FastML) explicitly."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""No explicit commitment to depositing reconstructed trees, ancestral models, or workflows in public repositories is detailed.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational/data scientist reviewing this from a bioinformatics infrastructure standpoint, the proposal has a commendably concrete resource request (20TB storage, 75,000 CPU core-hours, 650 GPU-hours) that reflects genuine planning rather than vague computational needs. The scale of ~300,000 sequences across 29 families is substantial and appropriately matched to the requested infrastructure. However, I am concerned about data heterogeneity and integration challenges: combining UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold DB, KnotProt, LassoProt, MobiDB, PhosphoSitePlus, Human Protein Atlas, MaveDB, DepMap, Open Targets, DisGeNet, NCBI Virus, and GISAID represents an enormous integration burden across incompatible schemas, identifiers, and update cycles. The proposal does not describe a data harmonization strategy, common identifier mapping, or version control practices for reproducibility, which is critical when synthesizing this many disparate resources. The request for an NCEMS Staff Scientist with LLM and structure prediction expertise is reasonable, but the proposal would benefit from a explicit description of the software pipeline/workflow architecture (e.g., Snakemake/Nextflow) to ensure reproducibility across the two-year project and multiple team sub-groups."",
      ""strengths"": ""Concrete, quantified computational resource requests; sensible use of GPU resources for structure modeling; clear staged plan matching computational effort to project phases; ambitious but scoped-down initial dataset per family (~10,000 sequences)."",
      ""weakness"": ""No explicit data harmonization/integration pipeline described despite using over a dozen heterogeneous databases; lacks discussion of workflow management tools or version control for reproducibility; unclear how conflicting or missing annotations across databases will be resolved."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Solid quantitative resource planning, but the proposal underestimates and underspecifies the informatics complexity of harmonizing many heterogeneous databases, which is essential for robust, reproducible synthesis."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The topic fits emergent structural phenomena but the proposal is framed more as descriptive bioinformatics cataloging than mechanistic exploration of emergence per se."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining this many databases at this scale to trace topology evolution is a novel undertaking not previously attempted at this breadth."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Quantitative resource estimates are rigorous, but data integration methodology, quality control, and error propagation across mismatched databases are not adequately addressed."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope (29 families, over a dozen databases, two publications) is large relative to the requested 8 months Staff Scientist FTE; feasibility risk exists."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Exemplary synthesis-only design, drawing exclusively from an extensive set of public databases with no new data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Impressively comprehensive and specific list of relevant public data sources spanning sequence, structure, function, and disease domains."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not explicitly commit to specific open science practices such as public code repositories, FAIR data standards, or workflow sharing platforms.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing as a systems biologist interested in emergence phenomena broadly, I appreciate that this proposal engages with a clear instance of emergent complexity: how simple linear polypeptide chains give rise to complex, seemingly improbable 3D topological features through evolutionary processes. This is conceptually aligned with the funding call's interest in emergence in molecular and cellular systems. That said, the proposal's framing of 'emergence' is largely structural/topological rather than dynamic or functional emergence per se—it does not deeply engage with how entanglement might emerge from underlying folding dynamics, energy landscapes, or the physics of chain topology, which could add a stronger conceptual bridge to emergence theory more broadly construed. The functional correlation analyses (catalytic/allosteric sites, thermal stability via Meltome Atlas, disease association) are a nice attempt to connect topology to emergent functional properties, but these are somewhat scattered secondary aims rather than tightly integrated with the central evolutionary question. I would encourage the team to more explicitly connect their findings to general principles of emergent complexity in biomolecular systems, beyond a purely descriptive cataloging of when knots/lassos appear."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addresses a genuine and underexplored case of emergent molecular complexity; attempts to link topology to function (active sites, stability, disease) which speaks to functional emergence; multi-database integration approach is well matched to a synthesis-type project."",
      ""weakness"": ""Conceptual engagement with 'emergence' as a scientific framework is thin; secondary aims (pathogenicity, stability, disease) are loosely coupled to the central evolutionary question rather than unified under a coherent emergence framework; limited discussion of underlying biophysical mechanisms driving topological emergence."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An interesting case study in structural emergence, but the proposal would benefit from deeper conceptual grounding in emergence phenomena and tighter integration of its multiple secondary aims."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""While topological complexity is an instance of emergent structure, the proposal treats it primarily as a bioinformatics cataloging exercise rather than probing underlying emergence mechanisms."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic evolutionary framing of topological emergence across many families is a significant and underexplored contribution to structural biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Approach is methodologically reasonable for bioinformatics analysis but lacks deeper mechanistic/biophysical framing connecting topology emergence to broader principles."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Multiple loosely related secondary aims (pathogenicity, stability, virus-specific patterns) risk diluting focus within the two-year timeline."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Strong reliance on public data synthesis across sequence, structure and functional databases, consistent with program goals."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Good identification of diverse relevant data sources, though the functional/disease datasets (DepMap, MaveDB, DisGeNet) are more loosely connected to the central emergence question."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Mentions an 'open, data-driven platform' as an outcome, but does not specify concrete open science deliverables or standards to be followed.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a reviewer focused on team science, training, and open science practices, I find this proposal reasonably compelling in its collaborative design but lacking in some of the explicit commitments the call emphasizes. The team includes a newly added collaborator (Dr. Cheng and student Virangi Hewage) bringing evolutionary expertise, which is a positive sign of interdisciplinary team-building, and the mention of 'personnel exchanges' suggests some cross-institutional collaborative activity. However, the proposal does not explicitly discuss training plans for graduate students/postdocs beyond a passing mention of a student's involvement, nor does it detail diversity in career stage, institutional type, or geographic representation as encouraged by the call. Regarding open science, the proposal mentions producing an 'open, data-driven platform' but provides no specifics on repository choice, licensing, code/workflow sharing standards, or adherence to community data standards (e.g., FAIR principles). Given NCEMS's strong emphasis on open science and workforce training as core RFP criteria, this is a notable gap that should be addressed before funding."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addition of new collaborator adds evolutionary expertise and interdisciplinary breadth; explicit mention of personnel exchanges and in-person meeting suggests genuine collaborative intent; project scope clearly exceeds single-lab capacity given breadth of tools and expertise required."",
      ""weakness"": ""No explicit training plan or description of trainee roles/mentorship structure; no detailed open science/data-sharing commitments (repositories, licensing, FAIR compliance); team diversity (career stage, institution type, geography) not clearly articulated beyond one new collaborator."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A scientifically reasonable collaborative proposal, but it falls short of the RFP's explicit expectations around trainee development and open science commitments, which should be strengthened for full alignment with program goals."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The scientific question is relevant to emergent structural complexity, though the proposal's framing centers more on descriptive cataloging than broader emergence principles central to the call."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic, database-scale approach to mapping topological emergence is a novel and significant undertaking not previously done at this scope."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The technical approach is reasonably rigorous computationally, though collaborative/organizational rigor (task division, milestones per subgroup) is less detailed."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Ambitious two-year scope covering many families and secondary aims; feasible core knot analysis but expansion to lasso topologies and disease correlations may be rushed."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Clearly synthesis-only, integrating many public databases without new experimental data generation, matching program requirements well."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Strong identification of relevant existing databases across sequence, structure, and disease domains."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Lacks specific, actionable open science commitments (repositories, licenses, workflow sharing standards) despite the RFP's explicit requirement for this criterion.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::2,y2,2,From Protein Knots to Pierced Lassos: Unraveling the Evolutionary Origin of Complex Topologies,Accepted,Sophie Jackson; Jason Parsley; Eric Rawdon; Joanna Sulkowska; Ryan R. Cheng,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"As a structural biologist working on protein topology and folding, I find this proposal scientifically well-grounded and timely. The team clearly has deep domain expertise in knot/lasso detection (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, AlphaLasso) and has already published foundational work in this space. The idea of pinpointing evolutionary 'turning points' where topological complexity first emerges is a genuinely open and important question that has not been systematically addressed despite the explosion of AlphaFold-based structural annotation. The choice of well-characterized knotted superfamilies (SPOUT, UCH, carbonic anhydrase, etc.) as a starting point is sensible and demonstrates methodological maturity. However, the proposal is more of a natural extension of the PI's existing computational protein-topology program than a qualitatively new field requiring a large working group; much of the sequence-structure-topology annotation pipeline seems to already exist in the team's toolkit from prior papers. I would have liked more discussion of how structural inference will be validated at ancestral branch points where no structures exist—modeling ancestral sequences with AlphaFold-type tools carries real uncertainty about whether predicted topology at a branch point reflects true ancestral folding behavior versus prediction artifacts. Overall a rigorous, if somewhat incremental, synthesis project with a clear NCEMS computational resource need.","Strong prior work and clear familiarity with topology annotation tools (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, LassoProt, AlphaLasso); well-defined starting set of superfamilies; concrete, quantified computational plan (GPU/CPU/storage estimates); logical staged approach from knots to more complex entanglements.","Heavy reliance on structure prediction at ancestral branch points without a clear validation strategy against experimental data; some secondary questions (pathogenicity, virus-specific patterns) feel appended rather than core to design; team composition doesn't obviously reflect broad disciplinary diversity beyond structural bioinformatics and evolutionary biology.",4,"A technically solid and well-scoped synthesis project addressing an underexplored question in protein topology evolution, though the novelty relative to the PI's existing body of work and validation of ancestral structural inference could be strengthened.",4,"Protein topological complexity emerging from simpler folds across evolutionary lineages is a clear example of emergent structural complexity in molecular biosciences, directly relevant to the call's focus.",3,"The question of evolutionary origins of entanglement is novel, but the analytical approach largely extends existing tools and prior publications from the same team rather than proposing fundamentally new methods.",4,"The computational plan is detailed with concrete resource estimates, but validation of ancestral structural predictions is underspecified.",4,"The 24-month plan with staged knot-then-lasso analysis is reasonable, though covering 29 families/superfamilies plus multiple secondary questions in two years is ambitious.",4,"The project appropriately integrates multiple independent public databases (UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold, KnotProt, OMA, TimeTree) without generating new experimental data.",5,"Excellent identification of specific, appropriate public databases and tools directly suited to the questions posed.",3,"The proposal mentions an 'open, data-driven platform' as an outcome but does not explicitly detail data/code sharing plans or reproducibility workflows.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:49:55.187875,0,success,,"This proposal outlines a scientifically well-grounded approach to identifying evolutionary turning points in protein topological complexity. The research leverages the team's strong domain expertise and existing computational toolkits, presenting a rigorous but somewhat incremental extension of their prior work. While the project addresses an important open question and clearly justifies its computational resource needs, it would benefit from a stronger rationale for requiring a large working group.","The project is built upon a solid foundation of prior work, utilizing established topology annotation tools and a well-defined set of starting superfamilies. Additionally, the proposal features a logically staged methodology and robustly quantified computational resource estimates.","The methodology relies heavily on structural predictions at ancestral branch points without a clear experimental validation strategy to mitigate potential modeling artifacts. Furthermore, the team composition lacks broad disciplinary diversity, and certain secondary research questions appear loosely connected to the core project design."
baseline::y2::2::claude-sonnet-5::002,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,2,baseline::y2::2::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:35:07.914448,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a structural biologist working on protein topology and folding, I find this proposal scientifically well-grounded and timely. The team clearly has deep domain expertise in knot/lasso detection (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, AlphaLasso) and has already published foundational work in this space. The idea of pinpointing evolutionary 'turning points' where topological complexity first emerges is a genuinely open and important question that has not been systematically addressed despite the explosion of AlphaFold-based structural annotation. The choice of well-characterized knotted superfamilies (SPOUT, UCH, carbonic anhydrase, etc.) as a starting point is sensible and demonstrates methodological maturity. However, the proposal is more of a natural extension of the PI's existing computational protein-topology program than a qualitatively new field requiring a large working group; much of the sequence-structure-topology annotation pipeline seems to already exist in the team's toolkit from prior papers. I would have liked more discussion of how structural inference will be validated at ancestral branch points where no structures exist—modeling ancestral sequences with AlphaFold-type tools carries real uncertainty about whether predicted topology at a branch point reflects true ancestral folding behavior versus prediction artifacts. Overall a rigorous, if somewhat incremental, synthesis project with a clear NCEMS computational resource need."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong prior work and clear familiarity with topology annotation tools (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, LassoProt, AlphaLasso); well-defined starting set of superfamilies; concrete, quantified computational plan (GPU/CPU/storage estimates); logical staged approach from knots to more complex entanglements."",
      ""weakness"": ""Heavy reliance on structure prediction at ancestral branch points without a clear validation strategy against experimental data; some secondary questions (pathogenicity, virus-specific patterns) feel appended rather than core to design; team composition doesn't obviously reflect broad disciplinary diversity beyond structural bioinformatics and evolutionary biology."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A technically solid and well-scoped synthesis project addressing an underexplored question in protein topology evolution, though the novelty relative to the PI's existing body of work and validation of ancestral structural inference could be strengthened."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein topological complexity emerging from simpler folds across evolutionary lineages is a clear example of emergent structural complexity in molecular biosciences, directly relevant to the call's focus."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The question of evolutionary origins of entanglement is novel, but the analytical approach largely extends existing tools and prior publications from the same team rather than proposing fundamentally new methods."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The computational plan is detailed with concrete resource estimates, but validation of ancestral structural predictions is underspecified."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The 24-month plan with staged knot-then-lasso analysis is reasonable, though covering 29 families/superfamilies plus multiple secondary questions in two years is ambitious."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project appropriately integrates multiple independent public databases (UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold, KnotProt, OMA, TimeTree) without generating new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Excellent identification of specific, appropriate public databases and tools directly suited to the questions posed."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal mentions an 'open, data-driven platform' as an outcome but does not explicitly detail data/code sharing plans or reproducibility workflows.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""From an evolutionary biology and phylogenetics perspective, this proposal tackles an intriguing question—when and how topological entanglement arose within protein families—but the phylogenetic methodology described is somewhat underdeveloped for the scale of inference being proposed. Reconstructing precise 'turning points' of topological change on a phylogeny requires robust ancestral sequence reconstruction and ideally ancestral structure modeling with uncertainty quantification, not just annotating extant sequences via OMA orthologous clades and TimeTree divergence estimates. The proposal does not discuss how ambiguous or convergent evolutionary origins of entanglement will be distinguished from single origins, nor how tree topology uncertainty will propagate into confidence about the location of the 'turning point.' That said, the use of established comparative genomics/evolutionary databases (OMA, TimeTree) is appropriate, and the selection of both strictly conserved and non-strictly conserved knot topology superfamilies is a thoughtful design choice to probe generality. The addition of Dr. Cheng, an evolutionary analysis expert, strengthens the team, though the proposal could better articulate specific phylogenetic methods (e.g., ancestral state reconstruction models, molecular clock choices) rather than relying on general database integration."",
      ""strengths"": ""Thoughtful selection of both conserved and variable-topology superfamilies for broader evolutionary generalizability; recruitment of an evolutionary analysis expert; use of established phylogenomic resources (OMA, TimeTree); staged design moving from simpler knotted proteins to more complex lasso topologies."",
      ""weakness"": ""Lacks detail on specific ancestral reconstruction/phylogenetic inference methods; does not address how convergent vs. single evolutionary origin of entanglement will be disambiguated; uncertainty quantification for branch-point localization is not discussed."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An evolutionarily interesting question with reasonable database choices, but the phylogenetic inference framework needs more methodological rigor and explicit uncertainty handling to support strong evolutionary claims."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Tracing emergence of a novel structural feature (entanglement) across a phylogeny is a strong fit for questions of emergent complexity in molecular evolution."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Systematic evolutionary mapping of topological emergence across many protein families is a novel contribution beyond prior single-family case studies."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Phylogenetic and ancestral reconstruction methodology is not sufficiently detailed given the centrality of 'turning point' identification to the project's claims."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Given the complexity of robust ancestral reconstruction across 29 families, the two-year timeline seems optimistic without more automated, validated pipelines."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Relies entirely on integrating existing sequence, structure, and phylogenomic databases, consistent with the synthesis mandate."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Appropriate databases identified (OMA, TimeTree, UniProt) though could include additional ancestral sequence reconstruction tools (e.g., PAML, FastML) explicitly."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""No explicit commitment to depositing reconstructed trees, ancestral models, or workflows in public repositories is detailed.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational/data scientist reviewing this from a bioinformatics infrastructure standpoint, the proposal has a commendably concrete resource request (20TB storage, 75,000 CPU core-hours, 650 GPU-hours) that reflects genuine planning rather than vague computational needs. The scale of ~300,000 sequences across 29 families is substantial and appropriately matched to the requested infrastructure. However, I am concerned about data heterogeneity and integration challenges: combining UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold DB, KnotProt, LassoProt, MobiDB, PhosphoSitePlus, Human Protein Atlas, MaveDB, DepMap, Open Targets, DisGeNet, NCBI Virus, and GISAID represents an enormous integration burden across incompatible schemas, identifiers, and update cycles. The proposal does not describe a data harmonization strategy, common identifier mapping, or version control practices for reproducibility, which is critical when synthesizing this many disparate resources. The request for an NCEMS Staff Scientist with LLM and structure prediction expertise is reasonable, but the proposal would benefit from a explicit description of the software pipeline/workflow architecture (e.g., Snakemake/Nextflow) to ensure reproducibility across the two-year project and multiple team sub-groups."",
      ""strengths"": ""Concrete, quantified computational resource requests; sensible use of GPU resources for structure modeling; clear staged plan matching computational effort to project phases; ambitious but scoped-down initial dataset per family (~10,000 sequences)."",
      ""weakness"": ""No explicit data harmonization/integration pipeline described despite using over a dozen heterogeneous databases; lacks discussion of workflow management tools or version control for reproducibility; unclear how conflicting or missing annotations across databases will be resolved."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Solid quantitative resource planning, but the proposal underestimates and underspecifies the informatics complexity of harmonizing many heterogeneous databases, which is essential for robust, reproducible synthesis."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The topic fits emergent structural phenomena but the proposal is framed more as descriptive bioinformatics cataloging than mechanistic exploration of emergence per se."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Combining this many databases at this scale to trace topology evolution is a novel undertaking not previously attempted at this breadth."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Quantitative resource estimates are rigorous, but data integration methodology, quality control, and error propagation across mismatched databases are not adequately addressed."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The scope (29 families, over a dozen databases, two publications) is large relative to the requested 8 months Staff Scientist FTE; feasibility risk exists."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Exemplary synthesis-only design, drawing exclusively from an extensive set of public databases with no new data generation."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Impressively comprehensive and specific list of relevant public data sources spanning sequence, structure, function, and disease domains."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal does not explicitly commit to specific open science practices such as public code repositories, FAIR data standards, or workflow sharing platforms.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing as a systems biologist interested in emergence phenomena broadly, I appreciate that this proposal engages with a clear instance of emergent complexity: how simple linear polypeptide chains give rise to complex, seemingly improbable 3D topological features through evolutionary processes. This is conceptually aligned with the funding call's interest in emergence in molecular and cellular systems. That said, the proposal's framing of 'emergence' is largely structural/topological rather than dynamic or functional emergence per se—it does not deeply engage with how entanglement might emerge from underlying folding dynamics, energy landscapes, or the physics of chain topology, which could add a stronger conceptual bridge to emergence theory more broadly construed. The functional correlation analyses (catalytic/allosteric sites, thermal stability via Meltome Atlas, disease association) are a nice attempt to connect topology to emergent functional properties, but these are somewhat scattered secondary aims rather than tightly integrated with the central evolutionary question. I would encourage the team to more explicitly connect their findings to general principles of emergent complexity in biomolecular systems, beyond a purely descriptive cataloging of when knots/lassos appear."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addresses a genuine and underexplored case of emergent molecular complexity; attempts to link topology to function (active sites, stability, disease) which speaks to functional emergence; multi-database integration approach is well matched to a synthesis-type project."",
      ""weakness"": ""Conceptual engagement with 'emergence' as a scientific framework is thin; secondary aims (pathogenicity, stability, disease) are loosely coupled to the central evolutionary question rather than unified under a coherent emergence framework; limited discussion of underlying biophysical mechanisms driving topological emergence."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An interesting case study in structural emergence, but the proposal would benefit from deeper conceptual grounding in emergence phenomena and tighter integration of its multiple secondary aims."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""While topological complexity is an instance of emergent structure, the proposal treats it primarily as a bioinformatics cataloging exercise rather than probing underlying emergence mechanisms."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic evolutionary framing of topological emergence across many families is a significant and underexplored contribution to structural biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Approach is methodologically reasonable for bioinformatics analysis but lacks deeper mechanistic/biophysical framing connecting topology emergence to broader principles."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Multiple loosely related secondary aims (pathogenicity, stability, virus-specific patterns) risk diluting focus within the two-year timeline."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Strong reliance on public data synthesis across sequence, structure and functional databases, consistent with program goals."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Good identification of diverse relevant data sources, though the functional/disease datasets (DepMap, MaveDB, DisGeNet) are more loosely connected to the central emergence question."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Mentions an 'open, data-driven platform' as an outcome, but does not specify concrete open science deliverables or standards to be followed.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a reviewer focused on team science, training, and open science practices, I find this proposal reasonably compelling in its collaborative design but lacking in some of the explicit commitments the call emphasizes. The team includes a newly added collaborator (Dr. Cheng and student Virangi Hewage) bringing evolutionary expertise, which is a positive sign of interdisciplinary team-building, and the mention of 'personnel exchanges' suggests some cross-institutional collaborative activity. However, the proposal does not explicitly discuss training plans for graduate students/postdocs beyond a passing mention of a student's involvement, nor does it detail diversity in career stage, institutional type, or geographic representation as encouraged by the call. Regarding open science, the proposal mentions producing an 'open, data-driven platform' but provides no specifics on repository choice, licensing, code/workflow sharing standards, or adherence to community data standards (e.g., FAIR principles). Given NCEMS's strong emphasis on open science and workforce training as core RFP criteria, this is a notable gap that should be addressed before funding."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addition of new collaborator adds evolutionary expertise and interdisciplinary breadth; explicit mention of personnel exchanges and in-person meeting suggests genuine collaborative intent; project scope clearly exceeds single-lab capacity given breadth of tools and expertise required."",
      ""weakness"": ""No explicit training plan or description of trainee roles/mentorship structure; no detailed open science/data-sharing commitments (repositories, licensing, FAIR compliance); team diversity (career stage, institution type, geography) not clearly articulated beyond one new collaborator."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A scientifically reasonable collaborative proposal, but it falls short of the RFP's explicit expectations around trainee development and open science commitments, which should be strengthened for full alignment with program goals."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 3,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The scientific question is relevant to emergent structural complexity, though the proposal's framing centers more on descriptive cataloging than broader emergence principles central to the call."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The systematic, database-scale approach to mapping topological emergence is a novel and significant undertaking not previously done at this scope."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The technical approach is reasonably rigorous computationally, though collaborative/organizational rigor (task division, milestones per subgroup) is less detailed."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Ambitious two-year scope covering many families and secondary aims; feasible core knot analysis but expansion to lasso topologies and disease correlations may be rushed."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 4,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Clearly synthesis-only, integrating many public databases without new experimental data generation, matching program requirements well."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Strong identification of relevant existing databases across sequence, structure, and disease domains."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Lacks specific, actionable open science commitments (repositories, licenses, workflow sharing standards) despite the RFP's explicit requirement for this criterion.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::2,y2,2,From Protein Knots to Pierced Lassos: Unraveling the Evolutionary Origin of Complex Topologies,Accepted,Sophie Jackson; Jason Parsley; Eric Rawdon; Joanna Sulkowska; Ryan R. Cheng,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"From an evolutionary biology and phylogenetics perspective, this proposal tackles an intriguing question—when and how topological entanglement arose within protein families—but the phylogenetic methodology described is somewhat underdeveloped for the scale of inference being proposed. Reconstructing precise 'turning points' of topological change on a phylogeny requires robust ancestral sequence reconstruction and ideally ancestral structure modeling with uncertainty quantification, not just annotating extant sequences via OMA orthologous clades and TimeTree divergence estimates. The proposal does not discuss how ambiguous or convergent evolutionary origins of entanglement will be distinguished from single origins, nor how tree topology uncertainty will propagate into confidence about the location of the 'turning point.' That said, the use of established comparative genomics/evolutionary databases (OMA, TimeTree) is appropriate, and the selection of both strictly conserved and non-strictly conserved knot topology superfamilies is a thoughtful design choice to probe generality. The addition of Dr. Cheng, an evolutionary analysis expert, strengthens the team, though the proposal could better articulate specific phylogenetic methods (e.g., ancestral state reconstruction models, molecular clock choices) rather than relying on general database integration.","Thoughtful selection of both conserved and variable-topology superfamilies for broader evolutionary generalizability; recruitment of an evolutionary analysis expert; use of established phylogenomic resources (OMA, TimeTree); staged design moving from simpler knotted proteins to more complex lasso topologies.",Lacks detail on specific ancestral reconstruction/phylogenetic inference methods; does not address how convergent vs. single evolutionary origin of entanglement will be disambiguated; uncertainty quantification for branch-point localization is not discussed.,3,"An evolutionarily interesting question with reasonable database choices, but the phylogenetic inference framework needs more methodological rigor and explicit uncertainty handling to support strong evolutionary claims.",4,Tracing emergence of a novel structural feature (entanglement) across a phylogeny is a strong fit for questions of emergent complexity in molecular evolution.,4,Systematic evolutionary mapping of topological emergence across many protein families is a novel contribution beyond prior single-family case studies.,3,Phylogenetic and ancestral reconstruction methodology is not sufficiently detailed given the centrality of 'turning point' identification to the project's claims.,3,"Given the complexity of robust ancestral reconstruction across 29 families, the two-year timeline seems optimistic without more automated, validated pipelines.",4,"Relies entirely on integrating existing sequence, structure, and phylogenomic databases, consistent with the synthesis mandate.",4,"Appropriate databases identified (OMA, TimeTree, UniProt) though could include additional ancestral sequence reconstruction tools (e.g., PAML, FastML) explicitly.",3,"No explicit commitment to depositing reconstructed trees, ancestral models, or workflows in public repositories is detailed.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:50:05.616672,0,success,,"This proposal investigates the evolutionary origins of topological entanglement in protein families using a thoughtfully designed comparative approach. While the integration of established databases and the inclusion of an evolutionary expert are notable assets, the phylogenetic methodology requires further development. Specifically, the proposal lacks necessary details regarding ancestral state reconstruction, the differentiation of convergent versus single evolutionary origins, and the quantification of tree topology uncertainty.","The project employs a thoughtful, staged design that investigates both conserved and variable-topology superfamilies to ensure broad generalizability. Additionally, the proposal effectively utilizes established phylogenomic resources and benefits from the inclusion of an expert in evolutionary analysis.",The proposal lacks specific details regarding the methods for ancestral sequence reconstruction and phylogenetic inference. It also fails to address how uncertainty in branch-point localization will be quantified or how convergent evolutionary origins will be distinguished from single origins.
baseline::y2::2::claude-sonnet-5::003,20260709_162605,baseline,claude-sonnet-5,batch5,3,baseline::y2::2::claude-sonnet-5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,claude-sonnet-5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:35:07.914448,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a structural biologist working on protein topology and folding, I find this proposal scientifically well-grounded and timely. The team clearly has deep domain expertise in knot/lasso detection (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, AlphaLasso) and has already published foundational work in this space. The idea of pinpointing evolutionary 'turning points' where topological complexity first emerges is a genuinely open and important question that has not been systematically addressed despite the explosion of AlphaFold-based structural annotation. The choice of well-characterized knotted superfamilies (SPOUT, UCH, carbonic anhydrase, etc.) as a starting point is sensible and demonstrates methodological maturity. However, the proposal is more of a natural extension of the PI's existing computational protein-topology program than a qualitatively new field requiring a large working group; much of the sequence-structure-topology annotation pipeline seems to already exist in the team's toolkit from prior papers. I would have liked more discussion of how structural inference will be validated at ancestral branch points where no structures exist—modeling ancestral sequences with AlphaFold-type tools carries real uncertainty about whether predicted topology at a branch point reflects true ancestral folding behavior versus prediction artifacts. Overall a rigorous, if somewhat incremental, synthesis project with a clear NCEMS computational resource need."",
      ""strengths"": ""Strong prior work and clear familiarity with topology annotation tools (KnotProt, AlphaKnot, LassoProt, AlphaLasso); well-defined starting set of superfamilies; concrete, quantified computational plan (GPU/CPU/storage estimates); logical staged approach from knots to more complex entanglements."",
