    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior evolutionary biologist and phylogenomicist focused on protein family evolution. The proposal poses an intriguing evolutionary question: can the appearance of nontrivial protein topologies be mapped onto phylogenies to infer when entanglement arose? This is well suited to an NCEMS-style synthesis effort because it requires integration of structural databases, topology annotations, sequence databases, orthology resources, and evolutionary dating tools. I particularly appreciate the decision to use OMA for orthologous clade selection and TimeTree for divergence estimates, as well as the recognition that no single existing database currently contains the desired evolutionary turning-point information. However, from an evolutionary perspective, the proposal needs much greater methodological specificity. It does not describe how gene trees will be inferred, how orthology and paralogy will be distinguished, how domain architectures will be handled, or how branch points will be dated when gene and species trees conflict. The language of identifying the evolutionary branch point at which topology first appeared is attractive, but it may not be robust without ancestral-state reconstruction, explicit models of gain and loss, and uncertainty estimates. The proposal also does not fully address sampling bias: proteins with known topological annotations are enriched for organisms and families with more structural data, and AlphaFold predictions are not uniform in reliability. Overall, this is a worthwhile and potentially high-impact synthesis project, but it needs a more mature phylogenetic analysis plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a major gap in understanding the evolutionary history of complex protein structures. It correctly recognizes the need to combine orthology, structure, topology, and function annotations. The selected protein families provide a useful starting point, including both strictly conserved and non-conserved topology cases. The addition of an evolutionary analysis expert strengthens the team. The project could produce a valuable public resource of topology-annotated phylogenies."",
      ""weakness"": ""The phylogenetic methods are underspecified. There is little discussion of gene tree/species tree reconciliation, paralogous expansions, horizontal gene transfer, domain-level versus full-length protein evolution, uncertainty in divergence timing, or ancestral-state reconstruction. TimeTree provides species divergence estimates but not necessarily gene-family-specific timing. The proposal also risks overinterpreting topology emergence if topology calls are incomplete or biased toward better-studied organisms."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The proposal is conceptually strong and highly relevant to evolutionary synthesis, but the current plan is not yet rigorous enough in phylogenetic methodology to confidently deliver the claimed evolutionary turning points."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The emergence of knots and lassos within protein families is directly relevant to emergence in molecular systems. The proposal would be even stronger if it articulated a clearer theoretical framework for topology as an emergent property of sequence evolution and folding landscapes."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""A broad evolutionary synthesis of protein entanglement is novel and could significantly advance understanding of protein evolution. The significance is high for structural evolution, though the disease and pathogenicity extensions are less clearly justified."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 2,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The proposal lacks essential detail on phylogenetic inference, ancestral-state reconstruction, orthology/paralogy resolution, statistical confidence, and bias correction. These omissions are central to the main objective."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is aggressive. Producing high-quality phylogenies and topology annotations for many large families within two years is possible only with substantial narrowing, automation, and clear quality control milestones."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The work is fundamentally a synthesis of existing public databases and computational annotations. It fits the NCEMS requirement of integrating distinct datasets without new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies many relevant data sources, particularly OMA, TimeTree, UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold DB, and topology-specific databases. However, the proposal does not fully explain how these heterogeneous resources will be harmonized or how metadata inconsistencies will be resolved."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal promises an open platform and public outputs but lacks a detailed reproducibility plan. It should specify code repositories, workflow systems, versioned database snapshots, FAIR metadata, and policies for datasets with restricted access such as GISAID.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 3: Senior computational systems biologist and data integration scientist. This proposal fits the spirit of the call by proposing a community-scale integration of many existing public resources to answer a question not readily addressable by one laboratory. The scientific idea is appealing: build an integrated knowledge base connecting protein topology, phylogeny, sequence features, residue-level annotations, organismal context, disease associations, and stability measurements. I view the strongest deliverable as a reusable, open, topology-aware data platform and set of annotated phylogenetic trees. However, the proposal’s data integration plan is not sufficiently developed for the complexity of the task. Many of the named datasets use different identifiers, coverage assumptions, licensing terms, evidence codes, species scopes, and update cycles. The proposal does not describe a data model, provenance tracking, reproducible workflow engine, schema for topology annotations, or strategy for versioning database snapshots. The request for an NCEMS staff scientist with expertise in large language models is not well justified; no LLM-based method is described, and the primary needs appear to be bioinformatics workflow engineering, database integration, topology analysis, and phylogenetics. The project could be very valuable if it is reframed around a robust, FAIR, reproducible integration pipeline with clear benchmark datasets and uncertainty metadata."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal identifies a rich set of complementary data sources and has a clear need for integration infrastructure. It could produce a valuable community resource that connects topology annotations to evolutionary and functional information. The project is data-synthesis centered and has plausible computational resource estimates. The plan to stage analysis from knotted proteins to additional topologies is reasonable."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal lacks an explicit data architecture, workflow design, provenance model, versioning plan, or benchmark/validation strategy. It does not explain how conflicting annotations across databases will be reconciled. The stated need for LLM expertise appears disconnected from the actual analytical plan. Open science commitments are too general for a project whose primary output may be an integrated public platform."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A promising synthesis project with strong data-resource potential, but it needs a clearer informatics and reproducibility plan to meet the standards of a community-scale data integration effort."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The proposal addresses emergence of complex topology from molecular sequence and structure. It is relevant to emergent phenomena, though the conceptual framing could more explicitly connect topology to collective constraints in folding and evolution."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""An integrated topology-phylogeny-function resource would be novel and significant. The work could enable future hypothesis generation and protein design. The broad claims about therapeutic targets are more speculative."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The project names relevant analyses but does not provide a detailed computational pipeline, statistical framework, data harmonization plan, or reproducibility strategy. The approach is plausible but not yet sufficiently rigorous."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The proposed computational resources and two-year period seem broadly plausible if the team prioritizes a manageable set of families and automated workflows. However, functional and disease analyses across all datasets may need to be scoped more tightly."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""This is an archetypal synthesis project: it integrates existing public sequence, structure, topology, phylogenetic, functional, disease, and stability resources without proposing new experiments."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal lists numerous appropriate databases, but it does not adequately discuss database access constraints, identifier mapping, data quality, evidence levels, or long-term maintainability of the integrated dataset."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that outputs will be open but lacks operational detail. It should specify GitHub or GitLab repositories, workflow containers, workflow languages, data licenses, persistent identifiers, documentation, database snapshots, and how restricted-access resources will be handled.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 4: Senior protein biophysicist with expertise in folding, stability, and protein engineering. This proposal focuses on a fascinating class of protein structures whose existence challenges simple models of folding and stability. I am enthusiastic about the central premise that entangled topologies may have evolved to tune function, mechanical resilience, thermal stability, or regulation. The inclusion of Meltome Atlas, MobiDB, disulfide annotations, and functional-site databases could help connect topology to biophysical properties. The project is particularly attractive because it could move the field beyond anecdotal examples of knotted proteins toward statistically supported principles. That said, the proposal tends to overstate what can be concluded from existing annotations alone. Correlation between entanglement and stability, pathogenicity, or functional hotspots will require carefully chosen control sets matched by protein length, fold class, organism, expression level, subcellular localization, and phylogenetic relatedness. The proposal does not describe such controls. It also does not adequately address that some topological features may be consequences of fold architecture rather than independently selected adaptive traits. The design implications are premature unless the project includes a clearer bridge from evolutionary patterns to testable design rules. I would support the project if it focused more tightly on robust comparative biophysics of topology rather than a very broad set of disease and design claims."",
      ""strengths"": ""The biological question is important and exciting. The proposal could reveal general principles linking topology, stability, and function. It uses existing data sources well suited to structural and biophysical synthesis. The focus on both strictly conserved and variably conserved topology families provides a natural comparative framework. The project may generate valuable hypotheses for later experimental or design studies."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal lacks a detailed statistical plan for controlling confounders in comparisons between entangled and non-entangled proteins. The functional and pathogenicity claims are broad and not yet mechanistically grounded. The thermal stability analysis using Meltome Atlas may be limited by organismal and proteome coverage. The connection to rational design is speculative and not directly supported by the proposed analyses."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A compelling protein biophysics synthesis proposal with high potential, but it should narrow some claims and strengthen comparative controls before conclusions about adaptive function, stability, or design are accepted."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Entangled protein topology is an excellent molecular emergent phenomenon, arising from the global arrangement of the chain rather than local sequence features alone. The proposal directly addresses how such topology emerges and persists."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The attempt to systematically connect topology with evolutionary and functional features is novel and potentially significant for protein folding and engineering. The significance is somewhat diluted by broad and speculative extensions to pathogenicity and therapeutic targeting."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The basic comparative approach is sound, but the proposal does not describe matched controls, null models, correction for phylogenetic non-independence, or uncertainty propagation from structure prediction and topology annotation."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The staged plan is appropriate, but the combined goals of topology mapping, functional-site analysis, disorder analysis, stability analysis, pathogenicity analysis, and design-framework development are too broad for the proposed timeline."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The work relies on existing public data and computational integration. The proposed structural modeling is derived from existing sequences and does not involve new experimental data."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies relevant databases for topology, structure, sequence, stability, disorder, functional sites, and disease. It should more clearly state coverage limitations and evidence standards for each resource."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal mentions open outputs and a data-driven platform, but lacks a specific plan for sharing code, processed data, model structures, phylogenetic trees, and documentation in reproducible form.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 5: Senior infectious disease systems biologist and open-science training leader. I evaluated this proposal with attention to the call’s goals of community-scale synthesis, broad collaboration, open science, and workforce training. The project is scientifically interesting and makes good use of existing databases. Its potential contribution to understanding protein topology in pathogens is intriguing, especially if entangled structures affect immune evasion, protein stability, host interaction, or mutational tolerance. However, the proposal’s pathogen and disease components feel secondary and underdeveloped. It lists NCBI Virus, GISAID, DepMap, Open Targets, DisGeNet, and MaveDB, but does not explain how pathogen-specific hypotheses will be tested or how disease associations will be distinguished from general annotation bias. GISAID is not fully open in the same way as many public repositories, which raises issues for reproducibility and redistribution. From the perspective of the NCEMS call, I also find the training and open team science components too thin. The proposal mentions a student and personnel exchanges, but it does not articulate a training plan for graduate students and postdocs, mechanisms for cross-disciplinary onboarding, authorship and collaboration policies, or community engagement. The project has promise, but it would benefit from a stronger plan for inclusive working-group operation, trainee development, and reproducible public dissemination."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project is well aligned with synthesis research and uses only existing experimental and public computational data resources. It addresses a novel structural biology question with potential relevance to pathogen biology. The requested NCEMS staff and computational resources are connected to a genuine need for large-scale integration. The working group concept is appropriate because topology, structural bioinformatics, phylogenetics, and disease annotation require distinct expertise."",
      ""weakness"": ""The pathogen and disease analyses are not sufficiently developed and may be vulnerable to annotation and sampling biases. The proposal does not provide a robust training plan despite the call’s emphasis on developing a data-savvy workforce. The open science plan is generic. Team diversity, geographic breadth, career-stage diversity, institutional breadth, and governance are not described in enough detail. Use of GISAID may conflict with fully open reproducibility expectations."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The core scientific synthesis is promising, but the proposal is weaker on NCEMS priorities related to open science, trainee development, working-group governance, and the pathogen/disease analysis plan."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""The core focus on protein entanglement is relevant to emergent molecular organization. The pathogen-focused portion could also be relevant but is not developed enough to show how emergence specifically affects host-pathogen biology."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 3,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The topology-evolution question is novel and significant, but the pathogen, disease, and therapeutic claims are speculative. The proposal would be more significant if it defined a smaller number of testable biological hypotheses."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The overall integration plan is plausible, but the disease and pathogen analyses lack controls for database coverage, organismal sampling, phylogenetic relatedness, and annotation bias. Methods for statistical testing are not specified."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The project is ambitious for two years, especially with broad extensions to viruses, disease databases, mutational scanning, cancer dependencies, and protein design. The timeline lists major outputs but lacks detailed milestones for training, data release, and community engagement."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The proposal clearly relies on synthesis of existing public datasets and does not propose new experiments. It meets the basic data-synthesis requirement of the call."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies many relevant databases, including several for disease and pathogen analysis. However, it does not sufficiently address access restrictions, coverage biases, identifier harmonization, or evidence quality across disease resources."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 2,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The proposal states that findings, data, and workflows will be public, but it lacks a concrete open-science implementation plan. It should include repositories, workflow containers, data licenses, documentation, release schedule, governance policies, and a plan for resources that cannot be openly redistributed.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::2,y2,2,From Protein Knots to Pierced Lassos: Unraveling the Evolutionary Origin of Complex Topologies,Accepted,Sophie Jackson; Jason Parsley; Eric Rawdon; Joanna Sulkowska; Ryan R. Cheng,3,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"Reviewer 2: Senior evolutionary biologist and phylogenomicist focused on protein family evolution. The proposal poses an intriguing evolutionary question: can the appearance of nontrivial protein topologies be mapped onto phylogenies to infer when entanglement arose? This is well suited to an NCEMS-style synthesis effort because it requires integration of structural databases, topology annotations, sequence databases, orthology resources, and evolutionary dating tools. I particularly appreciate the decision to use OMA for orthologous clade selection and TimeTree for divergence estimates, as well as the recognition that no single existing database currently contains the desired evolutionary turning-point information. However, from an evolutionary perspective, the proposal needs much greater methodological specificity. It does not describe how gene trees will be inferred, how orthology and paralogy will be distinguished, how domain architectures will be handled, or how branch points will be dated when gene and species trees conflict. The language of identifying the evolutionary branch point at which topology first appeared is attractive, but it may not be robust without ancestral-state reconstruction, explicit models of gain and loss, and uncertainty estimates. The proposal also does not fully address sampling bias: proteins with known topological annotations are enriched for organisms and families with more structural data, and AlphaFold predictions are not uniform in reliability. Overall, this is a worthwhile and potentially high-impact synthesis project, but it needs a more mature phylogenetic analysis plan.","The proposal addresses a major gap in understanding the evolutionary history of complex protein structures. It correctly recognizes the need to combine orthology, structure, topology, and function annotations. The selected protein families provide a useful starting point, including both strictly conserved and non-conserved topology cases. The addition of an evolutionary analysis expert strengthens the team. The project could produce a valuable public resource of topology-annotated phylogenies.","The phylogenetic methods are underspecified. There is little discussion of gene tree/species tree reconciliation, paralogous expansions, horizontal gene transfer, domain-level versus full-length protein evolution, uncertainty in divergence timing, or ancestral-state reconstruction. TimeTree provides species divergence estimates but not necessarily gene-family-specific timing. The proposal also risks overinterpreting topology emergence if topology calls are incomplete or biased toward better-studied organisms.",3,"The proposal is conceptually strong and highly relevant to evolutionary synthesis, but the current plan is not yet rigorous enough in phylogenetic methodology to confidently deliver the claimed evolutionary turning points.",4,The emergence of knots and lassos within protein families is directly relevant to emergence in molecular systems. The proposal would be even stronger if it articulated a clearer theoretical framework for topology as an emergent property of sequence evolution and folding landscapes.,4,"A broad evolutionary synthesis of protein entanglement is novel and could significantly advance understanding of protein evolution. The significance is high for structural evolution, though the disease and pathogenicity extensions are less clearly justified.",2,"The proposal lacks essential detail on phylogenetic inference, ancestral-state reconstruction, orthology/paralogy resolution, statistical confidence, and bias correction. These omissions are central to the main objective.",3,"The timeline is aggressive. Producing high-quality phylogenies and topology annotations for many large families within two years is possible only with substantial narrowing, automation, and clear quality control milestones.",5,The work is fundamentally a synthesis of existing public databases and computational annotations. It fits the NCEMS requirement of integrating distinct datasets without new experimental data.,4,"The proposal identifies many relevant data sources, particularly OMA, TimeTree, UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold DB, and topology-specific databases. However, the proposal does not fully explain how these heterogeneous resources will be harmonized or how metadata inconsistencies will be resolved.",3,"The proposal promises an open platform and public outputs but lacks a detailed reproducibility plan. It should specify code repositories, workflow systems, versioned database snapshots, FAIR metadata, and policies for datasets with restricted access such as GISAID.",/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:51:39.922033,0,success,,"The proposal presents a compelling synthesis project aiming to map the evolutionary origins of nontrivial protein topologies by integrating structural, topological, and evolutionary databases. While the foundational concept and chosen resources, such as OMA and TimeTree, are well-suited for this objective, the methodological approach to phylogenetic analysis is significantly underdeveloped. To achieve its high-impact potential, the project requires a more robust analysis plan that explicitly addresses gene tree inference, ancestral-state reconstruction, and inherent sampling biases in structural data.","The project addresses a significant knowledge gap by effectively integrating orthology, structure, topology, and functional annotations to explore the evolutionary history of complex protein structures. Furthermore, the selection of diverse protein families and the inclusion of an evolutionary analysis expert strongly position the team to produce a highly valuable public resource.","The proposed phylogenetic methods lack critical details regarding gene and species tree reconciliation, handling of paralogous expansions, horizontal gene transfer, and ancestral-state reconstruction. Additionally, the approach relies on species divergence estimates that may not reflect gene-family-specific timing, and it fails to adequately account for structural sampling biases or incomplete topological annotations."
baseline::y2::2::gpt-5.5::003,20260709_162605,baseline,gpt-5.5,batch5,3,baseline::y2::2::gpt-5.5::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gpt-5.5,0.9,2026-07-09T15:32:47.643156,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 1: Senior structural bioinformatician specializing in protein topology and structure-function relationships. This proposal addresses a compelling and underexplored question: how complex protein topologies such as knots, slipknots, and lassos arose during evolution and whether they carry functional consequences. The scientific premise is well aligned with the NCEMS call because it relies on synthesis of existing structural, sequence, topology, and phylogenetic data, and the question is beyond what a single laboratory could easily accomplish. The proposal is strongest in its clear grounding in existing specialized resources such as KnotProt, LassoProt, AlphaKnot, AlphaLasso, PDB, AlphaFold DB, UniProt, OMA, and TimeTree. The plan to begin with well-curated knotted families and then expand to lasso-type proteins is sensible. However, the approach remains somewhat underspecified in several key technical areas. The proposal does not describe in sufficient detail how topology calls from experimental structures and predicted structures will be validated, how AlphaFold confidence and domain boundaries will be handled, how false positives in knot and lasso assignment will be controlled, or how homologous but topologically divergent families will be aligned in a structurally meaningful way. The proposed modeling of ancestral or branch-point representatives is interesting but risks shifting the project from data synthesis into prediction-heavy analysis without a rigorous uncertainty framework. Overall, I find the project exciting and relevant, but its success will depend heavily on more explicit computational standards and validation procedures."",
      ""strengths"": ""The project tackles a fundamental and distinctive question in molecular biosciences: whether topological complexity is an emergent evolutionary and functional property of proteins. It uses multiple existing public resources and integrates structural, sequence, phylogenetic, and functional annotations. The proposed initial focus on known knotted protein families is appropriate and feasible. The working group includes complementary expertise in topology and evolutionary analysis, and the need for community-scale synthesis is credible given the number of databases and protein families involved."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal lacks sufficient detail on quality control for topological annotations, especially for AlphaFold-derived structures and predicted lasso assignments. It does not clearly distinguish experimentally verified topologies from predicted ones in downstream inference. The statistical framework for identifying motifs, disulfide patterns, disorder enrichment, pathogenic enrichment, or functional-site associations is not described in enough detail. The claimed ability to identify precise evolutionary turning points may be overly ambitious given uncertainty in phylogenies, incomplete sampling, domain rearrangements, and structural prediction limitations."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A strong and timely synthesis proposal with a compelling biological question and appropriate data resources, but it requires a more rigorous validation and statistical plan to fully realize its potential."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Protein knots and lassos are excellent examples of emergent molecular phenomena arising from sequence, folding constraints, covalent linkages, and structural organization. The proposal directly addresses how these complex topologies emerge over evolutionary time."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The evolutionary origin of protein entanglement is a novel and significant topic. Prior studies exist for selected families, but a systematic synthesis across knots, slipknots, and lassos would represent a meaningful advance. The significance would be strengthened by clearer hypotheses linking topology to function."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The data sources are appropriate, but the proposal does not specify enough detail on topology validation, phylogenetic uncertainty, structural alignment, multiple testing, null models, or treatment of AlphaFold confidence. The approach is promising but underdeveloped."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The two-year timeline is plausible for knotted proteins but ambitious for 29 families/superfamilies, approximately 300,000 sequences, branch-point modeling, and multiple functional/pathogenicity analyses. The staged plan helps, but the breadth may exceed the requested personnel effort."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The project is clearly centered on synthesis and integration of existing sequence, structure, topology, phylogenetic, and functional datasets. No new experimental data are proposed."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""The proposal identifies many specific and relevant databases, including UniProt, PDB, AlphaFold DB, KnotProt, LassoProt, AlphaKnot, AlphaLasso, OMA, TimeTree, MobiDB, PhosphoSitePlus, Human Protein Atlas, InterPro, PROSITE, Meltome Atlas, MaveDB, DepMap, Open Targets, DisGeNet, NCBI Virus, and GISAID."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 3,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The abstract mentions an open data-driven platform and public workflows, but the proposal does not provide a concrete open science plan, repository strategy, licensing approach, reproducibility standards, or data governance plan. Use of GISAID may also complicate fully open redistribution.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewer 2: Senior evolutionary biologist and phylogenomicist focused on protein family evolution. The proposal poses an intriguing evolutionary question: can the appearance of nontrivial protein topologies be mapped onto phylogenies to infer when entanglement arose? This is well suited to an NCEMS-style synthesis effort because it requires integration of structural databases, topology annotations, sequence databases, orthology resources, and evolutionary dating tools. I particularly appreciate the decision to use OMA for orthologous clade selection and TimeTree for divergence estimates, as well as the recognition that no single existing database currently contains the desired evolutionary turning-point information. However, from an evolutionary perspective, the proposal needs much greater methodological specificity. It does not describe how gene trees will be inferred, how orthology and paralogy will be distinguished, how domain architectures will be handled, or how branch points will be dated when gene and species trees conflict. The language of identifying the evolutionary branch point at which topology first appeared is attractive, but it may not be robust without ancestral-state reconstruction, explicit models of gain and loss, and uncertainty estimates. The proposal also does not fully address sampling bias: proteins with known topological annotations are enriched for organisms and families with more structural data, and AlphaFold predictions are not uniform in reliability. Overall, this is a worthwhile and potentially high-impact synthesis project, but it needs a more mature phylogenetic analysis plan."",
      ""strengths"": ""The proposal addresses a major gap in understanding the evolutionary history of complex protein structures. It correctly recognizes the need to combine orthology, structure, topology, and function annotations. The selected protein families provide a useful starting point, including both strictly conserved and non-conserved topology cases. The addition of an evolutionary analysis expert strengthens the team. The project could produce a valuable public resource of topology-annotated phylogenies."",
      ""weakness"": ""The phylogenetic methods are underspecified. There is little discussion of gene tree/species tree reconciliation, paralogous expansions, horizontal gene transfer, domain-level versus full-length protein evolution, uncertainty in divergence timing, or ancestral-state reconstruction. TimeTree provides species divergence estimates but not necessarily gene-family-specific timing. The proposal also risks overinterpreting topology emergence if topology calls are incomplete or biased toward better-studied organisms."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 3,
      ""overall_summary"": ""The proposal is conceptually strong and highly relevant to evolutionary synthesis, but the current plan is not yet rigorous enough in phylogenetic methodology to confidently deliver the claimed evolutionary turning points."",
