    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a data scientist focusing on bioinformatics workflows and machine learning, I evaluated the data integration and computational infrastructure aspects of this proposal. The project aims to synthesize a massive amount of heterogeneous data (1D sequence motifs, 3D structural metrics, mutational scanning scores) into a unified predictive ML framework. The rationale for NCEMS support is one of the strongest I have seen; the PIs clearly understand that their bottleneck is data harmonization and pipeline parallelization, hence their specific request for NCEMS staff scientists (19 months total FTE). The preliminary data showing the filtering from 1149 down to 8 high-confidence sequences proves they have a working prototype. My primary concern lies in the vague ML description. Predicting 'functional conservation' from this data requires careful attention to class imbalance and data leakage (especially given evolutionary relatedness). They do not specify how they will split train/test sets or which algorithms will be deployed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Exceptional justification for NCEMS resources. The preliminary data brilliantly proves the feasibility of the filtering pipeline. Strong awareness of dataset heterogeneity and cloud workflow automation needs."",
      ""weakness"": ""The machine learning section is completely black-boxed. Without knowing if they plan to use simple Random Forests, deep learning (e.g., transformers), or graph neural networks, it is hard to evaluate the technical rigor of this aim."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A structurally sound proposal with a perfect use-case for a synthesis center. The team clearly needs data engineering support to scale their prototype. The ML component needs to be fleshed out, but the database construction alone is worth the investment."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Tackles emergent phenomena by predicting macroscopic functional traits from mesoscale physicochemical patterning."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Creating a machine-learning-ready database of functionally constrained IDRs will be a massive boon to the computational biology community."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Pipeline rigor is high, but the machine learning methodology is severely lacking in detail regarding architecture, validation, and benchmarking."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Scales up a proven pipeline. Requesting NCEMS staff for workflow automation makes the timeline feasible."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""The entire project revolves around harmonizing disparate public data into a new, greater-than-the-sum-of-its-parts resource."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 4,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Great use of MaveDB and UniProt. Utilizing ProteinGym as an alternative is a smart data science choice for benchmarking."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Extensive use of CyVerse for shared compute and explicit plans for public database release.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""I review this proposal from the viewpoint of an experimental biophysicist who routinely struggles with the functional annotation of IDRs. For decades, our field has been bottlenecked by the paradigm that 'structure determines function'. IDRs defy this, and their sequence divergence makes BLAST-based annotation nearly useless. This proposal seeks to solve this long-standing puzzle by anchoring sequence alignment on experimentally verified functional constraints (SLiMs, PTMs, DMS). The team assembled (Mittal, Soranno, Zheng) is formidable and represents the exact cross-disciplinary mix needed. The preliminary data successfully identifying known players like p53, α-synuclein, and transcription factors validates the premise. However, predicting ELMs across 28,058 human IDRs will undoubtedly generate a massive number of false positives. ELM motifs are notoriously degenerate. The authors must incorporate a strong statistical background model to filter these predictions before running expensive MD simulations, or their dataset will be swamped by noise."",
      ""strengths"": ""Addresses a massive, fundamental biological problem. The inclusion of Deep Mutational Scanning (DMS) data from MaveDB to ground the computational predictions in reality is a brilliant approach."",
      ""weakness"": ""Over-reliance on ELM predictions for the proteome-wide expansion. Short linear motifs occur randomly with high frequency in disordered sequences; without a strict filtering step for cellular context or colocalization, the false positive rate will be staggering."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 5,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Despite the inherent risks of false positives with motif prediction, this proposal tackles one of the most critical missing pieces in molecular biology today. The synthesis of experimental DMS with MD and bioinformatics is top-tier."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Directly investigates the mesoscale interface between sequence, dynamic ensembles (emergent structures), and cellular regulation."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Providing a curated list of functionally constrained IDRs will revolutionize how experimentalists design mutations for disordered proteins."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""Strong reliance on experimental ground-truth (MaveDB). Slightly dinged due to the lack of a detailed plan to handle false-positive motif predictions."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The progression from data harmonization to MD simulation to public release is logical and well-paced over the two years."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Integrates computational biophysics, genomics, and experimental perturbation databases. A true multi-lab synthesis effort."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Identified the premier databases for this work (MaveDB, ELM, IDRome)."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""The end goal is a community resource. This is exactly what open science should look like.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a community leader in systems biology and open science, my review focuses on the team dynamics, training, and community resource generation aspects of the proposal. The proposal aligns perfectly with the funding call's goal of synthesizing public data to answer long-standing puzzles. The creation of a curated database for conserved IDRs is a highly desirable community asset. The proposal demonstrates a clear need for collaboration among the Mittal, Soranno, and Zheng labs. They have clearly thought out their computational resource needs (NCEMS, CyVerse). However, the proposal explicitly asks for working groups that will 'Train the Next Generation'. While the proposal mentions training 'interdisciplinary scientists at the interface of computation and experiment' in Section 6, it completely lacks a concrete training plan. Are there dedicated workshops? How will graduate students or postdocs be mentored across these three distinct labs? Will trainees be leading the synthesis groups? The scientific merits are stellar, but the training component is an afterthought."",
      ""strengths"": ""Phenomenal alignment with the synthesis center's core mission. The project generates a highly valuable public database. Excellent justification of necessary resources and utilization of NCEMS staff."",
      ""weakness"": ""The training plan is almost non-existent. The proposal mentions training in passing but provides no structure, mentoring plans, or specific roles for trainees (graduate students or postdocs) within the Working Group."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""Scientifically, this is a 5/5 proposal that will yield a highly impactful community resource. Administratively, it misses the mark on the required 'Train the Next Generation' criterion, pulling the overall score down. A stronger mentoring plan is needed."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Appropriately addresses how function emerges from dynamic, unstructured molecular ensembles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The resulting database will be a novel tool for the entire biological community, addressing a significant gap in protein annotation."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The data synthesis pipeline is rigorous and well-supported by preliminary findings. Incorporates multiple scales of data."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""Clear milestones and appropriate use of NCEMS staff to ensure the database is built and deployed within 24 months."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Exemplifies the working group model by synthesizing disjointed data from 5+ different global repositories."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Comprehensive and well-chosen datasets that represent the state-of-the-art in IDR biology."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong data sharing plans via CyVerse, but could be improved by specifying open-source trainee involvement and community workshops.""
    }
  ]
}",0,y2::4,y2,4,Conserved Disorder: Learning Sequence Conservation from Functional Constraints in Intrinsically Disordered Regions,Accepted,Jeetain Mittal; Andrea Soranno; Wenwei Zheng,2,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/human-proposals/human-proposals-y2.json,False,,,,,,"I review this proposal from the viewpoint of an experimental biophysicist who routinely struggles with the functional annotation of IDRs. For decades, our field has been bottlenecked by the paradigm that 'structure determines function'. IDRs defy this, and their sequence divergence makes BLAST-based annotation nearly useless. This proposal seeks to solve this long-standing puzzle by anchoring sequence alignment on experimentally verified functional constraints (SLiMs, PTMs, DMS). The team assembled (Mittal, Soranno, Zheng) is formidable and represents the exact cross-disciplinary mix needed. The preliminary data successfully identifying known players like p53, α-synuclein, and transcription factors validates the premise. However, predicting ELMs across 28,058 human IDRs will undoubtedly generate a massive number of false positives. ELM motifs are notoriously degenerate. The authors must incorporate a strong statistical background model to filter these predictions before running expensive MD simulations, or their dataset will be swamped by noise.","Addresses a massive, fundamental biological problem. The inclusion of Deep Mutational Scanning (DMS) data from MaveDB to ground the computational predictions in reality is a brilliant approach.","Over-reliance on ELM predictions for the proteome-wide expansion. Short linear motifs occur randomly with high frequency in disordered sequences; without a strict filtering step for cellular context or colocalization, the false positive rate will be staggering.",5,"Despite the inherent risks of false positives with motif prediction, this proposal tackles one of the most critical missing pieces in molecular biology today. The synthesis of experimental DMS with MD and bioinformatics is top-tier.",5,"Directly investigates the mesoscale interface between sequence, dynamic ensembles (emergent structures), and cellular regulation.",5,Providing a curated list of functionally constrained IDRs will revolutionize how experimentalists design mutations for disordered proteins.,4,Strong reliance on experimental ground-truth (MaveDB). Slightly dinged due to the lack of a detailed plan to handle false-positive motif predictions.,4,The progression from data harmonization to MD simulation to public release is logical and well-paced over the two years.,5,"Integrates computational biophysics, genomics, and experimental perturbation databases. A true multi-lab synthesis effort.",5,"Identified the premier databases for this work (MaveDB, ELM, IDRome).",5,The end goal is a community resource. This is exactly what open science should look like.,/Users/eveyhuang/Documents/NICO/human-AI-proposal/data/reviews/ai_reviews/baseline/ai_reviews_baseline_complete_20260709_143810.csv,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0,review_neutralization_v1,2026-07-09T22:55:10.958312,0,success,,"This proposal addresses the challenge of functionally annotating intrinsically disordered regions (IDRs) by integrating experimentally verified functional constraints into sequence alignments. The interdisciplinary research team is highly qualified, and preliminary data successfully validate the approach on known proteins. However, the proteome-wide prediction of short linear motifs risks generating numerous false positives, requiring robust statistical filtering prior to molecular dynamics simulations.","The project tackles a fundamental biological challenge by moving beyond traditional structure-function paradigms to annotate IDRs. Furthermore, the integration of Deep Mutational Scanning (DMS) data provides strong experimental grounding for the computational predictions.","The proteome-wide expansion relies heavily on ELM predictions, which inherently risk high false-positive rates due to the degenerate nature of short linear motifs in disordered sequences. Implementing a rigorous statistical background model that accounts for cellular context and colocalization is critical to filter out noise before running computationally expensive simulations."
baseline::y2::4::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::005,20260709_162605,baseline,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,batch5,5,baseline::y2::4::gemini-3.1-pro-preview::review_call_001,eval_ncems_criteria_batch5,v1,gemini-3.1-pro-preview,0.9,2026-07-09T15:41:42.863205,True,,"{
  ""reviews"": [
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a computational structural biologist with expertise in molecular dynamics (MD), I find this proposal to be a highly compelling application of computational synthesis to a longstanding problem: the structure-function relationship in intrinsically disordered regions (IDRs). The proposal aims to bridge sequence evolution and functional constraints using a combination of bioinformatics and all-atom MD simulations. The integration of sequence-derived motifs with ensemble properties extracted from MD is exactly the kind of mesoscale emergent phenomenon this call seeks to fund. However, I have significant concerns regarding the computational feasibility of the MD proposed. Simulating 1,500 IDRs with explicit solvent to a degree where ensemble properties (radius of gyration, pairwise distances) converge is notoriously difficult, especially for highly disordered and extended states. Requesting 1,000,000 GPU hours for 1,500 sequences averages to ~666 GPU hours per IDR. Depending on the sequence length and force field used, this may not yield sufficiently converged ensembles to reliably train a machine learning model. The ML component is also vaguely described. Nevertheless, the integration of databases (ELM, IDRome, MaveDB) to identify these 1,500 sequences is robust and well-justified."",
      ""strengths"": ""The synthesis of disparate datasets (DMS, PTMs, evolutionary sequence data, and MD trajectories) is highly innovative. The preliminary data showing a filtering pipeline that successfully narrows down to a high-confidence set of functionally relevant IDRs (like p53 and α-synuclein) is excellent."",
      ""weakness"": ""The MD simulation scope (1,500 sequences) may suffer from undersampling given the requested GPU hours. The machine learning framework lacks algorithmic specificity (e.g., what model architecture will be used to integrate 1D sequence data with 3D ensemble data?)."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""An ambitious and conceptually brilliant proposal that perfectly fits the synthesis call by blending computational biophysics with multi-omics databases. It requires some refinement in its MD sampling strategy but offers a massive payoff."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 5,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Directly addresses how macroscopic function emerges from highly dynamic, disordered mesoscale protein ensembles."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 4,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""The concept of identifying 'conserved disorder' using a combination of DMS and transient structural elements is highly significant and shifts the paradigm from focusing only on folded domains."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 3,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""While the bioinformatics pipeline is rigorous, the all-atom MD simulations for 1,500 IDRs risk being undersampled. ML integration is presented as a buzzword without a detailed methodological plan."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 3,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""The timeline is extremely tight for the amount of data generation (MD) and data integration proposed within 24 months, even with the requested NCEMS FTEs."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Epitomizes the synthesis mandate by creating a novel framework exclusively from existing public databases (ELM, UniProt, MaveDB)."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Excellent identification of appropriate public databases. The inclusion of fallback options (MobiDB, ProteinGym, ClinVar) demonstrates great foresight."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 5,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Strong commitment to building a public, open-access database of conserved IDRs using CyVerse, perfectly aligning with community open science principles.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""Reviewing this from the perspective of an evolutionary biologist and viral genomicist, I am highly supportive of this proposal. IDRs are classically a nightmare for sequence alignment and conservation scoring because traditional substitution matrices fail to capture the physicochemical conservation that occurs despite primary sequence divergence. The inclusion of a 'Viral high-variation synthesis' utilizing GISAID is a stroke of genius. Viral proteins, particularly RNA viruses, evolve rapidly and rely heavily on IDRs for host-pathogen interactions. Contrasting human proteome IDRs with viral proteomes will provide immense insight into how evolutionary selection maintains function through charge patterning and SLiMs despite sequence hypervariability. My main critique is that the proposal treats orthologue databases (OrthoDB, Ensembl) as an 'alternative strategy' rather than a primary tool. Cross-species alignment should be step one in defining 'conserved' human IDRs, alongside the DMS and PTM filtering."",
      ""strengths"": ""The dual approach of looking at relatively conserved eukaryotic IDRs and highly variable viral IDRs provides a phenomenal evolutionary contrast. The use of GISAID to stress-test their conservation metrics is a major strength."",
      ""weakness"": ""The proposal sidelines traditional orthology (OrthoDB) as a backup plan. To claim a region is 'conserved', cross-species phylogenetic analysis must be central to the primary pipeline, not just an alternative validation."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A highly innovative proposal that cleverly leverages both host and viral genomic datasets to redefine what 'conservation' means in the context of intrinsically disordered proteins. The evolutionary insights generated will be broadly impactful."",
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_score"": 4,
      ""relevance_to_emergent_phenomena_justification"": ""Links evolutionary sequence variation to functional outcomes in dynamic proteins, though the emergent aspect is slightly overshadowed by the pure bioinformatics approach."",
      ""novelty_and_significance_score"": 5,
      ""novelty_and_significance_justification"": ""Developing quantitative metrics for conservation in IDRs that transcend simple sequence identity is a major conceptual leap for evolutionary biology."",
      ""rigor_of_approach_score"": 4,
      ""rigor_of_approach_justification"": ""The filtering criteria detailed in the preliminary data are highly rigorous. However, phylogenetic control mechanisms for the viral dataset are not deeply explored."",
      ""scope_and_timeline_score"": 4,
      ""scope_and_timeline_justification"": ""24 months is reasonable given they are not generating experimental data, but the integration of GISAID will require substantial metadata wrangling."",
      ""synthesis_focus_score"": 5,
      ""synthesis_focus_justification"": ""Requires the exact integration of distinct datasets (GISAID, MaveDB, ELM) that this RFP supports. No single lab does this alone."",
      ""data_identification_score"": 5,
      ""data_identification_justification"": ""Flawless identification of necessary databases, spanning mutational scanning, motifs, and viral genomes."",
      ""open_science_commitment_score"": 4,
      ""open_science_commitment_justification"": ""Commits to open-access hosting, though it lacks details on specific licensing (e.g., MIT, CC-BY) for the code and data outputs.""
    },
    {
      ""review_text"": ""As a data scientist focusing on bioinformatics workflows and machine learning, I evaluated the data integration and computational infrastructure aspects of this proposal. The project aims to synthesize a massive amount of heterogeneous data (1D sequence motifs, 3D structural metrics, mutational scanning scores) into a unified predictive ML framework. The rationale for NCEMS support is one of the strongest I have seen; the PIs clearly understand that their bottleneck is data harmonization and pipeline parallelization, hence their specific request for NCEMS staff scientists (19 months total FTE). The preliminary data showing the filtering from 1149 down to 8 high-confidence sequences proves they have a working prototype. My primary concern lies in the vague ML description. Predicting 'functional conservation' from this data requires careful attention to class imbalance and data leakage (especially given evolutionary relatedness). They do not specify how they will split train/test sets or which algorithms will be deployed."",
      ""strengths"": ""Exceptional justification for NCEMS resources. The preliminary data brilliantly proves the feasibility of the filtering pipeline. Strong awareness of dataset heterogeneity and cloud workflow automation needs."",
      ""weakness"": ""The machine learning section is completely black-boxed. Without knowing if they plan to use simple Random Forests, deep learning (e.g., transformers), or graph neural networks, it is hard to evaluate the technical rigor of this aim."",
      ""overall_numeric_score"": 4,
      ""overall_summary"": ""A structurally sound proposal with a perfect use-case for a synthesis center. The team clearly needs data engineering support to scale their prototype. The ML component needs to be fleshed out, but the database construction alone is worth the investment."",
